window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['blomberg/kemp'] = {"blomberg-s01-001":["All our modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] are acquired",[]],"blomberg-s01-002":["(1) through experience;",[]],"blomberg-s01-003":["(2) through our own exercise; and (3) through instruction.",[]],"blomberg-s01-004":["From learning [*Erlernung*] there arises the spirit of imitation. This learning takes place in childhood. There are, indeed, certain skills which we acquire through imitation; but he who imitates shows the least degree of ability.",[]],"blomberg-s01-005":["The first origin of our knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] is empirical. Many things arise through copying [*Nachahmung*], and many modes of knowledge through imitation.",[]],"blomberg-s01-006":["We see what others have done before us, and we do it after them.",[]],"blomberg-s01-007":["The second kind is the exercise of the skill we have acquired. Through frequent exercise of our insights and talents we attain to a greater knowledge and to new insights, and these can be still further enlarged through our own experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-008":["The last [kind] is to attain to knowledge through universal rules; these are derived from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-009":["The first method of attaining to modes of knowledge must be abandoned as soon as our abilities acquire the capacity to cultivate [*excoliren*] themselves. We must seek to think for ourselves and to judge for ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s01-010":["This kind of exercise of our abilities attains the very same degree even when we think in opposition to what others have thought.",[]],"blomberg-s01-011":["The natural understanding has its own laws, according to which alone it can proceed. These are entitled natural laws.",[]],"blomberg-s01-012":["Besides the natural laws there are also precepts [*Vorschriften*], which are for the artificial understanding. The first [stage] is to proceed altogether without laws, and to make use neither of the natural [ ... ] laws nor of the precepts. If we then wish to cultivate [*excoliren*] our own reason, we first make use of the natural laws of reason. This takes place when we think often, or through exercise and repeated attempts, that is, when we consider an object according to particular as well as universal laws.",[]],"blomberg-s01-013":["The second use of the understanding takes place according to artificial rules, or according to precepts; and from this there arises the artificial understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-014":["We first learn to walk through being led; and so too we learn to think through first of all imitating. Thereupon we begin to use our feet by ourselves and unaided. In like manner we begin to judge for ourselves, and to use our understanding in judging for ourselves through frequent attempts, repeated by ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s01-015":["The merely natural laws are cultivated [*excoliret*] through experience; for the artificial use, however, I must have other rules. First we must imitate, then exercise ourselves, and last of all we receive precepts.",[]],"blomberg-s01-016":["Through imitation we can acquire crafts [*Handwerke*]; through precepts, however, a science. Versification may, as it were, be compared with the crafts.",[]],"blomberg-s01-017":["To judge of the beautiful we learn through rules; to compose beautifully, however, through frequent exercise.",[]],"blomberg-s01-018":["All knowledge takes place according to rules. These are either merely natural or artificial.",[]],"blomberg-s01-019":["The natural rules pertain to the common and sound understanding and to sound reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-020":["The artificial rules are the rules of learning [*Gelahrtsamkeit*]. The use of the former [i.e. the natural rules] we acquire not through imitation but through the frequent use of our reason; it is thereby that the common understanding is cultivated [*excoliret*].",[]],"blomberg-s01-021":["The common use of the understanding is that which is concerned with objects of experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-022":["The artificial [use] is that which proceeds according to precepts and rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-023":["Nowadays there is much talk of sound understanding, and it is praised too highly. Some have believed that sound reason differs from the sciences merely in degree. But this yields no clear boundary where the one ceases and the other begins. Sound reason and learning differ not merely in degree but also in kind [*specie*]; they are two distinct sources, each of its own sort.",[]],"blomberg-s01-024":["He is entitled a learned man whose learning is not grounded on common experience. In common usage all understanding is entitled common understanding in so far as it is met with in the same degree in most people.",[]],"blomberg-s01-025":["It is, however, a sound understanding when it is correct. It is not sound in virtue of its degree. Its correctness consists rather in this, that understanding and reason judge according to such laws as agree with experience. But if there are no other *principia* than experiences, sound reason none the less remains common reason. There is also an instructed understanding, which could not have been acquired through common experience; and this is a science. There is a logic [*Logica*] of the common understanding and of sound reason, and a logic of learning. The latter serves to enrich the common and sound understanding, and to cultivate [*excoliren*] this sound reason until it becomes learning.",[]],"blomberg-s01-026":["The logic of sound reason will have our own experience as its *principium*, and will therefore be empirical. It will not contain the rules according to which we ought to think, but will indicate the rules according to which we commonly do think. There are two kinds of rules. (A) The rules according to which we do proceed: these are laws of appearance, and are subjective. These subjective laws are the laws according to which reason commonly proceeds in judging and thinking. (B) Objective laws, according to which the understanding ought to proceed. These are entitled *dogmata*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-027":["The subjective laws set forth the errors. Sound reason is extended by attending to the rules according to which the understanding ought to proceed.",[]],"blomberg-s01-028":["The objective laws we extend through doctrines. To cultivate [*excoliren*] sound reason, however, we must give no universal rules and precepts; [whereas] learned reason is not grounded on empirical *principia*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-029":["The common understanding is the understanding [that judges] according to laws of experience; common reason, again, is the reason [that infers] according to laws of experience. This reason is called common because it is the lowest degree in respect of all the objects that come before us in this way. On the occasion of experience we always have a standard [*Maßstab*] with which we compare magnitudes with one another. We proceed thus when we wish to find the mean magnitude. We take that of which we wish to find the mean magnitude, for instance man; we count the size of each man by the standard, add these together, and divide them by the number of men, [including men] whom we have never seen in our lives; and then there results the mean size which is appropriate to every man. In the same way the mean magnitude of the understanding is arrived at from the understanding which we know through common experience. The common understanding is the measure. The common and sound understanding is a small but correct understanding, and its knowledge is of great usefulness. In a tract of country inhabited by peasants there are not nearly so many errors to be found as in a district of the learned of equal size. For the peasant always follows the guiding thread of experience: he judges about little, but about that little correctly. The exercises of common and sound reason are also easy. All knowledge from experience has more life and more of the intuitive than that which has arisen from universal concepts. The first advantage of common reason is simplicity; the second, correctness; the third, usefulness; the fourth, ease; and the fifth, clearness and vividness. For these modes of knowledge arise with the light with which the representations of experience are endowed. Sound reason will accordingly be sufficient for the fine arts [*schöne Wissenschaften*].",[]],"blomberg-s01-030":["Taste really requires no learning whatsoever in order to be quite perfect. A learned taste is in fact already a false, a corrupted taste.",[]],"blomberg-s01-031":["What is beautiful must please universally and everyone. Experience is required for judging of beauty, and the judgment of the beautiful and the ugly is passed in accordance with common and sound reason. Common and sound reason lies at the basis of all learning; learning becomes ridiculous if it is not grafted on to this stock. In history we require no sound understanding, and the same holds even of many mathematical propositions. But in philosophy, and in all other sciences in which the mere imitation of another's understanding does not suffice, but the spirit of genius itself is necessary, the common understanding is indispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s01-032":["The use of all the modes of knowledge of the learned, and their application, is possible only through sound reason. Someone may indeed be a learned man without a sound understanding, but such a man is a ridiculous pedant. All learning is either historical or rational. Historical knowledge is that which is borrowed, as regards its form, from common reason, and, as regards its matter, from common experiences. Historical learning comprises history, geography, etc. A historically learned man may also make reflections [*reflexiones*], but these already belong to rational learning. The matter in rational learning may also be grasped through common reason and experience; the form, however, is quite different. Men find more delight in the capacity of being able to procure themselves some pleasure than in the pleasure itself. When I derive the particular from the universal, I employ understanding; but when I infer the universal from particular cases, I require another method. The understanding knows everything *a priori*, sound reason *a posteriori*. We have modes of knowledge which are empirical, in which we ascend from below upwards; and in the rational we descend from above downwards. Logic, which is an *organon* of rational learning, is concerned not with historical but with rational modes of knowledge. Logic, as an *organon*, can prescribe rules for a learning already in existence, and it is then entitled *logica critica*; or [it can prescribe] rules whereby we may attain to learning, and it is then entitled *logica dogmatica*. All logics are not dogmatic but critical. Philosophy requires more genius than imitation.",[]],"blomberg-s01-033":["The whole of nature moves according to rules. Thus water moves according to hydraulic rules; nature operates according to rules; even inconstant weather has its definite rules, although we do not perceive them. Animals move according to rules of which they are often not even conscious. Man acts according to rules, and in particular employs his understanding according to certain propositions and rules. How often do men act according to rules without being conscious of them, as, for instance, in speaking their mother tongue? Our power is often exercised without our being conscious of it; and this, *primo*, because it already lies in our nature; [*secundo*], it often takes place through imitation, by means of an example which we imitate, in order gradually to employ the understanding in the way in which we see others employ it.",[]],"blomberg-s01-034":["(3) Our own use. Our own exercise brings us to a facility in using the understanding without being conscious of its rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-035":["As man learns to walk, so too he learns to think. This understanding, so formed, [is] *sens commun*. If it is correct, it is entitled the sound understanding, because the facility in using it is subject to a common use. The frequent use of our understanding in regard to objects of experience at length makes us competent to use it correctly. It can therefore become correct through mere exercise and use. The sound understanding is to be common; the understanding, therefore, which goes further than its senses teach it in experience is not common: that would be the learned use of the understanding. The sound understanding thus pertains to experience. It must proceed according to rules of which it is itself conscious; it is therefore not undisciplined. Only, it does not know these rules *in abstracto* but *in concreto*. Thus many trades require a sound and alert understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-036":["The causes of things do not fall within the senses so readily as their effects. We must therefore also know the rules according to which the sound understanding may be employed. In many cases we cannot rely upon our own exercise; the sole teacher of the sound understanding, however, is experience. Sound understanding and sound reason are, in the first place, of great necessity as the foundation of all abstract higher modes of knowledge. Sound reason is commonly opposed to the affected learning of proud scholastic wisdom. But this is not permissible, for learning always presupposes the sound understanding: art presupposes nature. They are indeed *distinctae*, but not *oppositae*. It has, however, become the fashion to praise the sound understanding to the detriment of the honour of the sciences. It is indeed no reproach to deny science to someone; but if someone has no sound understanding, he is by nature a minor, a child. From everyone, therefore, we demand at least sound understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-037":["There are certain intermediate kinds of perfection. The beauty of a woman, for instance, is, as it were, a merit; ugliness displeases; but a tolerable appearance is, as it were, the mean, which we demand, so to speak, of a human being. Beauty and ugliness single people out in a special manner. The same holds of human stature. Our eyes form for themselves a certain *idea* of the average, and, what is remarkable, men for the most part agree in it. Similarly, for instance, we speak of a man's means [*Vermögen*]: the mean between wealth and poverty we call being well-to-do. In all our concepts, therefore, we always make a mean our standard. So too the sound understanding is the mean among the abilities of men. Sound reason ought not to go a single step further than experience leads it. The sound understanding does not know and judge *in abstracto* but *in concreto*; it draws its judgments from experiences. It can, however, also know universally, *a priori* and *in abstracto*, much that experience teaches us. The sound understanding is thus the faculty of judging correctly *in concreto*. Very reasonable people are often quite unable to judge *in abstracto*, though they think just as often *in concreto*. So it is in moral cases: through an example in some occurrence, for instance, we know things *in concreto*. The understanding which can judge *in abstracto* is entitled the finer, scientific understanding. The natural faculty of judging is the understanding; the faculty of inferring is reason. Through experience we become prudent [*klug*], but not learned. Prudence does not consist in science; we can be and become prudent without any learning. Sound understanding and reason show their usefulness especially in practice [*in praxi*]. A certain lack of sound understanding and of application *in concreto* no science can in the least replace or compensate, although in other respects it may replace much. Our universal judgments cannot of themselves help us. With all rules I must know [first] whether they can be applied to the case",[]],"blomberg-s01-038":["[and] (2) how I am then to apply them here. Thus there can be very learned people entirely without sound understanding. There is, then, in the use of the understanding, something, as it were, which nature has reserved for genius, and for which no art can be a substitute.",[]],"blomberg-s01-039":["Through an all too great abundance of learning people often become the more absurd, and quite unfit to judge *in concreto*. To the sound understanding, which is small but correct, there belongs (1) simplicity. It keeps to the ground of experience and has no love for chimerical ideals. Just this simplicity makes the understanding more correct, more secure and more reliable than science. An Academy of Sciences in Paris hatches a thousand times more errors than a village full of peasants. (2) Its modes of knowledge are intuitive through instances and examples. It presents objects to us *in natura*, whereas the finer understanding shows only the shadow of things. The sound understanding is the controller of learning. The common sound understanding grows without instruction. In the same way the common living languages differ from the learned dead languages.",[]],"blomberg-s01-040":["To lay down universal rules as to how the reliability of a witness may be judged requires more than mere common and sound understanding; it requires art and learning. Languages existed before grammars of them, orators before rhetoric, poets before poetics [*Poesia*].",[]],"blomberg-s01-041":["Even when I have universal knowledge of the understanding, and universal rules of it as well, I must still have understanding specially for the purpose of judging whether this or that does or does not stand under this rule. All doctrine in the whole world is only a universal sum of the rules for using the powers of the understanding; but there must still be an understanding present, which nature alone can give. Persons who possess learning but lack sound understanding are far more absurd than the wholly ignorant. From this there arises pedantry. An obstinate blockhead cannot be called a pedant. [In a pedant] learning must always be present, but the ability to apply to particular cases *in concreto* the universal rules that have been known must be lacking; though a pedant, for all his science, certainly always retains a kind of stupidity. So it is, for instance, with every introduction of witty notions in company, and with ceremonious compliments made even by universal prescription. The Chinese have books of compliments. Universal rules always testify to the weakness of our understanding, as does a lexicon to a beginner's [grasp] of a language. Much regularity occurs without consciousness of the rules; so it is with the sound understanding. The use of the understanding according to rules of which we are conscious is, however, entitled science. There are cases in which one man without science can go further than another with science; but also cases in which the greatest sound understanding cannot go as far as science alone. Thus in mathematics and the art of surveying we cannot make progress through the mere understanding; some science must always be present. So too with the art of medicine. For judging of morality, of right and wrong, on the other hand, no science and learning is required.",[]],"blomberg-s01-042":["Otherwise the human race would be very imperfect. Here the sound understanding is the judge of science.",[]],"blomberg-s01-043":["In all sciences a precept does indeed serve to develop our abilities for every occurrence. But in all instruction in an art it is always necessarily left to the understanding of the subject whether, and in what degree, these universal precepts are to be applied in particular cases. Thus we cannot possibly become prudent according to universally valid rules and precepts; for prudence consists precisely in being able oneself to subsume particular cases in common life under its universal rules.",[]],"blomberg-s01-044":["The discipline which contains the rules of the good use of the understanding and of reason in general is entitled logic [*Logica*]. It determines no particular cases. Logic has its place in all the other sciences. Logic stands to the whole use of the understanding exactly as grammar stands to a language. Grammar has a source of the correctness of its rules, but the proof lies in experience.",[]],"blomberg-s01-045":["Logic shows the rules of the use of understanding and reason, which can themselves be known *a priori* and without experience, since they do not depend upon it. The understanding here has insight into its own rules and makes of them a discipline, an instruction, which can, however, be known *a priori*, and which is therefore entitled a doctrine.",[]],"blomberg-s01-046":["In logic reason is not, properly speaking, merely applied; reason is its object, the object of its consideration *in usu*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-047":["There are many modes of knowledge whose form is not rational, and in that case they are not a doctrine of reason [*Vernunftlehre*]. Such, for example, is the science of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s01-048":["Philosophy is in general the science of reason [*Vernunftwissenschaft*] concerning the qualities of things. By the former [characteristic] it is distinguished from the historical sciences, by the latter from the mathematical. It is entitled a science of reason because it sets forth modes of knowledge *in abstracto*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-049":["For the establishment of the first science, such as logic, nothing more is required than the common sound understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-050":["Logic is entitled, by a special name which properly belongs to it, the science of reason [*Vernunftwissenschaft*], because reason is its object. The many other *scientiae rationales* ought to be called sciences [proceeding] from reason [*Wissenschaften der Vernunft*].",[]],"blomberg-s01-051":["Logic is entitled a science because its rules can be proved *a priori* by themselves, without any use. On this account neither grammar nor aesthetic is a science; and there is in fact, properly speaking, no science of the beautiful at all, but whatever we ever know of the beautiful is nothing but a *critica*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-052":["Logic will therefore have no grounds and source other than the nature of the human understanding. For every mode of knowledge, and therefore also for logic, there are always (1) ... (a) objective and (b) subjective grounds. In every science we rightly study the object, what belongs to its greatest perfection; but also ... (2) the subject, that is, the means, the *substrata*, for attaining to the object. So too in the sciences of the understanding I must first of all study man. Since this, however, is seldom done, or done by few, there arise from it, for instance, the many faults in morals especially. Most moralists can indeed speak excellently of virtue, praise, commend and describe it, but are unable to instil into their hearers and produce in them the desire and love to practise it, because they have not studied man.",[]],"blomberg-s01-053":["Logic is a good philosophy [*Philosophia*] concerning the good use of reason and its rules. It therefore considers not only the nature of human reason, but also, and especially, its precepts.",[]],"blomberg-s01-054":["Psychology [*Psychologia*] develops from experience, and treats of, the nature of the human understanding. [Logic] will therefore also have its sources [there]; that is, we shall have to learn from psychology and experience a number of subjective laws of the use of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s01-055":["We can therefore, *primo*, make the subjective laws of our understanding, according to which we actually use the understanding, the object of our consideration.",[]],"blomberg-s01-056":["The science which examines the subjective laws of the rules of our use of understanding and reason is entitled psychology [*Psychologia*].",[]],"blomberg-s01-057":["Logic does not, properly speaking, contain the rules according to which man actually thinks, but the rules according to which man ought to think. For man often uses his understanding, and thinks, otherwise than he ought to think and use his understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s01-058":["Logic therefore contains within itself the objective laws of understanding and reason. In the same way the description of a good republic is often so opposed to an actually existing republic that it contains precisely the opposite of it.",[]],"blomberg-s01-059":["At bottom, then, we have only two objective doctrines; for there are only two objective powers of the soul, the cognitive power [*Erkenntniskraft*] and the faculty of desire [*Begehrungsvermögen*]. Logic, as well as morals, has for its object a practice [*praxis*], an exercise: [logic] the use of our understanding, [morals] the employment of our free choice [*Willkür*]. But they are not on that account entitled practical sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s01-060":["We can have a theoretical as well as a practical logic and morals. Disciplines are practical when their effects are practical.",[]],"blomberg-s01-061":["Logic is not a mere critique [*Critic*]; it is an actual doctrine, which can be proved. Its rules are taken *in abstracto* and proved. Every doctrine, however, is not always practical; it may be pure theory. If, now, logic is a mere theory of the conditions under which a mode of knowledge is perfect according to laws of understanding and reason, it is no means of [31a] execution: it would be a theory, but not an *organon*.",[]],"blomberg-s01-062":["There is a logic which is called a *scientia propaedeutica*, an introductory science.",[]],"blomberg-s01-063":["and so it will serve us as a means of criticism [*Critic*], or as an *organon*. But this it is not; it only sharpens the understanding in judging of modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s01-064":["Logic therefore contains the rules of the use of our understanding and of our reason. Everything else therefore stands under it. It prepares the way for all the other sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-001":["There are many rules of motion and of the understanding. Every ship, every fish, conforms to the rules of motion. We ourselves are subject to these laws; we observe them in walking and in other motions. Nature has implanted these laws in men and animals. In respect of them, however, we do no more than fulfil them, as unknowingly as the animals.",[]],"blomberg-s02-002":["It would be unnecessary to seek out these laws of motion and to refuse to take a single step without deliberation. Just as the fish in swimming observes these rules with the utmost exactness through exercise, so we too can acquire a facility in observing them merely through exercise. How quickly do we not know how to help ourselves when we are about to fall! This facility, however, would fail us if we wished first to deliberate what posture we ought to adopt.",[]],"blomberg-s02-003":["Borelli [*Borellus*] has written a book on regular motion.",[]],"blomberg-s02-004":["Besides the laws and rules of motion, however, there are also rules and laws of the understanding. We order all our judgments according to these laws. Just as a child in walking learns to observe the laws of motion, so in judging it seeks to form itself according to the laws of the understanding. All this, however, it does unknowingly, and it acquires the facility merely through exercise.",[]],"blomberg-s02-005":["For common knowledge it is not necessary that we be conscious of these rules and deliberate upon them; were we to do so, we should lose a great deal. If, however, our understanding is to rise to learned knowledge, it must be conscious of its rules and use them deliberately, since common exercise is not sufficient for it here. These rules, which the understanding has to observe in learned observation or knowledge, are prescribed to us by logic, which we are now about to expound.",[]],"blomberg-s02-006":["The author defines it quite rightly: it is a science which treats of the rules of learned knowledge and of learned exposition. A shorter definition would be: logic is a science which teaches us the use of the understanding in learned knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s02-007":["It is also entitled *philosophia instrumentalis*, because it can be regarded, as it were, as an *instrumentum* of all the other philosophical sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-008":["Through frequent observations the rules of the understanding have been sought out. Aristotle already laid down some; but these were nothing but signposts to false paths. It has cost much labour to forget such false propositions, to restore to the understanding its natural perfection, and to discover its true rules.",[]],"blomberg-s02-009":["A correct logic is like a straight line, such that we must deviate from it neither to the right nor to the left. It were to be wished that logic had already been brought to such correctness and perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s02-010":["In this paragraph [*Paragraphus*] the author shows us from what we must derive its rules if we wish to make it thus perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s02-011":["There may serve for this purpose, namely,",[]],"blomberg-s02-012":["(1) experiences of the operations of human reason. Bacon of Verulam (see his life in Formey's History of Philosophy, p. 293 ff., and in Gentzken's *Historia philosophica*, pp. 156, 157) was the first to show the world that all philosophy consists of figments of the brain if it does not rest on experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-013":["It would be as necessary to write an experimental logic as an experimental physics, in which it should be investigated how man can err through prejudices and precipitancy, as well as in other ways, so that rules may be prescribed to him as to how he is to guard against them.",[]],"blomberg-s02-014":["We ought further to make observations as to how we can judge most correctly, and to prescribe rules regarding this also in logic.",[]],"blomberg-s02-015":["(2) We must look to the nature of human reason, in order that the rules may be framed in conformity with it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-016":["It would be foolish to frame logic for rational beings in general, or even for angels, and to suppose that it could be of use to us as well. That would be just as if I wished to give a lame man, who has to walk on crutches, rules and precepts as to how he should walk and run as a healthy man. (3) Our rules must conform to the universal fundamental truths of human knowledge, of which ontology treats. These fundamental truths are the *principia* of all sciences, and consequently of logic also. We must here remark that at this beginning of philosophy we are not yet in a position to furnish a perfect logic. That requires insights which can be employed only with hearers to whom the whole of philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] is known. We shall here endeavour only to give preliminary rules for learned knowledge, whereby the learning of the other sciences will be made easier.",[]],"blomberg-s02-017":["The aim of the doctrine of reason [*Vernunftlehre*] may be divided into the immediate and the mediate. The immediate is the improvement of learned knowledge; the mediate, of common knowledge. The latter can be achieved when, in daily intercourse, we seek to improve men's concepts; but in doing so we must not come forward with scholastic *termini*, if we do not wish to be entitled a pedant.",[]],"blomberg-s02-018":["As regards the immediate aim of logic, namely the perfection of learned knowledge, this concerns both the learned by profession and others as well. Everyone can through it improve his learned knowledge as far as he will. The learned by profession are distinguished from others by certain *termini technici*, and also by this, that learning is their chief occupation, whereas by others, for instance by kings, it is for the most part pursued only as a subsidiary concern.",[]],"blomberg-s02-019":["Since the multitude of the sciences is now so great, it would be reasonable that to each person his particular field should be assigned; for it is impossible to perfect oneself in all the sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s02-020":["Among the persons who are not learned by profession we might also reckon women, who can likewise occupy themselves with learning in accordance with their abilities.",[]],"blomberg-s02-021":["There are sciences which require acuteness, much reflection and profundity. These are for the male sex. There are, on the other hand, also sciences which require wit and a kind of feeling, and these are suited to women.",[]],"blomberg-s02-022":["Here philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] is discussed. Its object is the universal qualities and properties of things. It is, however, to be carefully noted that philosophy does not consider *quotiens aliquid positum sit* [how many times something is posited], but only *quod positum sit* [what is posited]. It considers only qualities; it weighs only what is posited. Mathesis, on the other hand, investigates how many times the thing is posited, and investigates the *quantitates* of things, how great they are. Further, philosophy does not consider the entire qualities, the entire constitution of things, and all of them, but only those which are met with in many things and are therefore entitled universal.",[]],"blomberg-s02-023":["Philosophy is a science; but we must not therefore suppose that in philosophy we must always demonstrate and define. That is characteristic of mathematics.",[]],"blomberg-s02-024":["The definition which our author gives of *philosophia* is as follows. Philosophy [Weltweisheit], he says, is a science of the universal properties of things, in so far as these are known without belief [Glauben]. It is also commonly said: *Philosophia est Scientia, quae circa rerum rationes versatur* [philosophy is the science which is concerned with the grounds of things]. But since philosophy often concerns itself not merely with the grounds of things but also with their properties simply as such, it is easily seen that this definition is incorrect. A higher use of reason belongs to philosophy. It is, however, difficult to determine where philosophy begins and where it ends, just as it is difficult, for example, to determine the boundaries between poverty and wealth. A man is rich, we say, if he has, for instance, 50,000 or 20,000 Reichsthaler. But at what sum of money does he cease to be rich, and when does he become rich? It is just as difficult to determine the boundaries between avarice and thrift. At some point philosophy must part from the common use of reason, poverty from wealth, niggardliness from thrift; but where this point lies is hard to say.",[]],"blomberg-s02-025":["We shall now consider the author's qualifications. He says that in philosophy the properties of things must be known without belief.",[]],"blomberg-s02-026":["Our knowledge is of three kinds. We know something",[]],"blomberg-s02-027":["1. through experience, as is the case with events, and in general with many objects of history;",[]],"blomberg-s02-028":["2. through reason, which abstracts universal cognitions [Kenntniße] from individual things; and",[]],"blomberg-s02-029":["3. through belief in the testimony of another. In this last way we know that Paris, London, etc. are in the world [43a], although we have seen none of them. Nor do we doubt it in the least, since we rely on the testimony of others who have been there. In philosophy there is no place for belief; here no one may rely on the testimony of others. Even if someone were to swear to us the immortality of the soul, we should still not believe and trust him without proofs, since in philosophy belief has no place. How then can the name of philosopher be due to one who is a blind follower of another? The pupils of Pythagoras accepted everything their teacher told them for only five years; these supposed philosophers do it their whole life long, and are for ever saying αὐτος ἔφα [he himself said it].",[]],"blomberg-s02-030":["If there were nothing whatsoever in philosophy but belief, it could not be that now this man, now that, is set upon the throne of the sciences, and that such changes of fashion take place in the learned horizon. Great lords keep people who are learned for them, who conduct the sciences, give counsel, have a good heart for them, and so forth, while they themselves are occupied solely with digestion. In the same way Wolff and other true philosophers have done the preparatory work and laid the foundation for certain supposed philosophers, and these need do no more than acquaint themselves with it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-031":["If we wish to divide *philosophia*, we must presuppose that the activities of our soul consist of knowledge, feeling and desire. The science which treats of the use of the understanding is",[]],"blomberg-s02-032":["*Logica*; that which speaks of the universal objects of the understanding is",[]],"blomberg-s02-033":["*Metaphysica*; that which treats of corporeal objects is",[]],"blomberg-s02-034":["*Physica*; that which treats of feeling is",[]],"blomberg-s02-035":["*Aesthetica*; and the science which has to do with our actions and desires is entitled",[]],"blomberg-s02-036":["morals, or *philosophia practica*.",[]],"blomberg-s02-037":["The first philosophy doubtless began where objects could not fall under the senses; and objects of this kind were doubtless the objects of religion.",[]],"blomberg-s02-038":["To xxiv 35: Supplement. Excerpt from Formey, Kurzgefassete Histoire der Philosophie (1763); cf. pp. 33-40, 78-71, 174-181.",[]],"blomberg-s02-039":["As regards the history of philosophy, the following is to be noted. The Chaldeans had a philosopher in Zoroaster; among the Persians, too, a Zoroaster is known, who is, however, entirely distinct from the Chaldean. The philosophy of the Chaldeans was very obscure and uncertain. (Theology, then, was the first to extend philosophy, *ad pag.* 44.) It reaches back into the remotest antiquity, and what can be made out about it derives from very suspect reports. One must therefore be very cautious if one is not to confuse the teachings of the ancients with the fictions spread abroad in later times. The religion of these peoples was a medley of the most absurd superstition, a gross and abominable idolatry. The priests deceived the stupid populace by all manner of tricks. Soothsaying, enchantments, interpretations of dreams, childish and extravagant ceremonies made up the whole. If anything good did lie beneath this coarse crust, it is impossible now to pick it out. This philosophy therefore has the appearance of the most complete folly; but it must also have been a dangerous folly, for from the first times of the Roman monarchy the arts of the Chaldeans were banished as suspect and godless. Yet notwithstanding this obscurity of the Chaldean philosophy, it was very famous throughout antiquity. Its adherents divided into various sects, such as the Hipparenes, Babylonians, Orchenians, Marsypers, Borsippenes, etc. Zoroaster is given out as the originator of magic, that is, as the father of the grossest of all deceptions and of the most shameful superstition. It is said that he was killed by fire from heaven. Belus is said to have instructed the priests in *astronomia* and *physica*. Berosus, Marmaridius, Zabrates and Teucrus are known to us only by their names.",[]],"blomberg-s02-040":["All the philosophies of the most ancient peoples were to be found among the priests. The Egyptians and Persians were probably the first whose understanding overstepped its limits and who began to make *speculationes*. *Astrologia* and *cosmologia* arose earlier than the physical sciences. The very first object of their inquiry was the origin of things; for these are doubtless the most important questions, and such as must have occurred to men immediately, once they had provided for the necessities of the body. Yet this first inquiry will certainly have contained more superstition than philosophy. This much is certain: until philosophy in a nation had completely separated itself from the power of government and from the rule of the clergy, no philosophy of a proper kind could come about.",[]],"blomberg-s02-041":["The originator of the Persian *philosophia* was Zerdusht or Zoroaster. He lived at the time of the Babylonian captivity and was the head of religion. He gathered his teaching into a work entitled the Zendavesta. His Oracle is a book foisted upon him by the Neoplatonists. The priests of the Persians were entitled *Magi*, and their head *Archimagus*. The doctrines of their philosophy were as follows. The supreme God is the intellectual fire; from this, in order to bring forth the world, there proceeded two mutually opposed fundamental beings, or *principia*. The foremost being, from which all others proceed, and on which the law of emanations also depends, is entitled Mithra. The two fundamental beings are Oromasdes, a very pure, active and spiritual light, and Arimanius, the passive and material darkness, which is born of the limitation of the light and is bound up with it as a necessary consequence. From the mixture of these two fundamental beings all things beneath the moon have proceeded; and it is thus that the origin of evil on earth is to be explained. But a time will come when the darkness will be overcome and annihilated by the light. In their view the soul came from the gods and was, like them, immortal. Their moral teachings commended chastity, uprightness, equity and the shunning of sensual pleasures. They expressed this as follows: You must follow the light, and guard yourselves against the plague of darkness, or the generation of matter.",[]],"blomberg-s02-042":["Among the Chinese we note three great philosophers, Confucius, Keilau and Janzu, whom they worshipped under a three-headed idol. They held the opinion of Pythagoras concerning the transmigration of souls, and worship the sun, moon and stars, indeed not seldom the devil, so that he may not harm them. They therefore set his image on the prows of their ships and wear fearsome heads upon their clothing. Among the Chinese all philosophy is nothing but morals, some astronomy and mathematics, and a kind of political science and art of government. Confucius was a Solon of the Chinese.",[]],"blomberg-s02-043":["Among the Jews before the destruction of Jerusalem we note certain sects: 1. the Sadducees, which sprang from Antigonus Sochaeus. Out of vexation at the doctrine of good works, through which one can merit nothing with God, he fell into the complete opposite and said that one must serve God without any hope of future reward. Zadok and Boethus misunderstood this teaching, and accordingly denied the resurrection of the body and all rewards after this life.",[]],"blomberg-s02-044":["2. the Karaeans, Karaites or scribes, who rejected all allegorical interpretations of Scripture, but otherwise accepted all the Sadducean tenets. They adhered to the school of Shammai.",[]],"blomberg-s02-045":["3. the sect of the Pharisees. It has its name from the Hebrew word *parash*, which signifies people set apart. For they believed themselves to be greatly distinguished from the people, partly by their learning, which however consisted only in false interpretations of the law, and partly by meritorious works, which were nothing more than observances of external ceremonies and superstitious practices. Their teaching was chiefly supported by the school of Hillel.",[]],"blomberg-s02-046":["4. the sect of the Essenes. These departed furthest from Jewish customs. The persecutions of the Syrian kings, or rather the invasion of Cambyses, gave rise to them. Their way of life was very like that of the monastic orders. They served God in a very purely spiritual manner, and had set hours in which they attended to certain duties.",[]],"blomberg-s02-047":["5. the sect of the Therapeutae. They flourished in Egypt and were entirely monastic.",[]],"blomberg-s02-048":["From the times after the destruction of Jerusalem and of the Temple we note the rabbis Gamaliel and Judah, entitled the Holy, the chief originators of the Talmudic teaching. The Cabbalistic teaching is contained in the mysterious books Happlijah, Ha-Bahir and Zohar. The rabbis Akiba and Simeon, son of Zohar, propagated it, but as a secret teaching. Finally the philosophy of Aristotle mingled with it, although all the rabbis opposed this. Evidence of this is to be found in the work Cuzari. The celebrated rabbi Moses Maimonides was well versed in it. This mixed teaching has a great deal of similarity to positive and moral theology, which was founded on the interpretation of the divine law. Rabbi Saadia brought the former into a system of doctrine [Lehr-Begriff]. Moses Maimonides gave it a more philosophical turn and made thirteen articles of faith out of it, which he entitled roots. Finally the Jews in Egypt adopted the allegorical method, and even the philosophy of that country, namely the concept of emanations, which consisted in a mixture of the opinions of Zoroaster, Pythagoras and others. All this they sought to combine as well as possible with their own opinions. This teaching was already current among the Therapeutae, and Philo followed it. The fear of the Jews in the second century of being dispersed moved them to set this teaching down in writing; and thus they propagated these errors ever further. On this see more in Formey's History of Philosophy, pp. 72, 184.",[]],"blomberg-s02-049":["The philosophy of the ancients has this defect, that they did not make observations at all as is now done. So it was with the mathematicians, whose ancient writings are nevertheless so inestimable; only, mathematics was not combined with observations of nature, which afterwards became the cause of so many discoveries.",[]],"blomberg-s02-050":["Geography the ancients had not at all, or only very imperfectly.",[]],"blomberg-s02-051":["It was among the Greeks that the philosophers were instructed in all the tenets and doctrines, and in the form and aims of the government of their people, and that they contributed something to its perfection. Crude as philosophy was when it came to the Greeks, it was handed on by them to other peoples in a highly cultivated [excolirt] state. The merit of the Greeks in all the sciences of understanding and of taste is therefore the greatest. The government of the Greeks was formed by the schools of the philosophers. Among them we note in particular the so-called Seven Sages, namely:",[]],"blomberg-s02-052":["*1mo* Thales, who of them all deserves to be entitled a sage on account of his mathematics;",[]],"blomberg-s02-053":["*2do* Solon, a fine mind, a good poet, an able general and an excellent lawgiver. His motto was: Consider the end.",[]],"blomberg-s02-054":["*3tio* Chilon was conspicuous and esteemed among the Lacedaemonians for his justice and patience. His motto was: Learn not too much, but to know thyself.",[]],"blomberg-s02-055":["4. Pittacus of Mytilene, a courageous and good soldier. His motto was: Know the time.",[]],"blomberg-s02-056":["5. Bias of Priene in Ionia, an upright, magnanimous, virtuous and prudent man. His *symbolum* was: Love as though you might hate.",[]],"blomberg-s02-057":["6. Cleobulus, a native of Rhodes. He learned wisdom among the Egyptians. His daughter Cleobulina was the heir of his virtues and learning. Nothing, he said, is better than moderation.",[]],"blomberg-s02-058":["7. Periander, a prince of the Corinthians.",[]],"blomberg-s02-059":["Democritus deserves to be entitled the first philosopher. He was the teacher of the great and celebrated Epicurus, who is among the ancients what Descartes represents among the moderns, and who improved the previous method of philosophising. In Lucretius are found tenets of the Epicurean philosophy. But since Lucretius, as is well known, was at times insane, he may well have distorted these tenets.",[]],"blomberg-s02-060":["Pyrrho was a man of great insight. He had the saying *non liquet* [it is not clear], which he constantly called out to the bold sophists to subdue their pride. He was the founder of the Sceptics, who also called themselves *Zetetici*. This sect, however, in the end carried doubt so far, and became so extravagant, that it at last began to doubt everything, even the propositions of mathematics.",[]],"blomberg-s02-061":["Socrates applied himself to practical philosophy, which he demonstrated above all through his conduct of life.",[]],"blomberg-s02-062":["Pythagoras set great store by numbers. He held the number ten to be the most perfect; others, however, the number four, and thought that God had for this reason made four *elementa*. He wished to think out everything by means of numbers. Hence he said, among other things: *Animal est Numerus se ipsum movens* [an animal is a number that moves itself].",[]],"blomberg-s02-063":["Plato was very rhetorical and obscure, so much so that he often did not understand himself.",[]],"blomberg-s02-064":["Aristotle gained a blind confidence, and did philosophy more harm than good.",[]],"blomberg-s02-065":["Zeno was the founder of the Stoic sect.",[]],"blomberg-s02-066":["The Romans, finally, introduced no distinctive principal sects, but followed the Greeks.",[]],"blomberg-s02-067":["The Arabs revived the teaching of Aristotle, and the Scholastics also followed them. In some respects modern times have advantages over the ancients: these lacked empirical cognitions [Erfahrungs-Kenntniße]. Thus, for example, the natural phenomenon [*Eventus*] of ebb and flood, which has been discovered in modern times, was unknown to Aristotle and to all the Greeks. But neither did there then exist the means, now available, of arriving at it, for example of travelling so quickly; and it was above all through this lack that the extension of empirical knowledge was held back. Yet these are the chief sources from which reason is to draw.",[]],"blomberg-s02-068":["In general the ancient philosophers were all either 1. sceptical philosophers, and these were misologists or haters of reason; or dogmatists, and all these took their names from their *principia*. Moral philosophy, however, and the science and knowledge [Kenntniß] of nature, were poor and false among them.",[]],"blomberg-s02-069":["Bacon of Verulam, who pointed out the importance of the endeavour to bring the observations of nature under certain propositions. (And even to this day, though the outer observations and *phaenomena* of nature have been sufficiently extended, the inner have not.)",[]],"blomberg-s02-070":["Descartes, Malebranche, Leibniz and Wolff, the last of whom by his industry brought about a *systema* of philosophy, were in recent times the reformers and the real fathers of philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s02-071":["All the endeavours of our philosophy are",[]],"blomberg-s02-072":["1. dogmatic,",[]],"blomberg-s02-073":["2. critical.",[]],"blomberg-s02-074":["Among the critical philosophers Locke deserves first place. Wolff, however, and the Germans in general, have a methodical philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s02-075":["Finally Crusius has become famous; but for some good he has a very great deal that is incorrect, and he errs above all in wishing to prove a great many propositions merely from the nature of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s02-076":["As regards *logica*, Aristotle was the first to expound it, and he also invented the four syllogistic figures.",[]],"blomberg-s02-077":["The *logica* of the Scholastics consisted of nothing but subtleties.",[]],"blomberg-s02-078":["Locke's book *de intellectu humano* [on the human understanding] is the foundation of all true *logica*.",[]],"blomberg-s02-079":["At the time of the Reformation Petrus Ramus wrote a logic in France. He taught his countrymen, the French, to pronounce *quin*, *quisquis*, *quanquam* not in the French but in the Latin manner. Over this a great dispute arose between him and the teachers of the Sorbonne, so that, when the matter came before the government, many were dismissed merely on account of the wrong pronunciation. After him Wolff and also Crusius wrote a *logica*; the latter's, however, cannot facilitate the learning of the sciences, but is rather so obscure that a further logic is required in order to understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-080":["Now at last critical philosophy is most of all reviving, and the English have the greatest merit in it.",[]],"blomberg-s02-081":["For the most part the dogmatic method has fallen almost universally out of use in all the sciences; even morals is no longer expounded dogmatically, but more often critically.",[]],"blomberg-s02-082":["Logic treats either of the rules of certain or of probable learned knowledge; the latter is entitled *logica probabilium* [logic of probabilities]. In common life we act more according to probability than according to certainty, and for this reason a *logica probabilium* would be very useful.",[]],"blomberg-s02-083":["Bernoulli has indeed written one, but it is nothing other than a mathematics applied to games of chance. He shows, for example, how according to the rules of probability one can throw eight times.",[]],"blomberg-s02-084":["This *logica probabilium* has only examples, and is of use in burial funds. But the one we are speaking of here is to extend to the experience of all men, and such a one does not yet exist.",[]],"blomberg-s02-085":["Crusius' logic of the probable is held by many to be good; but it contains merely general reflections, which have come into repute through their learned tone.",[]],"blomberg-s02-086":["Just as a circle is easier to make than an ellipse, and rules for virtue are easier to give than rules for inconstancy, so too the rules for certain knowledge can be found more easily than those for probable knowledge. The grounds of certainty are determinate; those of probability are not. It is now to be shown how great they must be if something is to be probable, and this is difficult to determine.",[]],"blomberg-s02-087":["*Logica* can be divided",[]],"blomberg-s02-088":["*1mo* into theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s02-089":["*2do* and practical *logica*.",[]],"blomberg-s02-090":["Theoretical *logica* only shows us the rules of learned knowledge; practical *logica* applies these rules to particular cases.",[]],"blomberg-s02-091":["In this *paragraphus* the author speaks of the utility of the doctrine of reason [Vernunft-Lehre]. It furthers, *1mo*, the learning of the sciences in general. This, however, is to be understood only of logic in the objective sense, namely of that logic which is actually constituted as it rightly ought to be. A *logica* which is as obscure and excessively over-refined as that of Crusius cannot have this utility.",[]],"blomberg-s02-092":["It improves, *2do*, the understanding and reason. One who knows a language according to its rules cannot err so easily as one who has learned it only *ex usu* [by use]. The latter is therefore not so sure of his ground as the former, and hardly knows how to help himself in what he has not yet met with, whereas the other can arrange everything according to the rules which the language prescribes to him. In the same way, he who has before his eyes the rules by which he is to form his understanding, and applies them properly, will not err so easily as one who knows nothing whatever of these rules.",[]],"blomberg-s02-093":["Logic furthers, for example, virtue. In doubtful matters one can in fact proceed according to the rules of logic, and judge whether the thing is really good or not. This utility it has *directe* [directly] for virtue. *Indirecte* [indirectly] it furthers virtue, as do all speculative sciences and modes of knowledge, in that, once a man has acquired a taste for it, it draws him away from sensuous allurements and at the same time instils in him a kind of decorum. If a man wishes to occupy himself with speculations, he must be calm, decorous and content with the things outside him; and this can in the end, with the help of logic, become habitual to him.",[]],"blomberg-s03-001":["First Section. Of Learned Knowledge in General",[]],"blomberg-s03-002":["In this *paragraphus* the author tried to give a definition of representation; but since he was unable to do so, he helped himself out with a rhetorical flourish, as commonly happens when one can say nothing definite. To this end he says that a representation is like a picture which the painterly skill of the soul draws within it. What representation is cannot, properly speaking, be explained at all. It is one of the simple concepts which we must necessarily have. Every man knows immediately what representation is. Modes of knowledge and representations are one and the same; concepts, however, differ from them in some respect, as we shall see in what follows. Knowledge and representation are taken in logic as one and the same. Every representation is something in us which, however, relates to something else, namely the object. Certain things represent something; but we represent things to ourselves. Logic does not teach us at all how we are to represent something to ourselves through consciousness; rather, it presupposes consciousness of something as a matter for psychology.",[]],"blomberg-s03-003":["In every mode of knowledge we find something material, but also something formal.",[]],"blomberg-s03-004":["The thing [Gegenstand] which we represent to ourselves is the object; the manner of the representation is entitled the formal [element]. If, for example, I represent virtue to someone, I can attend partly to what I represent to myself and partly to how I represent it. The latter is the formal, the former the material [element] of the *repraesentatio*. Logic, however, has to do for the most part with the formal [element] of knowledge. But modes of knowledge can have different forms with one and the same matter. There are whole sciences whose philosophy differs from common understanding not in matter but only in form, that is, in distinctness. So it is, for example, with morals.",[]],"blomberg-s03-005":["When we distinguish a representation, and the object to which it refers, from other representations, we are conscious of the representation. Consciousness accompanies each of our states; it is, as it were, the intuition [Anschauen] of ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s03-006":["Yet we are not conscious of most of our representations, nor of the mightiest and most powerful of them. Who would imagine that? Only to those representations of which we are conscious can we prescribe rules. Consciousness cannot be discussed more fully here; the doctrine of it properly belongs to *metaphysica*, and in order to have a correct concept of it much from *metaphysica* must be laid down as a foundation.",[]],"blomberg-s03-007":["Here confused and distinct representations are discussed. When in a composite representation I distinguish from one another the parts of which it consists, this is a distinct representation. If, however, I do not distinguish its partial representations from one another, it is a confused representation. For example, when I see a cheese-mite, my representation of it is at first confused. When, however, I take up a *microscopium* and perceive in it a maw full of teeth, two rows of feet and two black eyes, then I had in fact represented all this to myself before as well; but I could not distinguish these partial representations of the individual members from [the representation of] the whole body. Through the microscope, however, my knowledge becomes distinct, since I thereby represent to myself the individual parts of a cheese-mite and distinguish them from its whole body. Similarly, when I view the Milky Way with the naked eye, I distinguish nothing in it; I am conscious of no parts in the *repraesentatio*. But if I view the Milky Way through a *tubus* [telescope], I see that it consists of fixed stars. These fixed stars I represent to myself as parts of the Milky Way, and I distinguish the representation of one fixed star from the representation of another. I therefore have a distinct representation, since what is required for such a representation is precisely that I distinguish the partial representations from one another; and the partial representations of the fixed stars are partial representations of the Milky Way.",[]],"blomberg-s03-008":["A simple representation, and simple modes of knowledge, can neither be nor become confused, nor yet distinct. Confusion and distinctness therefore occur only in composite modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s03-009":["A chief function [Eigenschaft] of logic will therefore be to analyse a confused mode of knowledge and to make it distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-010":["To distinctness is opposed indistinctness; to order, however, confusion. Something can be confused although I am conscious of the partial representations and it does indeed possess distinctness. A distinct representation is not always orderly. The more we reduce our representations to simple ones, the more we remove confusion.",[]],"blomberg-s03-011":["In a distinct representation the material [element] is the same as in a confused one. In the one case we represent to ourselves just as much as in the other. In the confused representation we have the partial representations as much as in the distinct; for if we did not have them, neither should we have the total [representation].",[]],"blomberg-s03-012":["In the confused representation, however, we do not distinguish the partial representations. A mode of knowledge can be distinct only in so far as it is a total representation; and likewise only a total representation can be confused. A simple representation is neither confused nor distinct, for in it I can distinguish no partial representation from another. Distinctness as well as confusion occurs only in total, that is, composite representations.",[]],"blomberg-s03-013":["Distinctness can occur",[]],"blomberg-s03-014":["*1mo* in intuition [Anschauen], when we can clearly distinguish the marks of what we intuit. This is distinctness in intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-015":["2. in thought, when we combine clear concepts and representations with intuition. Often one can intuit something distinctly without thinking anything distinctly in connection with it.",[]],"blomberg-s03-016":["We attain distinctness of intuition through greater attention, *per synthesin*. To distinctness of concepts, however, belongs the analysis of what I think, of what I already actually conceive in thought; that is, distinctness of the understanding *per analysin*. The whole of morals consists of nothing but concepts of the understanding. Empirical distinctness is acquired *a posteriori* through attention to the objects of experience; distinctness of reason, however, is acquired *a priori* through attention to the actions of my understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-017":["A ground is that from which something can be known, and a consequence is what can be known from the ground. With this definition we can make do here; in *metaphysica*, however, we shall not be satisfied with it. A ground from which everything in the consequence can be understood, and which therefore lacks nothing, is a sufficient ground; one from which only something is known is an insufficient ground. For example, if we say that the moon has inhabitants because there are mountains and valleys in it, this is an insufficient ground; from it we see only that it is possible and probable that there are inhabitants in the moon. But if, for example, a merchant has a fortune of 100,000 Reichsthaler, and it is said that he acquired 50,000 by trade and inherited 50,000, this is a sufficient ground. The relation of ground and consequences is connection [Zusammenhang]: for example, the tree is connected with its fruits; the tree is the ground and the fruits are the consequence.",[]],"blomberg-s03-018":["Every truth has its ground, that is, that from which one can distinguish it from what is false and hold it to be true. This much belongs here in *logica*; sufficient grounds are properly discussed in *metaphysica*.",[]],"blomberg-s03-019":["If I merely [know] the ground of a consequence, my knowledge is not on that account yet rational; for example, when saltpetre is given as the ground of thunderstorms. But when one distinctly sees how the consequence arises from the ground, for example how the storm comes from the saltpetre, our knowledge is rational. The author here already speaks of rational knowledge without first having spoken of distinct knowledge. He ought to have entitled it a knowledge of reason [Vernunft-Erkenntniß], which itself arises from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s03-020":["All perfections of knowledge are",[]],"blomberg-s03-021":["*1mo* aesthetic, and consist in agreement with subjective laws and conditions;",[]],"blomberg-s03-022":["*2do* logical, and consist in agreement with objective laws and conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s03-023":["All the *requisita* of these perfections of knowledge in general, however, are:",[]],"blomberg-s03-024":["*1mo* sensation, how I am affected by the presence of the object;",[]],"blomberg-s03-025":["2. judgment [Urtheils-kraft];",[]],"blomberg-s03-026":["3. spirit [Geist];",[]],"blomberg-s03-027":["4. taste.",[]],"blomberg-s03-028":["A mode of knowledge agrees with the subject when it gives us much to think about and sets our capacities in play. To this belongs in particular ease, [and] intuition; and to the latter belong *similia*, instances and examples. Sensation, however, holds only the second rank, and intuition the first. For by sensation one cannot judge, but by intuition one can; and for this very reason the former holds the lowest rank among the things that constitute aesthetic perfection, and the latter the highest.",[]],"blomberg-s03-029":["In knowledge there are two kinds of perfection: 1. that it agrees with the constitution of the thing;",[]],"blomberg-s03-030":["*2do* that it has an effect on our feeling and our taste. The former is a logical, the latter an aesthetic perfection, but both are formal perfections. We have one logic which makes, and can make, our cognitions [Kenntniße] logically perfect, and another which makes our knowledge aesthetically perfect. The former teaches us to form representations conformable to the constitution of the thing; this is done by the logic we are now about to treat. The latter, which aesthetics contains, must treat of those representations which have an effect on our feeling. To logical perfection belongs distinctness of representation, and also that it must be rational. Of the latter treatment was given in § 14.",[]],"blomberg-s03-031":["We can say that that mode of knowledge or representation is rational in which reason is employed, that is, in which I know the grounds. To the logical perfection of a representation, however, belongs above all truth. Another means to objective perfection is distinctness. Thus, for example, a demonstration is logically perfect when the proofs adduced are secure, distinct and certain, and agree with the properties of the thing. A proof can, however, also be easy, although this property does not properly relate to the object, but rather to the subject. Hence one and the same object can be hard for one subject to grasp and easy for another.",[]],"blomberg-s03-032":["As a rule [*ordinaire*] one must sacrifice something of logical perfection if one wishes to attain aesthetic perfection, and give up something of aesthetic perfection if one wishes to attain logical perfection. Something can never be quite distinct without being somewhat difficult, nor ever quite lively without being somewhat indistinct, *et vice versa*.",[]],"blomberg-s03-033":["No one has ever yet been able to determine with accuracy, and to discover, the right measure in which aesthetic perfection is to be combined with logical perfection. Much delicacy is required for this. What furthers our life, that is, what, as it were, sets our activity in play, pleases. A thing becomes easy for us when it is in order. Order is therefore a means to the agreement of our knowledge with the object to which it refers. Further, we are especially pleased by what is new, and no less by what is incomprehensible, precisely because it is incomprehensible, provided only that it is nonetheless true; [and so] in general by everything marvellous. Astonishment [Befremdung] always has something agreeable for sensibility, but also something displeasing for the understanding. That is why people so gladly listen to the most marvellous stories. An aesthetic perfection is a perfection according to laws of sensibility. We make something sensible when we bring it about that the object arouses a sensation and moves us, and when I make something capable of intuition. The greater art of taste consists in this, that what I first presented drily I now make sensible, clothing it in objects of sensibility, yet in such a manner that the understanding loses nothing thereby. A perfection acquires its value through being communicable. In everything that concerns taste, sociability lies at the basis, and taste is thereby greatly elevated. He who chooses only what pleases himself, but no one else, has no taste whatsoever. Taste cannot, therefore, possibly be isolated and peculiar [to the individual]. The judgment upon taste is thus never a private judgment. From this, then, we see",[]],"blomberg-s03-034":["*1mo* Taste must refer to the judgment of all. Taste is a judgment of the most universal approval.",[]],"blomberg-s03-035":["*2do* Taste carries with it something sociable, something social.",[]],"blomberg-s03-036":["Sociability, however, gives to life a certain savour [Geschmack] which it would otherwise lack, and this taste is itself social. If what pleases me sensibly must also, directly and without more ado, please others, then it is said that I have taste. There are some men who have nothing social in them, although they may otherwise be honest people.",[]],"blomberg-s03-037":["Solitaries [*Solitarii*], eccentrics, are all without taste. There is present in the human soul a certain principle which would well deserve careful study, namely, that our mind is communicable and sympathetic, so that man is as glad to communicate as to be communicated with. That is why men so very gladly communicate with one another; and a man seldom feels genuine pleasure when he cannot communicate himself, his thoughts or his inclinations, to someone. Our knowing is nothing if others do not know that we know it. Hence it comes that a man tests his judgment against the judgment of others, and is at once pleased when theirs agrees with his own. It is therefore altogether perverse to say that taste is a certain private judgment of a man upon what gratifies him, what pleases him. Such a man has no taste at all. Taste, too, has universally valid laws. But can these laws be known *in abstracto*, *a priori*? No; only *in concreto*, since the laws of taste are not, properly speaking, laws of the understanding, but universal laws of sensibility. Taste itself is an object about which we can do a good deal of reasoning [vernünfteln]. Such reasoning, however, does not constitute taste; it only enlarges it. The judgment of taste is [this]: one judges of this or that thing through and according to its appearance to the eye [Anblick]",[]],"blomberg-s03-038":["When a man has taste, he sees what he is choosing. That pleases almost everyone, and throughout",[]],"blomberg-s03-039":["The norm, the standard (how something ought to be) of taste, is not contained in the universal rules of reason; it is to be met with nowhere but in the actual works of taste. There must therefore be enduring models of taste, otherwise taste will soon cease to exist. In the same way there must be enduring models of style in a dead (learned) language, for such languages alone are unchangeable, namely, the Latin and the Greek. Much can be explained and known *a priori* that nevertheless cannot afterwards be put into practice at all.",[]],"blomberg-s03-040":["Man is polished, smoothed, not by instruction but solely by intercourse with others, and especially by intercourse with well-mannered and well-bred women.",[]],"blomberg-s03-041":["Genius and taste are to be very carefully distinguished from one another. Genius works, so to speak, in the rough, yet upon excellent material. It were to be wished that school instruction were so conducted",[]],"blomberg-s03-042":["1.) [as first] to work in the rough, but upon what is sublime",[]],"blomberg-s03-043":["2.) and then to polish.",[]],"blomberg-s03-044":["But [in the schools] everything must be polished, and become so, at once; more attention is paid to trifles and secondary matters than to what is essential, and compulsion is even employed in the process. Genius, on the other hand, is seldom cultivated. The harm that such school methods cause is very great; for example, in elegant Latin, where regard is never had to the thoughts, but always only to the expression, to the words.",[]],"blomberg-s03-045":["Only through intercourse and society is man polished. Models, models worthy of imitation, are necessary in order to form taste and to preserve it. The original pattern [Urbild] of all perfections, however, is always only in thought. It is an archetype [*Archetypon*], and merely an ideal. True beauty must, as it were, be sketched by a sensibility. Morals contains the archetype [*Archetypon*] of moral perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-046":["If, however, reason is not specially applied [*adhibiret*] in a representation, that representation is not rational knowledge, and is not for that reason irrational knowledge either. For example, if I know into what countries Europe is divided, I do not employ reason for this, but only memory; and on that account this is not rational knowledge. But what is not rational is not yet irrational; that holds only of knowledge which is contrary to reason. For example, if I have acquainted myself with the motion of the planets without employing reason, the knowledge I have of it is not rational knowledge, but neither is it irrational.",[]],"blomberg-s03-047":["Knowledge that is not rational, in which I employ not reason but only memory, and in which I do not see how a thing follows from its ground, is historical knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s03-048":["A perfect historical knowledge, the author says in § 19, is a *cognitio pulchra sive aesthetica* [beautiful or aesthetic knowledge]. But he is mistaken in taking beautiful and aesthetic to be the same, for aesthetic includes not only the beautiful but also the sublime. Historical knowledge is knowledge that is not rational. When, therefore, the author speaks of a perfect historical knowledge and takes it to be aesthetic, he can understand by it nothing other than a knowledge which, although it is not rational,",[]],"blomberg-s03-049":["can nevertheless still have a perfection. In this, however, he has explained himself very badly. For who can tell from it what sort of perfection this is supposed to be? It is just as if I were to say: a periwig is that which, although it is neither a hat nor a cap, can nevertheless equally well be put upon the head.",[]],"blomberg-s03-050":["We have already spoken at the outset of the aesthetic perfection of knowledge, and have said that it consists in the effect upon our feeling. From this we can readily gather what aesthetic knowledge is, namely, knowledge that acts upon our feeling (through pleasure or displeasure). Knowledge that is not rational is historical. For example, if someone has a great acquaintance with natural history, but does not know it in its connection with its grounds, he has no rational knowledge, but only historical knowledge. Elsewhere 'historical knowledge' signifies knowledge that refers only to individual things, and is opposed to dogmatic knowledge; but it is not taken here in that sense. The author holds every perfect historical knowledge to be beautiful. But knowledge can be historical without being beautiful. We may know from Homer everything mythological, all the travels of Ulysses, and so forth, and more besides, so that we have a quite perfect historical knowledge; and yet it will not be beautiful if it has no effect upon our feeling, if we regard everything coldly and feel nothing in the process. Only that knowledge, as we have already said above, is beautiful and aesthetic which has an influence upon our feeling of pleasure or displeasure. The beautiful in a mode of knowledge is diminished when its rational and distinct character is furthered, just as the beauty of a woman vanishes when she is examined through a microscope. The finest expressions, those that move us most, lose their force when they are analysed and known more distinctly by the understanding. Our understanding often rejects something, for example various kinds of clothing, which is nevertheless to our taste and moves our feeling. Hungarian dress is much better suited to the understanding than French; it would be better, more comfortable, and so forth. Again, it accords better with the understanding that the door should not stand in the middle of the house, thereby making the rooms small; but our feeling necessarily requires that everything be symmetrical, and that the door, for instance, should not stand at the corner of the house. Feeling is moved by confused knowledge, and is on that account very difficult to observe; so that a science of it, namely aesthetics [*Aesthetica*], is in general attended with very many difficulties. Baumgarten was the first to make a science of it.",[]],"blomberg-s03-051":["Rational knowledge has advantages over historical knowledge, inasmuch as it requires understanding and reason. Sometimes, however, historical knowledge has advantages over rational. Historical knowledge must lie at the basis of all rational knowledge. The brooder who shuts himself up in his room and seeks to acquire many modes of rational knowledge will not get far unless he has first procured for himself a historical knowledge, through experience drawn from books, or from intercourse and society with other people. Historical knowledge is given to me *a posteriori*; it already contains in itself everything that belongs to rational knowledge. It shows how something is; rational knowledge, on the other hand, shows how something ought to be (as is the case, for example, in morals). Rational knowledge only makes distinct what historical knowledge indicates; it alters only the formal element. Without historical knowledge reason has no materials [*Materialia*] which it is to make distinct. It is a great fault in our education that we occupy ourselves too early with rational knowledge, before we have a sufficient historical knowledge. Rational knowledge is not, however, more perfect than historical in all relations and in all cases. For example, when someone is to be moved, rational knowledge will have little or no effect.",[]],"blomberg-s03-052":["In respect of common life, historical knowledge is likewise of greater use, just as the peasants in a state are far more useful than all the mathematicians [*Mathematici*] and astronomers [*Astronomi*]. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-053":["There is no man who does not have metaphysical judgments. A science in general will consist of modes of knowledge which make up a whole.",[]],"blomberg-s03-054":["Philosophical knowledge has the same objects as historical and rational knowledge; only the form is different. It is undetermined where philosophical knowledge begins and rational knowledge ends.",[]],"blomberg-s03-055":["In the same way, for example, the degree in the case of the affects is undetermined. A greater degree of the sensuous desires is an affect; but how great this degree must be is uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s03-056":["In philosophical knowledge, then, we seek to discern, from the properties of things, their connection of grounds and consequences. Some suppose themselves to have philosophy when they are in fact without it, and others actually have it without being aware of it. Those who learn by heart the definitions [*Definitiones*] of Wolff and other philosophers think that they have philosophy. They have only a bare historical knowledge, and are quite unable actually to philosophise, and to think and judge of objects for themselves. At the least, they lack the facility to judge philosophically about a matter.",[]],"blomberg-s03-057":["In this paragraph [*Paragrapho*] the author speaks of the perfections of knowledge. He also explains what perfection is. The concept of perfection, however, properly belongs to metaphysics [*Metaphysicam*], and is here introduced in the wrong place. All perfection, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s03-058":["*1mo* practical",[]],"blomberg-s03-059":["*2do* theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s03-060":["Theoretical perfection properly concerns the quantity of [knowledge], as, for example, its extent and fruitfulness. Practical fruitfulness, on the other hand, is rather a perfection in respect of quality, as, for example, its importance and its beauty; and it is therefore again",[]],"blomberg-s03-061":["*1mo* logical",[]],"blomberg-s03-062":["2 aesthetic",[]],"blomberg-s03-063":["Beauty is properly a matter of art. Art, however, presupposes practice.",[]],"blomberg-s03-064":["In all perfection everything always depends upon our having before our eyes an end, a model, an exemplar and original pattern [Vor- und Urbild]. Only in respect of this can we properly judge and say whether one thing, and which, is more perfect than another. Such is, indeed, the constitution of human nature in general. Everyone who has seen many [men] forms for himself an original pattern, a model of the size and beauty of a man, which holds the mean in all the parts and kinds of the human figure. For example, among many faulty copies of a man, the original for the most part constitutes and holds the mean",[]],"blomberg-s03-065":["But from the combining of many partial beauties a perfect beauty cannot possibly be produced. Many beautiful but different garments sewn together would look ill. (*Nulla dies sine linea*, Apelles [no day without a line].) The artist, the painter for example, cannot possibly put together for himself the original pattern of a painting; rather he must create it. Logical perfections are those which are perceived only when the thing is viewed distinctly. Aesthetic perfections, on the other hand, are those which are felt through confused concepts. The beauty of all good poetic representations, for example of *Paradise Lost*, possesses an aesthetic perfection. It is felt only in confused concepts, and at once loses its value as soon as the concept is made distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-066":["There are, however, *1mo* beautiful objects,",[]],"blomberg-s03-067":["*2do* beautiful representations of objects.",[]],"blomberg-s03-068":["The ugliest woman can be beautifully painted, that is, excellently caught, and the most beautiful woman can be painted in an ugly fashion, that is, badly caught and disfigured. Beautiful objects can occur only in objects. Of a beautiful knowledge, however, no judgment can properly be made at all. For this we do indeed have examples, but these are not original patterns [Urbilder]. Models, again, are examples which come nearest to the original pattern.",[]],"blomberg-s03-069":["In mathematics the propositions and theses have logical perfections; the concepts in it are distinct. When the poet has given me the finest, most moving and most vivid representations of spring, when he has spoken of bleating, grazing flocks, of gently whispering zephyrs, of fragrant meadows, and so forth, my knowledge is aesthetically perfect. It is beautiful, but not distinct. I have not thereby obtained any distinct insight into the ground and causes of spring. This, however, the astronomer [*Astronomus*] can and must explain to me from the path of the sun. The French for the most part attend only to aesthetic perfection. When they inquire into the grounds of anything, and wish to have distinct concepts of a matter, they let themselves be drawn into what is beautiful, moving and pleasing, and thereby do great damage to logical perfection. There are learned men who are, properly speaking, nothing but artists of reason [Vernunft-Künstler], and imitative philosophers of this kind are commonly very obstinate in retaining opinions they have once adopted. Other learned men, on the other hand, are, as it were, so to speak, the masters and lawgivers of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s03-070":["The learning which is historical as regards its form but rational as regards its matter is entitled historical knowledge. There are very many *ciceroni* of philosophy, indeed many who do not even know what difference there is between philosophy and the science of philosophising.",[]],"blomberg-s03-071":["The imperfections of knowledge are also, in their turn, either logical or aesthetic. If the imperfections are met with in confused concepts, they are aesthetic; if they are found in distinct concepts, they are logical imperfections. For example, a Swiss poet says that, just as the Rhône flows through the Lake of Geneva and yet remains clear, so the truly virtuous ought to pass unharmed and undefiled through the midst of the crowd of the vicious. Here there is a logical imperfection. On closer investigation it is found that the Rhône flows into the Lake of Geneva muddy, so that it is through the lake that it becomes clear and pure.",[]],"blomberg-s03-072":["A knowledge which [lacks] what constitutes beauty in aesthetic perfection is dry, as for example in mathematical considerations. But when a knowledge is meant to be beautiful and this end is nevertheless not attained, it is ugly. This is the case with all those writers who wish to be witty and are not, and cannot be.",[]],"blomberg-s03-073":["We notice aesthetic imperfections far sooner than logical ones; for the former are at once repugnant to our feeling. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-074":["A historical knowledge is historical either",[]],"blomberg-s03-075":["1 *materialiter* [as regards its matter]",[]],"blomberg-s03-076":["2. *formaliter* [as regards its form]",[]],"blomberg-s03-077":["Further, to imitate another's science is entitled copying. There are sciences which can be learned, which are historical as regards their form but rational as regards their matter. Such are many sciences of reason; such, for example, is mathematics. Sciences of this kind can be acquired, and can indeed be communicated even in the degree of perfection appropriate to their nature. There are sciences of imitation, of learning, but there are also other sciences, those of genius, which cannot be learned.",[]],"blomberg-s03-078":["Philosophy, and the art of philosophising, cannot possibly be learned; mathematics, on the other hand, can. Thus I cannot teach any man new thoughts, wit, spirit, naïveté of expression, in the way that I teach him to write or to reckon. A philosophy could indeed be learned in a certain time if I acquired it by copying it out as it had been given to me. But then one would do no more than philosophise just so far as one had learned by heart; about what had thus been learned one would certainly not be in a position to philosophise in the least.",[]],"blomberg-s03-079":["The principles of mathematics, on the other hand, are for the most part intuitive, and their application likewise takes place according to a certain intuition [*intuitus*]; but this is impossible in philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s03-080":["The character of a true philosopher is this, that he does nothing but exercise the natural powers and capacities, and that by means of the searching inquiry of criticism [Critic].",[]],"blomberg-s03-081":["Philosophy can never be learned in half a year, as is commonly believed. Philosophy properly belongs, as a study, to men who hold office and have experience; although we think that philosophy is far easier than jurisprudence and medicine.",[]],"blomberg-s03-082":["Philosophy cultivates the universal capacities of nature by means of reason, and surpasses all the effects of these capacities. The inquiry which surpasses all [other] knowledge searches out the sources of the concepts of the understanding and their first origin; and this investigation is one of the most important. It necessarily follows that we do not in the least learn to philosophise when we copy out the philosophy of others and learn it with complete exactness. Rather, we may profit far more by accustoming ourselves to, and learning the science of, being able to criticise and judge the philosophy of others. My philosophy must be grounded in myself, and not in the understanding of others; it must not bind me to any original model. The method of teaching philosophy is therefore twofold:",[]],"blomberg-s03-083":["*1mo* that which cultivates the philosophical spirit, and which cannot be learned or imitated;",[]],"blomberg-s03-084":["*2do* or that which faithfully follows a particular author [*Autori*], explains him, and seeks in philosophy the aid of memory.",[]],"blomberg-s03-085":["This is indeed feasible in mathematics, but not in philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s03-086":["A knowledge may be merely learned, or learned and beautiful at once. In the former, distinctness alone is the aim, and such knowledge is dry. But that knowledge which not only instructs the understanding but also stimulates and allures feeling is a knowledge at once beautiful and learned.",[]],"blomberg-s03-087":["The latter knowledge, however, is the most difficult, and far harder to attain than the former. For if one goes only a little too far in beauty, one at once does damage to logical perfection; if, on the other hand, one seeks fully to further logical perfection, one becomes dry and loses the beautiful.",[]],"blomberg-s03-088":["To observe at once the learned and the beautiful in a mode of knowledge [presented] orally, and still more in writing, may thus truly be entitled the true philosophers' stone of the learned. But who possesses it?",[]],"blomberg-s03-089":["A perfect historical knowledge, if it is to be truly beautiful, is perfect not in respect of the material element but rather of the formal. For example, an extensive knowledge of geography is in itself, as historical, very useful and perfect, but not on that account beautiful. In the beautiful, everything always depends on the formal element, on the manner of the knowledge. If, therefore, M. de Voltaire, for example, presents the very same historical knowledge as Hübner, the material element of this knowledge will be one and the same in both presentations, but the formal element very different. In the former it will be quite other than in the latter: it will, namely, be beautiful, and will move feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-090":["The material element, as for example the distinctness and correctness of the knowledge, may be of the same character in the one as in the other. But the formal element, what moves, delights and flatters our feeling, is different. Distinctness and correctness therefore concern the material element, and consequently belong, as properties, to logical perfection. Being moved and delighted, however, which pleases all at once, concerns the formal element, and consequently belongs, as a property, to aesthetic perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-091":["In every aesthetic perfection I know things confusedly. Much acts upon me all at once, and thus puts me into a state of affect. For example, when a hero suddenly appears upon the stage, terrible enough to inspire dread, and with a great retinue, what is moving storms our soul. The understanding, on the other hand, leaves out much that seems to it superfluous; it seeks rather only to make its knowledge as distinct as possible. For that very reason it for the most part passes over all taste; all flattering ornaments, all paint, fall away; and what appears aesthetically very beautiful we nevertheless regard as very wretched when we consider it again through the understanding alone. The French nation has this peculiarity, that it attends more, and most of all, to the aesthetic, to outward adornment. The English nation, on the other hand, cultivates rather logical perfection. Hence it comes that our understanding commonly learns little or nothing from French books (though some are excepted from this). After reading them we can indeed recall that we were delighted by them as by music; but we know at the same time that the understanding has learned nothing from them, which is not often the case with English books.",[]],"blomberg-s03-092":["These two paragraphs contain perfections of knowledge. Extent, among others, through which many objects are known, is certainly a perfection. For example, he who has made progress in true geography [may know many places]; [but] he who knows many towns, countries, rivers, etc. by name, together with their positions, may possess perfectly only the so-called geography of names.",[]],"blomberg-s03-093":["In § 26 the author speaks of the magnitude of knowledge, as being likewise a perfection. In every magnitude there is always a multitude, but not in every multitude is there as yet a magnitude. In every magnitude I can consider the extensive and the intensive. In the first case, when I consider the extensive [aspect] of a magnitude, I look to the multitude of the units, to their number, how many of them there are. In the other case, when I consider the intensive magnitude, I look especially to the magnitude of each unit. So it is also with knowledge. If I consider only the multitude of the objects that I know, I look merely to the extensive magnitude of the knowledge. But if I know a single object wholly and quite perfectly, the knowledge is certainly [great] *intensive*. In the latter everything properly depends on the degree in which I know each unit.",[]],"blomberg-s03-094":["Truth is a perfection which belongs both to the logical and to the aesthetic perfection of knowledge. It is the chief logical perfection, upon which all the others depend. When I know a thing as it actually is, my knowledge is true. Truth is required also for aesthetic perfection. That is why Milton is censured for having represented Death and Sin as persons, as it were, since this does not accord with their nature. In the aesthetically perfect, however, so great a truth is not required as in the logically perfect. In the aesthetic, a thing need only be tolerably [*tolerabiliter*] true. In this way it is aesthetically true that Milton in *Paradise Lost* represents the angels as fighting and engaged in battle; for who knows whether this may not occur? A good fable must always contain an aesthetic truth. Such truth there is, for example, in Aesop's fable of the wolf, when he says of the sheep and the wolf at the river that the patient lamb excused itself quite meekly against the malicious accusation of the wolf and strove to appease his anger, but that the bloodthirsty and unjust wolf replied, or at least by his worthless answer signified: In short, I am now hungry, I must eat you –.",[]],"blomberg-s03-095":["Distinctness, further, is also a perfection of knowledge; and it has this peculiarity, that it belongs as a necessary property both to logical and to aesthetic perfection. An extensive distinctness, which is met with in the beautiful understanding, is an aesthetic one pure and simple; intensive distinctness, on the other hand, which is found in the deep understanding, belongs to logical perfection. The poet describes spring to me with extensive distinctness: he heaps many marks [Merkmale] upon one another. The philosopher, on the other hand, describes it to me with intensive distinctness: he does not look to the multitude of the marks, but seeks rather to represent only a few, or if need be even a single mark, quite clearly and distinctly.",[]],"blomberg-s03-096":["The beauty of the understanding rests upon our having many marks of a thing. The depth of the understanding, on the other hand, requires only that a few marks be clearly known, and that they be at the same time distinct and easy to discern.",[]],"blomberg-s03-097":["The author here defines certainty as ‘the consciousness of the truth of a knowledge’. As a provisional definition this is adequate and sufficient; we shall speak of it more fully later. Certainty belongs to the logically and to the aesthetically perfect. The uncertain, the wavering, is always displeasing. For aesthetic certainty authority [*Auctoritaet*] is often quite sufficient; that is to say, it is enough to appeal to the standing of great men who have believed, for example, in the immortality of the soul or in a future world. One will thereby be sufficiently convinced aesthetically, although this does not yet constitute logical certainty. What great men, highly esteemed by the learned world, say is therefore a ground for aesthetic certainty, but never for logical certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s03-098":["If a knowledge is to be aesthetically true, a very great deal must be heaped together in it, and a multitude of representations brought forward all at once. If, for example, I wish to prove the infamy of this or that deed, I shall not point out and demonstrate what is infamous in it according to morals; rather I digress, so to speak. I show, for example, how the rocks, trees, etc., tremble at this deed, how everything quakes, how the heavens wrap themselves in gloomy clouds so as not to be witnesses of a deed so abominable. He, then, who acquires such knowledge, or knowledge of this kind, must also be dazzled by its confusedness. Hence it comes that the judgments of other people are already sufficient for its certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s03-099":["We entitle practical knowledge that which has an influence upon our will, and *per consequentiam* [consequently] also upon our actions (which are to be governed by the will).",[]],"blomberg-s03-100":["From the consideration of all the perfections of the modes of knowledge we see that the chief faculties of our knowledge are properly the following: 1. understanding, 2. feeling, and 3. desire.",[]],"blomberg-s03-101":["If, therefore, 1. I make my knowledge perfect in respect of my understanding, it is logically perfect. If *2do* I make my knowledge perfect in respect of my feeling, it is aesthetically perfect",[]],"blomberg-s03-102":["If, finally, *3tio* I make my knowledge perfect in respect of my desires, it is practically, or also morally, perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s03-103":["Moral perfection rests upon logical and aesthetic perfection taken together. To logical perfection, however, the following three elements belong:",[]],"blomberg-s03-104":["*1mo* distinctness",[]],"blomberg-s03-105":["2 truth, and",[]],"blomberg-s03-106":["*3tio* certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s03-107":["Distinctness, however, is the first and chief property, upon which everything especially depends in a logical perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s03-108":["Truth and certainty alike are of little help if they are not distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s03-109":["The three chief characteristics [*Caractères*] of an aesthetic perfection, however, are",[]],"blomberg-s03-110":["*1mo* truth",[]],"blomberg-s03-111":["*2do* certainty, and especially *3tio* extent",[]],"blomberg-s03-112":["For in an aesthetic perfection the knowledge is not distinct but confused. Yet truth, because of what is confused and heaped together in the knowledge, cannot indeed be met with in any high degree in aesthetic perfection; for such perfection does not properly occupy my will, but rather only my taste. And so it is also with certainty. Not every sensible certainty is on that account aesthetically perfect, but properly only that which moves our feeling and flatters it. One may, for example, have the greatest acquaintance with geography, etc., which, although it is perfect, is nevertheless not aesthetically perfect, because it does not move our feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-113":["The author here sums everything up, and says that we ought to make our knowledge at once logically and aesthetically perfect. The whole of the knowledge that we possess ought to have every possible kind of perfection. But who can achieve [*prästiren*] this? In each part of our knowledge it very often cannot take place at all. If, for example, I wish to make a book logically perfect, I neither must nor can at the same time introduce the aesthetic and the practical everywhere; and if, on the other hand, I wish to make a book aesthetically or practically perfect, I cannot in so doing everywhere think of introducing the logically perfect. –",[]],"blomberg-s03-114":["Many theological propositions do not, after all, immediately bind our will [*Willkür*]. We can therefore leave out much of the logical and aesthetic perfection without any harm to man. A wise teacher conceals much that is fine in what he knows, and forgoes it, when he knows that the minds of his hearers are so constituted that they might become habituated to speculation and so be turned away from the practical. In just the same way, in certain cases, we must often in turn abstract from the practical in one matter or another. Many things can be true and yet harmful to man. Not all truth is useful. At the same time it is also certain that whatever harmfulness a truth may ever have is always merely contingent. Thus there are certainly recommendations of certain modes of knowledge and of certain judgments which may in the end amount to bribery [*Bestechungen*]. And there are, again, disparagements, contempt and censure of other modes of knowledge or judgments, without any enquiry into how these modes of knowledge are really constituted. To avoid both these faults, therefore, we should, in every examination of a mode of knowledge, take it entirely by itself, apart from all extraneous questions of utility or harm, and especially when this utility or harm is of concern to ourselves. For otherwise partiality at once pronounces the judgment and stifles all the cool reflection of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-115":["Opinion [*das Meinen*] has at all times been the fruitful source of all the errors of the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-116":["All the actions of men proceed from the impulse towards perfection. This perfection is attained when our feeling of pleasure and displeasure is affected. If the feeling presupposes rational knowledge, it is a rational pleasure and feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-117":["If, however, it follows only from confused knowledge, it is a sensible pleasure and feeling. We must make our knowledge perfect in respect of both kinds of feeling.",[]],"blomberg-s03-118":["What affects our rational feeling is logically perfect; for instance, when I take delight in thorough instruction. What affects our sensible feeling is aesthetically perfect.",[]],"blomberg-s03-119":["In general, the horizon of our knowledge as practically determined is far narrower than the horizon of knowledge as logically determined.",[]],"blomberg-s03-120":["Here the author shows how logical perfections can be combined with aesthetic ones. Logical perfections, for instance, are found in Wolff and in other writings, but no aesthetic ones. In works of belles-lettres [*schönen Büchern*], on the other hand, we find fewer logical and far more aesthetic perfections. The authors of such books, however, hold the lowest rank. Everything that charms and moves us is detrimental to our power of judgment [*Urteilskraft*].",[]],"blomberg-s03-121":["Aesthetic perfection, and especially emotion [*Rührungen*] and charm, is in many respects opposed to logical perfection, since the former concerns sensibility, the latter the abstract. In many respects, however, it is required in addition to [*nach*] logical perfection, namely, for whatever belongs to sensible aesthetic intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s03-122":["Whoever wishes to convince [others] truly and lastingly of the truth of his propositions must abstain from all sensation, impression, charm and studied beauty. Otherwise the reader may believe that he means merely to persuade him, to deceive and flatter him, so that he may give assent to propositions which the author is not in a position to prove to him.",[]],"blomberg-s03-123":["The whole of the knowledge that we possess ought to be both logical and aesthetic. If, therefore, we wish to increase its logical perfections, we must not destroy the aesthetic ones. And if we wish to promote the beauty of knowledge, we must not wholly neglect the logical. Whoever, then, wishes to cultivate his understanding together with his taste should not read merely comedies, novels, *galant* tales and other books of that kind, in which there is indeed beautiful and aesthetic knowledge, but no logical perfection whatever. Feeling is thereby indeed affected and taste refined; but it is equally certain that the understanding suffers harm, being rendered duller and less serviceable. With children we ought to begin from what is sensible. Geography could be presented to them in such a way that they are all the while feeling and being moved. Instead of making them learn the names by heart *verbotenus* [word for word], we might rather tell them of the remarkable things of the sea, the peculiar customs of foreign nations and peoples, and so forth; and not, as commonly happens, merely point out on the map, without any comment: that is Paris, that is London, etc. From history, again, the events that have taken place could be related to them in the liveliest and most moving manner, so that their feelings and desires would thereby be improved. In riper years we could also begin various exercises of the understanding with them. We could let them read for themselves well-chosen poems and good speeches, and reflect upon them.",[]],"blomberg-s03-124":["We must not, however, as commonly happens in school, force them into oratory, often before they are yet able to think. It is of no use to them afterwards, except that they forget it; for what we have had to learn unwillingly, or even under compulsion, we very soon forget of ourselves. Dry modes of knowledge, which are nevertheless so much in favour, such as reciting the names of places and emperors, dates and the like, corrupt the young in the worst way. The child learns them with vexation and does not retain them, but seeks only, from fear of punishment, to recite its lesson readily. That what it has once learned should remain in its memory is not at all its aim. Even if we tell it that otherwise it cannot be or become happy, it pays no heed; it is still entirely without self-interest, and content only with its present pleasure, and so abhors whatever would diminish that pleasure. It will therefore naturally retain, and seek to retain, only what pleases it and affects its feeling. If, then, everything were presented in this manner, it would be able to retain everything willingly and with pleasure.",[]],"blomberg-s03-125":["Truthfulness is the greatest virtue in the world, the one on which all the others rest; and all the other virtues are in fact, without truthfulness, nothing but dissimulations. This virtue, therefore, ought to be taught to children first, and at the same time made habitual in them everywhere, if not yet by words, then at least by looks.",[]],"blomberg-s03-126":["We should endeavour above all to implant in children a strong disgust for each and every untruth, under whatever pretext it might be excused, such as they have, for instance, for a caterpillar or other vermin. They will then gradually become accustomed in their lives to lie as little as to touch a caterpillar. They will always be honest, and will hate every possible dissimulation.",[]],"blomberg-s03-127":["Every man has, further, a natural and as it were innate impulse to benevolence. Just as we rejoice when we can make another laugh with our sallies, so we rejoice still more when we have made others happy through our agency, or merely given them joy, pleasure, etc. (Only a few malicious minds, who wish for the misfortune of their fellow-men or even promote it, are an exception.) We should therefore know how to preserve this impulse in a child also. Give a child something, for instance, to give to other children who are poorer, but not only when these, being in misery, beg for it as beggars. For in that way we merely accustom children to compassion [*Mitleiden*], which is still very different from true mercy [*Barmherzigkeit*]. Rather, we should let the child share something with others merely for the sake of giving, because it is fine and praiseworthy to share what one has to spare, and because the others deserve it, even though they do not ask for it, or have not the heart to do so.",[]],"blomberg-s03-128":["When so much has been instilled into it through sensation, this will certainly at once improve its moral character; and it will take pleasure in moral stories.",[]],"blomberg-s03-129":["Once this has been achieved, we may teach it to read. It will then see for itself what help the ability to read affords it. It will itself acquire a desire for it, and will strive to gain facility in it the sooner the better, since reading enables it to read letters, poems, moral episodes and other fine books for itself, and no longer to need to ask others to tell it something. When this has been done, we should seek to promote its historical knowledge, since this must underlie rational knowledge. We should on no account burden it with an enormous multitude of names; of these it naturally sees no use, and consequently has no desire for them.",[]],"blomberg-s03-130":["If the histories are treated morally, it will take pleasure in them, and will regard the names only as accessory matters, but will retain them nevertheless.",[]],"blomberg-s03-131":["Finally, we should seek also to extend and promote its rational knowledge. We should tell it the grounds when it asks the cause of something, but not burden it with remote grounds. If it asks why no corn grows on this or that field, we should tell it: because the soil is sandy, etc., but not give it a lengthy explanation of fertility. That serves no purpose except to confuse its understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s03-132":["In this section [*§pho*] the author says that small perfections must be given up when greater ones are thereby hindered. Greater logical perfections must be preferred to small aesthetic ones, and greater aesthetic perfections in turn to smaller logical ones.",[]],"blomberg-s03-133":["If we follow this rule, we shall be able to write in a manner both logically and aesthetically fine, and to express ourselves [likewise] in an oral discourse [*Vortrag*].",[]],"blomberg-s03-134":["The opposite of any perfection is always twofold: either, first, the contradictory opposite, when the perfection is merely lacking; or, secondly, the real opposite, which wholly cancels the perfection that might otherwise be present on some other ground. An example of the first would be such medicines [*medicamenta*], often enough prescribed, as do no good at all but also no harm; these may fitly be compared with the contradictory opposite of healing. Those remedies, on the other hand, which are not only of no use but even do harm, constitute the real opposite of healing. And the case is just the same with modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s03-135":["The imperfection which is the contradictory opposite is entitled lack [*Mangel*]; the imperfection which is the real opposite of the perfect is entitled fault [*Fehler*]. Thus, for instance, ignorance of the immortality of the human soul is the contradictory opposite; whereas error, in which one even believes that the human soul is not immortal, is the real opposite of the knowledge of the soul's immortality. In ignorance only grounds are lacking, and it can therefore easily be remedied. In error, however, there are actual grounds, which are opposed to true knowledge as counter-grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s03-136":["Faults, *vitia*, are therefore to be avoided far more than lacks, *defectus*. It is surely far worse to cancel what has already been actually proved on other grounds than to know nothing as yet, since in the latter case I can more readily be instructed than in the former. For example, he who does not define a thing at all can still make shift with his common knowledge; but if he frames false definitions [*definitiones*] and afterwards applies them, there arises a far greater and more serious harm, which could not have arisen from a mere lack. With *vitia* knowledge is not only not increased, but rather diminished; indeed, even what is already present is actually cancelled.",[]],"blomberg-s03-137":["Common and historical knowledge can be much more perfect than learned knowledge. They are to be regarded as heterogeneous [*heterogenea*], but they can nevertheless also be considered in relation to a focus of perfection [*focum perfectionis*], as, for instance, whether they are lively, fruitful, and so forth. By historical knowledge we understand not aesthetic knowledge, but merely dry knowledge. This dry knowledge in a man, of the circumstances and events of his life, for instance, is, however, much more fruitful and more extensive than learned knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s03-138":["A mathematician who otherwise possesses much knowledge will nevertheless not get as far in practice [*in praxi*] with his science of engineering as another who has not studied the theory, but has acquired a merely historical knowledge of the practice itself.",[]],"blomberg-s03-139":["Machiavelli, for example, had great learned knowledge of how to draw up an entire army in order of battle. But when he was once actually charged with commanding an army, instead of ordering it he threw the whole army into disorder, because he had no historical knowledge of the attendant circumstances.",[]],"blomberg-s03-140":["In many matters the learned man, even with his solid knowledge, actually accomplishes not nearly as much as another whose knowledge is historical, since the former [knowledge] is not so generally useful as the latter.",[]],"blomberg-s03-141":["Rousseau holds that the sciences have brought more harm than benefit. We have capacities far greater than are needed for this life. Our theoretical capacities are stronger than our practical ones, and we can improve the former more than the latter. From this arises a disproportion, a monstrosity, in which the head is too large in relation to [*en régard*] the other members of the body. Were we to expect no other life, learning would certainly be more harmful than useful to us; for here we often obtain far from sufficient advantage for the pains we take. Yet learning has already procured us many advantages even here. Navigation, the art of government and so forth would certainly have remained very obscure and imperfect without it. How great a benefit it is that through it alone, as by a light, the darkness of superstition has been dispelled and superstition so happily rooted out! Many old women can now grow old with honour and end their lives in peace, whereas formerly they very often fell under suspicion, and indeed had to lose their lives miserably.",[]],"blomberg-s03-142":["Learned knowledge considers the universal, and therefore gives occasion for inventions and improvements.",[]],"blomberg-s03-143":["When learned knowledge is at the same time beautiful, it is the most useful and serviceable, especially in practice [*in praxi*]. We know how the knowledge of the Scholastics, those dry philosophers who bound themselves to a very constrained method, as it were destroyed the moral qualities and capacities of men, and debased them to the point of superstition. An irreparable loss! All this, however, could indeed not have been otherwise. It arose solely from the fact that their modes of knowledge, and consequently also their teaching of morals, etc., were merely learned, and not at the same time beautiful, but rather entirely dry. So harmful, then, is a knowledge that is beautiful but not learned, and one that is learned but dry.",[]],"blomberg-s04-001":["The extensiveness of learned knowledge is opposed to its poverty. This poverty concerns either the material or the formal [element]. When someone lacks historical knowledge, he is in complete ignorance. Thus, for instance, the ancients were in complete ignorance of the existence of the New World; they had no historical knowledge of it. The common man is still entirely ignorant in respect of many things. But one can also be in ignorance in respect of the grounds of a thing: 1. in that it is wholly unknown to us, so that we have no historical knowledge of it; for instance, when we do not know at all whence storms, rain, snow, etc., arise.",[]],"blomberg-s04-002":["2do. When we do not at all see into the connection [*nexum*] of the thing with its ground, although the grounds are known to us; for instance, when I know indeed that the ebb and flow of the tide have the moon as their cause, but do not yet understand and see how it comes about that ebb and flow proceed from the attraction of the moon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-003":["As regards ignorance, it can be divided",[]],"blomberg-s04-004":["1: into necessary ignorance, and on the other hand",[]],"blomberg-s04-005":["2: into voluntary ignorance.",[]],"blomberg-s04-006":["Where no data are given us for settling something, the ignorance is necessary; for instance, to settle where the human soul will be after the death of the body; likewise [*item*] concerning the generative power of animals and men.",[]],"blomberg-s04-007":["Thirdly, the place we occupy in the world makes ignorance necessary for us. If, on the contrary, from certain motives we voluntarily dispense with certain things, so that we deliberately do not wish to know them, when we see that something is too difficult for us or without use, this is a logical ignorance.",[]],"blomberg-s04-008":["Logic is rather a doctrine of skill, which belongs to the youth; the doctrine of wisdom, however, belongs to the man.",[]],"blomberg-s04-009":["The circle within which we can see things is entitled our horizon. The sum [*Inbegriff*] of all things which man can know in a learned manner without detriment to his other perfections is the horizon of learned knowledge. What is above the horizon can, in the geographical sense, be seen. Here, however, it is to signify such things as we cannot know even if we wished to, and ignorance of which is necessary. Of many things we are ignorant even as regards historical knowledge; for instance, of the joys of heaven, and of the mysteries, perhaps because they cannot be expressed in our words. Of some things we are often ignorant on account of our weak capacities, of others in respect of circumstances, time and place.",[]],"blomberg-s04-010":["We determine the horizon of knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s04-011":["1. logically, merely by our faculty, by the measure of our powers;",[]],"blomberg-s04-012":["2. practically, according to ends.",[]],"blomberg-s04-013":["It is believed that it is always good to know more than one need, and better to know too much than too little. But all effort directed to an aim in a greater degree [than the aim requires] is superfluous and ill applied. It is therefore necessary that all our efforts be proportioned to the ends we have once set ourselves; otherwise man squanders the capital of his powers, which is after all very limited. We say that something goes beyond our horizon when it goes beyond the capacities of our knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s04-014":["Many more things are above the learned than above the historical horizon; the former is therefore far smaller than the latter. Much that I know historically I cannot always know in a learned and beautiful manner. Of the *commercium animae et corporis* [intercourse of soul and body], for instance, I have indeed a historical, but not a learned, knowledge. We must therefore not engage with all objects, but must always bear in mind that we have a horizon in knowledge. Thus it is also not necessary to embark upon enquiry into the state of the soul after death, which is above our horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-015":["It is very difficult to determine what is really above our horizon. Even if something often appears to me to be above my horizon, so that I even hold it to be impossible, it may nevertheless in time, through enquiry and diligence, come beneath my horizon. To assert of all things that they are above our horizon is the true philosophy of the lazy.",[]],"blomberg-s04-016":["But if someone says: I can indeed know something, but I do not need it, it is superfluous to me, or even harmful to me, I ought not to know it, this amounts to saying: it is beyond my horizon. According to taste the horizon is determined",[]],"blomberg-s04-017":["1. According to the taste of the individual [*des Menschen*].",[]],"blomberg-s04-018":["2. According to the taste of the age.",[]],"blomberg-s04-019":["If, however, it is true that most men draw their horizon of knowledge only according to taste, the result is merely superficial knowledge, [acquired] in order to appear learned to everyone and to be able to judge of everything in a learned manner. This, however, is as it were only the froth of knowledge, and it even requires a certain audacity.",[]],"blomberg-s04-020":["Our age is very nearly such a one. But the harm of this is also inevitable, namely, that in the end all the sciences are treated only cursorily, on the surface.",[]],"blomberg-s04-021":["The more a science is restricted to a single aim, the greater the degree of perfection it can attain. In our times one might almost think that everything was refined and learned. Women, instead of doing coarse household work as formerly, now read refined writings. This is called being well read, and in it they seek a distinction. But it is precisely this great extension that causes science to lose its value. The logical horizon in respect of our capacities is 1.) historical, which is and must be the most extensive;",[]],"blomberg-s04-022":["2.) rational, however, it is very narrow.",[]],"blomberg-s04-023":["The historical horizon is to be cultivated especially in our youth; but the aim for which I acquire historical knowledge [*Kenntnis*] must also be known.",[]],"blomberg-s04-024":["The polyhistor is he who possesses all the historical knowledge of all possible sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s04-025":["Philosophy is the field of all historical knowledge, of the theologian, the physicist [*Physicus*]",[]],"blomberg-s04-026":["A polyhistor brings together the materials for a science; his head is a veritable library [*bibliotheca*]. To polyhistory, however, there also belongs in particular philology, the science of the instruments of learning.",[]],"blomberg-s04-027":["The more perfect a science is to become in its kind, the narrower it becomes. Those who possess all historical and all rational knowledge are the great, universal minds; but of these there are only very few.",[]],"blomberg-s04-028":["Everyone must bear in mind that, in accordance with his capacities, be they great or small, he has a determinate horizon suited to them. For instance, he who knows something of geometry, even if it be not yet much, must and can well think that finding the quadrature of the circle is above his horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-029":["Our horizon of knowledge changes with time. What is now above it may well come, in time, beneath it, when I acquire more capacities and cultivate them properly and better.",[]],"blomberg-s04-030":["A beginner commonly thinks that nothing is above, but everything beneath, his horizon, and that he is perfectly able to know and settle everything. In time, however, one sees through the illusion, and knows how to limit one's horizon; or one may even fall into the contrary thought that everything is difficult for us, or above our horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-031":["How many there are, too, among the so-called philosophers, philosophers in name only, who are vain of their outward lustre and wholly taken with it, and even think that they are able to be teachers of philosophy, although this lies very far beyond their horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-032":["Things are degraded beneath the horizon of learned knowledge when they are unworthy of our learned knowledge. Some absurd opinions of the ancient philosophers are not even worthy of our historical knowledge, such that we should acquaint ourselves with them.",[]],"blomberg-s04-033":["In and about nature, however, not the least thing is unworthy of our historical knowledge, unless one were to devote oneself to it so much as thereby to neglect more important things.",[]],"blomberg-s04-034":["Delusion [*Wahn*] and methods have also brought a great deal beneath the horizon of our knowledge. The examination of urine, for instance, is at present, owing to men's delusion, held to be almost indecent, although it serves very greatly in the diagnosis of diseases.",[]],"blomberg-s04-035":["Those things are outside the horizon of our learned knowledge which are excluded because they cause us to neglect more important things. For an unlimited understanding nothing can be outside the horizon; this occurs only with a limited understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s04-036":["We must be sparing with our capacities, so that we may also be able to apply them to important things.",[]],"blomberg-s04-037":["Whatever, then, does not belong to the science which we have chosen to cultivate properly and in earnest is already outside our horizon, whether it be above or beneath it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-038":["To occupy oneself chiefly with matters foreign [to one's science], to the neglect of more important ones, is properly entitled overstepping one's horizon. The historical horizon may be made as wide as possible. Very seldom is a thing outside the historical horizon, but more readily outside the learned horizon.",[]],"blomberg-s04-039":["As the understanding grows, more and more objects come beneath our horizon, and it gradually widens.",[]],"blomberg-s04-040":["Rousseau says quite rightly that all who have written on education, and likewise most parents, wish to make perfect men out of children, whereas they ought first to form them into perfect children. They bring beneath the horizon of the child what properly belongs beneath the horizon of the man.",[]],"blomberg-s04-041":["Within our horizon is that which is neither beneath, nor above, nor outside it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-042":["The determination of the learned horizon is too difficult to be made by a youth.",[]],"blomberg-s04-043":["Men of insight, who have themselves gone astray in their own way, can best determine the horizon. They will show the learner what is necessary and what is unnecessary, and how very easily one can go astray here.",[]],"blomberg-s04-044":["It is better to seek at first to gain insight into very many things than to wish to learn only a few, and these quite thoroughly, since the former is much easier and more attractive for a youth. We should seek to form a general concept [*Hauptbegriff*] of many sciences, and afterwards take up one science after another in due order. This would have the advantage that, when one had resolved to treat this or that single science quite thoroughly, one could see its connection [*nexum*] with other sciences, and in each case bring in elucidation from the other. It is therefore very perverse when parents seek to determine their children's horizon and way of life while they are still in the cradle; when, for instance, from the fact that a child makes a serious face and can shout and scold lustily, they infer that it will be well suited to the pulpit; and from the fact that another in its youth likes to examine all sorts of things closely, to dissect and take them apart, they conjecture that he must one day become a skilled anatomist.",[]],"blomberg-s04-045":["Ignorance is always an imperfection, and can therefore certainly never be praiseworthy. It can, however, well be blameless, namely when we renounce certain objects so as not thereby to neglect more important modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s04-046":["Leibniz might have had a blameless ignorance in many matters; for instance, in chemistry, in antiquities, and in general in very many other things which overburden a great genius all too much.",[]],"blomberg-s04-047":["Ignorance can be divided into common and learned ignorance. For a learned man first to see properly that he knows nothing, great learning is required.",[]],"blomberg-s04-048":["*Quantum est quod nescimus!* [How much there is that we do not know!] But no one is wiser in his own conceit than a beginner. For he does not yet understand at all what it means to know little, or much, or nothing; nor does he himself know whether he knows anything or not. In time, however, one learns to estimate one's insights, and to see that they still have very great defects and imperfections.",[]],"blomberg-s04-049":["Socrates held it to be his greatest learning that he saw that he knew nothing. – A beginner believes himself able to define and demonstrate everything, but does not at all see what a labyrinth so many matters are for him. This he learns to recognise only when he already possesses more learning. The learned man is in many things just as ignorant as the common man; but he is nevertheless able to philosophise about this very ignorance, and to indicate its ground and its degree.",[]],"blomberg-s04-050":["Only when one has already undertaken many enquiries, learned much and investigated much, does one first learn to recognise one's ignorance. Who would imagine that of substances we as yet know only a merely external determination, and no other whatsoever? Only he who has already sufficiently traversed the fields of learning knows this, and is able to see how slight, how defective and imperfect, all his modes of knowledge are.",[]],"blomberg-s04-051":["Knowledge which is extensively great, or which comprises very many objects, the author entitles polyhistory. Those who study for the sake of fashionable accomplishment [*aus Galanterie*] commonly devote themselves to this polyhistory. Their knowledge resembles a great country which contains many blank places, and which can be not nearly so fruitful as a country which, though smaller, is better cultivated.",[]],"blomberg-s04-052":["Knowledge becomes ample and complete when it is sufficient for a certain end. It is precise when it contains no more than is required for the end which this knowledge has in view.",[]],"blomberg-s04-053":["Many a preacher, for instance, may have knowledge enough for the office of schoolmaster, though not for the office of preacher. He would certainly have become an excellent schoolteacher, whereas now he is a wretched preacher.",[]],"blomberg-s04-054":["On the other hand, many a schoolteacher, who now can discharge his office only poorly, would have become a better preacher than he now is a schoolmaster.",[]],"blomberg-s04-055":["He who merely runs through learning, acquainting himself with everything only historically so that he may be able to talk of everything in company, has only a meagre learning, and as it were nothing but a skeleton of it",[]],"blomberg-s04-056":["Such a man is contemptible in the same degree as he who makes a great show with his clothes, but within himself suffers the greatest torment from a shrivelled stomach, or from other ailments and wretched conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s04-057":["He who possesses meagre knowledge can never satisfy himself, and sees well enough that it is a mere sham [*Blendnuß*]. By himself he cannot be content and happy; he is unable to amuse himself alone. He must seek out others, go among others, and takes pleasure in the fact that others, ignorant people perhaps, at least hold him to be learned.",[]],"blomberg-s04-058":["We must always be intent on extending our learned knowledge. If we do not go forward, we certainly always go backward.",[]],"blomberg-s04-059":["We must have an immediate inclination towards learning if we are to increase it constantly and never let it decline. [Otherwise] one commonly acquires thereby only as much as one needs to earn one's bread and be able to live.",[]],"blomberg-s04-060":["Certainly we should never overload our heads with learning and do ourselves harm, if we duly observed this and took the easy before the difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s04-061":["It would be better if all the sciences were at first presented quite easily, so that not much beyond the common use of the understanding would be needed for them. Once the sciences had been presented in this way, they could then be presented in their full perfection and subtlety, which is what commonly happens at the outset,",[]],"blomberg-s04-062":["although this perfection is not needed by all, and much harm often arises from it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-063":["Many would be better satisfied at the outset with a preliminary presentation of the sciences, for instance after Gesner's *Isagoge*, than with a more extensive presentation of them, which thereby becomes obscure and difficult",[]],"blomberg-s04-064":["Hippocrates says wisely: *Ars longa, vita brevis* [Art is long, life is short]. Negligent people commonly complain of the length of the time they have still to live. But the time which they have already spent wholly idle and dreaming seems to them to have been very fleeting, and appears very short, because they have done nothing in it, and consequently can remember nothing of importance that took place in it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-065":["Many sciences are of such a kind that, in the course of time, their compass comes to exceed human capacities.",[]],"blomberg-s04-066":["History, for instance, is already very extensive; and with time and its length more and more is still happening. More and more events continue to take place in the world. All these accrue to history, and this science will in the end become and grow so extensive that our memory will be far from large enough for it, since even now it is so difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s04-067":["The same will perhaps befall mathematics. For what it now contains is already far too great for our slender capacities. In the course of time, perhaps, it will grow so great that our descendants will no longer even venture to undertake it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-068":["In time, perhaps, the writings of mathematics will stand like the Egyptian pyramids, which no one imitates, and which serve only to recall those who went before us in former ages.",[]],"blomberg-s04-069":["But although learning [*Gelahrtsamkeit*] is so very extensive, our life is still long enough for us to survey it properly, if we were willing to devote all our time to it.",[]],"blomberg-s04-070":["To earn one's bread is an ignoble and unworthy end for learning. Great insight is of no service whatsoever in earning one's bread. A small measure of learning, boldness (*hardiesse*),",[]],"blomberg-s04-071":["the art of disputation, and the effrontery to exalt oneself and to belittle other great minds, are nowadays the most serviceable means of earning one's bread. Anyone at all may attain to office, the deserving alone excepted.",[]],"blomberg-s04-072":["To guard against forgetfulness, says the author, one must learn much, so that one may still know something even when in the course of time one has forgotten a part, and may not be stripped all at once of all knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s04-073":["The best course, however, is doubtless to acquire once for all the skill to philosophise upon the objects themselves and to cultivate [*excoliren*] the sciences. He who possesses this skill is like a musician who has indeed forgotten the pieces he learned by hand, but who can play at sight whatever is set before him, and can also compose for himself.",[]],"blomberg-s04-074":["A scholar who brings his science very much into his social intercourse is entitled a pedant. To be a pedant, however, one must actually possess a certain skill, which one then seeks to display everywhere, even where it is often unfitting. He must not be wholly stupid and ignorant. There can be pedants in every station, not only among scholars. Thus among the nobility, for instance, there are hunting-pedants, who talk of nothing but their hunting, their hounds, the foxes that have played them tricks, hares, roe-deer, stags, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s04-075":["So, too, there are pedants of dress, who cannot fret enough and know no end of complaining when a cuff, a curl or a ribbon has been spoiled or tangled. In company a man is entitled a pedant when he introduces into his conversation needless inquiries and distinctions [*distinctiones*], which would far sooner and better befit the professorial chair than a party of pleasure.",[]],"blomberg-s04-076":["In learning one becomes a pedant when one is always wanting to define and to raise needless scruples. The methods now in use for instructing women are ways of making pedants of them.",[]],"blomberg-s04-077":["To charlatanry belongs this: that with one's small insight one thinks oneself great, gives oneself airs, and even sets oneself above others.",[]],"blomberg-s04-078":["The mathematical method, with which men still made a great show in former times and sought to appear thorough, was nothing but a kind of charlatanry. To play or to boast with one's demonstrations or with one's wit is likewise charlatanry.",[]],"blomberg-s04-079":["To avoid this effectively, let one acquire a thorough learning. Once this has been done, one will at once perceive and recognise how much we lack, and how little reason we have to make a show of our plain sciences and acquirements [*Kenntnisse*]; how much greater reason we have, rather, to exclaim in humility: *Quantum est, quod nescimus* [How much there is that we do not know].",[]],"blomberg-s05-001":["In the preceding second section we spoke of the extent of learned knowledge. Extent, however, properly belongs to the magnitude of knowledge. The author has therefore done wrong in treating of its extent and of its magnitude in separate sections.",[]],"blomberg-s05-002":["In the preceding section we have, properly speaking, considered the extensive [magnitude] of knowledge, and it now remains to consider its intensive [magnitude]. Here, however, the author makes a distinction that is really quite unnecessary.",[]],"blomberg-s05-003":["The degree of dignity [*Dignitaet*] in the way we have insight into things depends especially, and first of all, upon our choosing the right things. For this a correct taste and a good feeling are required; by virtue of these one is able to choose important objects, which have either many or few consequences, but always important ones.",[]],"blomberg-s05-004":["The concepts of politeness and of inconstancy have consequences which, though not great, are yet many in common life, and they are therefore of very great importance.",[]],"blomberg-s05-005":["The multitude of consequences often makes up very well for what a single consequence, or a few, lack in importance.",[]],"blomberg-s05-006":["The correction of error, [or the correction] of conscience, on the other hand, has single but great consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s05-007":["Further, it belongs to the dignity of our insight that we do not spend too much labour upon one thing where it is not really so necessary, while withdrawing our diligence from other objects. A special good feeling and a true soundness of the imagination are required for the art of rightly estimating the importance of modes of knowledge. I can easily show another the consequences; but to feel the magnitude of these consequences is something that cannot be taught him at all.",[]],"blomberg-s05-008":["Of metaphysics I can tell him that he will obtain enlightened concepts of his soul, of providence, of the supreme being, of the world. But if he is without feeling for this great utility, no description will help him.",[]],"blomberg-s05-009":["A mode of knowledge is great in so far as it has a universal use. But all such modes of knowledge are disadvantageous in this respect: although their use is great and many things are known through them, the less is known in these many things themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s05-010":["[In such knowledge] I think little of many objects.",[]],"blomberg-s05-011":["There is, however, another kind of knowledge, through which one knows much in few things. The former defect arises for the most part from always thinking *in abstracto*, and from this many others arise. Baumgarten says: beauty is a *perfectio phaenomenon*, perfection in the appearance. But can one really, from this definition, already discern beauty in objects or in modes of knowledge? The more a mode of knowledge comprehends under itself, the less it comprehends in itself. A deficiency in knowledge is not always blameworthy, provided it does not conflict with an end that has been proposed. As soon as it does so, however, error arises from the deficiency. It follows of itself that those who promise to give a definition of a thing involve themselves in the danger of being charged at once with even the slightest missing mark. This would not have been charged against them had they indicated that they did not intend to unfold the thing completely, but only to explain it in accordance with a certain point of view. The same holds of demonstrations [*Demonstrationes*] and proofs.",[]],"blomberg-s05-012":["The limits we set to our desire for knowledge are so necessary that without them we hardly come to know any science properly. In order to be in a position to know a mode of knowledge completely, we must therefore voluntarily impose upon ourselves, as it were, a deliberate deficiency in respect of other modes of knowledge, perhaps opposed to this one, that is, a kind of ignorance of them, so that we may devote all our diligence to this one and gain a quite perfect insight into it.",[]],"blomberg-s05-013":["Deficiencies (*defectus*) are nothing but the limits of a thing that is to begin. They are therefore its *termini*, its bounds [*Schranken*]; and deficiency is indeed quite compatible with the perfection of a beginning.",[]],"blomberg-s05-014":["Acuteness is not at all required for estimating modes of knowledge, but only feeling. Mathematicians may be very acute and yet have no taste for metaphysics, or for any other logical or moral inquiry.",[]],"blomberg-s05-015":["Modes of knowledge often seem to us dry and of no use. Later, when we first traverse them and penetrate into their interior, their advantages soon disclose themselves. Thus electricity, for instance, was nothing but a nice plaything; subsequently, however, its utility has so shown itself that it may now serve to cure many a disease. Perhaps in time, with the help of electricity, it will even be possible to drive away thunderstorms.",[]],"blomberg-s05-016":["Euclid thereby discovered many properties of the circle, and only now, in the course of time, have their wide-reaching advantages been perceived. Many things have been held in contempt, and become contemptible, only through misuse. For instance, the Wolffians talked so long and so much of monads that they came to be mocked by merry poets.",[]],"blomberg-s05-017":["The expressions concerning monads, the best world, and sufficient and insufficient reason [*Grund*] have been so dishonoured by the learned rabble that one now actually hesitates to use them. Much that is in fact possible and good can also very quickly be made ridiculous and represented as absurd.",[]],"blomberg-s05-018":["Thus Voltaire, for example, ridiculed the thought of Maupertuis, who held that the Egyptians, in order to make for themselves an immortal name, ought rather to have buried books in the earth instead of [building] pyramids. He thereby made Maupertuis wholly ridiculous, although he was perhaps one of the greatest men of his time.",[]],"blomberg-s05-019":["Meanwhile let no one apply his learning to useless objects, nor yet carry it with him into ordinary company, as happened, for instance, with the doctrine of the best world, which in the end was applied to everything possible.",[]],"blomberg-s05-020":["Finally, it is to be noted that many a thing is passed off as useless subtlety because one is too weak for it and unable to see into it and know it.",[]],"blomberg-s05-021":["»Just as many a man often despises a beautiful woman because he cannot win her as he pleases, and just as the fox cries down the grapes as sour when he cannot snatch them. –«",[]],"blomberg-s05-022":["One can never, or at least very seldom, discern and know in advance all the utility of this or that mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s05-023":["When Euclid devised his propositions, did he ever think that from them the distances of the heavenly bodies would be ascertained?",[]],"blomberg-s05-024":["The author says that intermediate truths must not be neglected, since they are often very necessary for the principal truths. That a mode of knowledge is difficult is not in itself a reason for esteeming it highly.",[]],"blomberg-s05-025":["Because something is imposing, it sometimes pleases even when it is of no use. To this class belong, for instance, the sophistical questions of the scholastics, the fallacies, the four figures of the syllogism. One of the scholastic scholars is said to have studied himself so thin over the sophistical proposition which they called the Liar that he finally had to wear leaden soles, so that he might at least have some weight and a strong wind might not blow him off the bridge and cast him down.",[]],"blomberg-s05-026":["When many trifles come together, they finally make up a great mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s05-027":["A mode of knowledge can be great on account of its many consequences, even though these consequences are not very great and [not very] exact. Such is the case, for instance, with arithmetic.",[]],"blomberg-s05-028":["What is great for one is small for another. The Latin language, and likewise metaphysical questions and inquiries, are important and great for learned men.",[]],"blomberg-s05-029":["For women, however, they are unnecessary. So, too, persons of rank, princes, etc., do not need all modes of knowledge; in many sciences and in various matters they can rely upon the learned, just as someone who does not understand this or that particular craft relies upon the craftsman when he commissions him to make something. For such lords as princes, etc., it would be unnecessary to enter far into metaphysics and its inquiries.",[]],"blomberg-s05-030":["On the other hand, one ought to instil into them the true learning that they need. That is to say, one ought, pre-eminently and first of all, to teach them [to understand] justice, the worth of man and of their subjects, hatred of flatterers and of flattery, true care for the citizens of the land, and to distinguish the true advantages of the state from the false; and one ought to impress the knowledge of all this firmly upon them. Then many monarchs who are now ill-disposed will be full of noble magnanimity and of exalted sentiments worthy of their station. We shall have happy princes, subjects and lands. A great mind, guided by its feeling, does not occupy itself with trifles; it perceives what is important and chooses that as its object.",[]],"blomberg-s06-001":["The supreme general question of one who wishes to learn logic is naturally this: What is truth?",[]],"blomberg-s06-002":["This question, so suited to the human understanding and especially to that of a beginner, will prove more difficult to answer than one would suppose. There have indeed been men who clearly saw that one is not, strictly speaking, in a position to give any distinct, complete and precise concept of it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-003":["So it has always been. None of the old Roman jurists, for instance, could properly say what is right and what is not right (*quid sit jus*); and nevertheless the concept one has of it is complete for use, and good enough.",[]],"blomberg-s06-004":["In order to perceive the difficulties that may arise in connection with the concept of truth, we note the following. First, if a mark, a *criterium*, of truth is to be given, a judgment, a rule, will have to be given of it. The supreme *criterium* therefore cannot be given.",[]],"blomberg-s06-005":["If a mode of knowledge does not agree with the constitution of the thing which we represent to ourselves and wish to know, it is false, since it cannot be consistent with truth. If, on the other hand, a mode of knowledge conforms to the constitution of the thing which we represent to ourselves, it is true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-006":["The sceptic, however, said: Something is true if it agrees with the object. But I cannot perceive this without first considering and knowing the object. Truth will therefore consist in nothing else than this, that the knowledge of the object agrees with the knowledge of the object. But this is surely *identitas*, *idem per idem*, nothing; it is, said the sceptics, a circle in the definition of truth. This argument was entitled the diallelon; and since the sceptics reasoned thus, they finally derived from it the main conclusion that all distinction of the true and the false is nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-007":["In false knowledge I do not know and comprehend the thing itself which I wish to represent to myself, but another. For instance, if I represent the polyp to myself as a worm, I have a false knowledge of a polyp.",[]],"blomberg-s06-008":["False learned modes of knowledge are those which are supposed to be learned, or even appear learned, but in fact are not. This always happens when indistinct common modes of knowledge are clothed in *termini occulti*, by which one obtains no correct concept of the thing, but merely explains *idem per idem*. For instance, if one asked for the cause of the propagation and increase of the human and animal [kind] [*Verstandes*], and wished to have a distinct concept of how this came about, the ancients would say with an affectedly learned air: the cause of the propagation of men and animals is the *vis plastica*, the formative power.",[]],"blomberg-s06-009":["One then fancies that one has given a real ground of the thing, and yet one says no more than what one actually asked and wishes to know, only in other words.",[]],"blomberg-s06-010":["One clothes the concept of such a thing somewhat differently, sets, so to speak, a plume of feathers upon it, and then esteems it exceedingly highly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-011":["Such *qualitates occultae* are very frequently found in the writings of the ancient philosophers, and in the writings of Crusius they are as frequent as they can ever have been among the ancients. He says, for instance: something is true because no one can regard and hold it otherwise than as true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-012":["The one says just as much as the other; this is therefore a *qualitas occulta*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-013":["When one does not wish to be questioned further about the cause of this or that, one often gives a ground which is no ground. For instance, if I ask why one man yawns when he looks at another, the ancients gravely said that the ground of it is sympathy. But this word signifies nothing but a suffering-with [*Mitleiden*]. They therefore said nothing more than that it came about because the one must have a fellow-suffering with the other. But how does this apply to yawning? They thus explain nothing by it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-014":["In the truth of learned knowledge the formal element is also of account. The knowledge itself must actually be, not common, but learned knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-015":["The material element in a mode of knowledge may very often be true; the knowledge may conform to the constitution of the thing, and so have truth as common knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-016":["But if this same knowledge is supposed to be learned, without actually being so, it thereby becomes a false learned knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-017":["To spread *qualitates occultae* is the grave of philosophy. One then believes oneself to possess a genuinely learned knowledge, whereas it is nothing but a merely common one.",[]],"blomberg-s06-018":["What Crusius says of probability in his writings pleases very many people well. In fact, however, it is nothing but what one already knows through merely common knowledge, except that it is set forth with a certain pomp which is always very characteristic of Crusius.",[]],"blomberg-s06-019":["Hence it naturally follows that it carries with it the semblance, but not the essence, of learning and of learned knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-020":["The first step in every matter, mode of knowledge, science, art, etc., occasions great difficulty.",[]],"blomberg-s06-021":["Truth and error never reside in concepts, but solely in judgments. He, therefore, who never judges will never err; but neither will he ever utter a truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-022":["The concept is always the *datum* upon which I am to judge. Concepts are the materials for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-023":["There are methods of avoiding all error, but they are so simple that for that very reason they are not heeded. They consist, in brief, in this: do not judge so much; do not decide often, indeed very seldom, except in certain cases where it is necessary. Do not always, but very seldom, settle whether something is true or whether it is false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-024":["There is, then, and there is needed, in order to avoid error, a certain moderation in thinking and in judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-025":["But how many philosophers of our time like always to decide!",[]],"blomberg-s06-026":["Against this bold assertion of opinions once adopted or self-devised, against these decisive judgments upon propositions of reason, customary even in ancient times, Pyrrho cried out: »*Non liquet*« [It is not clear]. – Many authors entangle themselves in such doubtful circumstances through decisive judgments and subsidiary thoughts, which are often taken [*capirt*] for errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-027":["A household remedy for errors is: to distinguish nothing, to judge little. Hence it comes that a village full of peasants is found to have not nearly so many errors as an academy of sciences; thoughtlessness preserves them from errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-028":["Yet we shall not on that account, in wishing to avoid all errors, be permitted to resolve to leave much unconsidered.",[]],"blomberg-s06-029":["This tells us, and indicates only, what the bounds of our judgments are; and it is upon these very bounds that one must judge. But if one had exceedingly important ends, such that every error, even the least, would be dangerous, then it is certainly better to remain in one place and to judge only of what concerns our affairs. Such is the case, for instance, in morals.",[]],"blomberg-s06-030":["Ignorance is an empty space in our head, and for that reason does not hinder any true knowledge [*Kenntnis*], if it does not spread it. Error, on the other hand, is a hindrance to truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-031":["So long as our modes of knowledge are only speculations, we may well still venture to err. But as soon as an error becomes harmful, and this happens as soon as it bears upon the practical, it is very dangerous to retain it",[]],"blomberg-s06-032":["Judgments are actions [207a] of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-033":["The senses, however, do not judge. Not all concepts spring from the understanding; some spring from the senses. But all judgments come solely from the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-034":["Men often take a certain judgment of their understanding for a sensation and representation of the senses, whereas it is nothing of the kind.",[]],"blomberg-s06-035":["There is no disputing about a sensation. It is a *datum*, and not yet an error. Often, however, what is taken for such a sensation is properly the judgment of the understanding upon occasion of such sensations.",[]],"blomberg-s06-036":["Appeals to such feelings and sensations are very much the fashion at present. Some would masterfully feel and sense right and wrong, and that through an inner moral feeling peculiar to themselves alone. Ridiculous! The understanding must necessarily be in play here.",[]],"blomberg-s06-037":["Truth is nothing but the agreement of a mode of knowledge with the laws of the understanding and of reason; and, conversely, the conflict of a mode of knowledge with the laws of the understanding and of reason is nothing but falsity.",[]],"blomberg-s06-038":["No power in nature deviates in its actions from its laws, or from the conditions under which alone it can act. The understanding, taken by itself, therefore cannot possibly err.",[]],"blomberg-s06-039":["All judgments [of the understanding] agree with the laws of the understanding; all judgments of the understanding are therefore true. Even in our erroneous judgments the understanding must always have done something; hence in them everything can never be false, but there must still always be something true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-040":["It is wholly impossible for a man to err entirely when he judges.",[]],"blomberg-s06-041":["In error, then, there is still truth. But there we judge in a mixed manner, and mingle in the effects of the other powers of the mind. From this arises the erroneous, which is so contrary to the understanding, and which we nevertheless falsely think, and often quite confidently believe, to be an effect of the understanding. In fact it is nothing but, as it were, a bastard of sensibility and understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-042":["Since, as we have already shown, no judgment occurs without an ingredient [*ingrediens*] of truth, it necessarily follows that we shall have to moderate greatly our judgment upon the critique [*Criticam*] of truth, and upon the judgments, or indeed errors, of others. For it is a sure principle: one can never arrive at truth if one is always quarrelling, if each always contradicts the other.",[]],"blomberg-s06-043":["Every discovery of an error that is in fact gross and manifest is very sad. Let us, then, rather not make such sad discoveries, but help one another in common and in friendship, support one another, and not always act [*agiren*] against one another.",[]],"blomberg-s06-044":["Instead, therefore, of refuting a thing, we shall have merely to examine it, [to see] whether there is not actually some truth in it, [and] what is lacking, so as to supply it; thus everywhere to act in common, and then to make it intelligible to the one who errs, in the least cutting and indeed in a kindly manner, that it is not at all surprising, since he could very easily and readily have erred.",[]],"blomberg-s06-045":["This moderate [*müßige*] manner of judging, this so well-placed good-heartedness of disposition, is just as necessary for the attainment of upright knowledge as it may always be in common life.",[]],"blomberg-s06-046":["Naturally, therefore, there is no total error. Otherwise the understanding would have to contradict itself and its own prescriptions, and act against them.",[]],"blomberg-s06-047":["Errors, however great and important they may seem, are all only partial.",[]],"blomberg-s06-048":["One can instruct no one except through the remnant of understanding that is still in him. One can improve no one except through the remnant of good that is still in him.",[]],"blomberg-s06-049":["In the instruction of the understanding and the improvement of the will, I must certainly always presuppose something true and something good.",[]],"blomberg-s06-050":["The judgment of every other man is always the judgment of one of those men whose judgments, taken all together, are my judge, and the greatest assessor of all the products of my understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-051":["A contradiction is, indeed, nothing but an occurrence in which one says Yes, and the other, on the contrary, No.",[]],"blomberg-s06-052":["It follows, then, that it must in a certain measure naturally disquiet every rational man. But it is a universal duty of a philosopher, in such a case, always to take the part of humanity in general, and to think in this humane fashion: these opinions, which seem so bizarre and absurd, are perhaps not so ill-conceived at all, not so absurd as they seem.",[]],"blomberg-s06-053":["Everything that makes men united and compatible actually contributes very greatly to promoting the perfection of the whole human race. Conflict, however, produces nothing. Everywhere, and so too in the whole world, among the learned, it holds:",[]],"blomberg-s06-054":["*Concordia res parvae crescunt* [By concord small things grow]",[]],"blomberg-s06-055":["*Discordia dilabuntur* [By discord they fall apart]",[]],"blomberg-s06-056":["Further, the bounds, that is, the narrow limits by which the human understanding is surrounded, are not in the least to be regarded as sources of error, as so many would-be philosophers suppose. They are indeed in fact causes of man's ignorance in many matters, but not of error. As soon, however, as there is joined to this ignorance a self-conceit, a presumption, a learned arrogance that judges more than one knows and is able to, error can spring from it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-057":["The sole cause of error, then, is the ill-placed presumption of overstepping the bounds and limits of one's own understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-058":["The understanding alone does not err. Nor do the senses alone err; they are passive and do not judge at all.",[]],"blomberg-s06-059":["The human understanding alone judges.",[]],"blomberg-s06-060":["There must therefore also be certain materials [*materialia*], stuff, for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s06-061":["The understanding must necessarily combine with sensibility in order to be able to judge, and this does not in the least depend upon choice",[]],"blomberg-s06-062":["There are, therefore, naturally also subjective conditions of our understanding and thinking; and these we reckon all to sensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-063":["Sometimes the understanding is, as it were, in a certain embarrassment; its horizon is quite clouded. But since concepts must always be furnished [*fourniret*] to the understanding and laid before it for choice, we perceive that sensibility must always assist the understanding. Only it often does not do so sufficiently. [217a]",[]],"blomberg-s06-064":["All distinguishing of the true from the false requires knowledge from inner sense; that is, I must be, and become, conscious of what properly lies in my concept, and of what I think.",[]],"blomberg-s06-065":["Inner sense is often dull and its horizon clouded, and it does not give us help enough.",[]],"blomberg-s06-066":["But since inner sense also belongs to sensibility, and the understanding cannot judge without it, the understanding must judge with the help of sensibility. And this very combination and mingling of the understanding with sensibility is the source of all errors, namely, when the effects of the understanding are taken for effects of sensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-067":["As we thus perceive that the understanding and sensibility, each alone, cannot err, so we perceive how an error is possible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-068":["All errors are, so to speak, curved lines, which we determine in being driven on the one side by the understanding and on the other side by error.",[]],"blomberg-s06-069":["We see before us the play of the things of the world, but we do not on that account yet have a complete concept of their motion.",[]],"blomberg-s06-070":["The senses do not judge. Inner sense, which alone teaches us, and through which we become aware whether or not we have all the requisites [*requisita*] of knowledge, does not continually render us the right service; and from this arises the semblance that the objects themselves lack something requisite for their being known.",[]],"blomberg-s06-071":["One may, for instance, have correct rules for calculating, and nevertheless go wrong in carrying them out. That is commonly a fault of inner sense; it happens through oversight.",[]],"blomberg-s06-072":["The sole means of avoiding error will therefore be that I discover certain criteria [*criteria*] by which, in every judgment, I can properly distinguish what proceeds from the understanding from what outer or inner sense supplies [*suppeditiren*] to us.",[]],"blomberg-s06-073":["But the understanding, forsaken by inner sense, judges at once, let the outcome be what it may, when inner sense offers it either something wrong or nothing at all",[]],"blomberg-s06-074":["All criteria of truth, however, are either",[]],"blomberg-s06-075":["A: internal, or B. external",[]],"blomberg-s06-076":["The former are objective, and contain the ground why something is actually true or false. The latter are subjective, and contain certain circumstances through which one is in a position to presume the truth or falsity in a matter.",[]],"blomberg-s06-077":["That through which true modes of knowledge are distinguished from false is the mark of truth",[]],"blomberg-s06-078":["The principle of identity is the formal mark of truth in affirmative judgments, and the *principium contradictionis* [in] negative judgments.",[]],"blomberg-s06-079":["Material marks are the *notae intermediae*, through which I prove a judgment: the *principia materialia*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-080":["In a single and wholly simple idea [*idea*] neither truth nor falsity is naturally to be found; it occurs only in comparison.",[]],"blomberg-s06-081":["When I ascribe to a thing a mark which either contradicts it or is even wholly contrary to it, or deny of it a mark which is nevertheless identical in it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-082":["That, however, through which I know whether the comparison with a thing is correct or not is entitled the *nota intermedia* (*principium materiale*). For instance, if I wish to prove that a body is divisible, I take as *nota intermedia* that it is composite, and reason as follows: everything composite is divisible; *atqui* every body is composite; *ergo* every body is divisible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-083":["Some propositions are so constituted that they have no material mark of truth whatsoever, but carry with them only the formal one. These are properly the immediate judgments which we have of a thing, and which we cannot prove or derive from anything further; for instance, that a body is composite.",[]],"blomberg-s06-084":["Simple concepts have the mark that they are at all times true. For instance, if I represent the moon to myself as round, this is a true concept, because I actually so think it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-085":["But if I compare this representation of the moon with the representation of other planets, and doubt whether the moon is round or oval, I can very easily err in this, and my knowledge can be either true or false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-086":["Among all the marks of truth, the first internal sign, or principal mark, of it is internal possibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-087":["Through experience we are taught the possibility of many things which otherwise would really not have been held possible.",[]],"blomberg-s06-088":["Possibility, nevertheless, is far from being a sufficient ground of truth. That something is possible, and that we can well represent it to ourselves and think it through our reason, does not yet make it actually true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-089":["Yet possibility is at least a ground of probability. If something is possible and can be thought by us, we at least believe and hope that it can be true, even though we are not in a position to assert it with certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s06-090":["Impossibility, on the contrary, is an always adequate and sufficient [*sufficienter*] ground for infallible proof of the untruth or falsity of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s06-091":["When we perceive that something is wholly impossible, we commonly also at once hold it to be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-092":["Here, however, it must further be observed that we can err in both cases. We very often hold something to be useful, and *per consequentiam* [by consequence] also to be true and correct, which, when we examine it more closely, is in fact altogether impossible and false. Conversely, we hold much to be impossible and false which is in fact both possible and true, or, if not true, at least useful.",[]],"blomberg-s06-093":["If the consequences of a mode of knowledge are true, the modes of knowledge themselves are also true. For example, it is true, and settled for everyone, that there are volcanoes, hot springs, etc. Since this is true, it may well also be true that there is a central fire within the earth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-094":["From this we can indeed infer, with some ground, the central fire of the earth. For these volcanoes, hot springs, etc., can be regarded with considerable conjecture, and even with probability, as consequences of such a central fire; and from the consequences one can very often infer the ground correctly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-095":["If all the consequences of a mode of knowledge, without exception, are true, then the knowledge is also certainly true. For were even a single falsity to be found in the knowledge, falsity would already have to lie in the consequences as well. But if this is not the case, nothing in the knowledge can be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-096":["If, for example, a successful engagement is reported to us in the newspapers, and the victory of a hero is made known; if the announcement of it is even made from the pulpit, if solemn ceremonies are arranged and services of thanksgiving held on its account — in short, if all its consequences are true — then the engagement itself must also be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-097":["Sometimes, however, we cannot know all the consequences of a thing, and in that case some false ones may well, on occasion, have become mixed in and be found among them, even though we hold the knowledge itself to be true. For example, if we assume a central fire within the earth, it will in turn follow that the strata of the earth lying not very far from this central fire would be wholly parched and ruined by the excessive and violent heat, and would at last have to collapse into one another.",[]],"blomberg-s06-098":["But since, as is readily seen, this consequence is false, it is also quite easy to perceive that the central fire cannot be held to be certain beyond all doubt, nor be asserted with confidence.",[]],"blomberg-s06-099":["If a mode of knowledge is to be possible in [its] connection [with other knowledge], it must have correct grounds, and important and also true consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s06-100":["Knowledge of the possibility of a thing arises from experience; reason cannot instruct us in it. For example, that the magnet attracts iron.",[]],"blomberg-s06-101":["We can see by reason the possibility of composite bodies, if we are convinced of the possibility of simple effects.",[]],"blomberg-s06-102":["But the first possibility of causes and effects becomes evident solely through the senses, from experience. Reason, therefore, offers no objection [in favour of] the impossibility of a thing whose possibility or actual existence we clearly see through experience. For experience, by the actuality of a thing, naturally instructs us also of its possibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-103":["From these instances of experience reason can indeed derive certain laws of the possibility of this or that thing; but the first *principia*, the *data* and *materialia* [materials] of possibility must necessarily be given to us from experience, and arise from it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-104":["We must not, however, hold something to be impossible because we cannot think it or represent it through the senses. For example, a man born blind can form no representation of colours by means of reason, since experience denies him the *data* for it; nevertheless he cannot, and may not, by any means infer their impossibility. If we were to accept the former [principle], all philosophy would be abolished.",[]],"blomberg-s06-105":["There are logical truths. These are those which relate solely to understanding and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-106":["On the other hand, we can also think an aesthetic and a practical truth. The former relates to the relation of taste; the latter, by contrast, to the rules of free will.",[]],"blomberg-s06-107":["Logical truth, however, is not always aesthetically true, nor does it always bring practical perfection with it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-108":["Every truth is always in itself a logical perfection, that is, a perfect mode of knowledge according to the rules of understanding and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-109":["Much can be aesthetically true without being logically true; it need only charm and give pleasure. Such, for example, is the case with novels.",[]],"blomberg-s06-110":["Even practical truth need not always be grounded in understanding and reason. For example, if I slander someone to another person, I can thereby arouse his *affectus* [affects], and in this way [move] his will, although this slander is not logically true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-111":["Logical truth is distinguished from aesthetic truth in this: that when something delights and moves me, I can abate something of its truth. Logical truth can therefore very often yield to aesthetic truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-112":["Truth frequently does not please us most; indeed, a lie often does. We are far happier in fables than in logical truths. He, for example, who takes nature as the basis in painting does not always accord with delight, charm and pleasure. Fiction is often far more moving.",[]],"blomberg-s06-113":["Our wishes often belong among the *fictiones*. They are often directed to something better than what nature actually offers us. Thus, for example, enthusiasm in friendship pleases us; we are moved by the earthly pastoral life. It is indeed true that in aesthetic truth there must always be a certain logical truth. But the more we attend to what is aesthetically true, the less logical truth can be found in the subject which we wish to represent to ourselves. Thus, for example, it is aesthetically true that a man, when he is dead, will not rise again, although this runs directly counter to logical (to say nothing of moral) truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-114":["What all men say is true according to the rules of taste, and so aesthetically true; for this a universal *consensus* suffices. Yet what is true according to the rules of taste is very often false according to the rules of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-115":["Men often think what they hope for, or at least wish for, to be possible and actual, because they would like to have it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-116":["All fables contain an aesthetic, but seldom a logical, truth. For example, the speaking of animals. Here, indeed, there must actually be something true; but aesthetic truth has precedence over what is logically true. In [the fable] something is represented as possible. Nor does it involve any absurdity that animals should be able to speak.",[]],"blomberg-s06-117":["Under a certain *hypothesis*, which may be fictitious, a certain degree of logical truth can prevail even in a fable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-118":["But the degree of truth which must be found therein no scholar has yet been able to determine precisely, and the greatest aestheticians have not yet succeeded in indicating it. Objective marks would often not suffice us. Here one can appeal only either to the agreement of our modes of knowledge among themselves, or to their agreement with the modes of knowledge of others; this is what happens, for example, in calculation.",[]],"blomberg-s06-119":["We always test a proposition against the reason of others, or against our own at different times and on different occasions, and in different situations of our reason. But very often we also rely on the appearance [*Schein*], that is, on the way it first appears to us. We wish only to gain the approval of others.",[]],"blomberg-s06-120":["We are glad to make our judgments public when we have, or believe we can gain, some merit thereby.",[]],"blomberg-s06-121":["Since the human understanding has a natural law to extend its modes of knowledge as far as is at all possible, it must not be forbidden the means by which alone it is able to distinguish the true from the false, and not only to enrich its modes of knowledge but also to correct them.",[]],"blomberg-s06-122":["No obstacles, therefore, must be placed in the way of the publication of judgments, that is, of submitting them to the insight of all. This is indeed the universal right of every man, and the only sure way of attaining to truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-123":["Even mathematics derives the proposition, and the triumphant and invincible certainty in its apodictic proofs and convictions, not so much from the objective as rather from the subjective criteria of truth; for in this science there is something which agrees with every reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-124":["But where modes of knowledge rise very high, only soon to be cast down all the lower, there is strong suspicion that the universal approval accorded them at the outset and all at once was grounded only in particular minds, and therefore cannot be lasting.",[]],"blomberg-s06-125":["A mode of knowledge is either wholly true, or partly true, or partly false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-126":["But a mode of knowledge can never be altogether false, although it can often be wholly true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-127":["Every judgment arises from the understanding; it must therefore also agree in some measure with the laws of understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-128":["It is therefore impossible that a man should pass a wholly false judgment and even accept it as wholly true",[]],"blomberg-s06-129":["For otherwise the judgment would have to contradict the laws of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-130":["A mode of knowledge, therefore, which is declared false throughout will nevertheless always be only partly false, and will always contain a certain degree of truth. Indeed, even in the judgments of an insane man (however strange this observation may appear), at least a partial truth will always be found on closer examination.",[]],"blomberg-s06-131":["If we were to assume that some judgments could be altogether false, it would have to be possible for the understanding to depart from all laws. Under this supposition the whole authority of our doctrine of reason [*Vernunft-Lehre*, logic] would fall and vanish, and we could place very little trust in it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-132":["If another were capable of judging wholly falsely, this would be just as possible for us as for him. We should therefore be in no other condition than one of great uncertainty in the whole employment of our understanding and our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-133":["When, therefore, we believe that we find falsity in the judgment of another, we must by that very fact be convinced that some partial truth must prevail in it, however hidden it may be.",[]],"blomberg-s06-134":["And in this principle there is in fact much fairness. Such a way of thinking is moral and sympathetic, in that one does not at once deny others all truth in their modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-135":["Just as exclusive self-love lets one promote only one's own welfare, without the least care for the good of others or any contribution to it, so too the exclusive judgment — in which one ascribes to oneself alone the possession of all true modes of knowledge, while denying them entirely to others and imputing to them nothing but false ones — is nothing but a pernicious conceit, in which one esteems oneself alone and despises all others beside oneself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-136":["Minds possessed by this conceit imagine that in their own understanding alone there is a Goshen, while all else is shrouded in Egyptian darkness.",[]],"blomberg-s06-137":["Many judgments and concepts are true in respect of their essential parts, but false in respect of their accidental parts.",[]],"blomberg-s06-138":["In theory we may often judge falsely; nevertheless our judgments may still be true in respect of practice.",[]],"blomberg-s06-139":["Even in natural theology there may in fact be many erroneous ideas and *speculationes* which are nevertheless true *in praxi* [in practice]. For example, as regards the omnipresence of God, one may represent and think it either as nothing, or as an extension throughout the whole of space.",[]],"blomberg-s06-140":["This concept is indeed false; nevertheless it is true in respect of practice. Let God be omnipresent however, and in whatever manner, he will; enough that he actually is so, and we must therefore stand in awe of his omnipresence, in whatever it may consist.",[]],"blomberg-s06-141":["If a speculation is in fact erroneous, but the error has no influence on *praxis*, that is, on the morality of conduct, then it is indifferent in respect of conduct, and not so abhorrent as if it also corrupted conduct.",[]],"blomberg-s06-142":["When, therefore, our judgments are in conflict with the judgments of others, we must, in order to be able to compare these judgments with and against one another, make use of the following rule. A mark [*Kenntniß*] of truth is this: that our judgments agree with the judgments of others who can have knowledge of the matter; or, what is the same, that they are universally valid.",[]],"blomberg-s06-143":["If, therefore, something is to be found in my judgment which is contrary to the judgments of others, I am not very sure of the truth of my judgment. I must therefore first endeavour to seek out some agreement, even a hidden and far-fetched one, between my judgment and the judgment of others; and this agreement will in fact, at least in most cases, be very easy to find. For in our concurring [*übereinstimmenden*] judgments there is more agreement than we suppose.",[]],"blomberg-s06-144":["It is necessary only that we understand the judgment of others and do not misinterpret it.",[]],"blomberg-s06-145":["Nor, apart from mathematics, is there any *systema* of universal truths of reason that would be altogether true, just as no mode of knowledge can be wholly false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-146":["A mode of knowledge can be *partialiter* [partially] true, in that it is indeed wholly true, yet does not extend to the whole object but only to a part of it. Such modes of knowledge are nonetheless always certain.",[]],"blomberg-s06-147":["Of anyone, therefore, who at once, all at one time and without much reflection, confidently assents to the truth of another's entire philosophical *systema*, it can always be correctly inferred that he does not yet understand it and is [not] in a position properly to distinguish the true from the false. For this much is certain: no *systema* can be wholly true; some falsity is always contained in it, even if it does not touch the essentials of the system. In philosophical truths errors and faults are always so intermingled with truth that one always finds something in them to correct",[]],"blomberg-s06-148":["A mode of knowledge is exact when it has as much truth as is required simply for attaining the principal end; it is crude, on the other hand, when the principal end cannot be attained by it. (Exactness is a [mode of] knowledge, but it cannot properly be opposed to its crudeness; rather, its crudeness must be *opponirt* [opposed] to its unity.)",[]],"blomberg-s06-149":["*Praecision*, punctiliousness, is this: when I indicate no more of what is true in a mode of knowledge than is necessary for seeing into it. Transgression of this rule becomes a great fault. That in a mode of knowledge is entitled crude for which only slight attention [is required]; that is entitled subtle for whose clearness greater attention is required.",[]],"blomberg-s06-150":["It is very often necessary to be subtle, because such great confusions arise from the neglect of subtlety; although subtlety too, since it demands excessive attention, is often censured.",[]],"blomberg-s06-151":["In respect of practical modes of knowledge one can very well appeal to, and rely upon, sound understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-152":["A rule for properly testing oneself and one's knowledge of any matter is this: if I myself perfectly understand and see into a thing, I must be able to convey and represent it to another man, who has nothing more than sound understanding, so clearly and distinctly that he sees into it just as perfectly as I do. If I cannot do this, it is a sure sign that I do not yet properly understand it myself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-153":["Every mode of knowledge is subject to a partial untruth; but this untruth concerns only the greater or lesser exactness, and the knowledge can still be exact enough to attain its purpose. For example, when I measure a mountain, I shall never be able to measure so exactly and infallibly that I do not miss by at least a few inches; nevertheless the *quantum* resulting from my measurement is sufficient, and exact enough, to determine the whole height of the mountain. Many of our judgments of reason are passed only by eye, and therefore may not, by the standard of more refined reason and learning, be exact enough; nevertheless they are quite exact for their purpose.",[]],"blomberg-s06-154":["But the highest and most laborious exactness, which must be attained with much accuracy and learning, yet is of no use in respect of the principal end, is entitled pedantry. In mathematics, however, the case is quite otherwise. There exactness must always obtain; nothing must be lacking.",[]],"blomberg-s06-155":["A mode of knowledge may be correct when considered historically, and yet incorrect when considered *formaliter* [formally]. For example, [the proposition] 'the soul is immortal' is *materialiter* [materially] true and correct; but the form in which one represents this may often be wholly false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-156":["A mode of knowledge is *materialiter* true when we have material knowledge [*Wißenschaft*] of it; *formaliter* true when the grounds for proving and deriving it are correct.",[]],"blomberg-s06-157":["Much that is certain beyond doubt in respect of practice is false in respect of reason. For example, a part is smaller than the whole; or, the world must have a beginning.",[]],"blomberg-s06-158":["A mode of knowledge can be *materialiter* true and *formaliter* false, but not conversely; it is then a formal error. This occurs in philosophy more often than is believed.",[]],"blomberg-s06-159":["Thus it is not a material but a formal error that lies in the *qualitates occultae*, which are given as the cause of a thing, as its ground, and in which the question posed is answered by the question itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-160":["All definitions which explain *idem per idem* [the same by the same] are *formaliter* erroneous.",[]],"blomberg-s06-161":["These formal errors, where one excuses the philosopher the proof of a mode of knowledge, being already prepossessed in its favour *anticipando* [by anticipation], and even holding the proof to be dispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-162":["Where my sound understanding already precedes the knowledge of the truth, I am not in the least scrupulous, nor need I be, in examining the rigour of the proof. This rigour [*Schärfe*], which is accorded to certain propositions, often arises also from the fact that they must be regarded not only as very true but also as very necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s06-163":["Such is the case with the two articles of faith of natural religion",[]],"blomberg-s06-164":["1: There is a God.",[]],"blomberg-s06-165":["2: There is another world.",[]],"blomberg-s06-166":["Those who wish to examine such shallow proofs of these necessary propositions, and to make some fine *distinctiones*, at once fall into ill repute, because it is believed that they may well be enemies of the propositions themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s06-167":["These are the *piae fraudes* [pious frauds], which are so suited, so very natural, to man.",[]],"blomberg-s06-168":["One thinks, and believes oneself right in so thinking, that one does not deceive someone when one instils something good into him under all manner of pretext, dissimulation, etc.; indeed, one often seeks merit in it. But, even without regard to the moral aspect of this deception, which nonetheless always remains, it is in itself reprehensible. The recommendation of virtue must itself be virtuous. –",[]],"blomberg-s06-169":["Much is often known as true without any marks. Such truths, irrefutable in themselves, are entitled indemonstrable, that is, because one cannot doubt them; otherwise it would not be necessary to prove them specially.",[]],"blomberg-s06-170":["A mode of knowledge whose consequences are all true is also true. But if even one consequence is false, the knowledge is also false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-171":["Nothing true can arise from a false mode of knowledge, for a false mode of knowledge is the veritable nothing. But the consequences of a mode of knowledge which is partly true and partly false can be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-172":["In examining a mode of knowledge, therefore, one will be obliged to attend not so much to its true consequences as to its false ones.",[]],"blomberg-s06-173":["It is indeed true in general that the so-called apagogic proofs, or *demonstrationes ad absurdum contrarium* — in which, namely, in order to maintain and strengthen one's opinion, one makes clear and shows how ridiculous and wholly absurd it would be to accept its opposite — are very easy. But they do not give me sufficient light in respect of the sources of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-174":["The connection of modes of knowledge with their grounds is therefore surely the safest and best mark of the truth of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-175":["Historical modes of knowledge are distinguished from rational ones by the rational form of the knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s06-176":["A mode of knowledge is historical when it does not agree with the form of reason, rational when it does agree with it, and this without regard to the object. Here, however, we must also reflect upon the object, if we wish to establish the distinction between dogmatic and historical knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s06-177":["1. *mo* Dogmatic knowledge is a universal knowledge which arises *a priori* from reason",[]],"blomberg-s06-178":["2. *do* Historical knowledge, on the other hand, is not always universal, and rests on reports of things that have happened, on the testimony of others; it thus arises *a posteriori*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-179":["All morals is dogmatic. In it one teaches, censures and indicates, not what is commonly abhorred by men, but what ought to be censured.",[]],"blomberg-s06-180":["*Physica*, however, is not dogmatic. One part of it would be pure physics, in which we derive the properties of bodies from universal rules and concepts of bodies.",[]],"blomberg-s06-181":["But the weight of bodies already belongs to experience, and is a historical knowledge *a posteriori*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-182":["All *dogmata* ought properly to be grounded in the first place on reason; but they can also often be historical.",[]],"blomberg-s06-183":["The object of *dogmata* is not individual things, but universal properties and characteristics of things. The object of history, on the other hand, is individual things.",[]],"blomberg-s06-184":["But although *dogmata* have universal properties of things as their object, they can often be known historically, although by their nature they are rational.",[]],"blomberg-s06-185":["All *dogmata* are by their nature objectively rational, although they can be subjectively historical.",[]],"blomberg-s06-186":["We must now also speak of doctrinal systems [*Lehr-Gebäude*]. The author restricts the meaning of this word far too much, in applying it solely and exclusively to dogmatic truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-187":["A better definition of a system will therefore be the following:",[]],"blomberg-s06-188":["A system is a multitude or manifold of many simple and interconnected modes of knowledge and truths, such that, taken together, they make up a whole. Every system must therefore carry a unity with it; but this unity may be grounded either in coordination, as with historical modes of knowledge, or in subordination.",[]],"blomberg-s06-189":["A *systema*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s06-190":["A. historical",[]],"blomberg-s06-191":["B. rational",[]],"blomberg-s06-192":["The form of a doctrinal system is in most cases regarded as arbitrary.",[]],"blomberg-s06-193":["Men have different kinds of understanding. He who likes to attend to the parts is subtle; he who attends to the whole is the great [understanding].",[]],"blomberg-s06-194":["A proper doctrinal system must be built by one person alone. It cannot possibly be a patchwork to which one man adds this and another that.",[]],"blomberg-s06-195":["This capacity to form a *systema* is so excellent because it is so rare and at the same time of such great use.",[]],"blomberg-s06-196":["One must always have some kind of *systema*, for each of our modes of knowledge must have a logical place.",[]],"blomberg-s06-197":["The first *systemata* are naturally exceedingly defective. Gradually they become larger and more complete.",[]],"blomberg-s06-198":["We have already established above the distinction between logical and aesthetic truths.",[]],"blomberg-s06-199":["Much can be logically true without on that account having aesthetic truth, and much, on the other hand, can be aesthetically true without on that account having logical truth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-200":["To this belong all *fictiones*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-201":["To this belong all aesthetic truths which are true according to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s06-202":["In these modes of knowledge a kind of fashion does in fact prevail. To this belong, for example, proverbs, which are in fact subject to a kind of fashion, although they are in fact not even always propositions that accord with taste.",[]],"blomberg-s06-203":["Aesthetically true is everything that is in conformity with the rules of taste and agrees with the rules of appearance.",[]],"blomberg-s06-204":["Poets who wish to write only what is aesthetically true are surely not to be tested on the gold-balance of logic; one would do them great injustice thereby. They are poets, not moralists and logicians, and they always retain their worth.",[]],"blomberg-s06-205":["In order to be aesthetically true, a degree of untruth may often be admitted, which must not, however, be wholly contrary to truth. For example, when that philosopher exclaimed, 'My dear friends, there are no friends', this is aesthetically true and a good thought, because good friends are very rare and a complete accord of minds is very uncommon.",[]],"blomberg-s06-206":["We now come to a very important doctrine of the whole of logic, namely, the doctrine of the origin of error. Does error arise from lack of reason?",[]],"blomberg-s06-207":["Answer: No. From lack of reason nothing arises but ignorance; and ignorance is very different from error.",[]],"blomberg-s06-208":["He who knows little can nevertheless judge quite correctly of this little.",[]],"blomberg-s06-209":["The second question is:",[]],"blomberg-s06-210":["Does error not arise, then, from lack of knowledge combined with the desire to judge?",[]],"blomberg-s06-211":["Answer: He who knows for certain that he is ignorant will certainly not presume to judge of anything he does not understand.",[]],"blomberg-s06-212":["But suppose that he did not feel himself incapable, and thought he knew much, and so wished to judge of much; still nothing more would come of it: he would expend his efforts to judge in vain.",[]],"blomberg-s06-213":["But that the understanding should still seem very difficult to see into — how can this come about? That the understanding should depart from its laws in judging?",[]],"blomberg-s06-214":["No power can of itself conflict with its own laws. For example, when a body is left to its weight, it falls downward according to the laws of gravity, and one is in fact able to calculate the velocity of its fall.",[]],"blomberg-s06-215":["If, therefore, in our judgments by the understanding no [other] power were active, no power interfered, we should in fact never err, but only often be ignorant.",[]],"blomberg-s06-216":["No error, therefore, properly arises from the understanding; rather, if an error is to arise, another power must be active in the understanding at the same time. All powers of nature depart from their laws only when foreign laws become mixed with the given laws.",[]],"blomberg-s06-217":["If, then, we had nothing but understanding, we should indeed judge only little, but all this little truly.",[]],"blomberg-s06-218":["Further, if we had a [274a] wholly pure will, we could never sin, even supposing that we did only very little good.",[]],"blomberg-s06-219":["Departure from the rules of the pure will constitutes moral evil, and this arises only when, and because, other effects of other powers become mixed with the otherwise pure laws of the will — for example, the inclinations and *affectus*. In just the same way, when foreign powers become mixed with the correct laws of the understanding, a mixed effect arises, and error springs from the conflict of our judgments with the laws of understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-220":["Every error is a *phaenomenon*, a riddle as regards the concept of its possibility.",[]],"blomberg-s06-221":["It is, as it were, a quite unusual, unnatural appearance, which contradicts the laws of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s06-222":["Not everything that is contrary to the laws of nature is for us an object of investigation; but that which seldom occurs and is contrary to the laws of nature is so.",[]],"blomberg-s06-223":["Error, it is true, occurs often; but it conflicts with the laws of understanding and reason, and is therefore worthy to be an object of our investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s06-224":["Error proceeds solely from understanding and reason, because foreign powers become mixed with them; never, however, from the senses and imagination.",[]],"blomberg-s06-225":["While our understanding and reason judge objectively, there are nonetheless other, subjective grounds of our judgment, which, however, do not agree with understanding and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-226":["All error arises when we take subjective grounds of our judgments for objective ones. [276a]",[]],"blomberg-s06-227":["The whole business of ghosts is grounded, not in the judgment of the understanding, but in the imagination, which, in the observation of an object, associates with it other similar objects, although they do not exist.",[]],"blomberg-s06-228":["The understanding combines concepts with a thing in accordance with its own laws and with the thing, the object, itself.",[]],"blomberg-s06-229":["The imagination, however, adds other images, which relate not to the object but to the subject.",[]],"blomberg-s06-230":["From this mixture of imagination with understanding arise such effects [276b] as do not wholly agree with the rules of understanding and reason.",[]],"blomberg-s06-231":["Many errors arise from habit; others merely because a change has taken place in the subject. For example, when we make a spot on the wall.",[]],"blomberg-s06-232":["Many errors arise because the images once had are constantly renewed in the mind, so that everything similar is taken for the thing itself. For example, when a youth has been harshly treated by his teacher, so that he consequently hates him, and he afterwards sees a man who resembles him, he is very often inclined to hate this man too, merely [276c] on account of his resemblance to his taskmaster.",[]],"blomberg-s06-233":["One can often reject something by the understanding and, through the effect of another power, accept it again. All this arises merely from the mixture of powers which, even if not contrary to one another, are yet of different kinds.",[]],"blomberg-s06-234":["We know the size of things in no other way than by judgment in respect of distance; for example, the difference between a fly and an elephant.",[]],"blomberg-s06-235":["We perceive a fly well enough near at hand, but an elephant, etc., also at a distance; and from this we [276d] merely infer that the latter must be larger than the former.",[]],"blomberg-s06-236":["But imagination can greatly alter these judgments, and produce in our soul other impressions, other representations of the thing, than those we ought to form of it when we see into its true properties.",[]],"blomberg-s06-237":["Remove, therefore, imagination, sensuous wit, from man, and the judgment of the understanding will always be true.",[]],"blomberg-s06-238":["The understanding, mixed with other powers, errs; and this error we must in turn see into by the understanding itself. From this [276e] it is evident that we must see into and recognise the faults of a power by means of the very power whose faults we wish to detect.",[]],"blomberg-s06-239":["No error can arise from understanding and reason alone, for no power can act contrary to its own laws.",[]],"blomberg-s06-240":["That ignorance in itself cannot in the least be a source of error has already been shown more fully in the preceding *paragraphus*.",[]],"blomberg-s06-241":["Error is either avoidable or unavoidable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-242":["If we make no judgments at all, we can of course avoid all errors: he who does not judge does not err. An error can also be prevented through ignorance. This ignorance, however, is not the ignorance of deficiency but the ignorance of intention [*Vorsatz*], and the latter is in fact a kind of wise ignorance. Many *speculationes* are nothing but a road to the greatest errors. Many errors, however, can be avoided if we resolve to settle nothing and to decide nothing. But many things are of such a kind that they cannot possibly remain undecided. For example, a judge is required to settle disputes. Suppose, then, that he errs in these matters and cases; the error is, so far as he is concerned, unavoidable.",[]],"blomberg-s06-243":["We can hold an opinion about something, and indeed on good grounds, without erring. But if we pass off this mere opinion, this defective conjecture, as a certain truth, we frequently err.",[]],"blomberg-s06-244":["The propensity to decide is therefore the surest road to error, and is a certain dogmatic pride (habitual with many so-called philosophers). If, on the other hand, we do not yet trust the marks of truth that we have so far recognised, and therefore do not yet pass judgment, but often withhold it, we do in fact avoid many errors.",[]],"blomberg-s06-245":["A [mode of] knowledge is",[]],"blomberg-s06-246":["1. evidently or 2. covertly false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-247":["This division of false knowledge is relative.",[]],"blomberg-s06-248":["We have a kind of average understanding, which is abstracted from the understanding of all men and is, as it were, a standard, that is, an ideal.",[]],"blomberg-s06-249":["If, then, we take an error that is compatible with this average degree of understanding, the error is still tolerable. But if it is not compatible even with this, it is no longer to be endured, and it is then an absurdity.",[]],"blomberg-s06-250":["For absurdity presupposes not only a false mode of knowledge, but also that the common understanding (*le sens commun*) can see it to be false.",[]],"blomberg-s06-251":["Any covertly false mode of knowledge can be made evidently false; we then reduce it *ad absurdum*. He who has hitherto held that knowledge is not thereby absolutely absurd; but he would be evidently absurd, and would act contrary to all reason, if he still chose to hold it true after all the grounds of its falsity had been laid before him.",[]],"blomberg-s06-252":["It is foolish, therefore, when scholars call one another absurd and yet afterwards wish to dispute with one another whether what they have written is true or false. For in calling one another absurd, each denies the other all true knowledge at a stroke. And if I suppose that someone possesses no knowledge whatsoever, and is not capable of possessing any, 282] how can he then see my grounds for the falsity of his knowledge, or even boast of any true knowledge and believe that he possesses it? And how, in that case, am I in a position to dispute?",[]],"blomberg-s06-253":["In § 114 the author treats of the necessary and the contingent, and of contingent truths; but here we have nothing further to remark.",[]],"blomberg-s07-001":["All grounds of knowledge are either internal or external. The former are determinations in the thing itself, through which it can be known even without comparison with other things. Through the latter, however, I obtain knowledge of a thing only in so far as I compare it with other things; and these external grounds of knowledge are entitled marks [*Merkmale*] or characteristics [*Kennzeichen*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-002":["The internal [grounds of] knowledge, however, cannot properly be entitled marks or characteristics of a thing at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-003":["In comparing one thing with another we can always represent to ourselves one of two things: either sameness, *identitas*, or difference, *diversitas*. For example, the marks of sameness in men and animals are that both have a perishable body; the marks of difference are that the former are endowed with a rational soul, while the latter have no reason at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-004":["In most cases we use the marks of identity, but in many also the marks of diversity.",[]],"blomberg-s07-005":["All *genera* and *species*, for example, arise from the marks of identity, not from those of difference.",[]],"blomberg-s07-006":["If, on the other hand, we wish to distinguish the metals from one another, we must have *notae diversitatis*, for example, that gold is heavier than all [other] metals. For this is the chief distinguishing mark of gold from silver, copper, etc., and the remaining metals.",[]],"blomberg-s07-007":["A mark is therefore not merely a *ratio disjunctionis*, as some have defined it, but also a *ratio identitatis*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-008":["A mark in general is entitled a *nota*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-009":["All marks in general, however, are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-010":["1 internal",[]],"blomberg-s07-011":["2 external",[]],"blomberg-s07-012":["*Sunt vel externae, vel internae omnes notae* [All marks are either external or internal].",[]],"blomberg-s07-013":["1: The marks that I know of a thing are internal when I consider the thing solely in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-014":["2: The marks that I have of a thing are external when I compare the thing with other things; and such marks are entitled *characteres*, grounds of distinction, characteristics in the proper sense.",[]],"blomberg-s07-015":["The use of all marks can therefore naturally be likewise",[]],"blomberg-s07-016":["1: internal",[]],"blomberg-s07-017":["2 external",[]],"blomberg-s07-018":["If I know the internal marks which, taken together, make up the whole concept, these marks are complete, and sufficient for knowing everything in a thing that can be known of it *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-019":["Absolute knowledge of things is, however, far more difficult than the knowledge that we acquire through comparison. The latter is far easier and more natural to us, because it lies, as it were, in our very nature that in comparison with others we far more readily perceive, and are able to note, agreement or difference in objects. Thus ugliness, for example, strikes the eye *en comparaison* with beauty, and a true beauty does so when she is found among many ugly persons. And a man himself, when he passes judgment upon himself, mostly does not ask how good he is and how much he still lacks, but rather whether he is not as good as others, or even better. It is from this, however, that it comes about that Herr von Wolff, and even our author, take marks and characteristics to be one and the same.",[]],"blomberg-s07-020":["At first it is mostly the case that most things are taken, if not to be wholly the same, then at least to differ very little. In time the differences are gradually discovered, and as we attend more and more to things, more and more marks are found. But this gradual search for the marks of a thing requires great subtlety of understanding, just as does the ability to find, among things in which the greatest differences seem to be, or actually are, present, nevertheless a certain similarity, a sameness and agreement.",[]],"blomberg-s07-021":["In this § the author speaks of mediate and immediate marks. If a thing can be known through a certain mark without the mediation of a mark distinct from it, this is an immediate mark. A mediate mark, on the other hand, is a mark of a mark. For example, perishability is a mark of man, but a mediate [one; the text reads *unmittelbares*, immediate]: for perishability is a mark of a mortal being, that is, of a body; and man is mortal and has a body.",[]],"blomberg-s07-022":["There are, therefore, degrees of remote marks, and accordingly degrees of mediate marks. The first degree is when a thing can be known only through one *nota intermedia*. Among all marks of which one is the mark of another, a relation of subordination is always to be found. But there is also a relation of coordination, and this holds of immediate marks, where each particular mark is a new ground of knowledge of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-023":["Through experience we can perceive and know only coordinate marks of a thing, that is, marks placed side by side. Reason alone, however, is able to supply [*suppeditiren*] us with subordinate marks of a thing, that is, to present to us series of marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-024":["In the subordination of marks I have no more than a single immediate mark, and the remaining *notae* are marks of marks. In the *coordinatio notarum*, however, all the marks are immediate.",[]],"blomberg-s07-025":["In the series of subordinate marks, that is, of marks ordered one under another, there is always a first; that is, the concepts are limited.",[]],"blomberg-s07-026":["In coordination, however, there is no limitation.",[]],"blomberg-s07-027":["There are countless marks that belong immediately to a thing, and our understanding is not able to discern all its properties and to determine them exactly.",[]],"blomberg-s07-028":["In subordination the immediate mark is entitled the *nota infima*, and the last mark of all the marks is entitled the *nota summa*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-029":["Through the subordination of marks I obtain a deep, that is, an intensive magnitude; through the coordination of marks [*notarum*], on the other hand, we obtain an extensive magnitude, that is, knowledge that is extensive and worked out [in breadth; the transcript reads *nach dem Grund ausgearbeitete*, which appears confused].",[]],"blomberg-s07-030":["The series of coordinate marks is, as it were, a line without bounds, which is infinite. For it is always possible, and indeed to be expected, that posterity will in time be able to discover still more immediate marks of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-031":["The series of subordinate marks, however, is bounded and has its limits, and often very soon, namely when no further mark of a mark can be given in it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-032":["Metaphysics is that science by means of which we seek out the *terminus a priori* of subordinate marks. Through it we obtain simple and unanalysable concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-033":["Too much beauty is also harmful, and indeed arouses suspicion. Beautiful simplicity is frequently found through nature. The greatest art, however, consists in so concealing the art that has actually been employed that it is not noticeable at all, but seems to be pure nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-034":["Profundity is in a certain way opposed to beauty: knowledge becomes dry the more abstraction is made in it. Merely abstract modes of knowledge in general cannot be fully understood unless I present and discern a case *in concreto*. But the dry also often passes into the droll, and for this art is frequently required, as for example in witticisms in company; and this is customarily entitled simplicity [*Einfalt*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-035":["In every description we seek rather to understand [*verstehen*] the thing than to have insight into [*einsehen*] it. In every explanation, on the contrary, we seek rather to have insight into the thing than to understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-036":["The multitude of coordinate marks increases experience.",[]],"blomberg-s07-037":["The extensive magnitude of a mode of knowledge is, as it were, its *volumen*; the intensive magnitude is its density. In certain cases the extensive magnitude of knowledge is more useful, in others the intensive. In morals, for example, the intensive magnitude of knowledge is more necessary, whereas in physics and medicine its extensive magnitude is very often more indispensable.",[]],"blomberg-s07-038":["Dry modes of knowledge are not at all suited to sensibility, and therefore not to beauty. Thorough, deep, solid modes of knowledge, however, can and must, without reproach, be dry. This dryness is then made good by considering the abstract *in concreto* in a particular case. (Analysis proceeds *ascendendo*, synthesis *descendendo*.)",[]],"blomberg-s07-039":["Through negative marks of a thing I think something that is not actually to be found in the thing; through affirmative marks I think what is in fact present in it. Negative marks, however, are possible only in so far as we have first represented to ourselves the opposed marks, which affirm. Thus, for example, I cannot know what darkness is unless I first have a clear *idea* of light.",[]],"blomberg-s07-040":["Negative marks serve only to avoid errors, for example, animals have no rational soul. Affirmative marks, on the other hand, serve to obtain an extensive [and] unelaborated knowledge of a thing; for example, God is omniscient, etc.; gold loses nothing of its *massa* and weight in fire; it is ductile.",[]],"blomberg-s07-041":["Besides ignorance there is another and still more dangerous evil in the understanding, namely error. Through affirmative marks we avoid ignorance, through negative marks error. This is the purpose of the two kinds of marks. Through negative marks we do not, indeed, obtain any new knowledge at all. Nevertheless they are certainly very necessary and important, for through them we guard ourselves against erroneous modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-042":["The fewer means we have at hand for arriving at true modes of knowledge, the more negative marks we shall need in order not to err in our knowledge of the thing. Thus, for example, there is no science with a greater number of negative marks than rational divinity, natural *theologia*. For in it we have no means at all at hand for arriving at extended knowledge. Thus we are, for example, at every moment inclined to err in the concept, *idea*, and representation of the divine being, and of God himself in general, because we are very prone to compare him sensibly with the things that we perceive on earth, which yet can be no standard for God. God cannot be known by us through the senses, and consequently our whole knowledge of him can be only very limited.",[]],"blomberg-s07-043":["If, therefore, in *theologia naturalis* we consider God and remove all the negative marks through which we speak of him, nothing will remain to us but the *idea* of the all-sufficient and all-perfect being.",[]],"blomberg-s07-044":["Negative judgments are indeed often foolish, because what is denied is often already impossible in itself. For example: man has reason, therefore he is not a stone. This is true, a settled truth which no one will doubt. But since it is also evident that no one will think of seriously taking a man for a stone, this judgment is idle.",[]],"blomberg-s07-045":["Every mark is a ground of knowledge, which is estimated by its consequences. According as the consequences are of value in respect of reason, or of our perfection and happiness, so the marks, as grounds of knowledge, will also be of value.",[]],"blomberg-s07-046":["From the former consequences the logical value of marks, and from the latter their practical value, is to be derived.",[]],"blomberg-s07-047":["If many consequences are to be derived from a ground, it is fruitful, or has an extensive magnitude; for example, many consequences flow from the sun in respect of the earth. Thus the rules of morals are very fruitful, in that they have a very great influence upon our life, that is, upon our conduct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-048":["A mark is extensively great when I can know many consequences of a thing; a mark is intensively great when I can know from a thing not so much many as great and important consequences. The former mark is entitled fruitful, the latter sound [*richtig*; doubtful reading, perhaps for *wichtig*, important].",[]],"blomberg-s07-049":["Empty speculations have a logical value. They often have many consequences in respect of reason, although they have, of course, no influence on human happiness; for example, that the moon is inhabited, etc. This proposition has no influence on the will, on the state, etc. No one becomes more perfect, happier, or better in respect of his outward understanding [*äußeren Verstandes*; sense doubtful] through this knowledge, although he does become somewhat more perfect in respect of his reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-050":["Other modes of knowledge are aesthetically sound [*richtig*], because they stimulate and gratify our taste; for example, poetry delights because it accords with our taste. All importance of knowledge, however, obtains only when knowledge is considered by itself alone, without comparison with other modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-051":["Marks are sufficient either for our purposes, and then they are *comparative sufficientes*; or for distinguishing a thing from all others, and so for all logical purposes, and then they are *notae absolute sufficientes*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-052":["All experiences have only a relative *sufficientia*. For example, if I look closely at a pear tree, I can easily distinguish its leaves from the leaves of all the other trees in the wood. This knowledge is therefore sufficient in relation to other modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-053":["*Sufficientia relativa* or *comparativa* suffices to distinguish a thing from such things as we have so far known, but not from all things that are possible and could ever exist. For example, gold is distinguished from all other metals by its weight. But a metal has also been discovered, entitled platina, which is white in colour but has the same weight as gold. Consequently this mark, this ground of distinction of gold from all other metals, is not sufficient enough",[]],"blomberg-s07-054":["Things that fall under our senses can be known and distinguished only through marks that are relatively sufficient, not by means of marks that are absolutely sufficient.",[]],"blomberg-s07-055":["The *sufficientia notarum*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-056":["1: *externa*",[]],"blomberg-s07-057":["2. *interna*",[]],"blomberg-s07-058":["The former consists in the marks of a thing being sufficient to distinguish it from other things by comparison. The latter consists in a mark of a thing being sufficient for deriving from it all its other determinations. From this *sufficientia* arises the division of grounds of knowledge into",[]],"blomberg-s07-059":["A: internal and B: external",[]],"blomberg-s07-060":["From the latter nothing can be derived; for example, gold has a yellow colour; man is an animal that laughs. The former, however, are fruitful, so that much flows from them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-061":["External grounds of knowledge can often be very important in themselves, and yet be unfruitful and useless in respect of internal sufficiency.",[]],"blomberg-s07-062":["Necessary marks are those *ad essentiam pertinentia* [belonging to the essence], without which the thing cannot be thought at all. *Pertinent autem ad essentiam 1: vel solum ut causae aut 2: ut causata* [They belong to the essence either 1: solely as causes, or 2: as things caused].",[]],"blomberg-s07-063":["*Omnia autem ad essentiam pertinentia* [All things belonging to the essence are] A: *essentialia*, for example, the",[]],"blomberg-s07-064":["extension; B. *attributa*, for example, the divisibility of bodies. Contingent marks, on the other hand, are *extraessentialia*, without which the thing can still be thought; for example, learning in a man.",[]],"blomberg-s07-065":["If the *essentialia* are marks, they are not subordinated but coordinated to one another. The *attributa*, however, are not coordinated but subordinated to the *essentialia*, for they follow from them [*aus einander*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-066":["The *extraessentialia* marks are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-067":["A. internal or B. external.",[]],"blomberg-s07-068":["The latter are also entitled relations. The internal, again, are",[]],"blomberg-s07-069":["1: necessary",[]],"blomberg-s07-070":["2: contingent",[]],"blomberg-s07-071":["The former are, again,",[]],"blomberg-s07-072":["1. complete, or marks that are sufficient for deriving from them everything contained in the thing, or",[]],"blomberg-s07-073":["2: incomplete, when the marks are not sufficient for deriving and knowing from them everything that belongs to the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-074":["The internal contingent relations [*Verhältnisse*; sense requires 'determinations'] are entitled *modi*. A modification of external relations does not modify the man [*den Menschen*; perhaps for *das Ding*, the thing] himself, although among the things of nature a change of relation mostly seems to be bound up at the same time with a change of modification.",[]],"blomberg-s07-075":["Summer and winter are not, properly speaking, changes in the sun itself, but only altered *relationes*, relations of the sun to our earth.",[]],"blomberg-s07-076":["*Modi* are nothing but internal determinations and changes that can be thought in a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-077":["*Relationes*, on the other hand, are changes that occur in a thing in relation to others and with reference to other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-078":["We now ask the question whether, in the *completudo notarum necessariarum internarum*, a *praecisio* may not also have place. [The question is whether] superfluous marks are to be found there, which are no longer needed to distinguish the thing from all others and to derive from it all its proper determinations, so that everything may be left out which is such that, even if it is lacking, the concept will still be complete without it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-079":["*Responsio*: Yes, it is often possible. The *notae rei necessariae internae sufficientes* constitute the *praecisio*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-080":["When the marks contain no less [than], [and no more than,] is necessary for distinguishing the thing, this is a *praecisio*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-081":["Finally, when the marks of a thing are wholly adequate to it, they are *notae adaequatae*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-082":["The adequate concept of a thing, in so far as it can no longer be derived from any other, is the concept of the essence, *essentia*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-083":["The essence of a thing is the first sufficient fundamental concept, which suffices for deriving everything that belongs to the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-084":["The *notae necessariae sunt* [The necessary marks are]",[]],"blomberg-s07-085":["A: *primitivae* or B: *derivativae*",[]],"blomberg-s07-086":["The former constitute the *essentialia*, the latter the *attributa*. For example, in the triangle the three angles are just as necessary and indispensable as the three sides. But the angles are in turn derived from the sides, and are therefore *attributa*; the sides themselves, however, are fundamental concepts, *essentialia*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-087":["To know what is essential in a thing, the following is required. To know the *attributa*, I must know the internal necessary marks which are, however, accompanied by others [*von anderen begleitet*; perhaps for *von anderen abgeleitet*, derived from others]. To know the *essentialia*, I must see that the marks can no longer be derived from any other. To know the *essentia* itself, I must know that the marks, the *essentialia*, are complete and, taken together, sufficient for knowledge of the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s07-088":["The coordination of marks, when it is complete, constitutes the *completudo*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-089":["The fundamental concept of all that is necessary to a thing is its essence. To investigate the essence of things is the business and the end of philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s07-090":["The marks which, taken together, make up the essence of a thing are the *essentialia*. The consequences from the essence, the *rationata sive consectaria*, are the *attributa*. *Modi sunt notae internae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur* [*Modi* are internal marks whose possibility alone is determined through the essence].",[]],"blomberg-s07-091":["The *modi* are therefore not *rationata essentiae* in respect of their truth [*Wahrheit*; perhaps for *Wirklichkeit*, actuality], but only in respect of their possibility. *Modi* are therefore nothing but contingent marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-092":["*Modi*, as well as *relationes*, do not belong to the essence at all, *ad essentiam non pertinent*, either as *constitutiva* or as *attributa*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-093":["All marks of a thing either belong to its essence, *essentialia*, or are not *essentialia*: *ad essentiam pertinentia sunt vel essentialia, vel attributa. Extraessentialia autem sunt vel notae, sive notae internae, vel relationes, sive notae externae, quarum possibilitas tantum per essentiam determinatur* [Those belonging to the essence are either essentials or attributes. The extra-essentials are either marks, that is, internal marks, or relations, that is, external marks, whose possibility alone is determined through the essence].",[]],"blomberg-s07-094":["Essence is divided",[]],"blomberg-s07-095":["1: *mo* into logical",[]],"blomberg-s07-096":["2 *do* into real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-097":["The complete fundamental concept of a thing is, in general, its essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-098":["The first ground, however, of all that is thought by me in the concept of the thing is the logical essence. The first fundamental concept of all that belongs to the thing really and in fact is the real essence",[]],"blomberg-s07-099":["From this it naturally follows that logical and real essence must necessarily be very different from each other.",[]],"blomberg-s07-100":["When I utter words and connect a certain concept with them, what I think under this word and expression is the logical essence. For example, when I utter the word matter, everything that is inseparable from the concept which I connect with the expression matter is the logical essence of matter. The logical essence can always be represented and thought by itself alone. Thus, for example, in the case of matter I always think extension, impenetrability, and a certain constant inertia and lifelessness, such that it is not able to change its place or move of itself, but only through the agency of another, foreign force.",[]],"blomberg-s07-101":["All these are the *essentialia* of the word matter, and consequently, taken together, make up its logical essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-102":["The subjective fundamental concept is what is contained in an internal concept of the thing. This, however, does not prove that there cannot be still more marks in the object [*beweißet noch nicht, daß ... noch mehrere Merckmahle daseyn können*, rendered according to the evident sense]. I connect with the thing only what I represent to myself. Hence, for example, still other properties may have place under the word matter.",[]],"blomberg-s07-103":["The logical essence is the subjective fundamental concept. This concept, however, holds only for me, not for others. Another may represent the thing with more or fewer marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-104":["The essence of our concept is not always, indeed is seldom, the essence of the thing itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-105":["The logical essence is far smaller than the real essence. The logical essence is a concentrated fundamental concept of a thing; it comprises only what we represent to ourselves in the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-106":["This contracting, combining of concepts is indeed very well suited [*gemeßen*, for *gemäß*] to the human understanding, and quite necessary: it must be sparing with its powers. Yet such a *compendium* is just as good as an extended concept of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-107":["The real essence comprises, in general, everything that can ever actually belong to a thing. This eduction is the only means of making our concepts really distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-108":["Distinctness, however, is one of the most essential logical perfections. Through it my knowledge becomes far more fit for use; through the real essence, on the other hand, it becomes extended.",[]],"blomberg-s07-109":["The logical essence, however, must not be confused with the real essence. For in the concept that I have of the thing I do not yet think everything that lies in the thing and belongs to it, or can lie in it and belong to it. For example, if we take the concept of matter, more recent philosophers have discovered that the force of attraction also lies in the essence of matter. I should certainly never have thought of this property at once under the word matter; and perhaps still far more marks may belong to matter which have not yet been discovered and which only a philosopher will recognise.",[]],"blomberg-s07-110":["From this it is evident that infinitely more is contained in the real essence than in the logical essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-111":["Of no single object of experience, or even of the senses, can we properly know the real or objective essence. At most it is possible for us to gain proper insight into its logical or subjective essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-112":["What we think of a thing under the word is very small and slight, indeed often very little; but all that actually, *revera*, belongs to a thing is often immeasurable and cannot be determined at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-113":["To seek out the real essence of empirical concepts and objects is therefore an entirely vain endeavour; but we can indeed represent the logical essence of concepts of reason and duly clarify it for ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s07-114":["Our empirical concepts are very mutable, because our experience is daily more and more extended. The logical essence is therefore also very changeable and mutable, because in time we come to think more and more determinations in the concept of a thing",[]],"blomberg-s07-115":["[think]. All moral concepts, however, are pure concepts of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s07-116":["For there are pure concepts of the understanding which have their origin not in experience but solely in pure reason. Whoever wishes to find the real essence must know all the marks that constantly belong to the thing. He must then further seek out their ground and endeavour to explore it, and this is then the real essence. For example, [that it is] a body that grows through internal development belongs to the logical essence of a plant. But the fundamental concept of its constant determination is the real essence, to which may also belong the fact that plants generate their like.",[]],"blomberg-s07-117":["The concept that water is a fluid *elementum*, without smell or taste, fourteen times lighter than mercury, etc., is the logical essence of water. For if I am somewhat versed in physical knowledge, I think all this as soon as I name the word water. From this, however, I am still far from able to derive all the other properties which are determined to water [*dem Waßer bestimmet sind*; sense doubtful], which belong or can belong to it, and which perhaps have not yet all been discovered, although we do not always think them along with it. Consequently it is not yet the real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-118":["Of individual things we can never give the real essence, but we can of *genera*, for example, of body in general.",[]],"blomberg-s07-119":["Nevertheless we do not know all the concepts and determinations of all things which can constantly belong to them; and further, we are not always able at once to give the true ground of all these determinations. Consequently we can only very seldom exhibit their real essence.",[]],"blomberg-s07-120":["Here clear and obscure knowledge is discussed. In the first section of the *logica* the author first treated of confused and distinct concepts, without first giving any thought to clear and obscure knowledge. How is it, then, that he now begins to speak of it?",[]],"blomberg-s07-121":["*Responsio*: At the beginning of logic the concept of clear and obscure knowledge is something that can be accepted without proof, and must accordingly be presupposed and laid down as a foundation. Here, however, it can be derived in turn from what has previously been treated.",[]],"blomberg-s07-122":["Here, therefore, the order is exactly reversed. A representation, however, is obscure (to return to our subject) when we are not immediately conscious of it. Mediately, however, namely by means of reason, we can well become conscious of this knowledge. Thus there still remains a way of making an otherwise obscure mode of knowledge [329 (339)] clear and distinct to ourselves, and so of making insight into it easier. For example, when I look at the Milky Way with the naked eye, I see in it nothing but a white band. But if I take the *tubus* to my aid, I at once perceive its individual parts as individual stars, and I then immediately judge, by means of reason, that these must be the very stars which I saw with the naked eye only as a mere white band. Of this representation, therefore, I am mediately conscious, but not immediately. It is thus obscure only at first, but afterwards *mediate* distinct or clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-123":["A mode of knowledge is either obscure in and for itself, so that it cannot become clear at all despite all the effort expended; or it is relatively obscure, so that it can become clear under certain circumstances but not under others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-124":["The former, knowledge obscure in itself, is, as described, entirely impossible. For everything can become clear, however difficult it may seem to us; and what seems to us most obscure is, when we examine it, always only relatively obscure.",[]],"blomberg-s07-125":["Logic prescribes to us the rules that we are to apply in order to know how we should proceed with modes of knowledge which we are conscious of actually possessing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-126":["All clearness is the right road to distinctness. Clearness itself, however, is not yet a logical perfection, as distinctness is. Logic therefore already presupposes clearness; it supposes it and does not itself produce it. It deals with modes of knowledge and representations that have already been made clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-127":["Distinctness is thus also not the proper object of its concern [*nicht*; reading doubtful, the argument seems to require that it is]. Distinctness, *disjunctio* [perhaps for *distinctio*], is to be carefully distinguished",[]],"blomberg-s07-128":["from evidentness, *perspicuitas* [the text reads *Dispicuitaet*]. In so far as there is clearness of marks in a mode of knowledge, so far the knowledge is distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-129":["A mode of knowledge is brought *in perspicuum* only when it is so bright and clear that even the meanest understanding sees it. On the other hand, a mode of knowledge is *imperspicua* or *non perspicua* when it is too great for the whole horizon of the entire human race, when it can be entitled incomprehensible. *Perspicuitas*, by contrast, means nothing other than comprehensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s07-130":["Often a mode of knowledge ceases to be comprehensible in the very degree in which it was meant to become really distinct. This, however, cannot be avoided except by not speaking of the matter at all.",[]],"blomberg-s07-131":["Returning now to the distinction between clear and obscure knowledge, we have further to note: all obscure modes of knowledge can be",[]],"blomberg-s07-132":["a) objective",[]],"blomberg-s07-133":["B) Subjectively obscure. Strictly speaking, the former does not occur; only the latter does.",[]],"blomberg-s07-134":["When, however, the relation of a thing to the limits of human knowledge is such that the thing cannot be known clearly enough and always remains obscure, the blame is for the most part laid not on him who fails to know it clearly enough, but on the thing itself, or on him who expounds it. Yet the fault frequently lies with the former rather than with the latter. The knowledge which the one does not, and cannot, have insight into is then entitled *objective* obscure. A knowledge is, on the other hand, *subjective* obscure when the reason why the thing remains obscure, despite all the effort expended, lies not in the thing and the knowledge itself, but in him who seeks to know something. This may be because he is very ignorant, and therefore naturally unable to know the thing; or it may even be because he does not apply to it the diligence that is required.",[]],"blomberg-s07-135":["This latter obscurity, however, is not really grounded in the nature of the knowledge itself, nor, as some hold, in the nature of all men and of their understanding. For were that so, the knowledge could not be clearly known by a single man. It lies solely either in the incapacity and ignorance, or in the negligence, of this or that individual subject.",[]],"blomberg-s07-136":["Hence it comes about that the very knowledge which is clear to one subject may be obscure to another, and, conversely, the very knowledge which is obscure to one subject may be clear to another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-137":["Few men have even a proper concept of what it means to have a properly clear concept of a thing or of a knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-138":["No man can, so to speak, any longer properly understand himself. He always judges that everything he says is perfectly clear to him; and precisely for this reason he is kept from examining properly whether he does in fact understand himself or not.",[]],"blomberg-s07-139":["Metaphysics and morals, these two pure sciences of philosophy, are of such a kind and so constituted that a man is never able to represent to himself distinctly even the least concept in them, unless he can first acquire a complete and exhaustive concept of the whole, that is, of the entire science.",[]],"blomberg-s07-140":["Either one who wishes to study metaphysics has a quite pure and quite distinct concept of it, or he knows nothing whatsoever about it. There are only these two ways; there is no middle course between them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-141":["On the other hand, someone may be very easy to grasp [*faßlich*], especially when he knows nothing as yet, or at least no errors, and his hearer goes away again just as wise as he came to him. This is very easy; and such an exposition, which does no harm but also does no good, may be very easy to grasp and to comprehend.",[]],"blomberg-s07-142":["In some cases, therefore, it is not possible to combine easy graspability with complete distinctness. Those certainly judge wrongly who take the easiest exposition to be the best, and who regard one somewhat more difficult, less childish, and designed to provoke reflection, as obscure, unintelligible, and even overstrained.",[]],"blomberg-s07-143":["To make the obscure clear is so difficult that no one has yet devised a universally valid means of doing so.",[]],"blomberg-s07-144":["Completeness [*Ausführlichkeit*] is the sufficiency of a knowledge for a certain purpose. The consequence, when it serves to pursue and to judge the purpose, is entitled the end.",[]],"blomberg-s07-145":["In all *completudo*, therefore, we compare the knowledge with a certain purpose. This *completudo*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-146":["a) *externa* [external], which appears in the comparison of the knowledge with others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-147":["B) *interna* [internal], which holds in itself and lies in the knowledge itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-148":["The *completudo comparativa*, however, can often be so very useful to us that we have no need at all of the *completudo absoluta* or *interna*, and can dispense with it. Completeness, however, rests solely upon the multitude of marks that are coordinated, that is, placed alongside one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-149":["Philosophy has the purpose of teaching us how we are to learn to understand ourselves ever better.",[]],"blomberg-s07-150":["All new modes of knowledge which deviate greatly from the customary way of judging are at first wholly obscure, and are not understood even by the best mind. This frequently happens not because these modes of knowledge are obscure in themselves, but because it is commonly very difficult to depart from the customary way of judging and to regard this or that thing from another side. Thus, for example, one who has learnt to dance under a poor master, and is afterwards to begin learning under a skilful one, finds it really very hard to break himself of the old steps and placings of the feet to which he has once grown accustomed, and to follow the newer and better method. The same holds of knowledge. Everything that departs from the old, once established manner of knowledge, of exposition, of style, etc., is hard to understand and obscure.",[]],"blomberg-s07-151":["All inventors of new methods in all the sciences are therefore at first obscure. Indeed, for this very reason, that men are all too fond of the old and will not depart from it, they are often even despised, until in time men have accommodated themselves to the new method also; and then it is found that it is often far better than the old.",[]],"blomberg-s07-152":["Locke and Newton suffered this fate: their writings were at first exceedingly obscure and unintelligible, or at least seemed to be so, until they were afterwards examined more closely and their merits discovered.",[]],"blomberg-s07-153":["The doctrine of obscure modes of knowledge is not logical at all, but only metaphysical. Logic is not a science of the nature of the subject, the human soul, which seeks to know what really lies hidden in it. It presupposes clear concepts, and treats of the employment of our understanding and of our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-154":["All modes of knowledge already lie actually hidden in our soul, and nothing more is really needed than to unfold them and bring them into a brighter light.",[]],"blomberg-s07-155":["Rational philosophy teaches us nothing new; it seeks only to make distinct to us what we already know, and to bring us to consciousness of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-156":["Empirical philosophy, on the other hand, is occupied in furnishing us with new modes of knowledge which we did not hitherto possess.",[]],"blomberg-s07-157":["In this paragraph the author speaks of complete [*ausführlich*] clearness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-158":["The *completudo*, however, is",[]],"blomberg-s07-159":["1) *externa*",[]],"blomberg-s07-160":["2) *interna*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-161":["The former consists in this, that the marks ascribed to a thing suffice to distinguish it from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-162":["The latter, again, consists in this, that the marks of a thing are sufficient for deriving from them all its other possible determinations.",[]],"blomberg-s07-163":["The *completudo externa* always presupposes the *completudo interna*. Where the latter is not, the former is certainly not either; and, conversely, the former, namely the *completudo externa*, can be attained by means of the *completudo interna*. I cannot know immediately that a thing and its marks are sufficient to distinguish the thing from all other things; this must come about through the *completudo interna*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-164":["Inner *completudo* is thus not only a means to outer completeness; the latter cannot even be attained or acquired without the former.",[]],"blomberg-s07-165":["No empirical concept, therefore, can become internally complete. Inner completeness does not occur in objects of experience. From this it follows at the same time that outer completeness is likewise not to be found in objects of experience; for the inner is always the ground of the outer completeness. In metals, herbs, plants, etc., for example, I cannot know the thing in all its determinations, and consequently I cannot always distinguish it from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-166":["In many books on physics we find definitions of objects of experience. It is, however, a certain proposition that these never are, and never can be, correct and sufficient; for every definition must be complete.",[]],"blomberg-s07-167":["No one, therefore, can obtain a complete concept of objects of experience such as would serve to distinguish them from all other possible and actual things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-168":["Hence Locke took pains to show that no one, however learned, can give a correct definition of man. From this it is evident that none of our concepts of objects of experience can ever be *absolute* complete. They can, however, have a *completudo comparativa*, namely, when the marks of a thing suffice to distinguish it from everything that we have hitherto come to know in experience.",[]],"blomberg-s07-169":["Pure concepts of reason, on the other hand, can be complete both internally and externally.",[]],"blomberg-s07-170":["In empirical concepts, things outside us, the judgments [*Urtheile*], are the *exemplaria* [originals], and our concepts are the *exemplata* [copies]. In pure concepts, by contrast, the concepts themselves are the *exemplaria*, and that of which we have the concepts is the *exemplata*: for example, the concept of virtue, of right and wrong, of goodness, of the rightfulness and wrongfulness of actions, of the simple and the composite, and of the contingent and the necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s07-171":["These concepts do not arise from objects at all. Hence I cannot represent their determination [as given] even in part; they are arbitrary [*willkührlich*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-172":["Reason is the creator of these concepts, and consequently the thing has no other determination than that which reason has ascribed to it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-173":["Mathematics is of this kind. It has nothing but pure concepts of reason, which can therefore be completely complete, both internally and externally. For example, mathematicians think a *conus*, a cone, as a right-angled triangle revolving about its *cathetus*, that is, about one of its sides. Here the mathematician thinks everything that suffices to distinguish the thing from all others. For the cone is not a thing outside him, which he has come to know in part according to certain determinations; it is a thing in his pure reason, which he arbitrarily thinks for himself. He ascribes to it, accordingly, certain determinations, through which he intends that the thing shall be distinguishable from all other things.",[]],"blomberg-s07-174":["Only pure concepts of reason, therefore, are complete, and not objects of experience, as is clearly evident from what has been said.",[]],"blomberg-s07-175":["No one, therefore, should venture to define empirical concepts; we can, however, have correct definitions of pure concepts of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-176":["Here the author speaks of knowledge which is wholly or partly clear, wholly or partly distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-177":["Representations are wholly clear when the thing I think is composed of no more determinations than those I represent in it. Representations are partly clear when I think, not each and every determination of the thing, but only some of its determinations.",[]],"blomberg-s07-178":["Some of the ancient philosophers therefore divided concepts into *rationes archetypas* and *ectypos* [ἔκτυπα]. The former are to be regarded as archetypes and *originaria*, the latter as copies and *exemplata*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-179":["To the former belong the pure concepts of reason, to the latter the concepts of experience. The former can be wholly clear; the latter, on the contrary, are never wholly but always only partly clear. If, for example, I see a house before me and cannot walk round it, the house is only partly clear to me, namely as regards the side I have seen; the rest is wholly obscure.",[]],"blomberg-s07-180":["In concepts of experience I know only those determinations of the thing which affect our senses, and affect them in such a way that we are conscious of them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-181":["Liveliness and strength of knowledge are not *opposita*; both are bound up with each other. Liveliness is an aesthetic perfection. Distinctness, however, the *rationabilitas*, or rather profound distinctness, is a logical perfection. The more profound the distinctness of a knowledge, the more its liveliness diminishes; and conversely, the livelier a knowledge is, the shallower it is.",[]],"blomberg-s07-182":["Liveliness in a knowledge is attained by means of extensive combination [*Verbindung*]. Distinctness in a knowledge is attained when we seek to produce it by means of extensive separation [*Absonderung*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-183":["Through coordinated marks we make a knowledge lively, through subordinated marks profoundly distinct. Extensive distinctness is bound up with the liveliness of a knowledge; profound distinctness, on the other hand, with its dryness. In describing spring, for example, I represent it in a lively manner through a multitude of marks coordinated with one another. So does the poet. He shows, for example, the sprouting flowers, the young green of the woods, the frisking herds, the renewed rays of the sun, the fair and pleasant air, the reviving of all nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-184":["In all eloquence and poetry one seeks to adduce the marks, coordinated with one another, which are immediately perceived in the thing to be described, in order to make the concept of the thing lively.",[]],"blomberg-s07-185":["By this means aesthetic perfection is attained in a knowledge. The philosopher, however, who represents a thing to himself through profound distinctness, singles out only one mark of the thing and leaves aside the rest; but he seeks out marks, and the grounds of the marks, which he thinks in the thing. In this, therefore, it is chiefly his reason that is occupied.",[]],"blomberg-s07-186":["The form of the knowledge is thus quite different when we represent the thing through subordinated marks from when we represent it through coordinated marks. Aesthetic is concerned only to paint the thing and to make it distinct through coordinated marks. Reason, on the other hand, ascends from one mark to another and so leaves aside many coordinated marks; the knowledge thereby becomes dry.",[]],"blomberg-s07-187":["The poet and the orator know much in few things. The philosopher, on the other hand, considers many objects, and knows little in many objects. His modes of knowledge are therefore universal; they bear not merely upon single *objecta*, but upon whole kinds of them. He represents to himself only a few marks, but represents them with profound distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-188":["Rational distinctness is a distinctness *a priori*, which I know through marks *a priori*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-189":["Through extensive distinctness we know *objecta* without needing to employ reason, without reasoning subtly [*vernünfteln*]. For profound distinctness, however, reason is necessarily required.",[]],"blomberg-s07-190":["For lively knowledge sensibility is required. Sensibility is the perfection of knowledge whereby we represent a thing as we represent objects of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s07-191":["*Rationabilitas* belongs to things in so far as we think them through universal concepts; sensibility, on the other hand, in so far as we represent a thing through singular concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-192":["All sensibility produces liveliness; conformity to reason [*Vernunftmäßigkeit*], on the other hand, produces dryness. Our concepts of reason acquire sensibility when we represent universal judgments *in concreto*. This is done, for example, through examples and *similia* [similitudes].",[]],"blomberg-s07-193":["The sensibility of a knowledge is in no way an imperfection of it. In it we think the universal, which we have already discerned in abstract modes of knowledge, *in concreto*, in those cases in which it actually occurs.",[]],"blomberg-s07-194":["Extensive clearness of knowledge, combined with sensibility, produces liveliness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-195":["Here our understanding always, as it were, wavers between two rocks. If we seek to know something with aesthetic perfection, we soon lack sound grounds, the solid element in knowledge, and fall into shallowness. If, on the other hand, we know something with logical perfection, we very easily lack aesthetic liveliness, etc., and lapse into dryness. Indeed, it is truly very difficult to hit upon the right mean between these two dangerous by-ways.",[]],"blomberg-s07-196":["It is nevertheless possible to unite both perfections with each other in a high degree.",[]],"blomberg-s07-197":["The clearness of knowledge is, just like its completeness, *completudo*,",[]],"blomberg-s07-198":["A:) *extensiva*",[]],"blomberg-s07-199":["B ) *intensiva*",[]],"blomberg-s07-200":["*Claritas extensiva* rests upon a multitude of coordinated marks which are immediately known in a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-201":["It is the right way to liveliness, since it brings much sensibility with it; for sensibility consists precisely in the *coordinatio* of marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-202":["The consummation of the perfections of all our modes of knowledge is, finally, to give them sensibility, so that we represent the universal in particular circumstances and cases, and think the *abstractum in concreto* in a single, individual, sensible case: for example, when I think friendship, true love, and the readiness to serve one another that flows from it, in Damon and Phintias. Here, then, I think the universal in single cases. By this means my knowledge becomes lively; as, for example, when I think patriotism in Cato.",[]],"blomberg-s07-203":["Without sensibility our pure knowledge through reason is very meagre indeed.",[]],"blomberg-s07-204":["For extensive clearness and liveliness only understanding is required, since with coordinated marks I do nothing but judge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-205":["*Claritas intensiva*, however, rests upon subordinated marks, and for it reason is necessarily required, since in it we infer, and derive one mark from another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-206":["*Claritas extensiva* serves for the description of an object. *Claritas intensiva*, on the other hand, investigates the grounds of the thing. From one mark belonging to the thing it seeks [to show] that another mark must also belong to it. It thus seeks to discover the causes of things, and always asks: Why? Why this, why that also, indeed why is it not otherwise?",[]],"blomberg-s07-207":["The senses give us a lively, but not a wholly distinct, knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-208":["He who can relate completely and in order everything he has ever seen possesses much liveliness of knowledge; indeed, by such a narrative he can often produce a lively knowledge in others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-209":["Sensible clearness is nothing but liveliness; clearness in intuition, however, is sensible clearness. Clearness through universal concepts is logical clearness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-210":["Mathematics is the only science which is able to judge in accordance with its concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-211":["He who is able to combine in his knowledge both liveliness and profound distinctness has in fact already attained the highest degree of perfection of human knowledge. This, however, is so difficult that it seems to everyone well-nigh impossible.",[]],"blomberg-s07-212":["Through sensibility the perfection of reason is not in the least lost; rather, it merely acquires a greater liveliness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-213":["From this, then, we see quite distinctly that sensibility in no way conflicts with the perfection of reason, but rather in certain cases promotes it, and often even secures for it greater correctness. For *in abstracto* we often leave out, and must leave out, marks of things which really belong to the nature of the thing, but which can be restored when the thing is considered *in concreto*. Orators and poets can therefore often be of very great help to the philosopher.",[]],"blomberg-s07-214":["It is a most universal rule of a good, and especially of a learned, exposition: combine [sensibility] with an exposition, and give sensibility to your instruction.",[]],"blomberg-s07-215":["Logical clearness, however, rests upon the subordination, sensible clearness, on the other hand, upon the coordination, of marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-216":["In like manner we can also have A: a sensible distinctness according to aesthetic laws,",[]],"blomberg-s07-217":["and B: a logical distinctness according to logical laws",[]],"blomberg-s07-218":["We must often abstract; but this is in fact very difficult for us.",[]],"blomberg-s07-219":["*1mo* Sensible distinctness according to laws of aesthetic is nothing but the distinctness of intuition.",[]],"blomberg-s07-220":["*2do* Logical distinctness, on the other hand, according to laws of understanding and reason, or of logic, is nothing but the distinctness of reflection",[]],"blomberg-s07-221":["Something can be very lively, and yet all the less distinct. Again, something can be skilful, colourful, and lively, and yet be at the same time but little distinct, and confused.",[]],"blomberg-s07-222":["That our knowledge, as the author asserts, becomes distinct solely through analysis [*Zergliederung*] is false.",[]],"blomberg-s07-223":["Our knowledge can be made distinct in two ways",[]],"blomberg-s07-224":["a) *per synthesin*",[]],"blomberg-s07-225":["B) *per analysin*",[]],"blomberg-s07-226":["We must, however, carefully distinguish here the science of making a distinct knowledge from the science of making distinct a knowledge which was previously obscure. That is to say, we either make a distinct concept, and this is done *per synthesin*; or we make distinct a concept which was previously confused, and this is done *per analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-227":["In *synthesis* we generate, and as it were create, a concept which previously did not exist at all, wholly new both *quoad materiam* and *quoad formam*, and at the same time make it distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-228":["All the concepts of mathematicians are of this kind; for example, the concepts of the triangle, the square, the circle, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s07-229":["All concepts invented [*fingirt*] by reason are at the same time distinct, but only *per synthesin*. If a concept is to be made distinct *per analysin*, it must already be given. We are then occupied in making clear and distinct what is confused and obscure in this given concept, in unfolding it, setting it out, and so illuminating it. Here belong, for example, each and every concept of metaphysics.",[]],"blomberg-s07-230":["In these we are solely to become conscious of the marks which belong to the thing, as, for example, in the concepts of virtue and of vice. We therefore need do nothing more than analyse, set out, and dissect, in order to make the confused concepts distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-231":["The matter, therefore, is already there; we are only to give the thing a form. By means of analytic distinctness we know in a thing no more than we have already thought in it. We know only what we really knew already, but know it better, that is, more distinctly, more clearly, and with more consciousness. In the case of the concept of perfection, for example, I shall first lead someone to the cases in which he makes use of the expression 'perfection', in order thereby to show him what he really understands by perfection, what concept he forms of it, and what he thinks when he utters the word 'perfection' and ascribes it to a thing. Here he will naturally find that he calls many things perfect which are in fact very imperfect, or even vicious. The voluptuary, for example, takes his *vaga libido* [roving lust] to be the greatest possible perfection; for were it not so, he could not possibly be so strongly given to it, nor find in it so very great a pleasure, one that eclipses everything else for him. Analysis, therefore, properly aims at making the confused distinct. The composition of knowledge, on the other hand, or *synthesis*, serves solely to produce something new, and at the same time and in one act to make it distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s07-232":["In general, all concepts of the human understanding are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-233":["*1mo conceptus dati* or *2do conceptus facti*",[]],"blomberg-s07-234":["A *conceptus datus*, a given concept, is one which is produced either by the nature of our understanding or by experience.",[]],"blomberg-s07-235":["*Conceptus facti*, made concepts, are such as are arbitrarily generated or invented by us, without having previously been given.",[]],"blomberg-s07-236":["All these invented concepts are produced together with their distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-237":["For in them we arbitrarily feign something for ourselves, and do so together with consciousness of it.",[]],"blomberg-s07-238":["There are many such *conceptus facti* in aesthetic and in mathematics. In philosophy, on the other hand, there are *conceptus dati*",[]],"blomberg-s07-239":["Knowledge does not become distinct through analysis alone. For the act whereby a knowledge becomes distinct is one and the same as that whereby its marks become clear.",[]],"blomberg-s07-240":["A knowledge can acquire a distinctness of reason *per analysin*, but also a distinctness of understanding *per analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-241":["Through analysis I merely bring the marks of a knowledge under more universal marks, which I previously discerned *a priori per analysin*. *Per synthesin*, on the other hand, I learn new marks of a knowledge in addition, *a posteriori*; as, for example, in the concept of the essence of gold.",[]],"blomberg-s07-242":["The latter is empirical distinctness; the former is distinctness of understanding, which has its origin in my reason and is effected by means of analysis. Morals, for example, is nothing more than the setting out of our moral representations of virtue, of good and of evil. Socrates said: I am the midwife of my hearers' thoughts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-243":["To make a knowledge is entitled to invent [*dichten*]",[]],"blomberg-s07-244":["We shall now briefly indicate the degrees of representation in all knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-245":["*1mo* »To represent something in general is the most universal, the commonest, and also the easiest knowledge of a thing. But to represent something with which consciousness is also combined differs from representing in general, in which we are often not even conscious to what these representations really refer.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-246":["*Sibi aliquid repraesentare id est cognoscere, repraesentatio.* [To represent something to oneself, that is, to know; representation.]",[]],"blomberg-s07-247":["But when there is further combined with such a representation the capacity to bring what I represent under a universal concept, and so to be able to know to what my representation really refers, this is the",[]],"blomberg-s07-248":["second degree, namely to be acquainted with [*kennen*]: »I am then acquainted with what I represent.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-249":["3.) »The next degree is to understand something, that is, to be acquainted with it through the understanding, or to be acquainted with something distinctly through a distinct concept.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-250":["Nature offers us many things with which we are indeed able to be acquainted, but which we are not on that account really able to understand. Further,",[]],"blomberg-s07-251":["4: To have insight [*Einsehen*]. »Much that is to be found in nature we do indeed understand, but we do not yet have insight into it; for example, the growth of plants.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-252":["To have insight is to know something through and by means of reason, that is, *a priori*. »In this case, therefore, the thing need not even be given to me. Men often believe that they already have insight into something when they are merely able to give an explanation of it.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-253":["To make [*facere*] a knowledge is entitled to invent [*fingere*] it",[]],"blomberg-s07-254":["A *conceptus factitius* is therefore at the same time a *conceptus fictitius*. To make a knowledge arbitrarily and with consciousness is thus to make a distinct knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-255":["All mathematical definitions, for example, are merely invented, and are therefore nothing but made, arbitrary, distinct concepts of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-256":["5: »To comprehend [*begreifen*] something is, finally, the last and highest degree: it is to have insight into something through reason, but in such a way that it is sufficient for a certain purpose; this is *comparative* insight.«",[]],"blomberg-s07-257":["But when I have insight into something sufficient for each and every purpose, this is *absolute* comprehension, comprehension without qualification.",[]],"blomberg-s07-258":["Much that I really understand, am acquainted with, and have insight into, I do not on that account yet comprehend; and, again, much that I comprehend *comparative* I do not on that account yet comprehend *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-259":["*Absolute*, that is, sufficiently for every purpose, we have insight into nothing whatsoever in the whole of nature.",[]],"blomberg-s07-260":["*Sufficientia notarum rei est completudo, et est* [The sufficiency of the marks of a thing is completeness, and it is]",[]],"blomberg-s07-261":["a:) *externa*, that is, when the marks I have of a thing are complete and sufficient to distinguish this thing from all others. The whole concept of it must therefore be known.",[]],"blomberg-s07-262":["A concept that is externally sufficient is therefore always also internally sufficient. Further, conversely, a concept that is internally sufficient is always also externally sufficient. And a concept that is not internally sufficient is also not externally sufficient; and one that is not so externally is, again, not so internally either",[]],"blomberg-s07-263":["*Philosophia est vel pura, vel applicata* [Philosophy is either pure or applied]",[]],"blomberg-s07-264":["In *philosophia pura* each and every concept is given through the understanding and through reason; for example, the concepts of the possible and, again, of the impossible, of the necessary and of the contingent.",[]],"blomberg-s07-265":["*Omnes conceptus sunt vel* [All concepts are either]",[]],"blomberg-s07-266":["1: *pure rationales* [purely rational]",[]],"blomberg-s07-267":["2 *empirici* [empirical]",[]],"blomberg-s07-268":["All concepts which were previously obscure and confused are made distinct through analysis [*Zergliederung*], that is, *per analysin*. For through it we learn to have insight into the partial concepts of a whole concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-269":["A distinct concept, on the other hand, arises *per synthesin*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-270":["In *analysis* the concept either becomes completely distinct or it does not.",[]],"blomberg-s07-271":["All my concepts of reason can become completely distinct; empirical concepts, however, are excluded from this, and always remain incompletely distinct",[]],"blomberg-s07-272":["Here in this paragraph the author speaks of the comprehensible and the incomprehensible. In order to obtain a better *idea* of it, we shall first once more set out the degrees of human knowledge. This [representation] cannot be further described, nor defined.",[]],"blomberg-s07-273":["The first degree is to know [*wissen*] something, that is, to represent something with consciousness.",[]],"blomberg-s07-274":["The second degree is to be acquainted with [*kennen*] something, that is, to be able to know something as distinguished from other things through comparison with them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-275":["The third degree is to understand something, that is, to know it distinctly through the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s07-276":["The fourth degree, finally, is to have insight into something, or to know it through reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-277":["When I have insight into something, I know it through mediate marks. I therefore infer, and in doing so seek out the *nota notae*, the mark of a mark.",[]],"blomberg-s07-278":["If I am to understand something, the understanding must be occupied in it. I must know a thing through immediate marks, but in so doing I must also judge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-279":["To understand something is far easier than to have insight into it. For in the first case I need only coordinate the marks of a thing, whereas in the other I must subordinate them. To understand what gold is, I need do no more than know the properties of this metal: that it is ductile, yellow, heavier than other [metals], etc., and that it does not rust. But to have insight into what gold is, I must examine one of its marks in particular and separate out its ground: for example, why it does not rust, why it is ductile, why heavier than others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-280":["The fifth and highest degree of our knowledge, finally, is entitled to comprehend something, that is, to have sufficient insight into something, or in the degree required for a certain insight.",[]],"blomberg-s07-281":["We can comprehend something either *absolute*, or *totaliter*, wholly, or else *partialiter*, that is, *relative*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-282":["I comprehend something *absolute* when I have such insight into it as is required for this or that end.",[]],"blomberg-s07-283":["We never comprehend anything *totaliter* or *absolute*. Even the most learned among us cannot boast that he has such insight into anything as would suffice for all purposes. The common man, provided he has a sound understanding, always comprehends in matters of religion just as much as the greatest theologian of what he needs to know in order to live uprightly and to order all his actions according to the laws of God.",[]],"blomberg-s07-284":["This, however, will never be sufficient for all his ends.",[]],"blomberg-s07-285":["In respect of the practical perfection of knowledge, therefore, we all have equal insight, although we differ in insight as regards logical perfection. Yet even he who has greater insight and more fully elaborated modes of knowledge comprehends nothing *absolute*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-286":["The author wrongly renders the word *begreifen* by *concipere*, whereas *concipere* really means nothing other than to have insight [*Einsehen*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-287":["The six degrees of our knowledge, of which we have already spoken more fully above, are rather to be entitled",[]],"blomberg-s07-288":["1. *repraesentare*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-289":["to represent",[]],"blomberg-s07-290":["2. *Scire*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-291":["to know",[]],"blomberg-s07-292":["3. *Noscere*, to be acquainted with.",[]],"blomberg-s07-293":["4. *intelligere*, to understand",[]],"blomberg-s07-294":["5. *Concipere*, to have insight",[]],"blomberg-s07-295":["6. *Comprehendere*, to comprehend",[]],"blomberg-s07-296":["What is comprehensible to one man is often incomprehensible to another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-297":["Something is not comprehensible to us at the outset which can nevertheless very easily be comprehended by us in the course of time. Yet we can often err in believing, and fully holding, that others will comprehend very well what we ourselves do not comprehend; and *vice versa*, when we hold that what we comprehend well is incomprehensible to others.",[]],"blomberg-s07-298":["If I am to make my concepts of a thing distinct, my representation of it must first of all be clear. I must then attend to the various marks of the thing. Next I must take many marks in one thing together, that is, compare them [*compariren*], holding them against one another and comparing them with one another. In this comparison [*comparation*] there takes place not merely a collection or assembling of the marks, but also, and still more, a placing of them side by side, a coordination of them.",[]],"blomberg-s07-299":["Finally there is added the *abstractio notarum* [abstraction of marks]. This is the act by which I leave out of account all those marks of a thing which could hinder and disturb me in making the representation distinct, or which at least are of no use to me and are therefore superfluous for that purpose.",[]],"blomberg-s07-300":["In this § the author shows the distinction of distinct knowledge into *cognitio totaliter distincta* and *cognitio partialiter distincta* [totally and partially distinct knowledge].",[]],"blomberg-s07-301":["We often know an object distinctly in one part, but indistinctly in another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-302":["Much is very hidden from us in experience; in logic, however, more distinctness is already to be met with.",[]],"blomberg-s07-303":["Indistinctness arises, properly speaking, from want of attention to the parts of a whole concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-304":["The blame for this indistinctness is commonly ascribed either to the thing itself of which we have distinct knowledge, or to the person, the subject, in whom the indistinctness of the knowledge is found. When a thing has so very many marks that we are unable to survey them all at once, the blame for the indistinctness [of our knowledge] of it necessarily lies in the thing itself.",[]],"blomberg-s07-305":["In empirical concepts there is always more confusion than in concepts of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s07-306":["For the concepts of experience arise through the affecting [*Rührung*] of our senses. The senses, however, cannot discern all the determinations of things; on the contrary, many of these remain hidden from us and unknown.",[]],"blomberg-s07-307":["Our modes of knowledge are either",[]],"blomberg-s07-308":["1: *adaequat*, that is, complete [*vollständig*], or",[]],"blomberg-s07-309":["2) incomplete and not *adaequat*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-310":["In this paragraph the author, following Baumgarten, as Baumgarten in turn followed Wolff, has entitled a certain kind of distinct modes of knowledge complete [*vollständig*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-311":["When the marks of a thing are known completely distinctly, this is the intensive clearness of knowledge of the first degree. But when I know the immediate marks of a thing distinctly, and from them derive other, mediate marks, this distinctness of knowledge is the intensive clearness of the second degree, and, in Wolff's terms, the completeness of the knowledge. For example, when I say: vice is a facility in sinning.",[]],"blomberg-s07-312":["Now a facility [*Fertigkeit*] is an ease in acting. To sin, again, means nothing other than to live contrary to moral rules and laws. Vice is therefore a facility in performing [*ediren*] actions which are contrary to the moral laws.",[]],"blomberg-s07-313":["This second degree of the intensive distinctness of knowledge cannot, however, possibly be completeness. For otherwise the third degree of distinctness would have to be still more complete, and so it would go on without end. Here all comparison falls away: if something is already absolutely complete, there can be nothing found that would be more complete still.",[]],"blomberg-s07-314":["The author, however, has in an improper sense entitled complete knowledge *adaequata cognitio*, and incomplete modes of knowledge *cognitiones inadaequatae*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-315":["Completeness [*Vollständigkeit*] itself, however, consists in nothing other than *completudo* [exhaustiveness] and *praecisio* [precision].",[]],"blomberg-s07-316":["Knowledge is therefore complete (*cognitio rei adaequata*) when the representation of a thing is truly in conformity and commensurate with it, so that it contains nothing more, but also nothing less, than actually lies in the thing: *si repraesentatio rei de re quadam nec supra nec infra rem est* [if the representation of a thing is neither above nor below the thing].",[]],"blomberg-s07-317":["All distinctness through subordination of marks admits of different degrees. Knowledge, therefore, cannot be called complete until its marks are no longer subordinate to any other concept.",[]],"blomberg-s07-318":["*Completudo*, or exhaustiveness [*Ausführlichkeit*], we have already considered above; of precision we shall speak more fully later on.",[]],"blomberg-s07-319":["*Completudo* consists in nothing other than the sufficiency [*Sufficienz*, *Zureichung*] of a distinct concept of a thing for deriving from it all the remaining marks of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-320":["*Omnis cognitio sufficiens est* [All sufficient knowledge is]",[]],"blomberg-s07-321":["a) *extensiva*, that is, exhaustiveness [*Ausführlichkeit*];",[]],"blomberg-s07-322":["B: *intensiva*, that is, thoroughness [*Gründlichkeit*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-323":["Thoroughness therefore requires profundity [*Tiefsinnigkeit*]. To it accordingly belongs proof; and it is attained by ascending to the highest mark of many marks subordinated to one another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-324":["A certain complete thoroughness is required in regard to morals.",[]],"blomberg-s07-325":["Yet even here we very often err. Such, for example, is the case with a certain *casus necessitatis* [case of necessity], such as a lie of necessity [*Nothlüge*], a Christian deception, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s07-326":["Exhaustiveness consists, properly speaking, in nothing other than this, that nothing is lacking, so that the concept can be whole.",[]],"blomberg-s07-327":["Nevertheless, even with this exhaustiveness a fault can occur, namely when my concept contains more than it should contain in order to be complete.",[]],"blomberg-s07-328":["There is therefore a further perfection to be added, which consists in this, that neither more nor less than is necessary be contained [in the concept]; and this perfection is entitled exactness [*Abgemeßenheit*], precision [*Praecision*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-329":["Knowledge is therefore precise [*abgemeßen*] when it has neither too many nor too few marks.",[]],"blomberg-s07-330":["*Cognitio est praecisa, quando non abundat notis* [Knowledge is precise when it does not abound in marks].",[]],"blomberg-s07-331":["Precision, then, is the determinate degree of the marks, according to which they contain no more than is necessary for deriving from them all the remaining determinations of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-332":["It requires more effort to make knowledge precise than to bring it to a redundant exhaustiveness, that is, to make it more than complete [*complet*].",[]],"blomberg-s07-333":["Precision is in truth a great and difficult art. This is more easily seen in particular cases, and even by means of experience. Thus the mathematician, otherwise so precise, can nevertheless find no precision, for example, in the quadrature of the circle. The calculations of it hitherto made have all been such that the resulting number has been either too large or too small, and it has not yet been possible to hit upon it exactly.",[]],"blomberg-s07-334":["To be able to observe such precision in regard to philosophical concepts certainly requires far more art and skill. A concept which is at once complete and precise is commensurate with the thing, and such a concept is then entitled a *conceptus adaequatus*, *cognitio rei adaequata*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-335":["The case with marks is here really the same as with a measure. A measure is commensurate with the thing when it contains neither more nor less than is necessary for the magnitude to be measured. A mark, likewise, is commensurate with the thing of which it is to be a mark when it contains neither more nor less than lies in the concept of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s07-336":["In *completudo* I take care that there is nothing too little in the knowledge; in precision, on the other hand, we see to it that there is nothing too much in it. *Completudo*, as is easily seen, is always far more necessary than *praecisio*. For superfluity in knowledge, although it too is a fault which we must endeavour as far as possible to avoid, can nevertheless be tolerated far sooner and more easily than deficiency in knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s07-337":["Precision is, properly speaking, a perfection only relatively. For it serves merely to let us deal sparingly with our knowledge, and not expend our powers too lavishly upon one thing, squandering them, as it were, where it is not necessary, so that we afterwards have no capacity left for other and more useful modes of knowledge. Precision is therefore nothing but a rule of economy, which for that very reason has also a certain inner beauty. It is to be met with especially in mathematics, in geometry and in mechanics.",[]],"blomberg-s07-338":["The superfluous, the *abundans*, in a concept arises solely when one concept already lies in another; and this superfluity is always a consequence of a want of *praecisio*.",[]],"blomberg-s07-339":["We say by many words, and more than are required, what we could have expressed and said in a few. It is the same when in arithmetic we express a fraction in very large and lengthy figures, though we should be able to express it just as well and correctly in few and small numbers",[]],"blomberg-s07-340":["There is also a certain kind of precision in proofs, according to which there is not a single proposition too many or too few for producing conviction. Such excellent precision in proofs is likewise found in mathematics. This fine science is thus, as we see, a model in many respects; and men have always endeavoured to introduce these properties of it, and among them especially precision, into philosophy also.",[]],"blomberg-s07-341":["In systematic exposition and knowledge, precision is a chief perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s07-342":["In beautiful, that is, aesthetic modes of knowledge, on the other hand, precision is a perfection that can be dispensed with.",[]],"blomberg-s07-343":["In eloquence and poetry we are not sparing of expressions, but often even too lavish. We very often say a thing more than once, only in other expressions and under other images. Precision is therefore merely a rule of economy and of prudence, and not at all of taste or of aesthetic. Since precision is only a rule of prudence, it is also hard to attain. And that precision in which one is so exact and sparing in one's expressions that another thereby attains a determinate degree of clearness of the concept is a special talent, and therefore also a work of genius, which by mere art can be attained only with great difficulty, indeed hardly at all. All maxims [*Sinnsprüche*] are of this kind; all proverbs have a certain kind of precision or commensurateness. Through them a whole wisdom is said in few expressions. Precision therefore belongs solely to logical perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s07-344":["In § 151 the author entitles the precision of knowledge determinate knowledge [*bestimmte Erkenntniß*]. This expression, however, is entirely mistaken.",[]],"blomberg-s07-345":["Indeterminate marks are those which contain too little; yet our author says, on the contrary, that indeterminate marks are those which contain too much, or more [than is necessary]. Something can be complete without having any precision. We shall now say a little more about profound modes of knowledge. The intensive distinctness of knowledge has, as we have already heard above, various degrees.",[]],"blomberg-s07-346":["A greater degree of distinctness of knowledge through subordinate marks is deep distinctness. A higher degree of distinctness of knowledge through coordinate marks, on the other hand, is extended distinctness. I can attain to depth of knowledge only by passing from one mark to another, and in this way knowing one mark of the thing mediately through another.",[]],"blomberg-s07-347":["Profundity [*Tiefsinn*] is nothing other than the capacity for deep distinctness, that is, for a long series of marks [reached] mediately, by ascent. Here I go up from the experiences I have already had to the first grounds, or from the first grounds to the first experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s07-348":["That which is first, from which I here begin, can be either the first or, if I end with it, the last.",[]],"blomberg-s07-350":["In this paragraph the author teaches us nothing further than a mere name. He does not show us here how concepts can and should be made bright and clear, but merely teaches us to know the name for a mind [*Kopf*] that has clear concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s07-351":["Some men are so constituted that they foolishly imagine that in their own heads alone is there light, while other people have nothing but obscure and confused concepts. “In their heads alone is a Goshen, whereas everything else, on the contrary, is covered with an Egyptian night.”",[]],"blomberg-s07-352":["A learned man, however, must take especial pains to obtain clear and distinct concepts of the things which are, or can be, the objects of his consideration.",[]],"blomberg-s08-001":["Certainty is nothing other than subjective necessity in the quality of judgments. The opposite of a judgment is the denial of what is affirmed in it, or again the affirmation of what is denied in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-002":["Many judgments are so constituted that their opposite seems to me as if it must be wholly impossible; and thereby [the judgment] is subjectively necessary",[]],"blomberg-s08-003":["Whatever is true is by that very fact at the same time subjectively certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-004":["Objective necessity is therefore, properly speaking, truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-005":["In a multitude of cases we find that we judge many things to be true which, subjectively, we nevertheless in certain cases always find to be uncertain, and even find their opposite to be possible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-006":["It is therefore clear that the uncertainty of knowledge rests solely on its objective falsity, and the certainty of knowledge, on the other hand, on its objective truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-007":["If something is true, it is also at the same time certain; if something is false, it is also always, without doubt, at the same time uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-008":["Objectively, what is true cannot be false; that is, of the constitution of a thing, or of a true property, it cannot be uncertain whether it is true or false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-009":["All uncertainty is therefore grounded in the subject and is subjective; that is, it is grounded in him who is unable to discern and know something either as true or as false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-010":["Every ground of truth is a ground of holding-to-be-true [*Vorwahrhaltung*]. Every ground of knowledge, even supposing it to be insufficient, is nevertheless a ground of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-011":["All holding-to-be-true from grounds of which we do not examine whether there is in them a great or a small degree of truth is plausibility [*Scheinbahrkeit*]. If the degree of truth is greater than the degree of the grounds of the opposite, the knowledge is probable. There is therefore in it as yet not the least established truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-012":["In probability there is indeed [no] sufficient ground present; but this ground of truth is nevertheless greater than the grounds of the opposite, and outweighs them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-013":["Holding-to-be-true for the sake of the semblance of knowledge is entitled persuasion [*Überredung*].",[]],"blomberg-s08-014":["Here, then, we accept any degree of truth whatever, in order to be able to assent to a mode of knowledge, without examining whether or not the grounds of the opposite have a greater degree of truth. Judgment according to the measure of probability is not persuasion; [persuasion] is judgment from mere plausibility. Persuasion is in truth a kind of delusion;",[]],"blomberg-s08-015":["for in it we always consider only the one side, without reflecting in the least upon the opposite side, which is in the highest degree harmful.",[]],"blomberg-s08-016":["The consciousness of a mode of knowledge through the sufficient ground of truth is entitled conviction [*Überzeugung*]. This arises solely from the rational consciousness of the necessity of the knowledge. Where there is only contingent knowledge, which can be as it actually is but also otherwise, there is no conviction. We may fitly represent conviction to ourselves as composed of many moments or elements, that is, of the small degrees of persuasion and of holding-to-be-true, and may take all the moments or degrees of holding-to-be-true and of persuasion to be of the same kind.",[]],"blomberg-s08-017":["When the moments of holding-to-be-true make up exactly half of the sufficient ground, the knowledge is uncertain, doubtful, *ambigua ejus modi cognitio* [knowledge of this kind is ambiguous]. The mind then always remains undecided; it hovers, as it were, between fear and hope. There are on the one side exactly as many grounds as on the other, and they hold one another in equilibrium.",[]],"blomberg-s08-018":["But if there is even one degree of truth more on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, the knowledge is no longer *ambigua*, but already probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-019":["If, on the other hand, there is one degree of truth less on the side of the insufficient ground than on the side of the opposite, the knowledge is not only uncertain, and not probable, but even improbable. Nevertheless the knowledge still remains plausible. It cannot on that account be at once rejected, for plausibility requires merely the smallest degree of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-020":["All certainty is to be regarded as a unity and as a complete whole, and is therefore the measure of all our other holding-to-be-true and of each and every degree of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-021":["Certainty, however, arises from nothing other than the relation of equality between the grounds of knowledge which I have and the whole sufficient ground itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-022":["If the grounds of knowledge which I have are not equal to the whole sufficient ground, my knowledge is uncertain. It is true that there is a certain heterogeneity in objects and concepts; yet concepts can become homogeneous if the manifold is brought under a universal concept.",[]],"blomberg-s08-023":["This production of homogeneity arises solely from comparison. We compare [*compariren*] the grounds of truth with the grounds of falsity, holding them against one another; we then see whether the grounds of truth have a greater degree than the grounds of the opposite; and then, after more mature deliberation, we pass a more correct and apt judgment on the probability or improbability of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-024":["If we had no knowledge of the sufficient ground of certainty, we should be wholly unable to pass any judgment on the probability or improbability of a thing. For all these arise from the relation of our grounds of knowledge to certainty. We are, however, always disposed to assent first to that for which we have, and see before us, more numerous and more convincing grounds of truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-025":["The sufficient ground by itself is objective; the insufficient ground, however, is subjective. The former determines in the thing what is to be held of it; the latter, on the other hand, is very doubtful, for it is easily seen that we may have more grounds of truth for a mode of knowledge today and fewer again tomorrow.",[]],"blomberg-s08-026":["The sufficient ground is that whose opposite cannot possibly be thought and represented as true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-027":["It therefore naturally contains in actual fact everything that is to be found in the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-028":["Insufficient grounds, on the other hand, merely enumerate [*enumeriren*] what we think and represent to ourselves of the thing, and know of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-029":["Certainty, then, as we have just said, and with it the sufficient ground, are the standard of measure. All judgments are not probable but [merely] plausible when there is in them no relation of the grounds of knowledge to certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-030":["This plausibility, however, can alter very greatly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-031":["Probability and improbability are objective grounds; plausibility, however, is a subjective ground. That is to say, probability and improbability lie in the object itself, in the thing which is to be known; plausibility, on the other hand, lies in him who has no knowledge of a thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-032":["In a subject the state is the same whether the knowledge which it has is only a persuasion or a certain truth, a conviction, or not. The subject is not thereby altered outwardly in the very least.",[]],"blomberg-s08-033":["It is, however, altogether impossible to specify an infallible, sure and universally valid mark of conviction as distinguished from persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-034":["Since it is impossible to specify properly and surely which grounds of judgment and of persuasion lie in the object and which in the subject, we make here the following important general remark: “all error arises, properly speaking, solely from our confounding [*confundiren*] the causes of assent which lie in the object itself with [445a] the grounds which lie in the subject.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-035":["In order to examine whether something which we accept is in fact a mere plausible and invalid persuasion or an actual and sure conviction, we first of all weigh the grounds of the opposite separately.",[]],"blomberg-s08-036":["If I now find that I meet with grounds for the opposite of my knowledge just as great and just as weighty as those for what I have actually assented to, I readily see from this that my whole knowledge cannot have been in the least a conviction, but must have been merely a persuasion, a *phaenomenon* of conviction.",[]],"blomberg-s08-037":["All our representations whatsoever are *phaenomena* of this kind. In the rainbow, for example, it appears as if it rested upon the earth. It is therefore a mere appearance; on closer examination, namely, we see that this is not so.",[]],"blomberg-s08-038":["Experience teaches us that the strength of the persuasion in regard to a thing can be, and often is, just as great as the conviction of its opposite ever afterwards is.",[]],"blomberg-s08-039":["One means, therefore, of becoming sensible of what is properly needed and required for true conviction is this: to study diligently those sciences which open the surest way to it, that is, which actually convince us of certain truths, for example mathematics. Further, we should apply ourselves carefully to those sciences which confirm the grounds of reason by experience, as, for example, experimental physics [*Experimental-Physica*].",[]],"blomberg-s08-040":["Since, then, we lack a universally valid mark by which to distinguish the subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true from the objective, the *phaenomenon*",[]],"blomberg-s08-041":["of conviction from true certainty and conviction itself, correctly and infallibly, we must be content with endeavouring in particular cases to convince ourselves of the certainty of this or that thing, so that we may at least always be in a position to distinguish the false from the true. But in order to be able carefully to avoid error in this or that mode of knowledge, we must attend well to, and observe, the following:",[]],"blomberg-s08-042":["1mo “We must compare our knowledge of this or that object with the modes of knowledge of other people concerning one and the same object, and carefully hold them against one another. For it is not to be supposed that every man should have one and the same motives for holding one and the same thing to be true. On the contrary, it is most probable that what one man considers from one side, another, who thinks differently and forms different concepts of the whole matter, will here approach from the other side.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-043":["2do “We must assume and suppose [*supponiren*] the opposite of the knowledge which we have, putting ourselves, as it were, in the place of our opponent, who contests the truth of [our] knowledge, and then see whether [the opposite] can be thought as true, or even only as probable. If this is in any degree the case, we have always reason to place some distrust in the truth of the knowledge we have held. But if this is not in the least the case, and if I can impartially discern, and also maintain, that the opposite of the knowledge which I have is absurd, impossible, and would be foolish to accept, it is evident from this that my knowledge must be true and correct.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-044":["etc. etc. etc. There are, however, in respect of certainty three different kinds or degrees of holding-to-be-true:",[]],"blomberg-s08-045":["1.) Knowing [*Wißen*]",[]],"blomberg-s08-046":["2) Opining [*Meynen*]",[]],"blomberg-s08-047":["3 ) Believing [*Glauben*]",[]],"blomberg-s08-048":["1mo To know means to judge something with certainty and hold it to be true. Much is therefore required in order to know something. I can never say: I know that there are inhabitants in the moon; I have not been there, I have not seen them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-049":["2do To opine means to judge something incompletely. It occurs when the grounds we have for holding something to be true are insufficient and do not outweigh the opposite; and that is then an opinion [*Opinion*].",[]],"blomberg-s08-050":["In these opinions, however, the grounds, and thereby also the degrees of holding-to-be-true, can grow and increase. But it is also to be noted that in opining we always remain undecided",[]],"blomberg-s08-051":["In order, however, to opine something practically, there is required",[]],"blomberg-s08-052":["3.) Believing. Here I am always decided, without, however, finding it necessary in myself to be decided.",[]],"blomberg-s08-053":["Opinions are therefore nothing other than subjectively incomplete grounds of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-054":["Man is always opining.",[]],"blomberg-s08-055":["To accept something without a subjective necessity according to logical concepts is entitled believing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-056":["Logical necessity, however, is nothing other than the necessity of holding-to-be-true according to the logical laws of the understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-057":["The necessity, however, of accepting something according to practical laws is always subjective. Believing therefore means nothing other than accepting something of which I am not yet logically certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-058":["Belief is, further, a holding-to-be-true that is practically sufficient.",[]],"blomberg-s08-059":["Certainty, on the other hand, is a holding-to-be-true that is theoretically sufficient as well. [454a]",[]],"blomberg-s08-060":["In belief I am not even disposed to listen to the grounds of my opponent. For my grounds of holding-to-be-true are in that case always practically sufficient. In belief there is also a certain direction of our judgment by the will.",[]],"blomberg-s08-061":["If I accept something, I do not on that account yet hold it to be certain; and likewise, if I concede something, I am still far from holding it on that account to be true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-062":["A practical certainty would be firm belief; and this, both in respect of its conviction and in respect of its power to dispel all doubts, will be as strong as belief according to logical laws can ever be. This belief is often stronger than many an apodeictic certainty of a mode of knowledge. Such certainty, however, is found only in the universal modes of knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s08-063":["a) of reason",[]],"blomberg-s08-064":["B) of experience",[]],"blomberg-s08-065":["We can also believe something without thereby attaining a practical certainty of it, but at most only a greater degree of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-066":["There is also a practical holding-to-be-true, and a certain believing, the abolition of which would at the same time abolish and annihilate all the universal and necessary laws of the practical will, the whole of morals, ethics, theology, religion, etc., and whatever sublime thing can be conceived; and all this is then a firm belief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-067":["We have two such propositions:",[]],"blomberg-s08-068":["1:) There is a God.",[]],"blomberg-s08-069":["2) There is another world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-070":["These two propositions are the sole ground of all religion, morality, etc. These two propositions are the true *conditio sine qua non*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-071":["Belief in these two propositions is not historical but rational, derived from practical rules of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-072":["This belief is not an opinion but a certainty, though of such a kind that, while not actually apodeictic and irrefutable according to the laws of logic, it nonetheless far outweighs even all holding-to-be-true from logical grounds, and retains the advantage over them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-073":["Apodeictic certainty, which comes and arises solely from the understanding, has not, convincing as it otherwise is, the inner power to compel the subject always to adhere to it when what is at stake is the decision of our entire welfare, our fortune and misfortune.",[]],"blomberg-s08-074":["Even mathematical propositions and demonstrations, which otherwise have the very greatest and surest certainty, fare thus. Certain as it is that a circle has 360°, apodeictic as the Platonic theorems in geometry are, no one would wish to die for their infallibility and irrefutability.",[]],"blomberg-s08-075":["Belief according to practical, and especially according to moral, laws has, on the other hand, the full power to strengthen the subject that has it with complete confidence, and to bring it to the point of adhering to that belief always, in spite of all dangers and misfortune, even when the decision of everything that concerns its weal or woe is at stake.",[]],"blomberg-s08-076":["Men have indeed been seen to die for moral propositions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-077":["The resolve, therefore, to let oneself be turned aside by nothing whatsoever is rather a resolve of practical and moral belief than an effect of apodeictic certainty; and it is far more unshakeable, and indeed always carries with it a certain kind of merit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-078":["Knowledge is objectively certain when it contains those grounds according to which all men must agree in respect of it. Knowledge is subjectively certain, however, when it contains the manner in which men actually agree in respect of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-079":["Self-evidence [*Augenscheinlichkeit*] is the intuitive element in certainty, evidence [*Evidenz*]. Thus, for example, mathematical propositions are evident. But we are certain of many things without having any evidence of them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-080":["We have two methods of [testing] the grounds of holding-to-be-true. The concurrence of other men with our opinions, and the testing of our thoughts against the views of other men, is in truth a most excellent logical experiment of our understanding upon the understanding of others. Man stands much in need of this imparting, this communication [*Communication*] of his modes of knowledge, in order to be able to judge them rightly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-081":["Men have a natural inclination to impart to others the judgments which their understanding has passed; and from this alone originates the writing of books, the cause of which other critics of the human race, who like to put the worst construction on everything, place in vanity and ambition.",[]],"blomberg-s08-082":["Men who withdraw from all human society necessarily find in the end, when they begin to examine their condition and the causes of their misanthropy, that they themselves have not means enough to distinguish the true from the false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-083":["Freedom to impart one's thoughts, judgments and knowledge is assuredly the one surest means of rightly testing and verifying [*verificiren*] one's knowledge. Whoever takes away this freedom is to be regarded as the worst enemy of the spread of human knowledge, indeed of men themselves. For he thereby takes from men the one true means which they still possess of ever uncovering, perceiving and correcting the frequent deceptions of their own understanding and its missteps.",[]],"blomberg-s08-084":["To deprive men of the freedom to think unconstrained, and to bring these thoughts to light, is in truth to deprive them of the first rights, of the greatest prerogatives, of the human race, and especially of the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-085":["Men are, as it were, called to use their reason in common and to avail themselves of it together, just as [they are called to share] the temporal goods of this life.",[]],"blomberg-s08-086":["From this it follows quite naturally that anyone who holds the principle [*principium*] of self-conceit, that the judgments of others are wholly dispensable to him for the use of his own reason and for the knowledge of truth, thinks very badly and in a manner worthy of censure.",[]],"blomberg-s08-087":["This, however, is properly logical egoism, which would then of course also not need to impart its own judgments to others. This so-called logical egoism consists therefore in nothing other than the supposed, but frequently false, self-sufficiency of our understanding as existing wholly by itself and, so to speak, isolated, in which one believes that one knows enough by oneself alone and that all one's judgments are infallibly correct and incapable of improvement. It is easily seen that this conceited mode of thought is not only wholly ridiculous, but even in the highest degree contrary to true humanity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-088":["Sensible certainty is carefully to be distinguished by us from the certainty of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s08-089":["Through observations, experiments, and frequent and often repeated experience we attain a certainty of the senses.",[]],"blomberg-s08-090":["The sensible certainty of a mode of knowledge, however, does not properly arise through the actual intuition of objects by means of our senses, nor through the immediate sensible representation of objects, but solely through our making modes of knowledge distinct and convincing by images and examples, and capable of being discerned with less effort.",[]],"blomberg-s08-091":["A mode of knowledge can properly be called rational [*vernünftig*] in two ways, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-092":["1mo “in so far as it can be discerned through reason and also agrees with it; of this kind are all true, well-grounded, demonstrable modes of knowledge,” or",[]],"blomberg-s08-093":["2do “in so far as it arises solely from reason; of this kind are for the most part all logical modes of knowledge, which can therefore also be called, precisely and in the most proper sense, modes of knowledge of reason.”",[]],"blomberg-s08-094":["In regard to the certainty of a mode of knowledge, we may look either to logical certainty, the certainty of reason, or to aesthetic, sensible certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-095":["The latter mode of knowledge is indeed subject to a great many errors; but on the other hand it is also very easy to comprehend.",[]],"blomberg-s08-096":["A mode of knowledge which is perfect according to the rules of coordinate concepts is, in common parlance, entitled complete. It might, however, more properly and more correctly be entitled extended [*ausgebreitet*] knowledge, since the coordinate concepts are all set out together and are thus, so to speak, spread out over the object.",[]],"blomberg-s08-097":["Those grounds to which others are subordinated are not, strictly, *complementa* of the latter towards a sufficient ground; they are rather their origin. In the series of grounds coordinate with one another, on the other hand, one concept is indeed often a *complementum* of another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-098":["In the highest degree of rational certainty I attend to the series of marks subordinate to one another, in order to arrive at the first ground. In this way I seek to discover the intensive clearness of conviction. In the highest degree of sensible certainty, however, I attend always merely to the series of marks coordinate with one another, in order thereby to bring about a certain strict and accurate completeness according to the rules of extensive clearness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-099":["A mode of knowledge, however, can always be certain",[]],"blomberg-s08-100":["1.) mathematically, but also 2 ) philosophically.",[]],"blomberg-s08-101":["What is peculiar to mathematical certainty is this: although, like philosophical certainty, it arises from reason, we nevertheless proceed in it synthetically. That is, in the series of grounds subordinate to one another we ascend by composition from the lowest and simplest mark to the highest.",[]],"blomberg-s08-102":["In philosophical certainty, on the other hand, we likewise pass through the series of concepts subordinate to one another; but this is done not synthetically but analytically, that is, by means of dissection [*Zergliederung*]. We descend from the composite and highest concept to the simplest and lowest.",[]],"blomberg-s08-103":["The mathematical certainty of knowledge is always very distinct and intuitive, so that it can be comprehended very easily. This is because all mathematical concepts are synthetic and arise through arbitrary composition; for we can most easily be conscious of what we have ourselves devised. In philosophy, on the other hand, all concepts are analytic. They are not arbitrary, as in mathematics, where we can assume and lay down something as we please; they are already given in confused form, and are only to be made distinct and easy [473/474] by philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-104":["Since, then, the concepts in philosophy are already given, the philosopher cannot so easily be certain that he has touched upon all the marks which belong to a thing, and that he comprehends them quite completely. On the contrary, there may be many further *notae* belonging to the thing of which he knows nothing whatsoever.",[]],"blomberg-s08-105":["From the mathematician, however, no marks of a thing can escape or slip away; nothing can elude his keen eye. For he has himself arbitrarily, if not devised, at least assumed these marks and arbitrarily ascribed them to the thing; consequently he must surely be completely conscious of them all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-106":["In this paragraph the author speaks of shallow modes of knowledge. In our language and in our expressions we very often borrow analogies.",[]],"blomberg-s08-107":["We make use, that is to say, of certain images, in order by their help to give sensibility to modes of knowledge which are in themselves abstract.",[]],"blomberg-s08-108":["In just the same way we here in logic entitle certain modes of knowledge (as elsewhere [certain] kinds of understanding, modes of exposition, etc. etc.) shallow, *ad analogiam* of water.",[]],"blomberg-s08-109":["Water, as is well known, is entitled shallow according to physical principles when its bottom [*Tiefe*] is already to be met with not far beneath the surface.",[]],"blomberg-s08-110":["A mode of knowledge, however, is shallow *ad analogiam* when it is founded on mere experiences and on no inquiry.",[]],"blomberg-s08-111":["To the shallowness of knowledge is opposed, or *contra distinguiret* [contradistinguished], its thoroughness; this latter is, namely, the *terminus* of certainty",[]],"blomberg-s08-112":["Knowledge is superficial, on the other hand, when one is already content with mere semblance [*Schein*]; that is, when one lets it suffice, and rests, with that knowledge which one has been able to obtain at the mere first glance. In superficial knowledge, therefore, one knows nothing more than what immediately strikes the eye. Thus, for example, one has a superficial knowledge of geography when one can do no more than readily enumerate the names of countries, cities, rivers, etc. Thus one has a superficial knowledge of history when one knows certain striking events in the history of states without regard to their origin.",[]],"blomberg-s08-113":["Through superficial knowledge one sees, so to speak, the straws floating on the surface of the water, but one does not become aware of the pearls which lie on the bottom of the sea.",[]],"blomberg-s08-114":["Superficial modes of knowledge, which are occupied only with the surface of a thing and with what is known of it [*ihren Erkenntnißen*], are most closely akin to shallowness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-115":["Knowledge is thorough, on the other hand, when one goes to the ground [*auf den Grund*] of the matter, that is, when one goes as far as one can possibly go in order to attain complete certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-116":["A mode of knowledge must be deep in order to become thorough. When the series of grounds subordinate to one another is very long before one arrives at complete certainty, the knowledge is profound [*tiefsinnig*].",[]],"blomberg-s08-117":["All modes of knowledge, empirical as well as rational, can become thorough.",[]],"blomberg-s08-118":["Thorough empirical modes of knowledge, however, are not of such a kind that they can become profound; only rational ones can. The reason is that in empirical knowledge there is no series of subordinate grounds, but only of coordinate ones.",[]],"blomberg-s08-119":["Empirical modes of knowledge, however, are thorough when they contain as many coordinate concepts as are necessary for distinctness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-120":["One has a thorough historical knowledge, for example, when one goes back to the *autores* who first wrote of a matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-121":["A mode of knowledge can be thorough even though it is very limited in respect of extent and breadth. Thorough modes of knowledge, however, must always be preferred to extended and extensive knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-122":["For extensiveness without thoroughness is nothing but an empty shadow; it resembles a nought or zero, a figure in which there is nothing real.",[]],"blomberg-s08-123":["Here the author speaks of three different kinds of action which we must undertake in order to be certain of a mode of knowledge or to reject it. These three actions are nothing other than our different attitudes in respect of the assent which we believe we either owe or do not owe to modes of knowledge, namely:",[]],"blomberg-s08-124":["1: *mo* the giving of assent",[]],"blomberg-s08-125":["2 *do* the withdrawal of assent, and",[]],"blomberg-s08-126":["3 *tio* the withholding of assent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-127":["The first is a positive action, the second a privative one, while the third is no action at all. In the first case, namely, I take something to be true",[]],"blomberg-s08-128":["In the second case I take something to be false. In the third case, however, I do not judge at all, but suspend my *judicium*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-129":["In the case of any mode of knowledge whatsoever we can think either a",[]],"blomberg-s08-130":["plus or a minus, or a",[]],"blomberg-s08-131":["zero.",[]],"blomberg-s08-132":["Affirmative judgments increase our knowledge, and are therefore a plus",[]],"blomberg-s08-133":["Negative judgments do not increase our knowledge, and are therefore a minus. For they seek only to diminish what is false in our knowledge, and to put us in a position to accept nothing but what is true; in short, they serve only to avoid error",[]],"blomberg-s08-134":["When, finally, in the case of any mode of knowledge we neither affirm nor deny anything, this is the state of zero.",[]],"blomberg-s08-135":["Just as, in respect of our outward humour, we are in the state of plus in pleasure, in the state of minus in sadness, but in the state of zero in complete indifference and insensibility.",[]],"blomberg-s08-136":["The withdrawal of assent, it is true, brings no increase to our knowledge, but it nevertheless keeps us from error.",[]],"blomberg-s08-137":["When, however, we give our assent to a mode of knowledge, our knowing [*Wißen*] receives an *accrementum* [increment]",[]],"blomberg-s08-138":["The withholding of assent, on the other hand, brings about ignorance, although in many cases it can be quite useful. Here, however, we must of necessity further discuss the following question: whether our free choice [*freye Willkühr*] has any influence on our giving our assent to a mode of knowledge, or withdrawing it, or even withholding it?",[]],"blomberg-s08-139":["Answer: In most cases this procedure of giving our assent, or withdrawing it, or withholding it, does not rest on our free choice at all, but is rather, properly speaking, necessitated through and by the laws of our understanding and of our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-140":["So it is, for example, in *mathematica*. A merchant who sees clearly from his accounts that he owes a great deal, more than he has in prospect or in possession, will certainly not be able to withdraw his assent and *consensus* from this knowledge, which is so evident, however gladly he might wish to. Granted that he does not *positive* think much of it, he will nevertheless not be able *negative* to regard the sum of his debt as capital still in hand and existing. For he is all too plainly and evidently convinced of the correctness of arithmetic in this matter, and the reckoning of debts carries all too great an evidence with it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-141":["This much is certain: if we were able, and it pleased us, to give assent, now to accept, now to reject, something at our arbitrary pleasure, we should certainly thereby be put in a position greatly to promote, produce and increase our pleasure, our comfort, indeed our very happiness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-142":["On the other hand, we should in the same degree be able always to remove at pleasure whatever might bring us sadness and cause us vexation. In such arbitrary modes of knowledge, however, there would then either have to be no sufficient grounds for asserting their opposite, or, granted that there were such, we should have to have no need whatever to reflect upon them. In this way the debtor could easily take himself for a *creditor*, the man who has fallen from favour for a favourite of his prince, etc., and he who receives the saddest tidings for a happy man; or he could at least remain *in suspenso*. (Although, regarded from another side, hovering between fear and hope is far more burdensome and anxious, indeed more unbearable, than being conscious of one's misfortune.) And then, even if a complete ruin of his station, honour, etc., and of his entire fortune [*Vermögen*] were inevitably impending within a short time, he would still be able, with a contented soul and an untroubled [or moved, *gerührtem*] mind, to sleep well and peacefully, and also to eat and drink well.",[]],"blomberg-s08-143":["This free *arbitrium* in respect of assent, however, might perhaps belong among the felicities of the ancient poets. It falls away entirely; and in the presence of certain degrees of grounds it is always very difficult, indeed altogether impossible, to withhold one's assent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-144":["In very many cases, therefore, one can not only count upon, but even insist upon, the assent which someone must give to another; that is to say, one can extort and compel it from him.",[]],"blomberg-s08-145":["The mathematician, whose propositions are adorned with sufficient grounds and are proved, can always speak imperiously; he can rightly demand my assent to his propositions. And whoever, notwithstanding this, should still wish to remain *in suspenso*, and to go on doubting whether he ought to accept these propositions, etc., or on the other hand to reject them, would always make himself ridiculous, and would show that he does not even know what mathematics and its properties are.",[]],"blomberg-s08-146":["Notwithstanding this, although assent does not depend *immediate*, immediately, upon the choice [*Willkühr*] of men, it nevertheless often does depend upon it *indirecte*, mediately, *mediate*. For one seeks out at one's own free pleasure those grounds which might in some way or other procure assent for this or that mode of knowledge which cannot insist upon the *consensus aliorum debitus* [the assent owed by others].",[]],"blomberg-s08-147":["When assent does not arise immediately from the nature of the human understanding and of human reason, it still always requires a further direction of choice, of pleasure, or in general of our free will, upon the grounds of proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-148":["It is certainly true that in the human soul there takes place an arbitrary direction of the powers of the mind [*Gemüths-Kräfte*] upon what we like to see and wish for.",[]],"blomberg-s08-149":["We immediately give our assent to a mode of knowledge which is very pleasing and agreeable to us, without even taking the trouble to examine more closely the proper and true grounds of holding-to-be-true [*Vorwahrhaltens*].",[]],"blomberg-s08-150":["When something is very considerable for us and of very great importance, so that a great, indeed the greatest, part of our peace of mind and of our outward well-being and happiness depends upon it, the mind is already not free enough to consider the matter indifferently and impartially from both sides, the grounds on one side and the grounds of the opposite on the other",[]],"blomberg-s08-151":["side, to weigh them, to set the importance of all these two kinds of grounds duly against one another, and to pronounce the right verdict of preference. Rather the mind, and indeed often even our understanding, are as it were fettered and restricted, so that we readily approve at once, and give our assent to, what is advantageous to us, and on the other hand soon reject and disapprove what might turn to our harm or vexation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-152":["Finally, in the *suspensio judicii*, that is, in the withholding of our assent, one properly performs, as we have already remarked above, no action whatsoever.",[]],"blomberg-s08-153":["A judgment is dogmatic when one either actually accepts or affirms a mode of knowledge that is put before one, or on the other hand rejects and denies it. When, on the contrary, one does not pass one's judgment but withholds it, and so neither affirms nor denies anything, this judgment is sceptical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-154":["The sceptical [attitude] in judging, in which one makes no decision, settles nothing, but leaves everything unsettled, and harbours doubt about everything of which one can assert nothing with certainty, until [496/7] one has proper grounds of proof either to accept or to reject a mode of knowledge. This cautious and circumspect procedure in judging, in which one takes great care not to affirm outright anything of which one is not quite reliably convinced, and to deny nothing whose impossibility or non-being one does not quite distinctly see, is really the most appropriate and the most advantageous to distinct truth, provided only that one does not hold the constant resolve never to decide [*unterscheiden*] anything, to doubt everything for ever, and to leave everything undecided. Hence it is that a true philosopher is much inclined not to squander his assent needlessly on every occasion, but always to withhold it until he has adequate and fully sufficient grounds of the truth. But as soon as he does have sufficient grounds of the truth of a mode of knowledge, and himself sees its validity, he asks no further question; rather it is his custom forthwith to accede to the knowledge, and to recognise it as true, indeed to assert it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-155":["The insatiable desire, prevailing in many a so-called philosopher or dogmatist, to decide upon each and every mode of knowledge at once, at first glance and without the least inquiry, that is, either to accept or to reject it, has led many astray, and is always the surest road to endless errors.",[]],"blomberg-s08-156":["One of the chief causes, however, which very often mislead men into passing a false judgment, or into error, are, further, the *affecten* [affects].",[]],"blomberg-s08-157":["These perverters of men, which cause so much disorder in common life, are no less able to confuse the understanding of men and lead it into errors. These affects of men turn the grounds of certainty about a thing merely to one side, and do not allow a man to consider its other side as well. Hence we may indeed blame someone who has given assent to a false mode of knowledge, namely when the fault really lies in himself, in that he rejects those grounds which could have convinced him about the object of the knowledge he has, and so freed him from his error.",[]],"blomberg-s08-158":["Mathematics and pure and immediate experience alone are of such a kind as to leave us no grounds for the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-159":["All other modes of knowledge whatsoever, on the other hand, are of such a kind that they very often present us with just as great and weighty grounds of proof for asserting the opposite of a thing as they offer us proofs for accepting it. It is therefore certainly true prudence to know how, in most cases, to withhold one's assent until one has sufficient grounds for the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-160":["Deception is almost universal in all things; hence this is in fact a really safe, indeed in many cases infallible, way of avoiding many illusions [*Blendwercke*] of reason and of keeping free from error. Nevertheless this withholding of assent must certainly not be so constituted that one is at the same time inclined never to let oneself be convinced of the thing, and always to refuse it one's assent; for in this way we should never settle anything, and should thus remain ignorant in all things.",[]],"blomberg-s08-161":["The withholding of assent, namely with the aforesaid inclination never to settle anything, is really nothing other than a lazy doubt, a lazy addiction to doubt, or at least the road to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-162":["The *suspensio* is meant to serve only to accept nothing until one has sufficient grounds, but not on that account to reject at once, entirely, each and every hope of ever being able to attain complete certainty of a thing or of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-163":["Reflecting [*Überlegen*] is distinct from investigating [*Untersuchen*] and investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-164":["To reflect is to compare something with the laws of the understanding. To investigate, however, properly means to reflect mediately. Of many things we can well know without investigation what is true and what is false. Reflection, on the other hand, is always necessary for every judgment, and is indispensable in all individual cases for distinguishing the true from the false, whether in general or in a mode of knowledge, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s08-165":["The word *praejudicium* has a double or twofold meaning, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-166":["1 *mo* it means a provisional judgment, which, however, is not yet a real prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-167":["2 *do* it means a real prejudice. For example, when one already esteems the writings of a man far more highly than the writings of another before one has even seen them, this is not yet a prejudice proper but only a provisional judgment, in which reason infers something from certain grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-168":["Here, therefore, it properly decides nothing as yet, but merely judges from the few grounds which it has of the matter; in accordance with these grounds it thinks and represents something to itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-169":["This judgment is therefore by no means dogmatic, but is merely always still bound up with a certain deferment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-170":["This provisional judgment, namely, is no ground for already holding something to be certain, not in the least. Meanwhile this much is certain, that in the present state there are more grounds for accepting the thing than for rejecting it and asserting its opposite. Hence one readily praises a book said to come from a learned, famous and well-known man even before one has read it, merely because one imagines that a quite famous man must write nothing but things worthy of his fame, and that this book too will therefore likewise be well written.",[]],"blomberg-s08-171":["There has never been an inventor in the world, nor has there been anyone who invented something, who did not at the same time have to pass a provisional judgment about his invention and the thing invented. He was not certain of the thing; but the judgment opened for him the way to try and to experiment. For example, miners judge from the strata in the earth where they are to find metals, and keep conjecturing until they have obtained certain knowledge of the mineral veins [*Metallengruben*], which no longer deceives them and in accordance with which they attain their end.",[]],"blomberg-s08-172":["Modes of knowledge, however, arise in us either through knowledge of the object, or through the properties which belong to the subject; these are subjective grounds of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-173":["A subjective ground of holding-to-be-true prior to all reflection, as the action of the understanding necessary for that purpose and exceedingly useful, can be entitled prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-174":["Inclination is a disposition of the mind to love something and to be taken with it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-175":["Inclinations are very seductive; to overcome them steadfastly is certainly the greatest art, indeed almost impossible. And so it is in logic too, both with the *argumenta ab utili* and with the *argumenta ab odio*. This is particularly the case when this or that matter becomes entangled in an interest of men, whether near or remote, general or particular, and has a special share in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-176":["The prejudices which arise from inclination are of many kinds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-177":["A further subjective ground of holding-to-be-true is imitation, to which man is accordingly so very much inclined by nature; and the judgments arising from it extend over almost the whole life of men.",[]],"blomberg-s08-178":["The spirit of imitation is always really the worst of all that can harm and oppose the philosophical spirit. Imitation is the forming of one's understanding, one's will, indeed one's choice, after the pattern and example of others. When one is not oneself capable of thinking, one takes refuge in them and copies them quite faithfully, as the painter copies the original, except that this copy often turns out very unfitting, indeed comical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-179":["One thing, then, is very often opposed to that spirit of holding modes of knowledge to be true which will not err: custom, which very often conflicts with the understanding and, once rooted, is hard to eradicate.",[]],"blomberg-s08-180":["Those people, however, are very precipitate who blindly confuse a provisional judgment with a decisive judgment. A certain *suspensio judicii* is therefore very necessary and useful here, so that one may not be precipitate. This, namely, teaches us merely how we are to regard grounds which are specious and far from sufficient, and which we are far from holding to be very convincing grounds; and consequently in many cases it keeps us from many wrong ways and errors in knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-181":["That so dangerous enemy of men, of whom we have already spoken above, is one of the greatest causes of overheating. A calm man, who is not thrown into disorder, confused, heated, etc. by any violent emotion, will not be precipitate so quickly and so easily as one who is in the grip of an affect and is irresistibly carried away by it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-182":["Reflection [*Nachdencken*], however, is an important, very great and sure way, if not of wholly extinguishing the affects, yet of calming them, of preventing their dangerous consequences, and so of avoiding errors. For in it one allows and takes oneself time to investigate and to inquire carefully whether something is true or false, and whether one can accept its opposite or not. And by this way, calmly reflecting upon the matter, one is very soon convinced of the truth of a thing or of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-183":["Philosophy likewise strives greatly to subdue and eradicate the affects so far as possible. Hence learned men and philosophers can really keep their affects well in check, so that they do not readily let themselves be taken with a thing without first having sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-184":["They rather weigh everything which they choose as the *objectum* of their considerations with cool blood, that is, with a calm mind. Further, in every matter they carefully consider both the one side and the other, and in this way arrive best, most easily and most surely at the right knowledge of the truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-185":["Through provisional judgments we seek to help ourselves towards a closer conviction; but we judge before we have more closely considered and come to know the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-186":["Truth is not met with unless it is sought; but even before seeking it [*selbst ohne sie zu suchen*], one must already pass judgments about the way by which one expects to find it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-187":["We never arrive at complete certainty except by means of investigation; but a provisional judgment must still precede all investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-188":["Between a provisional judgment and a prejudice there seems to be, and really to be, only a small difference, since both are judgments of the kind which always precede the investigation of a thing or of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-189":["The provisional judgment, however, as a means to investigation, combined with the consciousness and the intention of thereby finding the ways to investigation, not only befits our understanding in the highest degree, but is also a sure, indeed an infallible, way of arriving at the truth of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-190":["Without it, indeed, no important invention, no invention of consequence, has ever been made.",[]],"blomberg-s08-191":["But when, before investigation, one judges and accepts something with the intention of undertaking no further investigation of the whole matter, but of resting content with it altogether, this is a prejudice that is in fact culpable, and that is not only not conducive to the knowledge of truth, but is even a hindrance and harmful to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-192":["The former judgment, namely a cautious, provisional judgment made with the intention of investigating the knowledge more closely in order to arrive at the truth, is therefore really useful to truth, and afterwards also directs the investigation which one then actually undertakes into the knowledge. What is essential to all prejudices, however, is that they are general rules for judging.",[]],"blomberg-s08-193":["It is not a prejudice when one accepts a proposition without lengthy investigation; rather, one accepts this proposition through a prejudice. Hence we have the two states and particular characteristics of the human understanding:",[]],"blomberg-s08-194":["1 *mo* to have a prejudice, or to be occupied with one, and then",[]],"blomberg-s08-195":["2 *do* not to have a prejudice, but to accept something through it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-196":["Almost everyone, be he who he may, regards the fashions and customs of his country or fatherland as the best and most fitting. With us, for example, we uncover our heads, but the Turks do not; we therefore regard them as rude, although it is not yet settled whether it is rudeness to cover the head or to uncover it. The Turks perhaps in turn regard us as impolite. This, however, is really not itself a prejudice, but only something which is accepted through a prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-197":["No prejudices are founded on reflection. Reflection is lacking in them; for otherwise they would be real judgments of the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-198":["Reflection, of which we have already spoken above, is nothing other than the comparison of a mode of knowledge with the laws of the understanding and of reason. We are, however, directed not merely by the laws of the understanding and of reason, but also very often by the inclinations, or by our taste. In the inclinations there is no reflection whatsoever.",[]],"blomberg-s08-199":["Prejudices, however, have very many and various sources.",[]],"blomberg-s08-200":["Custom is likewise one of the causes and grounds of judging without reflection. Without having any inclination towards something, one nevertheless very often takes it to be true from mere custom. Hence, if one wishes to purge someone of his errors, one must not only convince him of the opposite of what he believes, but also accustom him to it slowly and gradually in the course of time, and allow him time to accept the truth. Even with the most convincing, evident and certain proofs, he who has long hovered in error and been possessed by doubt cannot be brought at once and all at once to the point of being so convinced, and so thoroughly overcome by the irrefutability of the proofs, that he immediately abandons all doubt and resolves and is disposed to accede to the truth forthwith and without hesitation. On the contrary, a long time is very often required for this.",[]],"blomberg-s08-201":["There are, however, the following 3 sources of prejudices, which are the commonest and the greatest",[]],"blomberg-s08-202":["1) Imitation",[]],"blomberg-s08-203":["2) Custom",[]],"blomberg-s08-204":["3) Inclination",[]],"blomberg-s08-205":["In youth one has as yet no facility in judging; hence one easily lets oneself be carried away by imitation, and soon accepts as certain and undoubted what is asserted by others in whom we place particular trust or who stand in particular esteem with us, or else what the general *votum* of many prescribes to us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-206":["If to this imitation there is further added the custom of judging thus and not otherwise, this prejudice becomes all the stronger and gains ever more ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-207":["A child, for example, certainly always accepts the first impressions made on its mind, for the most part very easily and as the greatest and most important truths; indeed it also retains them the longest of all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-208":["According to the given rules of the laws of the imagination, such prejudices are indeed not easily removed from the soul once they have taken root in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-209":["Here, then, there is properly as yet no reflection at all; one simply accepts what others before us have asserted, without oneself reflecting whether it is reasonable to accept it or not.",[]],"blomberg-s08-210":["Hence it is that, when false concepts, images, representations, etc. have been instilled into children in their tender youth, these can afterwards in no way be eradicated from their imagination [*Imagination, oder Einbildungskraft*]. There is therefore great reason to proceed very cautiously here, and to take care not to corrupt the understanding of children prematurely and in a certain way render it unserviceable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-211":["Custom, further, is as it were a very fertile source of many pernicious prejudices. When one has often performed an action, has, so to speak, fared well by it, and has thus once become accustomed to it, there finally arises from this [526a] in the course of time a certain kind of necessity of acting so and of not proceeding otherwise.",[]],"blomberg-s08-212":["To dress fashionably (yet in accordance with one's station) is praiseworthy and good; for it is always more tolerable, and better, to be a fool within the fashion than one outside it. But to judge, infer, think and write, in turn, after the fashion is always foolish, and a proof of no reflection at all. Ceremonies, modes of dress and compliments may well be brought under a fashion, but not the understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-213":["Further, inclination too is a cause and ground of many judgments; and in this case one judges and infers that something is good, agreeable and perfect because it attracts us, because it is pleasant to us and suited to our taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-214":["This inclination has the further effect that we always conduct our examinations and inquiries from one side only, namely from the side on which we wish that things should be so and not otherwise. Meanwhile we leave the other side, which might perhaps furnish us with grounds for the opposite, entirely unexamined. We even seek, [it may be,] to find grounds by whose help we can establish the opposite of what we wish. Here too the laws of understanding and of reason are, as it were, absent and not at home. There is no reflection of our own at all; only certain sensations are present.",[]],"blomberg-s08-215":["All prejudices arise from imitation, habit, or inclination. Without these three sources there would never be any prejudices in the world, prejudices which are yet so very often a hindrance to truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-216":["Whatever is immediately certain requires no investigation at all. All modes of knowledge must be reflected upon, that is, they must be compared with the laws of understanding and of reason and held up against one another. But not each and every mode of knowledge may be investigated; for to investigate is nothing other than to compare something mediately with the laws of understanding and of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-217":["All modes of knowledge whose agreement with the laws of our understanding and of reason is immediate are indemonstrable. They can be accepted without investigation; they need not be proved; indeed, one cannot even undertake an investigation of them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-218":["In the strict sense one is not in a position to do so. A prejudice, however, is nothing other than a certain general ground for judging without any reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-219":["But a mode of knowledge which is accepted merely by means of a prejudice is not yet itself a prejudice at all. If we are to speak strictly, we must understand that there are in fact",[]],"blomberg-s08-220":["really only a few prejudices, but on the other hand infinitely many errors arising from these existing prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-221":["A prejudice always arises when one judges without reflection, that is, when one already judges about a thing or about the knowledge of it without having first compared this knowledge with the laws of understanding or of reason. Prejudices, therefore, cannot properly lie in the defects or faults of our understanding and our reason; they must arise from other powers.",[]],"blomberg-s08-222":["Understanding and reason themselves, left to themselves, never err; so much is undoubtedly established and certain. For this would be an obvious *contradictio in adjecto*, as we have already remarked above: they would then have to conflict with their own powers, and no power can conflict with itself. Error comes solely from this, that other, foreign powers mingle with the powers and laws of our understanding and reason. From this there naturally arises a mixed effect, and from that in turn an error.",[]],"blomberg-s08-223":["In the first place, prejudice cannot possibly arise from the laws of the understanding and [cannot] correct itself [by them], since it is a judgment without reflection. But what is accepted by means of a prejudice is not on that account always false in its matter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-224":["Further, prejudices are not altogether necessary sources of errors. But this much is certain, that each and every mode of knowledge which is accepted through a prejudice is always in itself false as regards its form.",[]],"blomberg-s08-225":["Through a mode of knowledge a certain intended end may often be very well attained, while the method of attaining this end may very often not be the genuine and right one. And so it very often goes with the actions of men also.",[]],"blomberg-s08-226":["Any man can, if he only wills it, very well take his revenge on one who has offended him or treated him with excess, that is, on his enemy, and make him pay for the wrong done. But the manner of taking revenge, the form of avenging oneself, is wrong and impermissible: one ought not to be one's own judge. The person on whom one takes revenge cannot complain in the least; no wrong whatever has been done to him; he has merely been paid back in the same coin.",[]],"blomberg-s08-227":["But this is not at all the proper manner of avenging oneself on anyone. It would give rise only to so great a disorder that in the end it would become almost incurable. Rather, divine and human laws prescribe to us the lawful ways by which we may obtain reparation for our loss, our honour, etc., without having to be ashamed of the manner in which we have obtained it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-228":["It is just so with prejudice. In respect of the object, the judgment and the knowledge may very often be quite true, but not in respect of the form.",[]],"blomberg-s08-229":["One must therefore take great care not to reject each and every prejudice straightway. One must first test them and investigate them well, to see whether something good may not still be found in them. Indeed, one can actually meet with a kind of prejudice against prejudices themselves, namely when one straightway rejects everything that has arisen through prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-230":["In this way one can very often reject the greatest and most important truths, and thereby, though innocently, deprive of them those who are not capable of investigating and inquiring into them, and are not equal to it. For example, if one accepts what all men say and always holds it to be wholly certain, this is a prejudice. Yet it is in fact so constituted that very many truths rest merely on what men say, and have, or can have, no other ground; and these nevertheless prove on closer investigation to be really assured truths.",[]],"blomberg-s08-231":["Were one to reject all these truths straightway, many men would thereby be deprived of quite good modes of knowledge, men who do not possess sufficient capacity, or enough strength of mind, to investigate everything according to the laws of understanding and of reason and to test it with due reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-232":["There are some men so constituted that their mind is hostile to what is true, and in whom, therefore, such prejudices take root first and most easily: men who seek to cast suspicion on every truth that others assure us of and firmly and earnestly maintain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-233":["Beyond all doubt very many useless prejudices are to be met with. But whether our procedure is fair and lawful when, merely because a prejudice may be useful, beneficial, or at least not harmful to another in a certain case, we not only do not suppress it in him but even generate, produce, preserve and increase it, is another and quite different question. To generate and maintain prejudices in other cases with the intention [of profiting by the fact] that they are useful in this or that case is really a kind of deceit, and a breach of faith and honesty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-234":["A prejudice considered in itself may indeed produce some benefit. Notwithstanding, the methods which one employs in order to procure and bring about benefit by means of it are not right and not permissible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-235":["Nevertheless it also remains true that it would be manifest presumption to wish, by a deceitful stratagem, straightway to deprive someone of a prejudice which is either not altogether harmful to him or even in a certain respect useful and beneficial, and so to throw him into unrest and confusion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-236":["But to strengthen a prejudice in someone is equally reprehensible. Such is the case, for example, when one proceeds irresponsibly in moral instruction, namely when, in order to keep a man from one vice or another, one makes use of what is often a superstitious delusion to restrain and deter him. This is the so-called *fraus pia* [pious fraud].",[]],"blomberg-s08-237":["But everything true that has been produced by means of one prejudice or another is *formaliter* [formally] false, although it can be, and is, *materialiter* [materially] correct. How wretched is the education which seeks to ground true moral rules in young men through prejudices and errors!",[]],"blomberg-s08-238":["Many prejudices are so constituted that, if one ventured to attack them, and sought to root them out and remove them, they would only make the man's mind the more embittered; indeed they would deafen it to hearing, and to attending to, true teachings and important *dogmata* which have great consequences for men.",[]],"blomberg-s08-239":["There are indeed very comfortable prejudices, with which a man fares very well; and it would therefore be great folly to tear from another such prejudices, which could contribute, and often actually do contribute, so very much to his well-being.",[]],"blomberg-s08-240":["It is otherwise with harmful prejudices, which can often affect not merely a single case or man but even the welfare of a whole commonwealth. These one may and must certainly always endeavour, as far as possible and with the greatest diligence, to root out, since one thereby serves and benefits not only him who holds the prejudice but also others, indeed a great multitude of people.",[]],"blomberg-s08-241":["We must therefore not despise other men if they happen to be very full of prejudices, whether through their own fault or not.",[]],"blomberg-s08-242":["The common man has and harbours very many prejudices, to which he often clings very obstinately. But it is equally certain that the scholar, even the greatest, always has just as many of them, and far more dangerous ones. He sometimes sets as much store by his canons as anyone else, and never departs from them, just as the common man [does not depart] from his old proverbs, inherited from his forefathers and confirmed by long experience. The only difference is that in the scholar the prejudices are concealed under the name of *canones*, in the common man under the name of proverbs.",[]],"blomberg-s08-243":["The author here distinguishes prejudices from one another. He entitles some prejudices logical, as though he meant thereby to indicate that there are still other kinds of prejudice which are not logical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-244":["Otherwise the aesthetic is commonly *contradistinguished* from the logical, and prejudices will therefore be, and can be, divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-245":["1.) into logical, and",[]],"blomberg-s08-246":["2.) into aesthetic prejudices.",[]],"blomberg-s08-247":["The former consist in, and occur when, our modes of knowledge [are contrary] to the rules of understanding and of reason; the latter, on the other hand, consist in our modes of knowledge being contrary to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-248":["Fashion is properly nothing other than a prejudice of taste, according to which one holds something to be beautiful or ugly through a prejudice. Fashion is thus the source of aesthetic prejudices. Fashions make one imagine that some inner beauty lies in this or that kind of clothing or dress; one thus thinks, and also judges, [as though] according to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-249":["Imitation and habit are the greatest sources of aesthetic prejudices. Frequent approval of one object or another produces in the soul, as it were, an archetype, with which one is quite unable to compare others that look different from this original, once fixed and [taken] as a model incapable of improvement.",[]],"blomberg-s08-250":["If each were to dress according to his own liking and taste, we should in the end have the most excellent kinds of clothing. But at present right taste in clothing is really lacking, and this because fashion rules in it, which everyone follows constantly and irresistibly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-251":["In general all fashion is harmful rather than useful, for it is contrary both to the rules of pure reason and even to the rules of taste. And he who is, or wishes to be, fashionable in his judgments of taste, or even of reason, certainly has no reason at all. He thereby shows his very poor talents and his complete want of all taste. Yet it is far more tolerable to be fashionable in taste than to be fashionable in thinking, in judging, and in *sentiments*; to be fashionable in clothes is tolerable and often good. But to wish to be fashionable in *sentiments* is the real logical prejudice. The prejudice of taste, however, rules not only in clothing but even in style.",[]],"blomberg-s08-252":["Almost every century has a style, or a form of its own",[]],"blomberg-s08-253":["At one time a great pomp of inflated words rules in writing; at another, laboured contortions of wit; at another again, a quite shallow manner of writing, which people are pleased to call natural because it is easy, costs little or no trouble, and can be produced without art. At another time again, people favour in all things the manner of writing they had been accustomed to use in letters. At yet another, they favour above all a difficult, obscure, riddling and enigmatic manner of writing, preferring it to the easy, complete and distinct one. It is supposed to betray a certain profundity and deep-thinking learning in the authors, because one must guess for a long time over it, and has, as it were, to solve a riddle, before one succeeds in guessing what the author meant, in discovering his proper sense and meaning, and in finding what he had in mind.",[]],"blomberg-s08-254":["All taste is greatly corrupted by imitation, [which is] a fertile source of all prejudices: one borrows everything, and thinks nothing beautiful that one might oneself invent or hit upon, but only what others have already devised and recognised before, and have declared to be a beauty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-255":["If, therefore, everyone were to endeavour not always to imitate so much, but rather to be an original himself, we should certainly soon behold the greatest geniuses, who would be sublime and great in judgments of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-256":["Likewise there are, again, various kinds of prejudice in the various fine arts and sciences, as for example in music, poetry, painting, sculpture, etc. Everywhere, in every taste, prejudices always rule, even in moral taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-257":["Habit and examples bring this about, and are therefore the causes why so many prejudices, so many fashions, rule in them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-258":["It has been remarked that the English nation has far more, and greater, originals than the French and other nations. This really comes from the freer and less constrained form of government. For there each man is almost his own master, at least [master] of his own genius. He therefore naturally thinks, judges and acts for the most part without being in any way moved by examples, or at least without conforming to them, following them, or imitating them. Each man there thus thinks and judges according to his own taste, and hence there are so few copies there.",[]],"blomberg-s08-259":["On the other hand, countries and states in which a monarchical form of government has been introduced and rules are naturally not in a position to produce such originals, or at any rate not so many for long. For here people commonly conform closely to examples, which often become the norm in thinking, acting and judging. And usually it is the magnates of the realm, the great men of the realm and of the court, who are for the most part the originals, the models, after which people seek to form themselves and which they try to imitate.",[]],"blomberg-s08-260":["The author further divides prejudices into prejudices 1.) of excessive trust, and 2.) of excessive mistrust. But both kinds of prejudice are in fact one and the same, and are inseparably bound up and united with one another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-261":["The cause of the prejudices of excessive trust is properly nothing other than the desire to extend one's knowledge, but without acuteness and true judgment of that with which one wishes to enrich it. Careful observation teaches that this occurs for the most part especially in youth. Age, on the other hand, which has more experience and knowledge than youth, and has also already suffered more delusions, often reflects more, and considers whether it is worth while and repays the trouble to strive for this or that knowledge, and whether it is correct or not. Through this thinking back and forth, and frequently repeated doubt, it often falls at last into the prejudice of excessive mistrust. And for the most part it turns out thus: the older we gradually grow, the more sceptical and mistrustful we become.",[]],"blomberg-s08-262":["In the prejudice of excessive mistrust, however, we give certain persons a certain precedence over ourselves; we must therefore ascribe to them more prudence, more learning, etc., than to ourselves, and so choose them as originals after which we endeavour to form ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-263":["People of rank are for the most part such that one is prepossessed by very many prejudices towards them and in respect of their abilities and qualities. One always holds them to be prudent, learned, sublime, noble-minded, etc. Even clothing often makes a man learned and prudent; a person in gold lace even arouses respect, and seems at once to possess more understanding than others, although very often he nevertheless sits in the deepest shadow of darkness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-264":["What a man of rank says is often attended to far sooner than what a common or poor man puts forward, though the latter is very often more prudent than the former. There is, further, a prejudice of the excessive multitude, in which one assumes and persuades oneself that many persons are more and better able to judge something than few. This is the common and lowest prejudice of authority.",[]],"blomberg-s08-265":["Hence many a man takes fright when he is for the first time to deliver a public speech, discourse, etc., before a large assembly. Speaking before 2 or 3 persons would perhaps not frighten him, but before some thousands one is afraid. For one judges that what meets with the approval of all is true and must really lie in the *objectum* itself, although it is often quite false.",[]],"blomberg-s08-266":["The prejudice of the excessive multitude arises for the most part from the prejudice of imitation. A great number of people, all attending to me alone and observing me alone with care, must necessarily have something venerable for me.",[]],"blomberg-s08-267":["In respect of morality, however, the prejudice of the multitude becomes very harmful, detrimental, indeed shameful. Hence it comes about that man, even in his actions which bear on morals, so readily follows the great crowd. He hopes, as it were, when he does something evil, to hide himself in it, and so to escape unseen the eye of the eternal heavenly Judge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-268":["A uniform judgment can easily be produced in a great multitude; none has the spirit of imitation so much as a great multitude.",[]],"blomberg-s08-269":["If something is so constituted that it receives the general approval of the people all at once, as soon as it appears and at first sight, it is always very often to be feared that it is not worth much. And a general and universal approval received all at once must really not only not gladden a rational man, but greatly humble him. Books which, as soon as they have come to light from the press, [are applauded] are certainly for the most part very shallow.",[]],"blomberg-s08-270":["What is good and in fact excellent, what brings forth correct but new truths, commonly meets at first with very great opposition. This lasts until, after closer testing and investigation, and after inquiry into and discovery of its proper qualities and of its difference from other modes of knowledge, one also discovers its beauties and what is especially excellent in it as compared with other writings, and begins to esteem them highly.",[]],"blomberg-s08-271":["All prejudice of excessive authority may fittingly be divided into three kinds. – – –",[]],"blomberg-s08-272":["namely, 1 *mo*) the judgment of the excessive authority which others have with us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-273":["2 *do*) the prejudice of the excessive authority which we place in ourselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-274":["3.) the prejudice of the excessive authority which a mode of knowledge has with us on account of certain perfections that it carries with it, from which one infers that still further perfections must be found in it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-275":["As regards the first kind of prejudice, we are for the most part inclined to ascribe more to others, and to credit them with more, than ourselves. We believe that another knows better something that we do not know. This prejudice arises not merely from the consciousness of our own weakness, for that is no ground for attributing more to others than to ourselves, but from a certain respect which we have for the whole human race, in that we judge that what is lacking in us will yet be found in others.",[]],"blomberg-s08-276":["Another prejudice arises from a certain laziness.",[]],"blomberg-s08-277":["Nothing is more convenient than to imitate and to draw one's knowledge from others. From this prejudice arise the various and manifold citations in books, in which one adduces the judgments of others and their opinions on this or that matter, and presents these *citationes* as proofs.",[]],"blomberg-s08-278":["There is indeed a certain kind of toil in this, a diligence, but no difficulty. One must work long, though without any particular exertion of one's powers. This prejudice of the excessive authority which others have with us thus greatly flatters our ease. One need not think for oneself at all, but can rely entirely on the genius, the understanding, of another, and merely imitate others.",[]],"blomberg-s08-279":["Yet another kind of prejudice of authority towards others arises from vanity. One thereby properly, and as it were really, subjects oneself to others; one makes oneself the slave of a great man and of another's understanding and power of judgment. But this often happens only so that one may command a great deal over others, and have great dominion over others who are still lower than we.",[]],"blomberg-s08-280":["Nowhere, however, is there greater bombast, nowhere greater pride, than in polyhistory. In it, out of ambition and vanity, one exalts other great scholars in order to be exalted oneself under this cloak, to be able to raise oneself, and so, as it were, to rise far above the so-called learned rabble. One presents to the eye of the learned world a great and renowned scholar, and seeks to persuade all others firmly that, notwithstanding all the diligence, labour and trouble they could possibly apply, they would always act in vain, since they could never be in a position to equal or approach this great man. Indeed, since one regards the pronouncements of this great archetype as irrefutable and unimprovable *oracula*, one rejects outright all their opinions, merely because they contradict, or seem to contradict, the judgment of this great man. One frightens, or seeks as it were to deter, all others from ever endeavouring to become equal to this scholar, as though it would be a vain undertaking to strive for it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-281":["So it comes about, for example, that most proud people, who do not like to be subject to and obey several [masters], or who envy their fellow citizens one power or another, would most like to have in a state the absolute form of government of a monarch, and also help to promote it; and this in order then to be equal to one another and to have no need to fear any of their fellow citizens. And this is in fact the true origin of all monarchies. But nothing is really more harmful to the human race than always to hold up others as unattainable examples and to take them, as it were, as models for imitation. One thereby copies the faults rather than the good qualities of the original set before one, since everything in the world is imperfect, and these models too cannot be wholly exempt from imperfection. Holy Scripture itself says that we should never choose anyone else as our model but God, this being most perfect in all respects. But always to conform and form oneself after other men, who are as highly imperfect as we are, brings about more harm than benefit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-282":["Such prejudices of authority and imitation are greatly grounded by a bad upbringing and inept instruction, namely when, to confirm one's judgments of the understanding, one adduces, cites and alleges not one's own reflection or experience but the judgments and opinions of others. And although this method, whether in oral or written delivery, is very agreeable and a witness to wide reading, it nevertheless often grounds only the prejudice of authority.",[]],"blomberg-s08-283":["Of the prejudice of the authority which one places in a great multitude of people we have already spoken above. It is to be presumed that what all people accept is universally valid. There already lies in our nature a special inclination to communicate our opinion to others, as we have already indicated above; so that, if someone were in a desert and had to dwell there without any human company, all his judgments would seem to him idle and vain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-284":["This inclination does not at all arise from vanity, but from a special and eminent communicative character of human reason. For when one judges alone and by oneself, one is never quite sure whether the judgment does not arise from a certain illusion that might deceive us in some way. Hence one is then eager to test and examine it precisely against the understanding of other men. One here makes, as it were, an experiment, and tries whether what we think is universally valid, whether others accept it, or whether it does not agree with reason. One thus, so to speak, whets the judgments one has, that is, of which one is the inventor, on the sure touchstone of the understanding and the insights of other men.",[]],"blomberg-s08-285":["Opposed to this inclination of human reason to communicate its judgments to others is logical egoism, of which we have also said something above: namely, when one believes and fully persuades oneself that in a judgment of the understanding one has no need whatever of the help of the judgment of others. It is indeed true that the judgment of others decides nothing in matters of the understanding. But it is not on that account superfluous or dispensable. Human understanding is by instinct already *communicatio* [communicative]. If, then, it is communicative, it must rightly also be participative, and concern itself with what others judge of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-286":["The prejudice of the authority which other people have with us is, further, the prejudice of antiquity, or the prejudice of novelty, on which liveliness of wit depends. The prejudice of antiquity is grounded on high esteem for what is old. What survives from old times down to ours always has the appearance of being good; for one infers that it would hardly have survived and come down to us if it were not good but worthless. Just so we also hold those buildings to be good which, though old, still stand without having fallen into ruin, whereas others have decayed through length of time.",[]],"blomberg-s08-287":["No judgment is more general than that the world is gradually growing old. Everything in the world deteriorates: animals, horses, etc., grow worse when they have lived long; bodies, for example clocks, grow worse with time. And this same judgment is passed on the whole world, on the whole of nature generally: it deteriorates more and more, it gradually grows old. Hence people admire old yet healthy, robust men, and say: where would one find such a man among us nowadays? So it is also with books which survive among thousands of others that have been lost. One holds them on that account, and not without some ground, to be especially good, because despite the usual decline that befalls such writings they have nevertheless maintained their repute for so long.",[]],"blomberg-s08-288":["Young people incline for the most part to the prejudice of novelty, old age on the other hand to the prejudice of antiquity, »just as youth is for the most part generous, while old age tends to be miserly.« Yet it is also true that the cause of these prejudices may often be the change of temperament.",[]],"blomberg-s08-289":["To the old belong diligence and industry.",[]],"blomberg-s08-290":["To the new, however, genius, liveliness and wit without steadiness. We do indeed see the individuals of nature decay and pass away; all works of art pass away. Rome itself collapses, rocks split, etc. – Man passes away and dies at every age. Up to the age of 15 years half of mankind dies; in the 16½th none; in the 50th, one in 100. Further, it is held that the perfections of the world itself gradually diminish in all things. Horace says: »our posterity will bring forth a still worse brood than we are.«",[]],"blomberg-s08-291":["But this prejudice is quite incorrect. Only in more recent times has this matter been reflected upon more, and better, than in ancient times.",[]],"blomberg-s08-292":["What nature produces has so great a constancy that nothing, no time, no age, can alter it. If one excepts the time before the universal Flood, everything in the world since then is equally good, equally strong, propagation and the power of propagation equally good and equally possible; although a single nation may indeed have some advantage over another in this respect, to which the particular circumstances of climate, means of subsistence, etc., must contribute something.",[]],"blomberg-s08-293":["Of many books one can tell at the first moment they appear before the world that they will not long be esteemed and held in regard. These books are like butterflies [*Papillons*], which appear in all their splendour and are admired by everyone, but last no longer than the summer and are soon afterwards forgotten. Other writings, however, which contain much thoroughness, are such that they are handed down from one age to another and always retain their authority.",[]],"blomberg-s08-294":["From this it naturally follows that one cherishes a kind of high esteem for them and holds them to be valuable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-295":["When one considers how time sifts out everything bad in the sciences, one may well ask: how will it one day fare with our present learning?",[]],"blomberg-s08-296":["A living language is always changing, a dead one never; all [its] authors are inexhaustible sources of it. The present German poet will certainly appear poor in 100 years. Dead languages have the advantage that they remain eternally beautiful and self-standing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-297":["Our present languages will perhaps never become dead ones, for only Holy Scripture and religion make a language dead. Thus the theologians are the true preservers of learning, and with them many languages would cease altogether.",[]],"blomberg-s08-298":["The *corpus Juris* is certainly the greatest and surest proof of human profundity. The discovery of the Pandects at Naples in the eleventh century is, however, the best find that men have ever been able to make among books. In general, the ancients are always inimitable models in style of writing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-299":["If we consider the ancient world as a whole, and all the individual creatures in it, we readily find that the longer the series of copies subordinated one to another, the greater is in turn the deviation from the *originale* [original].",[]],"blomberg-s08-300":["The first creatures were very perfect; but through generation and propagation they have become more and more unlike one another, at least. Notwithstanding this, we nevertheless find that no change at all has taken place in the species and *genera* of creatures, but that everything is still so constituted as it was at the creation of the world and of all things. It is therefore always a prejudice when we esteem what is old so excessively.",[]],"blomberg-s08-301":["Moreover, the high regard which we have for what is old arises also from a certain kind of illusion of our understanding and of our reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-302":["We always esteem learning highly; even in ourselves we always feel a certain regard, an *estime*, for ourselves when we are learned.",[]],"blomberg-s08-303":["Learning is required for the study of what is old. This is a further ground of the prejudice in favour of antiquity. Knowledge of what is old often requires great learning; hence it comes about that we hold what is old in high esteem, and that precisely because only through learning are we able to understand it. This high esteem for what is old often falls also upon ancient *autores*, because they have written something for the understanding of which learning is required. This is a kind of inner deception: we esteem what is old, and *per consequentiam* [consequently] the ancients also.",[]],"blomberg-s08-304":["The language of the ancients is itself already a matter of learning. From this it follows that whatever is written in such a language already acquires a certain kind of regard, because for the knowledge of the language alone a certain learning",[]],"blomberg-s08-305":["is required, without which one is unable to understand it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-306":["Hence so much already tells in favour of the ancients. Sometimes their faults are not even noticed; indeed, one is not even able to recognise whether there are faults in an ancient writing or not. Yet it seems entirely fair to esteem the ancients highly and with gratitude, since we are, after all, only their pupils. We ourselves hope that one day we too shall be called 'the dear ancients' by our descendants, and we then promise ourselves from them the same regard and the same thanks which we now pay to the ancients. The ancients are for us the original itself, and therefore those who are exalted above all judging and all criticism. What a prejudice it is when an author is so regarded that his language is no longer criticised or judged at all, but is assumed in advance to be in itself perfectly beautiful and incapable of improvement!",[]],"blomberg-s08-307":["If we come upon distorted expressions in a Cicero, a Horace, etc., and in other such *autores classici* once accepted as incapable of improvement, we shall not say: 'Cicero should not have spoken so; he could have avoided a fault had he expressed it somewhat differently', etc. On the contrary, we approve it, and we are permitted to speak in the same way, merely because Cicero did it so perversely. In the same way the ancient poets have the *licentia poetica* on their side in all their faults. Here we see what prejudices spring from the authority [Ansehen] which antiquity has with us. Hence there are so many champions of antiquity who, as soon as anything is discovered in more recent times, at once show that the ancients already knew all this; so that it is not, properly speaking, anything new, but only something searched out from the writings of the ancients and brought somewhat to light.",[]],"blomberg-s08-308":["Here, however, we must ask whether we have not cause to pass a certain provisional judgment in favour of antiquity and of the ancients, as regards their modes of knowledge and their insights.",[]],"blomberg-s08-309":["Answer. Certainly. The ancients quite certainly had this advantage over us, that they at least had no models set before them, no *originalia*, which they could have followed and were obliged to copy. Hence they had themselves to furnish originals and to become originals, whether they would or not, since they had no other guidance than nature and their own reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-310":["Nowadays, however, the all too great multitude of *originalia* produces nothing but copies, and many new *originalia* that might arise are stifled.",[]],"blomberg-s08-311":["The ancients had another advantage in respect of their thinking, in that their realms were for the most part divided into very many small territories and republics, all of which were very free and had no need to groan under the pressure of a monarchical and severe rule. (For freedom in thinking originates in a free form of government, as we have already had occasion to remark above.) This free condition, so very favourable to the extension of the arts and sciences, was to be found pre-eminently in ancient Greece. There nothing was known of monarchy; everyone followed his own inclination and governed himself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-312":["This manner of life, this form of state and of government, naturally brought it about that there arose among them, and showed themselves to the world, such great, inimitable and excellent geniuses and minds [Köpfe], especially in the fine arts [schönen Wißenschaften]: in painting an Apelles, etc., in oratory a Demosthenes, and in poetry.",[]],"blomberg-s08-313":["Even today we find that in those lands where the spirit of freedom prevails, much, indeed far more, is invented, many more *originalia* arise and far fewer copies appear, than where a despotic monarchical form of government has been introduced.",[]],"blomberg-s08-314":["England furnishes us with an example of this: there, even today, are to be found the most numerous and the best archetypes [Urbilder] and models. Everyone there thinks for himself, speaks and writes for himself, etc., without choosing a model for himself, imitating it, and wishing to follow it in everything.",[]],"blomberg-s08-315":["Lands, on the other hand, where monarchs and individual princes rule despotically, and one man has command over all, produce nothing but copies and seldom *originalia*. The court is there for the most part the original, and everyone strives to imitate what goes on at court. The style of the court is the style of everyone else (just as people like to wear the clothes that are customarily worn at court). This can be seen today in France, for instance: there one seldom meets with an original, but for the most part with nothing but copies. Everything there is a matter of fashion, in clothes as well as in judgments and writings.",[]],"blomberg-s08-316":["The style of English authors is therefore also very easily distinguished from all others: one always says something different from what another has said. The French, on the other hand, always write in one and the same style; one makes use of almost the same expressions as another. Hence it comes about that one French author is not easily distinguished from another, and one very seldom meets among them with anything particular, excellent, new and unusual.",[]],"blomberg-s08-317":["We now come to the prejudice of novelty. This is grounded partly on the provisional judgment which we for the most part pass in favour of, and to the advantage of, what is new. We often have cause to pass favourable judgments on what is old, but also on what is new.",[]],"blomberg-s08-318":["All those sciences which presuppose an extensive, historical and correct knowledge are becoming ever more perfect in more recent times; for it is well known that the more recent scholars strive more and more to extend historical modes of knowledge. In empirical modes of knowledge what is new always has an indisputable advantage over what is old. In sciences, on the other hand, which require sustained diligence and unremitting effort, what is new likewise has a more favourable judgment in its favour than what is old; and that not because it contradicts the old, but because it often adds still more to it. If, however, we esteem what is new because it *contradicit* [contradicts] the old, this is in truth a very harmful prejudice. In part, however, the prejudice of novelty is grounded also on a certain illusion of our understanding, our reason and our imagination [Phantasie]. The old man loves what is old, a young man what is new. Age makes a man much inclined to defend antiquity; youth, in turn, to speak up for novelty. If youth owes its knowledge and all its science to the ancients, and has merely learned it from them, not discovered it for itself, it has no merit of its own; and it is therefore very much inclined to discover new sciences and to add more to what the ancients have.",[]],"blomberg-s08-319":["Whole nations are often much inclined towards what is old, and others again towards what is new. The Spaniard and the Portuguese love what is old, the Frenchman, on the other hand, what is new. Sluggish, slow, melancholy and reflective minds are very fond of what is old; lively and cheerful minds, on the other hand, love what is new. The former are enemies, the latter friends, of new fashions. The Frenchman is fashionable in clothes, in systems, in medicines, etc. The Frenchman is the first author and founder of each and every fashion, and also the only one, and will always remain so; he alone has the flightiness and lightness requisite for it. The French are therefore too much given to the prejudice of novelty, which springs",[]],"blomberg-s08-320":["1.) from [the fact] that the new contradicts the old; or 2 *do* [secondly] from [the fact] that one loves the new because it, first, retains the old, but also, second, adds something new to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-321":["Inclination is also a great source of favourable prejudice. To this belongs insinuation, e.g. by outward bearing [Führung], by mien, by name, e.g. in books by the title. 'A good title sells its book'; and, according to the present fashion, a French, light, *légère*, impertinent title, e.g. when Beaumelle publishes a book with the title *Mes Pensées*, etc. Should we not esteem highly the ancient inhabitants of Greece, since in that favourable climate all learning arose, as we have said above?",[]],"blomberg-s08-322":["One kind of recommendation, however, which prompts us to esteem someone, originates also in this: when we have despised someone and then discover and become aware in him of something unexpectedly great, a fine insight, a genius that exceeds all our expectation, we then esteem him the more highly for it, and admire him the more, because we previously despised him. So it is, for instance, when we hear a woman, of whose insight and skill we have had only very poor concepts, speak with great insight, wit, etc.: we admire her the more, and our contempt or disdain is thus at once transformed into reverence. In just the same way we learn really to esteem and revere the great sage Epicurus, of whom we perhaps at first formed poor concepts, when we examine more closely his conduct, his sayings, his judgments and knowledge, etc. Whoever is already fond of viewing everything from its worst side, and everywhere strives to seek out faults rather than good qualities, has certainly a very malicious heart.",[]],"blomberg-s08-323":["It is always with vexation and repugnance that one reads books in which the renown and honour of the human race, together with its good qualities, are undermined. It is proper to human nature, when not corrupted by an evil heart, to assume and presume the best in every writing and every action; and one in fact always profits from this rather than suffers harm.",[]],"blomberg-s08-324":["Some time ago a dispute arose among certain French scholars: some exalted and defended the old, others, on the contrary, the new. The former held the old to be an original, and the new to be only copies, often corrupted. The latter, among whom Fontenelle also distinguished himself, maintained that the new can often greatly surpass the old. The old [Die alte] may often be capable of bringing forth products superior to the new; but often too the new can by far surpass the old.",[]],"blomberg-s08-325":["In oratory, poetry, sculpture, painting, in all the fine arts and sciences, then, the ancients have surpassed us. But whether more recent times could not also bring forth better products than the ancients brought forth is quite another question. Oratory, for instance, was so cultivated [excoliret] among the ancients because everyone was permitted to come forward before a free people, and in fact often did come forward and speak.",[]],"blomberg-s08-326":["Here, then, everyone who intended to deliver a public address before such a public assembly had of necessity to apply all diligence and effort in order to attain his end: to move the people and to win the approval of his hearers.",[]],"blomberg-s08-327":["Question. Would it ever be possible to extinguish and root out each and every prejudice in man?",[]],"blomberg-s08-328":["Answer. It seems almost impossible. Man is already accustomed to have in his soul incentives [Triebfedern] which he cannot resist, which run ahead of the judgments of the understanding, and in this way form a prejudice.",[]],"blomberg-s08-329":["Man can indeed learn to distinguish what springs from incentives and what from the understanding. That is in truth already a great deal; and he can then certainly no longer be deluded and deceived so often as hitherto, although the propensity to it is of course still present at every moment. But to rid and free himself of it wholly and completely seems to be impossible, and could never be done. Nevertheless each and every pious fraud, as in morals in common life so also here in logic in the employment of the powers of the understanding, is wholly unpardonable. By this is meant the intention and the endeavour to bring forth something true by the help of prejudices; this philosophy always opposes [verfolgt].",[]],"blomberg-s08-330":["A proposition is not a prejudice, however false it may be. For a prejudice is nothing other than the mere desire to judge, but without the requisite acuteness or reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-331":["How, then, do our prejudices arise?",[]],"blomberg-s08-332":["The propensity of the mind to judge according to authority, inclination, custom or fashion: that is prejudice. Proverbs are for the most part false, for many of them are of such a kind that they can be used indifferently [either way].",[]],"blomberg-s08-333":["Children at school for the most part learn Latin with great difficulty. Why is this? Because they are required to be able to speak pure Latin when they have barely mastered the rudiments of the language and the first grammatical rules. All the prejudices of which we have so far spoken spring, therefore, either",[]],"blomberg-s08-334":["1.) from a bad education, or",[]],"blomberg-s08-335":["2.) from examples and the imitation of them",[]],"blomberg-s08-336":["The prejudice of excessive confidence which one places in oneself is nothing other than egoism [Egoisterey]. Egoism, however, is either *cosmologicus*, when one holds that there is no other thinking being, no world at all, outside me; or *logicus*, when one holds that oneself alone judges rationally, and that no one else is able to judge anything or to understand it better. This latter, namely the *egoismus logicus*, does at first really seem to be permissible. One certainly has no need to consult the judgments of others when one oneself knows something correctly; one need not seek to bring about an agreement of others with the knowledge which one oneself has. But on closer examination one finds that one cannot be certain whether one has judged rightly or not unless one compares one's judgments with the judgments of others and tests them by the understanding of others. For a mode of knowledge is not correct when it agrees with my private understanding, but only when it agrees with the universal laws of the understanding of all men. Hence every man, as we have already shown above, has an inclination to",[]],"blomberg-s08-337":["communicate and impart his judgments to others. This is the true friendship of sentiment. The logical egoist therefore deprives himself of a great and exceedingly important advantage, very conducive and necessary to the enrichment and improvement of his understanding: that of being able correctly to distinguish the true from the false *per communicationem judiciorum, et mutuam dijudicationem eorum invicem* [through the communication of judgments and their mutual appraisal by one another]. Logical egoism, however, sometimes springs also from a certain kind of humility and faint-heartedness, when one has far too little confidence in oneself and one's insights, and believes that one's judgments are not worthy to be made known to others in the whole learned world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-338":["To logical egoism, however, yet another prejudice is opposed: namely, when one builds all one's judgments on the reason of others, judges nothing for oneself, but merely imitates, inasmuch as one exalts others above oneself and has no confidence in oneself. These, in short, are the prejudices of the lazy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-339":["There are, however, certain arts and sciences which are of such a kind that they can, indeed must, be learned by mere imitation. To these belong, for example, each and every handicraft, and likewise mathematics, in which one need only set forth certain propositions to someone and duly demonstrate and prove them; he commits them to memory, and is thereby in fact able to become a real *mathematicus*. Other sciences, on the contrary, are of such a kind that they cannot by any means be learned through imitation; genius is required for learning them. To these belongs, for example, the whole of philosophy. One may be quite sure that one will never be able to become a true philosopher [619a] if one does nothing more than imitate the philosophers of former times, leaf or read through their writings once or twice, accept as true what they have said, and, so to speak, wholly forget oneself in doing so: if, that is, one has not the least confidence in oneself, passes no judgments of one's own at all, but swears *in verba magistri* [by the words of the master].",[]],"blomberg-s08-340":["Nothing is more harmful than imitation in philosophy. Nothing is more wretched and ruinous for the understanding than, for example, to take up a Wolff, or a Crusius, or others, to learn and imprint upon oneself their *definitiones strictissime* and word for word, and to esteem them as stars of the first magnitude and oneself as nothing. On the contrary, one must learn to think for oneself and to judge for oneself, to reflect on objects for oneself, and, in order to become and to be a philosopher, to learn to philosophise. Philosophy [Weltweißheit], however, cannot in the least be learned from books, but only through one's own reflection and one's own meditation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-341":["We have, however, two kinds of prejudice which relate to learning: namely, a [pre]judgment for, and a prejudice against, learning and for common sound understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-342":["Scholars, and also other common people, have a prejudice in favour of learning: they often accept everything that has been said and taught by learned men. The moralists, on the other hand, have a prejudice in favour of common sound understanding, which *praejudicium* is also characteristic of many common people. This latter prejudice arises from the fact that no one denies himself sound understanding. There has never yet been a man who believed he had no *sens commun*, *sensus communis*, common sound understanding. That one has a poor, defective memory one readily and willingly admits. Another, again, admits that he has no quickness of comprehension, no wit, no witty ideas, no genius for this or that particular science, no taste for poetry. But everyone believes himself to possess the *sensus communis*; no one will lightly deny himself that.",[]],"blomberg-s08-343":["Now since very few of those who have sound understanding are learned men, and since there is a well-known and true proverb, *ut ars non habeat osorem, nisi imperitum* [that art has no enemy but the unskilled], it is certainly not at all to be wondered at that it very easily comes about that learning is by very many, whether from ignorance or from envy, if not despised, at least held in low esteem, while common sound understanding is extolled without limit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-344":["The author here further adduces the prejudice of the accepted system [Lehrgebäude]. In almost all branches of learning the prejudice of unity has place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-345":["A system is distinguished by nothing else than this, that there is unity in it. The human understanding, however, is so constituted that it approves everything in which unity can be found; and from this there certainly arises the prejudice in favour of unity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-346":["We always believe that we have made something perfect, or at least as perfect as possible, when we have managed to reduce it to a unity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-347":["Unity has also, in the same way, something in a certain manner ingratiating and pleasing, in that it seems to contain in itself an economy of the powers of our understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-348":["Further, where unity is to be met with, there order is also necessarily found",[]],"blomberg-s08-349":["Many beginners also have a certain prejudice in favour of the excellence and superiority of a thing which they have learned with much diligence and labour, and which has cost them much effort and many a bitter sweat. (For instance, so to speak: someone has had a terrible amount of work in learning the French language, and has had to take pains; but he was in earnest about learning it, and has at length attained his end; he then has a great prejudice in favour of the inner worth of this thing.)",[]],"blomberg-s08-350":["The prejudice of lazy trust arises from a certain kind of gross ignorance and sloth in attaining to this or that knowledge, and from a contempt for this knowledge which springs from it, a very ill-placed and contemptible contempt, merely because one cannot partake of it. So it is, for instance, with the fox in the fable, who despises the grapes which, in spite of all his leaps and efforts, he cannot reach, and calls them sour.",[]],"blomberg-s08-351":["In almost all branches of learning the prejudice of unity has place",[]],"blomberg-s08-352":["When very many properties and determinations of a thing are to be derived from one ground, we are commonly accustomed for the most part to pass the provisional judgment, and to form the conjecture, that perhaps all the remaining determinations of the same thing will also be derivable from it, and that therefore this one ground which we have of the thing will be almost the true and completely sufficient ground of the whole thing. That is to say, we sometimes assume a ground, and then infer from the consequences that this is the only true ground, although this is very seldom completely certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-353":["For example, when one entertains some conjecture or other that this or that man is a thief, one often infers merely from the consequences whether this conjecture may be true, or not true but false. If, for instance, one finds that this or that person who is connected with us at first had and possessed nothing at all, but afterwards suddenly squandered a great deal of money, and that this happened at the same time as this or that money of mine went missing and was presumably stolen, one confidently believes that he must be the thief of our money; although this conjecture can very often be false and incorrect, indeed even offensive and sinful. From this provisional judgment arises the prejudice of unity: namely, when one finds a ground of a thing which has very many consequences and determinations, one then derives all the remaining consequences and determinations from it. So it went, to give an example, at the very beginning with electricity. At that time everything that was in any degree marvellous was explained from it; indeed there were even some subtle spirits who went so far as to wish to maintain that electricity had an influence on religion, in that they sought, for instance, to explain even Christ's healing of the woman with the issue of blood from the powers of electricity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-354":["In general, whoever finds, or at least believes he finds, in one ground the source of all wisdom and knowledge is always much inclined to ascribe to it still more than is actually to be found in it. Another believes he has sound understanding, and yet wishes also to amuse and enjoy himself; but he is far more inclined to satisfy his inclinations as much as possible than to take the path of reflection and labour. And in this way an understanding which is neither exercised nor cultivated [coliret] is often wrongly taken for the merely common understanding. For this understanding may rightly be called, in a good sense, a plain [schlecht] understanding. For although at present a bad concept and an injurious interpretation are attached to this expression, it is nevertheless well known that the dear ancients had the proverb 'schlecht und recht' [plain and right], and that is good; and they here took the word 'schlecht' in a very good sense.",[]],"blomberg-s08-355":["All such prejudices of which we have so far spoken can, however, be divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-356":["»1) into prejudices of pride",[]],"blomberg-s08-357":["»2) into prejudices of humility and of excessive",[]],"blomberg-s08-358":["»timidity.",[]],"blomberg-s08-359":["The former, the prejudices of pride, arise when one attributes far too much to one's insights, knowledge and judgments.",[]],"blomberg-s08-360":["The prejudices of humility and timidity, on the other hand, spring from one's attributing far too little to one's judgments in general, or to the powers of one's understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-361":["Often there even arises in us a prejudice against a whole science, and that because it has perhaps often erred and we have closely noticed and observed its faults. So it has gone, for example, with *metaphysica*. And when it goes thus with many sciences, that they often err, there arises from this a kind of scepticism, a mania for doubt [Zweifel-Sucht], which might often spread over all the sciences. This, however, comes from a weak understanding, which does not wish to be laughed at, although it is not itself able to judge the modes of knowledge and the sciences. Such people commonly believe little or nothing; and this prejudice of mistrust, which originates from experience, arises, unfortunately, only too often among men.",[]],"blomberg-s08-362":["Fashions are nothing other than incitements of vanity, or recommendations of the practice of a certain vanity; and they therefore arise through the choice of respected, great and distinguished men. So it goes, for the most part, with style, with music, with architecture.",[]],"blomberg-s08-363":["A matter of vanity is one which rests not on true honour, but merely on the effort to please, that is, on fashion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-364":["Much, for instance, which [one] at present actually reckons as of lasting beauty in our style of writing is nevertheless nothing other than the fashion of our time. Very many scholars and *beaux esprits* [Schöndenckende Geister] are in fact often more harmful than useful to the learned world in general.",[]],"blomberg-s08-365":["A Young, a Klopstock, a Gleim, etc., for example, have in fact corrupted a multitude of weak heads.",[]],"blomberg-s08-366":["The ignorant man for the most part imitates the faults, but not the beauties, of the original he has before him, merely in order to become like a person who is able to make so great a stir by this or that book, poem, etc. So it would be, for example, if some man or woman sought to imitate the lisp of a beautiful person and thereby became ridiculous.",[]],"blomberg-s08-367":["Sometimes a fault helps a man's wit, talents, etc., which are otherwise not very considerable, nevertheless to shine forth and glitter.",[]],"blomberg-s08-368":["Fashion introduces clothes, manners, and indeed often even customs [sitten]; and it is in a certain way almost good to follow fashion, provided only that its usages do not conflict with the eternal and unchangeable laws of morality. For by its help men are, as it were, in a certain manner better united and brought into accord.",[]],"blomberg-s08-369":["The prejudice of shallowness, further, is the same prejudice of which we have already spoken above, namely the prejudice in favour of sound understanding. For this, in the matter [im gegenstand] of learning, is shallow.",[]],"blomberg-s08-370":["Voltaire is certainly one of the greatest defenders of shallowness. He seeks, that is, to derive almost everything merely from sound reason, and his judgment sometimes has no sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-371":["Sound reason is not so much to be rejected as is commonly thought; it is often in fact the source of many modes of knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s08-372":["Hence it is certainly, on the other hand, again a prejudice when one rejects the *sensus communis*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-373":["There is, further, also a certain prejudice against sound human reason, and also a prejudice in favour of sound human reason. In the former prejudice one believes that one can understand nothing at all by means of human reason; in the other case, on the contrary, that one can understand everything by its means. In the former case, therefore, one trusts sound human reason too little; in the other case one trusts it too much and ascribes too much to it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-374":["One must by no means despise the human race, hold all its prejudices to be worthless and wish to despise them; one must rather leave it the advantages that are its due. It is true that there is much one cannot understand through reason (namely, through common sound human reason); nonetheless it is not on that account to be rejected, but always has its worth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-375":["At the end of this *paragraphus* the author further speaks of the prejudice against antiquity and also of the prejudice against novelty. But both these prejudices have already been spoken of above.",[]],"blomberg-s08-376":["The prejudice against the old, that is, is always bound up with the prejudice in favour of the new; and, conversely, every prejudice against the new is always bound up, inseparably united, with a prejudice in favour of the old; and the one kind of prejudice cannot be thought without the other. For whoever loves the old very much cannot esteem the new highly, *et vice versa*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-377":["»Every mode of knowledge from merely insufficient grounds is uncertain.« When a mode of knowledge is known from merely insufficient grounds of truth, in such a way that I see far more grounds for the thing than against it, it is plausible [scheinbahr]. When, however, there are more grounds for than against the mode of knowledge, or at least more than half of the grounds are for it (whether I see these grounds or not), it is probable [Wahrscheinlich].",[]],"blomberg-s08-378":["Plausibility rests merely on the subject",[]],"blomberg-s08-379":["Probability [rests] on the object, in such a way that I thereby actually know more grounds for the thing than there are possible grounds for asserting its opposite. In plausibility an error is actually to be met with, and that in the form, namely in that one is not conscious of the insufficiency of the grounds. The first rule, therefore, is this: in all modes of knowledge, even in true ones, one should first of all seek to become conscious of the uncertainty which arises, or could ever arise, from the insufficient grounds. One should therefore put oneself, as it were, in the situation of the opponent, the doubter, who believes he has sufficient grounds for asserting the opposite of these modes of knowledge; for otherwise one will in fact almost always, for the most part, err and deceive oneself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-380":["One can never know whether one knows more grounds for a thing than against it, nor learn to see this, until one has knowledge of the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-381":["With every mode of knowledge, therefore, we must first acquaint ourselves with the sufficient ground. That is, we must seek to know and recognise how many grounds are actually required for the complete certainty of a thing, so that we may be able to distinguish properly whether something is plausible or probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-382":["If we do not know the sufficient ground of a thing, we also cannot determine how many grounds for its truth are needed to make the knowledge even probable in the first place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-383":["Suppose I subtract the grounds of holding-true [*Vorwahrhalten*] from the totality of possible grounds that are needed and required for certainty, and duly weigh and compare these with one another. The remaining grounds are then grounds of the opposite. I can thereby quite easily see which grounds preponderate, whether those for or those against the knowledge. Plausibility [*Scheinbarkeit*] can alter greatly, since its weight is grounded in the subject. Probability [*Wahrscheinlichkeit*], on the other hand, always remains unaltered and the same, and that solely because it is grounded in the object.",[]],"blomberg-s08-384":["The manner of writing [*Schreib Art*; presumably a slip for *Scheinbarkeit*, plausibility] is naturally very variable, and especially for this reason, that today we may come to know more grounds for a thing and tomorrow, on the contrary, more grounds against it, according as we direct our thoughts upon the thing in different ways, and investigate and reflect more upon this or that [ground], and endeavour to seek it out. The ground of assent [*Beyfall*] is always at least the beginning of the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-385":["It is, after all, a ground, even though it is not complete. An insufficient ground is quite wrongly, indeed erroneously, held to be no ground whatsoever; it is still material for further inquiry and investigation. An ounce [*Loth*] is always a ground towards lifting a pound, although it is not sufficient; for several ounces taken together make up a pound.",[]],"blomberg-s08-386":["All our conviction and assurance regarding any knowledge must necessarily pass through all such elements. At first we merely conjecture that something is true. Incompleteness is the beginning in each and every thing, whatever it may be. By investigation and untiring attention we make something complete.",[]],"blomberg-s08-387":["True sagacity [*Sagacitaet*] is always, as it were, a spying out, a scenting of the truth in advance. It is one of the chief talents of good heads and of reflection. So it is, for instance, with the discoveries of new parts of the world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-388":["In these [discoveries], grounds of conjecture have often been the causes of investigation and the causes of discovery. Thus the New World etc. was discovered, and so much more may yet be discovered. If we have before us some such insufficient grounds of truth, then we have either plausibility, *probabilitas* (and this is only the lowest degree), or probability, *verisimilitudo*, as a greater degree. The sufficient ground of holding-true, the unity, is that through which a thing is posited.",[]],"blomberg-s08-389":["No ground can be more than sufficient, for otherwise it is no ground at all; just as nothing can be more than true, more than false, more than good, more than evil, for otherwise it is nothing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-390":["The sufficient ground already comprehends everything in itself; and the concept of everything [*allem*] also contains at the same time a unity. But I can never say more than everything of a thing. Nothing is more than true; nothing is too true; nothing is too certain; nothing is more than sufficient, as we nevertheless often wrongly express ourselves in common life. The insufficient ground of holding-true produces plausibility.",[]],"blomberg-s08-391":["Probability is a fraction, in which the sufficient ground of truth is the denominator, while the insufficient grounds of holding-true which I possess are the numerator.",[]],"blomberg-s08-392":["The degree of probability or improbability of one or another empirical thing can actually be calculated mathematically: for instance, in all kinds of games, in lotteries, in the deaths of men according to their number of years, and in many other increments [*Augmenten*], such as changes in the world.",[]],"blomberg-s08-393":["With our philosophical truths we know nothing more than a plausibility of this or that thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-394":["We have certain grounds for the truth of the thing, and know fewer for its opposite, since there is no one who is in a position to adduce to us more grounds against than for the truth of the thing. Our knowledge is therefore nothing more than plausible.",[]],"blomberg-s08-395":["Formerly the moon was held to be merely a small luminous body, existing solely and entirely for the sake of our earth. Later, however, men gradually began to measure the size of the moon, and soon found that it was somewhat smaller than our earth, and that there are mountains and valleys on it. Again, some astronomers [*Astronomi*] even conjectured that it has inhabitants, while others held that it has none.",[]],"blomberg-s08-396":["This knowledge of the moon was therefore very variable, and for that very reason not even probable, but only plausible. For whatever is probable cannot be changeable at all, but always remains one and the same, since all probability is not subjective but objective.",[]],"blomberg-s08-397":["All plausible judgments, on the other hand, change daily, according as we know more or fewer grounds for and against the thing. But what is probable is, so to speak, probable for all eternity. For although the grounds of probability are indeed still insufficient, they are nevertheless already greater than the possible grounds of the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-398":["But in order to know whether the grounds for the truth of the thing are greater than the grounds of the opposite, or whether the grounds for the knowledge make up more than half of the sufficient ground, we must always first endeavour, as has already been remarked above, to obtain a solid and sound knowledge of the sufficient ground. Mathematics is the only science which is of such a kind that in it we can know the sufficient ground of a thing. In philosophy this is not possible at all. Hence it comes about that all our modes of knowledge in philosophy which are not indubitably certain can never be probable, but always only plausible. For in [judging of] probability we do not judge what actually is, but only what is possible under certain conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-399":["This possibility, however, remains always unalterable under one and the same conditions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-400":["By means of probability, therefore, what we properly know are the degrees of possibility. If these degrees are great, there is a great probability; if they are small, a small probability.",[]],"blomberg-s08-401":["Taken subjectively [*subjective*], a mode of knowledge is doubtful when the grounds for the thing which I know are equal to the grounds against it, that is, to the grounds of the opposite. Taken objectively [*objective*], on the other hand, something is doubtful when the grounds for the possibility of a thing or matter which I know are exactly equal to the grounds of its opposite, so that there are as many grounds for as against it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-402":["Something can be practically certain subjectively, while objectively it nevertheless contributes very little towards the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-403":["As regards certainty, there are three particular and quite distinct kinds of it, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-404":["*1mo*) logical,",[]],"blomberg-s08-405":["*2do*) practical,",[]],"blomberg-s08-406":["3) aesthetic certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-407":["Logical certainty is certainty according to the rules of the understanding and of reason, and is therefore an objective certainty, grounded in the thing itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-408":["Aesthetic certainty is a certainty according to the rules of taste.",[]],"blomberg-s08-409":["Practical certainty, finally, is a certainty according to the rules of the free will.",[]],"blomberg-s08-410":["A man is practically certain when the grounds which he has for holding a mode of knowledge true are sufficient to determine him to action. If, however, he is nonetheless unwilling to act [upon them], these grounds are still far from sufficient to make him logically certain. Practical certainty does indeed in many cases require very little knowledge; but in many other cases, again, far more is required for moral or practical certainty than for logical certainty: for instance, when it is a question of condemning someone, of sentencing him to death, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s08-411":["A man can be aesthetically certain when he holds the opposite of a thing or of a mode of knowledge which he has to be impossible, merely because it does not please him; as, for instance, when his knowledge consists in believing in a divine being, an eternal government [of the world], a future world, the reward of good and the punishment of evil actions, etc. Certainty here rests solely on feeling: according as something brings a man either gratification or displeasure, he either accepts or rejects it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-412":["In this way the human mind is in fact subject to very many illusions and deceptions. Often, merely because something pleases us, we hold it to be certain; and merely because something displeases or vexes us, we hold it to be uncertain. This certainty or uncertainty, however, is not objective but rather subjective. It can even often keep us from properly seeking out or investigating the opposite of a thing and the grounds in its favour",[]],"blomberg-s08-413":["We often find it more comfortable to hold something true, since otherwise we should have to put ourselves to inconvenience [*incommodiren*] if we chose to take the trouble of examining the contrary [*contrarium*] of the thing. We hold the opposite to be impossible merely because we have no desire to investigate it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-414":["Practical certainty, finally, is a certainty according to the rules of prudence and of morality; the latter [kind] is moral certainty. There have been some who believed the moral to be far less than the practical. Thus, for instance, it is said: it is morally certain that there are inhabitants in the moon, etc. But this has not the slightest connection with my actions. Yet if something is indeed already practically certain, it is often still far from being on that account morally certain; and even if something is morally certain, it is often not on that account yet logically certain. The [mere] possibility of a thing can produce in us a practical certainty. For instance, if someone believes no more than that it is possible that there is a God, this thought must nevertheless necessarily have an influence on his life, just as if he were fully assured and convinced of this knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-415":["The plausibility of a thing can likewise produce a greater degree of certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-416":["In the practical sense something can be completely certain, even though in the logical sense it is only plausible, or at most probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-417":["The poet can become certain of one thing or another only morally. Histories are of such a kind that they are only practically certain. Logical certainty is very often lacking, and one can easily set about finding grounds for the opposite and refuting historical reports.",[]],"blomberg-s08-418":["It can easily be proved that the historian [*Historicus*] has related, properly speaking, not true histories but only fables and fictions [*fictiones*]. There are in fact many people who occupy themselves with weakening the complete certainty of history. This requires no art, but is easily done, because what is necessary and sufficient for practical certainty is not sufficient [*sufficient*] for speculation and for logical certainty. Practical certainty is that which, in respect of certain actions, brings about the same as a complete certainty. For instance, if something of value disappears from my house, and at that very time my servant stays away, I have a practical certainty that this missing servant has stolen what is missing from me; although there is as yet no logical certainty whatsoever, and the servant may have left, and gone off, for quite other reasons and in some other way.",[]],"blomberg-s08-419":["Practical certainty, however, is twofold:",[]],"blomberg-s08-420":["1.) pragmatic and 2.) moral certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-421":["When a mode of knowledge which is in itself probable is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of prudence as if it were completely certain, this is a pragmatic certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-422":["But when a mode of knowledge which is in itself probable is just as secure a ground of actions according to the rules of morality as if it were completely certain, this is a moral wisdom [*Weisheit*; presumably a slip for *Gewisheit*, certainty].",[]],"blomberg-s08-423":["What is even logically improbable can nevertheless be morally certain. For instance, suppose I demand money from someone and do not know whether I have already received it or not, and the other, my supposed debtor, knows just as little whether he has already paid me or not. Then according to morals I can demand nothing further from him, although it is logically uncertain whether anything has already been paid or not. Of the existence of God, too, a logical certainty can be obtained, although this is very difficult. But all men can attain to moral certainty [of it], even without much logical speculation [*speculationes*]. If only we sharpen a man's moral judgments, we can thereby easily bring him to conviction of the existence of God.",[]],"blomberg-s08-424":["A logical certainty of a future life is very difficult to obtain. But we can well obtain a moral certainty of it if we consider that here on earth happiness is not always a consequence of good conduct, and that there is therefore another world to be hoped for, in which this [connection] will take place.",[]],"blomberg-s08-425":["Moral certainty or uncertainty must be spoken of and treated more fully in morals itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-426":["Most, indeed almost all, authors [*autores*] are entirely unacquainted with moral certainty, and take it for any probability whatsoever. For instance: it is uncertain whether there are inhabitants in the moon, yet it is always in some measure probable. Some take this for a moral judgment; but that is not the case, since such a judgment [*judicium*] has no influence whatever on conduct. It is a logical probability and mere speculation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-427":["Nothing is a moral judgment except what has a relation to my actions. If, then, someone should and must act, and the grounds are merely probable, the grounds which have the greatest probability constitute practical certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-428":["Every ground of the opposite of a thing of which we are conscious is entitled a doubt. Every hindrance to holding-true is a logical difficulty, and this is",[]],"blomberg-s08-429":["*1mo* objective, that is, doubt;",[]],"blomberg-s08-430":["*2do* subjective, that is, scruple.",[]],"blomberg-s08-431":["The latter, namely the subjective difficulty, is properly incomprehensibility, in which I do not reject a mode of knowledge but notice that I cannot come to terms with it. This subjective hindrance of the understanding is a fine task for a philosopher who wishes to study man. The study of scruples is of great importance.",[]],"blomberg-s08-432":["The study of doubts is somewhat easier. To detect scruples in ourselves, before we can say that we cannot comprehend a mode of knowledge, is very difficult. Scruples hinder a man from holding-true, and can be resolved neither by the man who has the scruple nor by another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-433":["Scruples, however, can become doubts [672a], namely when we become conscious of them; and this is also most necessary.",[]],"blomberg-s08-434":["A scruple is transformed into a doubt by considering the mode of knowledge which the scruple accompanies in all sorts of different relations and under different circumstances. We shall then at once, without any trouble, discover and notice which is the side on which we cannot yet be fully decided, and which, on the other hand, is the side where no scruple any longer remains.",[]],"blomberg-s08-435":["Scruples in themselves, therefore, are by no means to be despised, or straightway censured and rejected.",[]],"blomberg-s08-436":["There are, however, subjective scruples, which lie in the person who has this or that mode of knowledge; and these we must disregard. But objective scruples, which lie in the knowledge itself, we must seek to make clear. If someone puts before me nothing but plausible propositions, I have a scruple, and do not at once give him my assent. For instance, I consider the proposition that all moral judgments are supposed to lie in a special feeling and sensation. I give it assent, yet a scruple still remains with me: I cannot refute the proposition, and yet I cannot trust the knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-437":["When I consider the scruple more closely and reflect upon the matter better, a doubt arises from it. I see that if I accept this proposition as true, it necessarily follows that whoever lacks this feeling and this sensation must be incapable of passing moral judgments; and this is nevertheless seldom, or indeed never, the case.",[]],"blomberg-s08-438":["From this doubt, however, something further must necessarily arise, namely that I shall then raise various objections against the truth of the proposition; and upon these objections there finally follows a complete refutation from certain grounds, which is otherwise also entitled simply a reply.",[]],"blomberg-s08-439":["If I refute the doubt from grounds which only one person holds and accepts as correct, adequate and sufficient, then these grounds, as well as this reply, are valid only for a single subject; and this is then a so-called *responsio et refutatio ad hominem*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-440":["This is done with a kind of reluctance, and solely in order to be rid of one's opponent as soon as possible. If, on the other hand, I refute the doubt from grounds which are valid for everyone, and are consequently universally valid, but are also so convincing and assuring that no one is any longer able to raise anything against them, then this is a real categorical refutation, *refutatio categorica*. This is undertaken in order really to bring the truth to light, and as far as possible to clear false modes of knowledge out of the way.",[]],"blomberg-s08-441":["Scruples often remain permanently in a mode of knowledge and cannot be cleared away.",[]],"blomberg-s08-442":["The unfolding of scruples is a very fine science. By it a scruple is, as it were, drawn out of the darkness; and then, once it has been properly brought to light, it must be formally refuted as a significant objection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-443":["No more wretched condition of man can in fact be conceived than the condition which leaves us irresolute: the condition of indecision, especially when it concerns our interest. This condition of indecision is far more burdensome than the condition of speculation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-444":["Everything which holds us back and makes us inactive, which puts us into a certain kind of inaction, is very much opposed to the essential determinations of the soul. A man would even rather know of a future misfortune in advance than remain uncertain in the expectation of it, hovering between fear and hope.",[]],"blomberg-s08-445":["Physicians are often themselves to blame for the faint-heartedness of their patients; for the most part they feed them with empty words, and conditions, and fancies.",[]],"blomberg-s08-446":["Young people especially are for the most part of such a kind that they are very unwilling to remain in suspense [*in suspenso*], for it is their way always to want to get further. Yet amidst all this uncertainty there can still be a certain kind of certainty, namely that all our knowledge must be very restricted and limited, and consequently very imperfect and deficient.",[]],"blomberg-s08-447":["For we can always know the grounds of uncertainty with certainty and reliability. That is a certainty of subjective philosophy.",[]],"blomberg-s08-448":["Established [*ausgemachte*] truths are those truths against which no further objection can be made.",[]],"blomberg-s08-449":["To these belong, for instance, the *res judicatae*, that is, matters settled in court [*in foro*] by a judgment having the force of law. Thus, for instance, after a suit has been concluded and the judge has pronounced a sentence, it is a settled matter that in accordance with it this or that person must pay this or that other person, say, a certain sum of money assigned [to him].",[]],"blomberg-s08-450":["All new truths, without exception, always meet at the beginning with very many opponents and much resistance. A certain time must be necessary and required to establish them, after they have been better examined. So it went, for instance, at first with the writings of Descartes [*Cartesius*]. At first they met with very much contradiction, and thirty and more years passed without their truth being established.",[]],"blomberg-s08-451":["Through this establishing of a truth no new knowledge is in fact added to it. All that happens is that all other men cease to pass judgment on it and concur in it. So long, therefore, as a matter is still disputed, so long as disputes are still exchanged on this side and on that, it is not yet established at all.",[]],"blomberg-s08-452":["We can often have complete certainty of this or that thing, while the grounds of the certainty are often so constituted that much uncertainty is to be observed in the manner of inference. For instance: the soul is immortal. This is completely certain. But the grounds which philosophy gives for it are not so evident that we ought thereby to be completely convinced. Its philosophical form is still very uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-453":["Every uncertainty is a ground of a legitimate doubt, since I still find it possible to seek out grounds for the opposite of the knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-454":["Consciousness of the inadequacy of a ground for truth serves at least to assume, as still possible, that there are still grounds for the opposite of the thing. But doubt in the proper sense does not yet occur with the mere uncertainty of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-455":["The uncertainty of one thing or another, however, makes doubt entirely possible, so that we have [possible] grounds for the opposite. From having seen and observed that reason is very often in error there in fact often arises a kind of enmity towards reason, misology; much in the way that misanthropy, enmity towards men, arises from a suspicion of the whole human race, which is drawn from observing how little justly men act.",[]],"blomberg-s08-456":["‘That writer and author undoubtedly deserves the greatest thanks who teaches in his writings what will hold good perhaps a hundred years hence,’ says Terrasson. The world then still continues to be moved by its prejudices, which he sought to root out, until at last it begins to reflect correctly upon them,",[]],"blomberg-s08-457":["and in the course [685/6] of time gradually becomes reconciled, and concedes this.",[]],"blomberg-s08-458":["Nor can we always discover the grounds of the conflict in [men's] speech [*des Wiederstreites des Redens*; the reading is doubtful]. With people who have themselves written large books and works on this or that subject it is not good or advisable to dispute further about that subject. For we may well conclude for ourselves that they will not so easily be brought to regard so lightly, or to esteem so little, their own labour of many years etc., or to refute themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-459":["In an internal proof one is immediately conscious of the uncertainty in a mode of knowledge. An external proof, however, is the consciousness of the uncertainty of a mode of knowledge not immediately but mediately, according to the judgments of others. In many a question [*quaestio*] of reason there is to be found such plausibility that I cannot withhold my assent from it at all. But if I put it before others, they often hold the direct opposite, and only then do I become aware that, according to the heads and insights of others, my knowledge is not yet altogether indubitably certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-460":["In common usage the word ‘doubt’ signifies any uncertainty; and in this respect and in this sense doubt is either dogmatic or sceptical. The former is a doubt of decision, the latter a doubt of retardation, of postponement. From the former springs certainty; from the latter, closer examination and investigation, in order thereby eventually to attain to the right and indubitable certainty of the knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-461":["In dogmatic doubt we reject all investigation, and do not accept something against which we have, or believe we have, a well-founded doubt. We therefore decide summarily and say: in this matter it is quite impossible to attain any certainty. Dogmatic doubt thus regards very many modes of knowledge as if nothing whatever could be settled and established about them. A dogmatist [*dogmaticus*], consequently, is entitled that philosopher who passes the judgment upon universal truths of reason that they can be brought to complete certainty and reliability, but who refuses altogether to engage in the consideration of such truths of reason as are, or may be, still exposed to one doubt or another. The mathematician [*mathematicus*], for instance, is so constituted. All his proofs are decisive at a stroke: he propounds a proposition positively and at once proves it decisively [*decisive*], so that it is quite impossible that the judgment should not hold.",[]],"blomberg-s08-462":["The dogmatic method of philosophy [*methodus dogmatica philosophiae*] will therefore consist in this, that in all judgments we obtain a complete and secure certainty, are not content with irresolution, and leave nothing undecided. By this dogmatic method, however, we shall never fully establish the real certainty of a thing. We shall not thereby teach truths, but rather very often help to propagate errors. For in this method we seat ourselves, as it were, on the judgment-seat of human reason and pronounce that of this or that mode of knowledge no certainty whatever is to be obtained, and that all investigation is therefore always employed quite in vain. In this way we set, as it were, bounds and limits to the human understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-463":["All reflection, all endeavours to procure for oneself a true certainty of anything [691a] and to attain to conviction, are thereby entirely removed; and what else can arise from this but ignorance, and indeed even error?",[]],"blomberg-s08-464":["The dogmatic spirit of philosophy is therefore the proud language of the ignorant, who like to decide everything and care to examine nothing; whereas our understanding is in fact strongly inclined to test and examine everything carefully before it accepts and asserts it, and also first to look well about it, without blindly rejecting whatever comes before us. The dogmatists might be set in opposition to the dialecticians.",[]],"blomberg-s08-465":["Modes of knowledge are, *1mo*, such as can be known to be apodictically certain. These are modes of knowledge *a priori*, which are discerned through mere reason. In testimonies apodictic certainty can never be attained. Apodictic judgments are judgments *a priori*, and modes of knowledge of this kind are *dogmata*; such, for instance, is mathematics.",[]],"blomberg-s08-466":["In empirical modes of knowledge, however, no apodictic certainty whatever can be found. It is not a sheer impossibility that all those who tell us much of practice [*praxis*] in thinking, acting, judging and inferring should nevertheless deceive us, without on that account needing to be ill-intentioned. Everything that is apodictically certain is capable of a dogmatic mode of knowledge; everything else, properly speaking, only of a dialectical one.",[]],"blomberg-s08-467":["The ancients divided their philosophers into dogmatists and sceptics: into seekers, inquirers, observers [*speculatores*] of the truth, which they always sought but could never find.",[]],"blomberg-s08-468":["There were, further, intellectual philosophers, among whom Plato said: ‘Whatever good we wish to know and rightly discern must be done *a priori*, solely and entirely through reason. The senses by themselves contain nothing but deception.’",[]],"blomberg-s08-469":["The sensual philosophers, on the other hand, among whom Epicurus was reckoned the chief, said in turn:",[]],"blomberg-s08-470":["‘Everything that we are able to know *a priori* through reason is nothing but chimerical. The senses alone give the right and true certainty.’",[]],"blomberg-s08-471":["Thereupon, however, a third came and made peace between these two heated, contending parties, exclaiming: ‘I believe both equally little, or not at all [*gar nicht s*; the text breaks off here]",[]],"blomberg-s08-472":["Neither the intellectual nor the sensitive has certainty. Everything in the human understanding is nothing but confusion.’ From this arose formal scepticism, which appears to have originated with so celebrated and so highly estimable a man as Socrates. When the dogmatists either quarrelled among themselves, or asserted one proposition or another with all gravity and certainty, he would at first listen to them quite humbly. But soon, as it were an eager listener and pupil of the heated and impassioned dogmatists, he would begin to ask their counsel while they were disputing with one another. In this way he was then able to catch them in their own words, and knew how to confuse them so thoroughly that in the end they contradicted themselves outright, and so must often have become ridiculous to whole assemblies. He did this so often, and so opportunely, that the enemies of the proud dogmatists rejoiced in advance when Socrates visited their lecture-halls, while the dogmatists feared him as soon as he began to question them, since they were bound to answer him, as he knew how to present himself as a pupil eager for knowledge. From this there finally arose among his followers a pernicious propensity to doubt entirely each and every dogmatic mode of knowledge and its certainty. Pyrrho, however, may rightly be regarded as the proper founder and propagator of the sceptical sect. His *non liquet*, which he calmly cried out against the proud assertions of the dogmatists, and by which he at least often brought them up short, is well known everywhere, as are his careful and untiring endeavours to examine carefully all the propositions of the dogmatists.",[]],"blomberg-s08-473":["Meanwhile, if we consider it rightly, the sceptics have in a certain way done philosophy far more service than the proud dogmatists, although it is of course also true that the former finally degenerated, through misuse, into bitter mockery. But scepticism, or the method of sceptical doubt, in which we place distrust in ourselves, weigh the grounds for and against the knowledge which we have, and in this way endeavour to arrive at its complete certainty, is the cathartic [*katharcticon*], the best purgative of reason. It prevents errors as far as possible, leads men to further investigation, and is the way by which to arrive at the truth of the thing (though not, of course, all at once and suddenly, but slowly and gradually, through a more extended and closer testing).",[]],"blomberg-s08-474":["The doubt of postponement is therefore in fact a surer mark of the maturity of reason and of experience in the truth of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-475":["But if we were to ask in this connection which method of philosophising, the dogmatic or the sceptical, will be the most suitable and the best at the universities [*Accademien*], and will most please,",[]],"blomberg-s08-476":["we must nevertheless necessarily answer: the dogmatic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-477":["When a scholar mounts the rostrum here and dogmatically lays down something about this or that mode of knowledge, nothing can be easier for the listener. He need test nothing, examine nothing, but need only imprint upon his memory the little which the teacher says and propounds to him. In this way he remains in complete repose and comfort; he need only memorise. Doubting of modes of knowledge, on the other hand, is far from being so convenient [*commode*]; it is rather far more disquieting, and requires one's own reflection and investigation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-478":["Doubt can therefore be divided",[]],"blomberg-s08-479":["a) into the academic,",[]],"blomberg-s08-480":["B) into the sceptical.",[]],"blomberg-s08-481":["The school of Plato, the great dogmatist [*dogmaticus*], was entitled the Academy. Academic doubt is very different from sceptical doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-482":["1.) ‘The academic doubter proved and demonstrated by definition [*per definitionem*] that, properly speaking, nothing could be proved and nothing defined; which was, however, a *contradictio in adjecto*. And this was the doubt that has the appearance of being dogmatic.’",[]],"blomberg-s08-483":["2. The Pyrrhonian, that is, the sceptical doubter, on the other hand, said that to each and every one of our judgments, or at least to most of them, there can always be set up and opposed another judgment which asserts precisely the opposite of what is contained in the first. This, then, was really to be regarded rather as a very fine and excellent kind of observation than as a doubt deserving rejection. And it would indeed be desirable that this observation had been made correctly and carefully; consequences highly advantageous to the whole of human knowledge would certainly have arisen from it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-484":["But how is this? Is it not a *contradictio* to establish something in the very act of [committing] an error [*Fehler*], and to be a dogmatic doubter?",[]],"blomberg-s08-485":["Answer. The two are quite compatible. Dogmatic doubt is always bound up with a persuasion of certainty, namely, that in respect of this or that mode of knowledge nothing whatsoever can be established or asserted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-486":["Dogmatic doubt, therefore, consists simply in this, that one judges that in a given mode of knowledge one can never attain to its complete certainty, and that all further inquiry is therefore vain and expended to no purpose.",[]],"blomberg-s08-487":["Sceptical doubt, on the other hand, consists in this, that one is conscious of the uncertainty in a mode of knowledge, and is therefore impelled to inquire into it ever further, in order that by the help of careful investigations one may in the end none the less attain to certainty. The former, the dogmatist, rejects certainty entirely and at a single stroke; the latter, the sceptic, seeks it gradually. The origin of the expression ‘scepticism’ is properly as follows: σκέπτεσθαι. In Greek this word signifies to inquire, *scrutari*, *investigare*, *indagare*. The sceptic, therefore, is constantly inquiring; he tests and investigates; he places distrust in everything, but never without ground. In this respect he resembles a judge who weighs the grounds both for and against the case, and hears both plaintiff and defendant before he decides the case and passes judgment. He defers his final judgment a very long time, until he ventures to settle anything completely. These were the old and pure characteristics of scepticism and of a genuine sceptic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-488":["At the outset scepticism was indeed very reasonable; it was only the disciples who corrupted it and brought it into such ill repute.",[]],"blomberg-s08-489":["These latter were so subtle that they went so far as to say: everything is uncertain, even this is uncertain, that everything is uncertain. This was in truth a kind of purgative for human reason, of such a nature that, after it has completely cleansed our understanding of all impurities, that is, of all false illusion, prejudices and incorrect judgments, it carries itself off as well.",[]],"blomberg-s08-490":["Nothing, however, is in fact more harmful to the learned world than universal calm, agreement and peace; at the least these always hinder the improvement and increase of learning. It is much the same here as with the English, who, as soon as the Court and the subjects are in real agreement and wholly at one, already think themselves in danger and believe they have lost all their liberty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-491":["A universal resolve to doubt everything is of no use whatsoever; it is altogether absurd. There are, however, few men, or we might even say none at all, who would be inclined to so childish and harmful a mania for doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-492":["True scepticism, on the other hand, is certainly of great utility. As such it is nothing but an exact and careful examination of all *dogmata* that are put forward as apodeictic. If they really are so, and therefore hold good, it is only after this testing that they shine forth and strike the eye in their full value and their full strength.",[]],"blomberg-s08-493":["Every sceptical method is a genuine investigation of truth by way of deferment, in that one does not at once name [*nennet*] or reject anything, but first allows it to be disputed. By our very nature we are not at all inclined to deferment. In our own times those who are entitled sceptics are partly men who do not in the least deserve the name of philosophers (e.g. a Voltaire), and partly men who are not real *Academici*, but merely have, and as it were affect, a sceptical method, e.g. a Hume. Among the modern sceptics, however, one may reckon Bayle, etc.",[]],"blomberg-s08-494":["Among us, therefore, the name of a sceptic is at present for the most part regarded as something odious. One pictures, namely, a man who does nothing and applies himself to nothing save the endeavour to overthrow the most undeniable and certain truths. He is thus really taken for a dogmatic doubter, who believes that he is capable of judging about nothing, and that of no matter can one obtain a complete certainty which nothing can overturn. Formerly, however, an upright sceptic signified nothing other than one who merely defers his judgment until he has had occasion and time to consider this or that matter more closely, and only then ventures to conclude whether a mode of knowledge is to be accepted as true or rather rejected as false. The sceptical method is directly opposed to the dogmatic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-495":["The method of judging, however, in which one doubts more than one decides, and thus believes that our modes of knowledge may sooner be false than true, is *suspensio*: that kind of distrust towards the knowledge we have in which, though we likewise do not really settle anything, we none the less judge, and first investigate whether the grounds for or against the knowledge prevail.",[]],"blomberg-s08-496":["In this method we gather grounds of assent or of rejection, but we settle nothing; we do not yet decide at all. This kind of suspension is therefore directly opposed to dogmatic pride. The dogmatist asks: is something true or not? He thus decides at once. He, on the other hand, who suspends his judgment has no great confidence in himself; he always first inquires into, tests and investigates everything thoroughly, precisely in order thereby to discover the path to truth and to attain to genuine and true insight.",[]],"blomberg-s08-497":["Human nature is indeed far more inclined to decide than always to test, and to settle than always to inquire. For we are not at all content when we must leave anything, in particular, in our knowledge unfinished; we wish rather to settle everything, so that in a case of need we may have recourse to it as to a completely certain and reliable knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-498":["By decision our understanding is indeed better satisfied; for one believes that one has thereby already acquired something with which one may settle further modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-499":["The dogmatic manner of philosophising is therefore directly opposed to, and contradistinguished from, the problematic manner of philosophising.",[]],"blomberg-s08-500":["In the former one settles everything; in the latter one does not decide at all, but defers one’s judgments until one has duly sought out the grounds and counter-grounds and can then pass a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-501":["In physics one must above all philosophise problematically, and every circumspect scholar will without doubt employ this manner of philosophising. The doubt in the problematic manner of philosophising, however, is either sceptical, that is, inquiring, investigating and testing, or else dogmatic, that is, settling and deciding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-502":["When the history of philosophy comes to this point, namely, to the origin of the sect of the sceptics, it nearly always appeals first to Socrates. The sophists, who in earlier times, from vain ambition and a fancied science, believed they had the right to decide everything, and defended a case from whatever side happened to please them, were odious to Socrates, since he held them, and publicly declared them, to be merely sham sages (which indeed is all they really were).",[]],"blomberg-s08-503":["He judged that there are many things which it is not necessary for us to know; hence he had the motto, which sufficiently testified to his wisdom: »*quod supra nos, nihil ad nos*.« He laid it down, therefore, that our best knowledge is to come to know ourselves, and that man is the proper object of all philosophy. Hence he accepted nothing save what he had tested. He gave his assent blindly to no mode of knowledge, but inquired and investigated.",[]],"blomberg-s08-504":["When, therefore, anyone was inclined to decide something forthwith, he showed him the counter-grounds and difficulties which would compel him to suspend his judgment. This inclination to decide nothing without testing arose in him not from pride but from a high degree of modesty, since he did not trust himself overmuch, but really placed a kind of distrust in his modes of knowledge. This modesty he also impressed above all upon all his disciples. It did not, however, long remain with them. For his immediate disciple Plato already began to be somewhat more dogmatic, and from him there arose and originated the sect of the Academics, which was opposed to the sect of the sceptics.",[]],"blomberg-s08-505":["The latter [the sceptics] endeavoured to philosophise problematically; the former, the dogmatists, decided everything, and their whole philosophy consisted in deciding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-506":["Pyrrho was the first who brought the sect of the sceptics into repute, and his followers were therefore entitled Pyrrhonists.",[]],"blomberg-s08-507":["This sect did not properly doubt all truths, but prolonged the investigation and deferred the decision. By its doubts, therefore, it proved that the grounds for decision were not yet complete, and so led the dogmatists, who wished to decide everything, on to the path of circumspection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-508":["The sceptics, however, are also entitled *Zetetici*, seekers and inquirers, and that because they sought and inquired. With regard to all propositions, therefore, they will neither always decisively doubt them nor decisively hold and accept anything as true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-509":["The *Zeteticus*, therefore, is not one who has the maxim of rejecting each and every thing, or of deciding *positive* upon each and every thing without distinction, or of asserting blindly, but one who reflects upon modes of knowledge and tests them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-510":["Pyrrho had a certain motto for his sect: *non liquet*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-511":["To everyone who asserted his propositions with the greatest and most settled gravity he called out in reply *non liquet*, it is not yet settled, and then began to set forth his counter-argument.",[]],"blomberg-s08-512":["[This was] a skilful and learned way of humbling many a proud dogmatist and showing him his weak side. It is a safe motto for all pure reason. One must not accept everything at once, but test, and investigate and consider the objects. This sect of the Pyrrhonists, however, departed so far from its founder that it was afterwards supposed that Pyrrho himself had altogether denied the certainty of each and every *dogma*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-513":["That Pyrrho denied many *dogmata* and placed a just and well-considered distrust in them, and further that he rejected in particular many rational judgments, is indisputable and not to be denied. But that he denied each and every *dogma* is utterly false. He who accepts no *dogmata* at all can teach no morality whatsoever. There are certainly certain, so to speak, eternal principles of our reason which cannot be contested at all. Pyrrho was, however, further charged with having doubted the truth of all empirical judgments and with having distrusted them. But this is nothing but a fabrication without any ground. Pyrrho was a very wise man; but he would certainly have been a fool had he wished to reject all empirical judgments of whatever kind. At the outset, therefore, a Pyrrhonist did nothing but moderate his judgment. Afterwards, however, the sceptics did indeed seek to make everything uncertain, endeavouring to show that in all our modes of knowledge no certainty whatsoever can be found. And precisely from this arose *scepticismus problematicus, et dogmaticus*. The former doubted, indeed, but nevertheless first investigated, and sought by means of this investigation to attain to certainty. The latter, on the contrary, decided, and assumed that in no matter is complete certainty to be obtained.",[]],"blomberg-s08-514":["This inclination to hold nothing whatever as certain springs, in truth, from nothing other than a craving for [724a] completion. He who saw that in very many things one cannot attain to certainty, who doubted the truth of many things and *dogmata*, and had not accepted them until a testing had taken place, judged it better to doubt everything, and to contest the truth of each and every principle by adducing the counter-grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-515":["Of Pyrrho himself we properly have no writings left, except that Diogenes Laertius cites something of them. This much, however, is certain, that, much as with Epicurus, far more has been imputed to him and fastened upon him than he really did. It is said of him, e.g., that he denied the certainty of all experiences; that he therefore walked straight into all swamps and marshes; that he feared neither galloping horses nor deep chasms and ditches. And yet, alongside these reports so damaging to him, it is added that he lived to be ninety years old. This is a manifest proof that all such charges devised against him must be false; for with such recklessness he would surely have lost his life much sooner.",[]],"blomberg-s08-516":["Carneades, the founder of the last Academic school, departed widely from the teachings of Pyrrho, and actually asserted that in no matter can one obtain real certainty and arrive at certain truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-517":["Scepticism, however, is either *logicus*, logical. To this belongs, e.g., the *diallelus*: truth is supposed to consist in the agreement of a mode of knowledge with itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-518":["Many of the arguments of the sceptics had, and received, their names frequently from the *terminus medius* of the *syllogismus*. Thus one of their arguments, e.g., is entitled the Liar, which ran as follows: if you say that you lie, and in saying this speak the truth, then you speak the truth in lying; *atqui* you say that you lie, and in saying this; therefore you speak the truth in lying. The correct conclusion, however, should be: *ergo* you speak the truth in saying that you lie.",[]],"blomberg-s08-519":["Or scepticism is also *physicus*, physical, in which the certainty of all physical laws was called in question, e.g. the possibility of motion. Thus the ancient *Sceptici* had a certain argument which, again on account of the *medius terminus*, they named the Achilles. They reasoned, namely, as follows: if the swift Achilles were to run a race with a tortoise, the tortoise however having a mile’s start, then even if Achilles were able to run a hundred times faster than the tortoise, he would still never be able to overtake it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-520":["The grounds which he employed in defence of this argument were that all numbers are divisible *in infinitum*, and that therefore, however fast Achilles might run, there would still always remain a *spatium*, though a very small one, between him and the tortoise.",[]],"blomberg-s08-521":["Here, then, they reasoned merely through reason, without having recourse to experience. Had they done so, they would have found that Achilles must necessarily overtake the tortoise within 1¼ miles, provided only he could run 1000 times faster than the tortoise. Scepticism was further *vel moralis*; and this consisted in passing off all rules of virtue as fashions, or as customs and usages in the actions which men undertake. Thus, e.g., marriages between near kin were in Egypt not at all forbidden, as they now are among us. Among certain Negroes, again, the greatest, most excellent and last service of love which children who tenderly love their parents can render them is to strangle them when they advance in years. Among the Spartans, again, theft, provided only it could be done without being publicly noticed, was not punished at all; it was even permitted to steal, provided only it was done in secret.",[]],"blomberg-s08-522":["Scepticism was further *vel historicus*, historical, which consisted in believing each and every history and report, and also the narratives of ancient times, to be uncertain, fabricated and fabulous.",[]],"blomberg-s08-523":["Yet another kind of sceptic had the habit of always talking a great deal about the uncertainty of the senses and of occupying himself with it. They flatly denied the certainty of each and every experience. Thus, e.g., there was a sceptic who, having often maintained with many arguments that the senses cannot be trusted at all, wished to convince his friend of this quite surely and undeniably. He said to him: I have a large store in my larder every day; but when I go into it, count over the store, and come again the following day, this store is none the less smaller than it was the day before, a sure and irrefutable proof that the senses must deceive. His friend, who well perceived what must lie behind this, advised him to keep the key to the larder as carefully as possible. The sceptic’s servant, however, who was very cunning, soon managed to get hold of the key, and under this cover went on diminishing his master’s store as before, until his master at last caught him in the act and made him feel the just punishment for his theft, but in this matter also had to learn that the senses can well be trusted.",[]],"blomberg-s08-524":["Such a scepticism might well be entitled *dogmaticus*; for the *Sceptici* themselves were dogmatists, inasmuch as, though they doubted things, etc., they none the less believed they had a right to demand that every man should give assent to their proofs and accept their doubts. They thus contradicted themselves. For they said: everything without distinction is uncertain; and yet they asserted their propositions and ascribed to them an infallible certainty. They had, e.g., the proposition: everything is uncertain, and indeed in such a way that even this is uncertain, that everything is uncertain –",[]],"blomberg-s08-525":["As regards modern times in respect of scepticism, the dogmatic method was very customary at the beginning of the Reformation. Philosophy was then held and regarded as a necessary support of religion; hence one raised few doubts and counter-grounds against the certainty of philosophical propositions, but taught and laid down. When, however, a free way of thinking had later crept in among many nations, people began, with greater steadfastness and assurance, to doubt the truth of many propositions previously unchallenged, and not to believe and accept everything outright at once. Efforts were therefore soon made to resist the proud dogmatists in some measure and to show the groundlessness of many of their propositions. In the most recent times David Hume in particular is known as a *Scepticus*, though one who had a preponderant, indeed a somewhat extravagant, inclination to doubt. His writings, which appear before the learned world under the title Philosophical Inquiries, and are otherwise also entitled Miscellaneous Writings, contain political essays, literary treatises, and moral and also metaphysical essays; but all of them come out in scepticism. There is, however, in these writings of Hume a gentle, composed and unprejudiced examination. He first considers one side of a matter; he seeks out all possible grounds for it, and sets them forth in the best oratorical style. Then he takes up the other side also, likewise presents it for judgment quite impartially, and again sets forth all the counter-grounds with the same eloquence. At the end, however, in the conclusion, he appears in his true shape, as a sceptic proper: he complains of the uncertainty of all our knowledge in general, shows how little it is to be trusted, and in the end doubts, instead of concluding and settling which of these two modes of knowledge is true and which false. He would certainly be one of the best and most readable of authors, if only he did not have the preponderant propensity to doubt everything, but would seek, by means of the testing and investigation of modes of knowledge, to attain to true certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-526":["Cardanus, in his book *de vanitate Scientiarum*, likewise expresses scepticism and asserts that everything is uncertain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-527":["Voltaire is the *Scepticus* among the modern sceptics of our times. His scepticism, however, is far more harmful than it is useful. He brings forward grounds neither for nor against the matter. He inquires into and tests nothing at all, but doubts, without any proof, that a mode of knowledge is to be trusted at all. His grounds are therefore nothing but specious grounds, which may indeed deceive a simple man, but never an acute and reflective scholar. And for that very reason Voltaire is very dangerous for the multitude, and especially for the common man; for he *suppeditiert* [supplies] him with wholly false grounds for doubting the truth of this or that matter. If one desires closer instruction on the scepticism of modern times, one may with great profit read for this purpose the work which Haller published under the title Examination of the Sect that Doubts Everything. The above-mentioned writings of Hume are also in a certain way really to be recommended.",[]],"blomberg-s08-528":["In this *paragraphus* the author speaks of opinion in general. It is, however, contradistinguished from persuasion. In both there are uncertain modes of knowledge; but in persuasion one holds them to be certain, whereas in opinion one does not hold them to be certain. We must therefore above all endeavour to distinguish persuasion well from conviction. One and the same man who holds something as an opinion cannot yet hold it as a persuasion [*für eine Überredung halten*], and really errs as soon as he does so. Persuasion, therefore, is always blameworthy, since in it one holds something to be certain on insufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-529":["The distinction of persuasion from conviction, however, is rather difficult, and is therefore very seldom correctly determined. This arises from the fact that one has perhaps never yet tasted a true conviction, but only a sham one.",[]],"blomberg-s08-530":["Opinion, however, is properly no fault, but only an imperfect knowledge, a deficiency, in which something in our judgment has no sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-531":["We may always put forward many opinions without erring thereby, provided only we do not take them for persuasions. The rabble does this very often. Even if a mode of knowledge is wholly false, the opinion of it may none the less be quite certain and agree with the rules of understanding and of reason. E.g., if in ordinary life someone chooses a servant who comes of good parents and has also had a good upbringing, he is of the opinion that the servant is good and can be trusted. Here all reason is present and applied, although the opposite may very often be the case and my knowledge be false. Wolff held a quite reasonable opinion that there were inhabitants in the moon; for at that time discoveries were not yet so frequent as now, when it has been seen that the moon has no *atmosphaera*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-532":["An opinion may often be false as to its matter, although it is true as to its form; and conversely it may be true as to its matter but false as to its form. In the first case it is false, in the other always true. [*Sic*: the second clause seems inverted in the transcript.]",[]],"blomberg-s08-533":["If my knowledge agrees with the constitution of the thing itself, it is true; if it is contrary to the constitution of the thing, it is false. An opinion, however, may also be very true, reasonable and well-grounded as to its form, although as to its matter it does not agree with the constitution of the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-534":["An opinion is never a certainty; its opposite can always be the case. Nevertheless it may very often be reasonable and right.",[]],"blomberg-s08-535":["One acquires an opinion in favour of something from certain grounds which one recognises. The outcome of the matter, however, teaches us whether our opinion was true or false as to its matter. But whether the form of the opinion was right, this the *eventus* of the matter cannot properly show us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-536":["The chief fault of our modes of knowledge lies in what is formal in them, not in what is material. One may hold something to be true according to every form of reason, and have a well-grounded opinion in favour of something, if the *data* one now has for the matter outweigh the grounds of the opposite, although it is only the outcome of the matter that properly teaches one whether the opinion was true as to its matter, and [whether] the *eventus* was other than one had supposed.",[]],"blomberg-s08-537":["Each and every opinion is very changeable. To-day one may have more grounds for, to-morrow against, the opinion; whereupon one must at once change one’s opinion and adopt another, often opposed to the former. And this can in fact be in no way to the discredit of a true philosopher.",[]],"blomberg-s08-538":["Hypotheses are a quite special kind of philosophical opinion. Not all philosophical opinions, however, are hypotheses, but only some. They serve not [merely] to explain the appearances of the world, but to explain something in general, whether it arises from reason, or from experience, or from appearance.",[]],"blomberg-s08-539":["An opinion of the truth of a ground, [inferred] from its sufficiency for the consequence, is a hypothesis. When, namely, I hold something to be true *a priori* on account of its grounds, this is nothing other than an opinion. But when, on the contrary, I come to know the ground from the *sufficience* of the consequences [as leading back] to a ground, this is also an opinion, but not *a priori*; it is *a posteriori*, and therefore a hypothesis. A *hypothesis* is, as it were, a presupposition. Thus a physician frames a *hypothesis* when he treats the sick man; he must subsume everything under hypotheses and see whether there arise from them the consequences which he now sees before his eyes.",[]],"blomberg-s08-540":["If all the consequences which follow from an assumed *hypothesis* are true and agree with what is given, it is no longer an opinion but a certainty",[]],"blomberg-s08-541":["It is, further, a demonstrated truth. But since we are not at all, or very seldom, in a position to draw each and every consequence from it, [hypotheses] are for the most part still mere opinions. We must therefore say: if all the consequences which we have been able to draw from the *hypothesis* are true, I have great reason to infer that the *hypothesis* itself is certain and true. If a *hypothesis*, besides its sufficiency for its consequences, is also strengthened by other assurances, it is then confirmed. A *hypothesis subsidiaria*, however, is a hypothesis which presupposes a new hypothesis. The more I must assume in it, the less stable it is. The more unity there is in the hypothesis, the better it is; for otherwise fiction is for the most part added, and in this way there arises a philosophical romance, a romance of reason, such as, e.g., that of *Cartesius*, etc. etc., a world-system made of pure figments of the brain. Note, however, the rule: I may indeed invent in order to philosophise, but not invent in the very act of philosophising",[]],"blomberg-s08-542":["Every presupposition, however, has infinite consequences. Something is certain relatively when the grounds of assent outweigh all the grounds of the opposite; this is then a *comparative* certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-543":["Something is *absolute* certain, however, of which the opposite is impossible or absurd. Two presuppositions, if they are so constituted that one and the same consequences flow from the one as from the other, are one and the same representation, and not at all different.",[]],"blomberg-s08-544":["Thus the Copernican system is a hypothesis. The opposite can still always be represented as possible, however difficult this may be, and however easy this system is, and however rational and in agreement with all the *phaenomena* of the heavens. –",[]],"blomberg-s08-545":["With all hypotheses one is compelled, in order to procure them acceptance and certainty, [to show] that they can be confirmed and derived not merely *a posteriori*, through their relation to their consequences, but also through the *nexus a priori*, that is, through their relation to their grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-546":["From the sufficiency of a ground for a given consequence it can never be inferred with complete truth that this is the true ground of the consequence; for this is only an opinion. There is a ground of possibility, but not of certainty; for a consequence can often spring from several grounds and originate from more than one ground. The more sufficient a ground is, however, the more will one infer that it is the ground of the consequence. For all hypotheses are posited arbitrarily: I assume something, namely, and see whether or not it is sufficient for deriving a certain consequence from it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-547":["If a ground is sufficient for all the determinations contained in a certain given consequence, neither more nor fewer, if, that is, it is *adaequat*, so that neither fewer determinations can be inferred from it, and it is therefore complete, nor more, and it is therefore *praecise*, then it is the true ground, since it is constituted as it ought to be.",[]],"blomberg-s08-548":["Several grounds each of which is to be wholly *adaequat* to one consequence are impossible. Grounds which are different from one another cannot agree with one another in all cases. Grounds, however, which agree in all consequences are not different grounds, but one and the same ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-549":["If a ground is sufficient for something contained in the consequence, there is, according to logical rules, room for the opinion that the ground is the true one. If the ground is sufficient for more determinations of the consequence, the opinion is so much the more probable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-550":["Until finally, when the ground is sufficient for all the determinations, but also for no more determinations than are contained in the consequence, a true ground is present, and the hypothesis then ceases. The ground becomes a theory, a certainty.",[]],"blomberg-s08-551":["If a consequence is given me and I am to find the ground for it, this is a hypothesis. But if a ground is given me and I am to derive the consequences from it, this is indeed an opinion, but not a hypothesis. In a hypothesis the consequences are given, but the ground is invented.",[]],"blomberg-s08-552":["One of the most famous hypotheses is the Copernican. Copernicus assumed that the sun stands still and the planets move around it, and saw whether all the appearances of the heavenly bodies could be explained from this. This hypothesis of his, however, afterwards became a true opinion and a complete system, which deserves to find assent everywhere. When it is asked: why do all bodies have just this tendency to fall towards the earth? the consequence is here present, and the ground is to be found.",[]],"blomberg-s08-553":["*Cartesius* assumed that bodies form certain vortices; others again assumed that these vortices intersect; and so they sought to explain from this the fall of bodies, and that always towards the centre of the earth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-554":["In metaphysics there are many hypotheses. The proof of the existence of God is, to begin with, a hypothesis. We assume a being which is the most perfect of all, and then inquire whether from it we can derive grounds of explanation for that of which nature, experience, and the creatures upon the earth instruct us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-555":["Plato and Malebranche assumed, as a hypothesis, that men stand in a quite special connection with the Deity, and then considered whether they could explain from it the consequences which they discerned.",[]],"blomberg-s08-556":["Hypotheses are, however, in fact of very great use, and can never be wholly banished from human knowledge. All judges frame hypotheses, which upon better investigation they often reject again. Hypotheses are the way to theory, and often to a truth wholly opposed to them [ihr, i.e. to the hypothesis first framed].",[]],"blomberg-s08-557":["Through hypotheses we do not always find what we are seeking, but often something else. We make trial, we test; we assume something, and inquire whether or not the known consequences can be explained from it. If the former is the case, we accept the *hypothesis*; if the latter, we reject it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-558":["Since the truth of a ground may be inferred from its sufficiency for the consequence, and since this ground is invented [erdichtet], it follows that the more there is in a ground that is invented, the more follows from it *licite* [legitimately], and the newer it is. And the more hypotheses must be assumed in order to explain something, the more invention is involved.",[]],"blomberg-s08-559":["In some inferences we have to invent many *hypotheses subsidiariae* [subsidiary hypotheses]. When we have consequences and invent the ground for them, it may often happen that we have more consequences than the ground suffices to account for.",[]],"blomberg-s08-560":["We must then invent new grounds, and these new grounds are nothing other than the *hypotheses subsidiariae*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-561":["Such hypotheses, however, are really contrary to true philosophy, and display *effren is fingendi Licentia* [an unbridled licence of invention]. If I am prepared to invent everything, I can explain everything. Unity is therefore a chief property of hypotheses: that is, we assume a ground which suffices to explain all the determinations and consequences of the thing that we know, without needing further to help ourselves out by the assumption of *hypotheses subsidiariae*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-562":["The unity of a hypothesis is a strong ground for [holding] that the ground is the true one, and that there can be no other from which just as many consequences could be explained.",[]],"blomberg-s08-563":["A chief ground of the truth of a hypothesis is also this: that we show that the ground which we have devised for the sake of its sufficiency for the consequences deserves to be assumed on other grounds as well. For here we show from other grounds that what was invented must be assumed, and we thus confirm the truth of the hypothesis.",[]],"blomberg-s08-564":["All holding-to-be-true arises from a twofold connection: either because the ground suffices for the derivation of all the consequences, or because it agrees further with other grounds which have a similarity to it, and is, so to speak, *aequivoce* [equivocal]. In all hypotheses, therefore, we shall first show the sufficiency of the *Fiction is historicae* [historical fiction] for the consequences, and then also the agreement of the fiction with other grounds, so that there are still other grounds from which some of the consequences can be derived. Hypotheses must first be probable as inferences, but must at the same time also be capable of confirmation through other grounds. For example, he who would explain a thunderstorm assumes an electrical kind of matter in the air, and shows afterwards on other grounds that a sulphurous and electrical matter must actually be present in the air. On the one side hypotheses have something very prejudicial to philosophy, in so far as they give licence to invention. On the other side, however, they are again very advantageous, namely when the *fictiones* are heuristic, that is, have as their purpose the discovery of something.",[]],"blomberg-s08-565":["The hypothesis is thus a method of our reason and of our understanding which in philosophy is really quite indispensable. All hypotheses must be assumed as strictly problematic. Consequently we must not be obstinate about any *hypothesis*, holding fast to it in such a manner that we cannot give it up as soon as we discern its falsity or uncertainty. Often many attempts at invention are wholly in vain, since no truth is discovered by them. Hypotheses therefore have this further disadvantage: when they are ingeniously devised, and we have observed their agreement with many consequences, they take deep root in our understanding, and even when we afterwards discern their falsity we do not readily give them up. Thus, for example, if this or that hypothesis were sufficient for new and correct consequences, and only one consequence, say the hundredth, contradicted the ground, we would rather reject this one, although it is evidently true, than give up the hypothesis that seems so good. It is therefore a very sad state of affairs when in some age nothing but hypotheses prevail which are false, and therefore do not suffice for discovering the true ground of the matter. Such hypotheses do more harm than good. They do not suffice to explain everything; and if, in addition, we accept them as true, we obtain, instead of true, nothing but false modes of knowledge and representations. He who will not give up false hypotheses because he has hatched them and they seem to him so probable is like a man who has brought up a child with much trouble and care, and is then unwilling to cast it away, lest he lose all his labour, trouble and expense.",[]],"blomberg-s08-566":["Nevertheless such hypotheses, into which nothing but false hypotheses are forged, serve [the purpose] that in succeeding ages",[]],"blomberg-s08-567":["true hypotheses are discovered. For it is impossible that one who knows all the false paths that are possible should not at last find the right one. The remaining propositions which the author",[]],"blomberg-s08-568":["adduces are for the most part tautological, and therefore deserve no closer examination or explanation (the author often proceeds in this way). When in § 183, in the seventh rule, he says that one should not have devised too many opinions, we may remark: much may be a perfection as regards quality, but not a perfection as regards quantity, namely when it exceeds a certain degree which would constitute the perfection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-569":["This degree, however, is difficult, indeed almost impossible, to determine. For example, to say that one should not be too open-hearted requires no art. The art consists in determining quite precisely that degree of open-heartedness and of reticence which one must not overstep if one is not to be too open-hearted or too reticent.",[]],"blomberg-s08-570":["There are countless illusions [Blendwercke] in morals. Rules are given which say nothing more than what we have long known; and so there is nothing whatsoever new in them for our understanding.",[]],"blomberg-s08-571":["Certainty is either",[]],"blomberg-s08-572":["a) comparative or b) apodeictic.",[]],"blomberg-s08-573":["Comparative certainty is the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true to the grounds of the opposite.",[]],"blomberg-s08-574":["Apodeictic certainty, on the other hand, is absolute, and consists in the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true to the sufficient ground.",[]],"blomberg-s08-575":["History is merely comparatively certain, and never apodeictically. Morals, however, and especially the *jus naturale* [natural right] within it, contain many apodeictic certainties.",[]],"blomberg-s08-576":["Distinct again from apodeictic certainty is mathematical certainty in intuition, which is evident. Mathematics presents its propositions",[]],"blomberg-s08-577":["1: discursively",[]],"blomberg-s08-578":["2: intuitively",[]],"blomberg-s08-579":["Moral and physical propositions, and not even metaphysical propositions, can possibly be mathematically certain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-580":["Evidence is assuredly always lacking to them, although they are apodeictically certain. The title 'arbitrary truths' amounts to nothing; for can a truth then also be false? What a self-contradictory expression!",[]],"blomberg-s08-581":["A proposition, however, is 1: true",[]],"blomberg-s08-582":["2 false",[]],"blomberg-s08-583":["3 arbitrary; and then it is not settled whether it is the one or the other, that is, it may be true, but it may also be false. I cannot form the object [Gegenstand] arbitrarily; but I can arbitrarily dispose the subject so as to know the object [Object] in the way most convenient to me, and render the object comprehensible to myself in the most commodious and convenient manner.",[]],"blomberg-s08-584":["Everything that is mediately certain is demonstrable [erweislich]. What is immediately certain is indemonstrably certain, that is, it is so undeniable that it requires no means of certainty or of proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-585":["Proof [Beweis] as a means of apodeictic certainty is demonstration. Proof as a means of comparative certainty is probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-586":["A mathematical demonstration is, moreover, intuitive and evident: it speaks of the universal *in individuo* [in the individual]. Who can comprehend this? We can do so just as little as [we can comprehend] how we are able to see everything with our eyes, small as they are. We continually see objects that meet our eyes, without giving the least thought to how this comes about.",[]],"blomberg-s08-587":["In this paragraph the author says that we should guard against each and every false mode of knowledge, and avoid a sham conviction or sham persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-588":["This, however, everyone knows already. He would have said something excellent if he had stated the marks by which persuasion can be distinguished from conviction.",[]],"blomberg-s08-589":["Persuasion consists not in material falsity but in a false form, in that an opinion is taken for complete certainty. The matter itself may well be true; but this is not the manner, the way, of attaining to truth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-590":["In persuasion we need not err as regards the object; we err as regards the form, in that we take the grounds for opinion, [that is, for] holding-to-be-true on insufficient grounds, for grounds of certainty on sufficient grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s08-591":["In fact, however, we have always had only a theory of morals, a speculation upon the moral conduct of men, and no actual practical moral philosophy. Hence no one can demand that the *notae* [marks] be stated by which false persuasion is distinguished from conviction in the true sense. This has still to be discovered. Likewise we have only a theoretical and no practical logic whatsoever.",[]],"blomberg-s08-592":["[Some] persuasions spring from logical grounds, others from aesthetic grounds, which come not from the understanding and reason but arise from a certain inclination. We are often not indifferent whether we find a thing true or false, and in that case there is a strong ground for our being persuaded of a mode of knowledge. Man is in general subject to a great many illusions; they play with him as with a ball. He is most easily persuaded who is very readily inclined to be thrown into one affect or another.",[]],"blomberg-s08-593":["The multitude, the rabble, the common man, is therefore for the most part very easily, indeed most easily, persuaded by anyone who understands the art of winning people over. But he who can be easily persuaded can also be easily thrown into affect. The two are thus almost inseparably bound up with each other.",[]],"blomberg-s08-594":["Science [Wißenschaft] (which properly does not belong here at all, and the consideration of which the author has introduced here wrongly and in a certain way unfittingly) may be opposed to conjecture and to opinion. To believe or to opine something is very different from knowing [Wißen]. For to know means to hold something to be true with sufficient certainty and on sufficient grounds, so that no doubt remains or can remain.",[]],"blomberg-s08-595":["As regards the degrees of holding-to-be-true, they are the following:",[]],"blomberg-s08-596":["1: Opining, or holding-to-be-true on any insufficient ground which nevertheless has more weight than the ground of the opposite that I know and discern.",[]],"blomberg-s08-597":["2: Believing, or holding something to be true in such a degree that it suffices for acting and for resolving myself to act.",[]],"blomberg-s08-598":["3: Knowing, or cognising the truth sufficiently.",[]],"blomberg-s08-599":["The word 'wissen' [to know] is the origin or root-word of 'Gewissheit' [certainty]. Certainty, however, we have already explained above. A science is nothing other than a mode of knowledge through which we know something. The word 'science' is contradistinguished from art; hence in common life we usually say, conversely, 'that is not a science but an art.' This happens, for example, with riddles: we exclaim, 'that is not a science but an art' (a great difference).",[]],"blomberg-s08-600":["There are actions which require a certain art; but there are also, on the other hand, certain actions for which a science is requisite and necessary. (To set these concepts apart:) that is entitled an art which requires a specific perfection of practice; that is entitled a science which requires a specific perfection of theory. Every art involves a practical skill in acting. But the practical skill that belongs to an art cannot be acquired merely by rules and particular prescriptions; one must also possess a special aptitude for it. Further, science is opposed to criticism [Critic]. All theoretical knowledge is either a doctrine or a criticism. Criticism, however, is in fact nothing but a species of theoretical (and therefore not practical) knowledge concerning any existing product, which shows us the rules according to which this product has been executed. Doctrine, on the other hand, is that theoretical knowledge in which we find the grounds of how a thing can be executed, or the rules according to which a good product can be brought forth.",[]],"blomberg-s08-601":["A criticism is not, properly, a science; for otherwise we should at once be able to bring forth good products ourselves. With criticism, however, this happens only after a long time, when we gradually learn to apply the rules we have come to know to ourselves. In common usage the word 'science' is often falsified, and other concepts are bound up with it than the word actually contains.",[]],"blomberg-s08-602":["Certainty requires, not the truth itself of the thing that we know, but our holding it to be true. The greater the probability of a thing, the more is the understanding constrained to hold it true. This necessity of accepting the thing lies, however, not in the *objectum* itself but in the subject, because the grounds of the opposite in no way come up to those which I recognise as making for the truth of the thing. This subjective necessity of accepting something is very deceptive and changeable, since it is not grounded in the *objectum*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-603":["The author here speaks also of mathematical certainty of the first and of the second rank. The former rests on reason, the latter on experience; and the two certainties therefore differ in their *data*. To all mathematical certainty there belong a sufficient ground, precision, and a certain intuiting of the knowledge, that is, the intuitive. Mathematical certainty carries with it a certain sensibility, for it is found only in the case of quantities, never in that of qualities.",[]],"blomberg-s08-604":["In philosophy there are no intuitive modes of knowledge; these occur only in mathematics, which has to do with magnitudes. In mathematical certainty, as in philosophical, there are distinctness and sufficiency; but in the former alone are precision and evidence to be met with. Historical certainty can never possibly become apodeictic. The grounds of the truth of a historical certainty, however, are",[]],"blomberg-s08-605":["1: Purity of insight.",[]],"blomberg-s08-606":["2, correctness of the faculty of judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-607":["3 truthfulness; further",[]],"blomberg-s08-608":["4 the inclination to truth, or to speak the truth; and finally",[]],"blomberg-s08-609":["5: uprightness. For the last two grounds are the causes of the third, namely of the truthfulness of him who asserts the thing. But from consequences we cannot infer apodeictically, since there may be far more than one consequence of one and the same thing. Certain historical modes of knowledge will have such grounds of holding-to-be-true that they suffice for practical, though not indeed for theoretical, certainty. Apodeictic certainty is consequently distinguished from practical certainty not properly *in gradu* [in degree] but only *in specie* [in kind], even if some would maintain the former.",[]],"blomberg-s08-610":["In this paragraph the author calls hypotheses arbitrary truths. An arbitrary proposition makes something true, provided only that it does not contradict itself. Such an arbitrary proposition is entitled *positio*. Nothing that is objective can ever be assumed arbitrarily; only what is subjective is fixed by choice [Willkühr]. All arbitrary propositions concern what we ought to do, the practical, and so never teach what the thing is. A *positio* occurs only in respect of practice, and in this it differs from a hypothesis. Arbitrary *positiones* which concern actions occur chiefly in mathematics: for example, in the case of the circle, that it is divided into 360 degrees; in logarithms, the relation of the arithmetical to the geometrical number; proportions; progressions.",[]],"blomberg-s08-611":["The word 'hypothesis' therefore does not mean an arbitrary proposition at all, but an opinion on certain grounds *a posteriori* which are sufficient for these or those consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-612":["In the case of *positiones* I do not properly fix anything that lies in the object, but only what I arbitrarily wish and will to have in it. A mathematical magnitude rests on the arbitrary and optional repetition of unity. What holds here of a particular kind of triangles, circles, etc., holds at the same time of all triangles, circles, etc., of this kind. One triangle, circle, square, etc., stands in place of all the others that are possible. Every triangle, for example, contains 180 degrees; this holds of all other possible triangles. Every square has four equal sides and four right angles; this holds of each and every possible square, etc., etc. Here, then, the universal is always seen and considered in a single case.",[]],"blomberg-s08-613":["Every certain proposition is either indemonstrably, that is, immediately, or mediately, that is, demonstrably, certain. That mode of knowledge which contains in itself the ground of the truth of another mode of knowledge is the argument [Beweis-thum]; and the connection of the modes of knowledge",[]],"blomberg-s08-614":["with their truth is the proof. There are thus properly three elements to be found here, namely",[]],"blomberg-s08-615":["1: the argument, or the matter",[]],"blomberg-s08-616":["2 the proof, or the *consequentia*, or the form",[]],"blomberg-s08-617":["3 and finally the conclusion, which follows from the *consequens*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-618":["In every proof what we properly examine is not the proposition to be proved but the *ratio probandi* [ground of proof]. The matter of the proof is the *medius terminus* [middle term], and the form is the *consequentia*. Some proofs are empirical, others rational; and the latter are either mathematical, in which case they are entitled *demonstrationes*, or philosophical, and [then] incomplete *probationes*. If convincing proofs lack evidence, the intuitive, and precision, exactness, they are not yet *demonstrationes*",[]],"blomberg-s08-619":["All rational proofs, even supposing that they were sufficient, are therefore not yet *demonstrationes*; and still less are empirical proofs *demonstrationes*. Empirical proofs may often be very sufficient and evident; nevertheless they are not *demonstrationes*, for they are not rational. The word 'indemonstrable' here signifies, as we have already mentioned above, those certain modes of knowledge of whose certainty and truth we neither may nor can doubt.",[]],"blomberg-s08-620":["No demonstrable knowledge is clear through itself; it is held true only as a consequence, by means of other modes of knowledge. If, therefore, we hold demonstrable knowledge to be indemonstrably certain, we err in the form. The proofs of the existence of God, of His attributes and of His works, are not indemonstrable but demonstrable. In the case of indemonstrably certain modes of knowledge I postulate, I as it were demand, assent. In the case of demonstrably certain modes of knowledge, on the other hand, I as it were beg and ask the concept from others. All immediate experiences are indemonstrably certain, and cannot be proved at all: for example, there is a sun in the sky, the barometer has fallen or risen. All this is immediately certain, and therefore indemonstrable.",[]],"blomberg-s08-621":["When, however, I establish universal judgments from experiences, that is, propositions of experience, they are always demonstrable. All rational and indemonstrable modes of knowledge are *axiomata*, and they are either synthetic or analytic. In every judgment we conceive the subject as a mark of a thing which may otherwise be unknown to me; we know, therefore, only the mark, this is known to us. For example, the proposition 'a body is divisible' is analytic; for to the subject to which [the concept of] body belongs, divisibility also belongs, and to the subject to which divisibility belongs, [the concept of] body also belongs. But [the proposition] 'a body has an attractive force' is synthetic; for attractive force, apart from all experience, does not lie in the concept of a body.",[]],"blomberg-s08-622":["All analytic propositions, in which the predicate lies in the notion of the subject, come under the law of identity. Synthetic propositions, on the other hand, whose predicate lies not in the notion of the subject but in a mark distinct from the subject, do not come under the *principium identitatis et contradictionis* [principle of identity and of contradiction]. This principle relates only to analytic propositions. In synthetic propositions a predicate is added to the notion of the subject which is not contained in it; and all propositions so constituted can be known only intuitively, not through reason. Through reason we can know no co-ordination whatsoever, for reason only subordinates. All synthetic propositions are either immediate propositions of experience, or intuitive propositions which are produced by the understanding and do not lie in the notion of the subject. Analytic propositions, however, can be extracted *per analysin* from the concept of the subject; for they are contained in the notion of the subject and subordinated to it. Here reason can occupy itself, and analyse and set out the subordinate concepts. For example, 'gold does not rust': this is a synthetic proposition; it is evident solely from immediate experience, and cannot properly be derived at all *per analysin* from the notion of gold. If something is to be postulated, it must be so constituted that it convinces absolutely everyone. In a proof, falsity as regards the matter is only accidental, but as regards the form it is essential. False matter can be corrected, whereas false form is wholly *incorrigib ile*. Further, falsity of form is far more widespread and general than falsity of matter. The *proton pseudos* [first falsehood], in which someone infers absurdly, or else assumes false propositions, likewise lies in the form; for it is remarkable that the form should be subject not only to many errors but even to errors that cannot be corrected.",[]],"blomberg-s08-623":["To prove something *directe* is to prove it in connection with its grounds. That is an ostensive proof; [a proof] in which one shows that what is the opposite is false is, then, an apagogic proof.",[]],"blomberg-s08-624":["To prove something *indirecte*, on the other hand, is to prove it in connection with its consequences. This latter, however, can never take place otherwise than *negative*, negatively. But we never know all the consequences of a thing; many of them remain unknown to us. Hence it is that the indirect proof can never become a demonstration, though it can well become a philosophical probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-625":["If we wish to prove that a mode of knowledge is false, this can properly be done The opposite of such a false mode of knowledge, when drawn, already proves the partial falsity of the knowledge. Hence it is that the apagogic inference is so much favoured, since it is the only one that can yield an apodeictic certainty, whereas the direct inference is capable of yielding only a comparative certainty. The apagogic proof is also that which comes nearest to evidence. It is thus also far easier than the direct proof, since it reduces the opposite *ad absurdum*; and it is established that absurdity always strikes the eye far more forcibly than any truth whatsoever. Just so, the greatest pleasure when it has ceased is not nearly so keenly felt by me as the opposite pain, and a pleasure is heightened by preceding pain. Truth, for example, has not nearly so striking, so arresting a character as the representation of the absurdity of the opposite. The defect of this proof, however, is that while it can prove the opposite of the proposition to be false, it cannot prove the proposition from itself. In this respect the direct, the genetic, proof is far better than the apagogic; for it discloses not only the truth but at the same time its *genesis*, the source from which it is generated.",[]],"blomberg-s08-626":["The apagogic proof, then, however favoured it may be, is employed only *ad interim*, etc. Right and true insight is properly obtained only through the direct, genetic proof. A mode of knowledge is proved *directe* when its truth can be established from its relation to its grounds; *indirecte*, on the other hand, when its truth flows from its relation to its consequences. Direct proofs are properly genetic, and are conducted *per rationes essendi, non vero cognoscendi* [through grounds of being, not of knowing]; indirect proofs are conducted *per rationes cognoscendi, non essendi*. To infer affirmatively from consequences to grounds is not possible at all; this must always take place negatively",[]],"blomberg-s08-627":["For me to say that something has been demonstrated, the proof must be so constituted that it convinces everyone; that is, it must hold not for me alone but for others also. Demonstration requires evidence, such that the knowledge and its proof are so constituted that it is impossible to withhold one's assent. It is evident from this that the word 'demonstration' must be used very sparingly. Much may be a philosophical probation, but not a demonstration. Mathematical proofs, however, are of such a kind that they are all *demonstrationes*. Nothing, therefore, can be passed off as a demonstration until experience has taught that it is impossible to withhold one's assent from it and to oppose it, or until other men also agree that it is no longer possible to doubt the knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s08-628":["*Demonstrationes* are therefore very seldom to be met with in philosophy. No aesthetic proof, then, can be a demonstration; for an aesthetic probation arises solely from the agreement of the knowledge with our feeling and our taste, and is therefore nothing but a persuasion.",[]],"blomberg-s08-629":["I can prove something to someone aesthetically as soon as I know his passions and his feeling, and am able to move them. This probation, however, is not universally valid, and is therefore wrongly entitled a demonstration by our author.",[]],"blomberg-s08-630":["Everyone, indeed, has an inclination to demonstrate, and evidence pleases everyone; it is only a pity that it is so difficult and so seldom to be attained.",[]],"blomberg-s08-631":["An excessive inclination to demonstrate, our author says, is the mania for demonstration [Demonstrations-Sucht]. Charlatans have, properly, no mania for demonstrating themselves; rather they deceive those who are sick with it. The mania for demonstrating that springs from pedantry is the true mania for demonstration, and consists properly in an inclination either to demonstrate everything or to accept nothing that is not demonstrated and established beyond doubt. Pedantry in demonstrating thus confidently believes that in the whole of human knowledge one cannot make any progress whatsoever without demonstration. This pedantry may nevertheless very often be combined with a very sharp criticism of knowledge. It is therefore not, properly, ignorance, nor error; rather, one perhaps does not demonstrate at all, because something cannot be demonstrated, and prefers not to accept at all such modes of knowledge as cannot be demonstrated. This mania can be much nourished if one knows how to conceal the weak side of demonstrations. One lays it aside, however, when one knows and discerns that in many matters nothing whatever can be demonstrated, and so recognises that much is taken for a demonstration which has not been proved at all, or is only a probation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-632":["In our present age, however, no mania for demonstration prevails, but a certain shallowness, a kind of gallantry even in learned knowledge. The latter is nevertheless always far more tolerable than the former. For shallowness requires only an addition in order to become thorough; it is thus wholly capable of improvement. The mania for demonstration, on the other hand, is incorrigible: one very often takes false modes of knowledge for true, is even proud of this very falsity, and has no desire at all to be instructed in the opposite and rightly convinced of it.",[]],"blomberg-s08-633":["Sensation is the representation of our present state in so far as this state arises from the presence of a certain object. Sometimes representations arise in us of which we ourselves are the authors, but whose presence we derive from the existence of a thing; and then it happens that we often confuse fictions with sensations, and in this way commit an error. We can often imagine [erdichten] something to ourselves, but this is no sensation at all. Thus, for example, the fear of an impending pain is distinct from the pain that is present: the fear of the pain is a fiction, whereas the pain itself is a sensation. About sensations nothing further can be said or reasoned [raisoniren], that is, subtilised [vernünftelen]; for they are the *principium materiale* [material principle] of all our representations.",[]],"blomberg-s08-634":["A representation of sensation cannot be invented by us at all; it must actually have been given to us by means of the senses. Experience and sensation differ in form and in degree. In matter the two are the same; but in experience there is in addition a form, namely reason. Experience is nothing but a reflected sensation, or a sensation expressed through a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-635":["Experiences, that is to say, are not mere concepts and representations, but also judgments. For example, the representations of heat or of cold are concepts of experience. They are universal properties of things, and these can be discerned not merely through the senses but, properly, through judgments. Irrational animals therefore have no experience, but only sensations. Everyone who can describe the objects of his experience has experience; for description requires not merely sensation but also a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s08-636":["Mere sensations, without judging about them, cannot be described. Every experience is properly immediate, and mediate experiences are, according to our author, *contradictiones in adjecto*. The judgment which is made immediately on the occasion of sensations is the judgment of experience.",[]],"blomberg-s08-637":["Every judgment is either empirical or rational. Some may be empirical as regards the matter but rational as regards the form; others, however, may be rational both as regards the matter and as regards the form. The form of all our experiences is rational. All experiences have the form of reason, and without it they would not be experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-638":["In experiences we must be active, through reason; in sensations, on the other hand, we are merely passive. The proofs of certain modes of knowledge are always rational as regards the form; as regards the matter, however, they may spring either from experience or from reason. A proof without the form of reason is no proof at all. If we wish to prove something we must, after all, infer from certain *data*, even supposing that the matter springs from the senses, or is a sensation; and therefore every proof is rational. Nevertheless it is entitled empirical when its matter is a sensation, or when the ground of proof derives from experience. When we have an empirical proof, the ground of proof is immediately a sensation, and so, in accordance with experience, always derives from sensation. Immediate sensations cannot be proved.",[]],"blomberg-s08-639":["We must learn to distinguish carefully between inferences from experiences and the *judicia discursiva* [discursive judgments] drawn from experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s08-640":["Nothing, however, is more deceptive than that we should infer covertly from certain examples, without knowing that we are inferring, and should therefore pass off an inference from experience as an immediate experience. Immediate experience shows us only what is in our sense, without our inferring anything in the process.",[]],"blomberg-s08-641":["The judgment which expresses the appearance is an immediate judgment of experience. If, now, we confuse the judgment which expresses the appearance with those judgments which express the causes of the appearance, we mistake the *judicium discursivum* [discursive judgment] for the *judicium intuitivum* [intuitive judgment]. The *judicium discursivum* is, therefore, that which expresses the cause of the appearance; the *intuitivum*, that which expresses the appearance itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-642":["The care not to confuse *judicia discursiva* with *judicia intuitiva* is of especially great importance, and it is indispensable that they be properly applied, lest we fall into various errors. All proofs are derived from reason. The establishing of a mode of knowledge from an immediate experience is not a proof but a fact, an *inspectio ocularis* [ocular inspection], as it were.",[]],"blomberg-s08-643":["A proof from reason, however, of which the author speaks in § 204, is a proof which arises from reason both in respect of its ground of proof, that is, as regards its matter, and in respect of its form, and which therefore requires no experience. Proofs from reason have indeed as their ground of proof propositions which arise from reason; but these propositions, in their first origin, arise in part from experience. A proof from reason is not, therefore, always a proof of pure reason, without any employment of experience. A proof through pure reason can be carried out only in regard to such modes of knowledge as are not objects of the senses at all. If modes of knowledge are even in part *objecta* of the senses, they must already, as regards their first *principia*, be derived from experience. Proofs of pure reason have, therefore, a place only in mathematics and metaphysics. What can be demonstrated through pure reason not only does not require a proof from experience, but does not even permit of one. There have been some who have sought to devise an experimental geometry; but all their efforts have come to nothing but child's play.",[]],"blomberg-s08-644":["Experiences permit of no universal judgments except as to possibility. Experience can never teach me with apodeictic certainty that, for instance, all men must die, but only that all men who have lived hitherto have died. A proof from pure reason, on the other hand, proves universal propositions, and therefore cannot be further supported by proofs from experience, since these prove no universal propositions. A demonstration from pure reason is so different from a proof from experience that, were the two to be combined and joined together, a bastard would necessarily result. Proofs from pure reason are like a light before which all proofs from experience vanish, just as the light of the moon is wholly dispensable, and vanishes, when the rays of the sun all at once make day. Whatever can be proved through pure reason receives from experience no new proof, but only mere illustration and elucidation. To have the idea of a thing is very difficult.",[]],"blomberg-s08-645":["One can derive much benefit from a science, and can indeed have sound knowledge of it, without yet having an idea of it. This is evidently the case, for instance, with logic and with jurisprudence.",[]],"blomberg-s08-646":["Logic is a science which sets forth the actions that arise as regards form. All *conceptus* [concepts] are either (1) *empirici* [empirical], which arise from experience; (2) *intellectuales* [intellectual], which arise from the understanding; or (3) *arbitrarii* [arbitrary], that is, concepts arising from choice. There are intuitions in concepts, that is, *perceptiones communes*, universally valid or common representations. All *perceptiones*, however, are",[]],"blomberg-s08-647":["(1) *intuitivae*, intuitive;",[]],"blomberg-s08-648":["(2) *reflexivae*, requiring reflection.",[]],"blomberg-s08-649":["The *perceptiones reflexivae*, again, are",[]],"blomberg-s08-650":["(A) *communes* [common];",[]],"blomberg-s08-651":["(B) *singulares* [singular].",[]],"blomberg-s08-652":["In logic, however, all *perceptiones* must be *communes*. If they are applied separately, they receive the names of particular concepts. Through logical abstraction [Absonderung] no knowledge whatsoever can arise. Through a *conceptus communis* I can consider an *objectum*",[]],"blomberg-s08-653":["(a) *in abstracto*, or else (b) *in concreto*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-654":["The term *conceptus abstractus*, however, is a poor expression. To what end, then, may the art of the human understanding be able to form universally valid concepts, *conceptus communes*? They serve to assign to each concept its place, and they are, as it were, the representatives of a multitude of things; they therefore serve especially for elucidation. The common concept contains many [concepts] under itself; but these concepts which it contains under itself contain it in themselves: it is contained in them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-655":["[This is] a wonderful counter-relation. For instance, humanity extends to all men, and yet humanity lies in all men. The multitude of things which I can think under the *conceptus communis* constitutes the *sphaera conceptus* [sphere of the concept]. The more things a mark belongs to, the greater a concept is in its extension, that is, in its *capacitas*, and the less, on the other hand, does it contain in itself.",[]],"blomberg-s08-656":["The concept of something holds of everything and contains nothing in itself; and so it always is. Every *conceptus communis* can be regarded as a *conceptus superior*, a higher concept; yet it is specially so named only in so far as it in turn contains other *conceptus communes* in itself [in sich], as, for instance, man [contains] male. There is thus a subordination of our concepts. A *conceptus communis* is entitled, in respect of the concepts contained under it, the *genus*, the kind [Gattung]; in respect of the concepts in which it is contained, the *species*. Thus, for instance, man is the *genus* in respect of males, etc.; but in respect of a rational being man is in turn the *species*. The limit of mutually subordinated concepts is the *conceptus summus*, that is, the concept which is indeed a *genus* but not a *species*. The *conceptus infimus*, on the other hand, will be a *species* which has no further *genus*. A *conceptus singularis*, however, has no *sphaera*. The *genus summum* and the *individuum* are thus the two limits of logically subordinated concepts. Every *conceptus* has an extension of the concepts to which it applies, the *sphaera notionis*; and the proposition is well known: *Quo major est sphaera conceptus, eo minus in illo est contentum, et vice versa* [The greater the sphere of a concept, the less is contained in it, and conversely]. A *conceptus* whose *sphaera* is only a part of the *sphaera* of another *conceptus* is entitled a *conceptus angustior* [narrower concept].",[]],"blomberg-s08-657":["A *conceptus singularis* has no extension whatsoever, and therefore no *sphaera*.",[]],"blomberg-s08-658":["*Cognitio a priori* is a [mode of] knowledge, and *probatio a priori* is a proof from above downwards. *Cognitio a posteriori*, on the other hand, is a [mode of] knowledge, and *probatio a posteriori* a proof from below upwards. These expressions are far more fitting than the author's expressions 'from behind' and 'from the front'. Proofs *a posteriori* are proofs from experience; proofs *a priori*, on the other hand, are proofs from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-659":["When we prove something from pure reason, there is no place for experience. When, on the other hand, we establish something from experience, proofs from pure reason fall away. Some proofs, however, can be conducted *partim a priori, partim a posteriori* [partly a priori, partly a posteriori], namely when experience is the origin of the proofs of reason. Reason and experience are connected either by coordination or by subordination. In subordination I take an experience as ground and infer from it by means of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s08-660":["In coordination, on the other hand, I prove first wholly from reason alone, and then wholly from experience alone, so that the proof from experience serves the proof from reason as a proof *ad evidentiam* [for evidence]. In this way the *connubium rationis et experientiae* [marriage of reason and experience] is brought about. Experience has no need of the proof from reason, and conversely the proof from reason has no need of the proof from experience. Neither is subordinated to the other; together they constitute not one proof but two, for there is merely a coordination in the proofs. In this matter, however, we can be very greatly deceived. If the proof from reason is a demonstration, that is, a complete proof, the proof from experience serves to give it no greater strength, but only illustration; and if the proof from experience is a complete proof, the proof from reason is of no help to it either. Proofs from experience and proofs from reason make no coordination at all, and still less, even when they are coordinated, [do they make] distinctness. Many mutually coordinated insufficient proofs never yield a demonstration; they are mere *probationes*, and can never bring about apodeictic certainty. Proofs from experience and from reason cannot, therefore, possibly possess their evidence unless they are subordinated to one another. Where we find a great many proofs of a thing, we may already infer that we shall never attain to apodeictic certainty. A single proof from sufficient grounds is far better than a thousand insufficient proofs subordinated to one another, since through these we are still unable to attain complete certainty. It does indeed often happen, both in mathematics and in the other sciences, that more than one sufficient proof is given of one and the same thing; but this is done for the most part rather to display one's skill in demonstrating than because further proofs are required for the evidence of the thing. If, however, several mutually coordinated insufficient proofs are adduced, they contribute nothing to greater distinctness and certainty; the matter remains, as before, undecided.",[]],"blomberg-s08-661":["We can opine something without believing it, namely when we have more grounds for than against the [mode of] knowledge. In this case I hold something to be true without its having any influence upon our actions. To know something, on the other hand, is entitled nothing else than to cognise something with certainty. In belief, however, there is a high degree of holding-to-be-true, such that one would be willing to wager that something is true.",[]],"blomberg-s08-662":["Belief is either belief in things or belief in a person. We can hold a thing to be true immediately, solely because we have grounds for it; this is entitled believing a thing. On the other hand, we can also believe a thing immediately because a person declares it to be true, which we should not otherwise have held to be true of itself; and then we believe in a person. This belief which relates in a certain way to a person is of two kinds. Sometimes we believe anyone, be he who he may, merely because he says something, without attending to his person, his character or his station. If, for instance, someone says that it has snowed in his district, or that many ships have arrived, we believe it outright, without much inquiry; we thus pass no judgment at all upon the credibility of the witnesses. In certain matters, on the other hand, we do not believe just anyone; we require a special credibility in the person who declares something to be true before we accept it and hold it to be true. Belief in a person is either moral or historical. The former consists in trust in the uprightness of the other, even though he has made no statement by way of report. The latter consists in holding to be true what the other states, solely because he affirms it. Historical belief does not require any special morality in the other; we hold something to be true because he relates something that has happened. Moral belief, on the other hand, requires that we have a special trust in a person, that he will be kind and upright. If, for instance, a faithful servant, when great danger arises in his master's absence, abandons his own property and saves his master's, in the hope that his master will duly reward him for this service, that is a moral belief. In theology historical belief alone is required, namely that we hold something to be true on account of a statement. This belief, however, anyone, even the most profligate, can have. Without faithfulness and belief no republic, no commonwealth could subsist, nor could any *pacta* [contracts] take place. Faithfulness [Treue] is always required on the part of him who promises something, that he keep what he has promised; belief, on the part of him to whom something is promised, that he accept as true that the other will keep his promise. The two must be combined. For if everyone kept faith but no one believed, no agreements, etc., could take place; and just as little if all believed but no one would keep faith. Belief is thus very necessary in common life. If no one were willing to give credit, things would in fact often go badly, for one cannot always pay in cash at once. It also betokens a very poor cast of mind to trust no one in anything and to want to see everything promised and pledged to one immediately present and fulfilled. The Greeks were of this kind: they believed no one and wanted everything paid out in cash at once. Hence arose the proverb: *Graeca fide mercari* [to trade on Greek credit].",[]],"blomberg-s08-663":["Theological belief trusts someone solely upon his statement and holds it to be true. In historical belief the object of belief is always something that has happened, an experience of the other, not a judgment of reason. If, for instance, someone were to say to me with assurance, 'Believe me, there are inhabitants in the moon', I could naturally answer him: 'Have you been there? Have you seen them? What you have produced merely through reason I cannot take on your word.' Consequently all judgments in which the other is reasoning [vernünftelt] are not objects of historical belief. Theological belief is therefore merely historical. Religious belief, however, is not merely historical but moral, inasmuch as we hold something to be true on account of a promise and therefore undertake certain actions in the hope of one day reaping good consequences from them in the future. Through this belief we must hope that there will be a future world, and future rewards or punishments of these actions. Natural moral belief is found in all virtuous men: they believe that good actions will of themselves be rewarded, even though they see before them no special promise. Such a virtuous man is to be compared with a *creditor*: he hopes for the future good, even supposing that he has as yet no trace of it. Here, however, we are not speaking at all of this moral belief, although it is the most worthy of consideration and the most excellent, inasmuch as one gives credit and trusts not merely the present but the future, not wishing to be paid everything in this world but some things also in a future life.",[]],"blomberg-s08-664":["No *dogmata* permit of any other ground of holding-to-be-true than either one's own experience, or that one makes observations of reason upon them, that is, reasons about them. The experience of others thus has [no] place here. It follows, therefore, that they cannot be cognised through belief either. [841a]",[]],"blomberg-s08-665":["The subject who states something, and whom I am to believe, must have chiefly three properties. (1) A capacity to make an experience; it must therefore possess rational reflection. (2) It must have a capacity to retain and preserve faithfully the experience it has made, that is, a good memory. (3) It must also be able to declare distinctly the experiences it has made, to set them forth and make them known to everyone. The moral character of the subject, however, is that it should actually have the disposition of mind, *seu ut animum habeat* [or that it have the mind], to represent and declare the experiences faithfully, just as they were made by it, and not otherwise. And finally, the pragmatic principle for believing the subject is that it should have no grounds, or no advantages, to expect were it to state the opposite of the experience it has made.",[]],"blomberg-s08-666":["What, then, can we accept on belief? We cannot accept on belief dogmatic modes of knowledge which another states, but only experience. Universal modes of knowledge of reason cannot be accepted on belief, because in them we can very easily err. Statements of *dogmata* are therefore not testimonies; statements of empirical modes of knowledge, of experiences, are testimonies. A witness, then, is properly one who states an experience. He who states universal judgments of reason to be true is no witness; and the best historical belief therefore extends not to *dogmata* (save in so far as they are divine *dogmata*) but solely to experiences. The conditions under which a subject who has made experiences is to be believed are grounded partly in his own properties, partly in his situation [Zustand]. The properties of the subject are partly logical, namely that he is capable of having, making, knowing and stating experiences; partly moral, namely that he also has the will to state the truth, that is, to declare the experiences as they were. As regards credibility in respect of situation, however, we must also divide the situation",[]],"blomberg-s08-667":["into (A) the logical situation and (B) the practical situation. The most upright man may at times come into circumstances in which his uprightness succumbs, as, for instance, when he is kept from telling the truth by threats or promises. We must therefore also pay heed to his situation.",[]],"blomberg-s08-668":["A large part of our knowledge arises through belief, and without belief we should have to do without a great deal of historical knowledge. We should have no wider knowledge than, at most, of the place where we live and of the time in which we live.",[]],"blomberg-s08-669":["A great aid to historical knowledge is thus to adopt the experiences of others. By this means we can learn what happened a thousand years before us and a thousand miles away from us.",[]],"blomberg-s08-670":["Were there a very great deception of the senses, we could not trust the experiences which others have made. If, further, those who made experiences had so defective a memory that they could not recall them, and imagination presented other images to them; and if, finally, our language had such faults that we could not express ourselves properly and communicate our experiences, then the communication of the experiences of others would be wholly impossible. But even if it is presupposed, as is indeed actually true, (1) that we always have the capacity to make experiences, (2) that our memory is faithful enough to retain and preserve the experiences we have had without being disturbed by imagination, and (3) that we are able in our language to make known to others all the experiences we have made, it will still be required, for the credibility of this or that person who has made the experiences, that they be honest and have an inclination to truth. This inclination to truth, however, often springs more from love of honour than from honesty. For the whole *commercium* [intercourse] of men would be done away with if no one stated the truth, since then no one would trust another; and, further, lying is something harmful, and the statement of the truth is the surest way of escaping contempt. Geography, physics, history and other sciences, for instance, always presuppose the experiences of others. In common life we must not believe the common man in regard to such modes of knowledge as do not concern the interest of all men but are matters of indifference. For in such cases it is all one to him whether something be so or otherwise, since he does not see the importance of the [mode of] knowledge. The scholar, on the other hand, has much greater credibility in this kind of knowledge; for to him even the least thing in any [mode of] knowledge will be of importance, and from love of honour he will be more inclined to state everything as exactly as he has experienced it, since otherwise he would lose his credit.",[]],"blomberg-s08-671":["As regards, further, the credibility and honesty of witnesses who state experiences they have made, everyone is held to be honest and sincere until the contrary, namely that he departs from the truth, etc., has been proved. [This is] according to the well-known rule of equity:",[]],"blomberg-s08-672":["*Quilibet praesumitur bonus,*",[]],"blomberg-s08-673":["*Donec probetur contrarium.* [Everyone is presumed good until the contrary is proved.]",[]],"blomberg-s08-674":["In order, however, to attain to right knowledge of the truth, it is certainly required that we also inquire whether someone speaks the truth or lies.",[]],"blomberg-s08-675":["An eye-witness is one who has himself made experiences, that is, has seen something, and is therefore fully convinced of a [piece of] knowledge [Kenntniß].",[]],"blomberg-s08-676":["An ear-witness, on the other hand, is one who has heard, that is, received, something from an eye-witness, or who is a witness of the eye-witness's testimony. It is one of the greatest human failings that men, when they are ear-witnesses of a thing, are so very prone to wish to be eye-witnesses. They relate a thing with such certainty and confidence as if they had seen it themselves, whereas they have only received it from others. What, then, is the difference between the credibility of eye-witnesses and of ear-witnesses? The ear-witness has, as it were, only half as much credibility as the eye-witness; for in his case I must first examine his own credibility, and then also the credibility of him who saw the thing.",[]],"blomberg-s08-677":["In the series of mutually subordinated witnesses credibility diminishes; in the series of mutually coordinated witnesses, on the other hand, the probability and credibility of the testimony continually increase. If, for instance, someone writes the history of a scholar or of a king, and his successor does not hand down the history itself but clothes it in his own style, and this goes on thus *continuo* [continuously], the credibility thereby becomes ever smaller. But if many persons at the same time state one and the same thing, the credibility is all the greater, especially if they are men who value their honour.",[]],"blomberg-s08-678":["The series of mutually coordinated witnesses is entitled public report, *sive rumor*. The series of mutually subordinated statements is entitled oral transmission, *traditio oralis*. If, however, in the series of witnesses, whether coordinated or subordinated, the first author, that is, the eye-witness, is unknown, this is mere common hearsay, *sive rumor sine capite* [or rumour without a head]; and such a statement is little or not at all to be trusted, little belief is to be accorded it. There are, however, certain statements in which there is no need to examine the credibility of the witness at all, but which are rejected prior to all examination.",[]],"blomberg-s08-679":["This seems indeed at first sight very harsh, but it is nevertheless certain. Modes of knowledge which are wholly and manifestly false surely require no examination, and certainly deserve utter contempt. Of this kind are the statements about the divining-rod. These deserve no examination; they can be rejected at once; they deserve no refutation, for they are false in and by themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s08-680":["There are, therefore, grounds for rejecting a testimony which are not historical, that is, not taken from the character of the witness, but rational, and which lie in the character of the thing itself. Yet we must be very cautious here, and not reject a [mode of] knowledge until it is evident that the thing is manifestly false. Much seems at first sight wholly false, but on closer examination we find the opposite. No empirical judgments concerning the possibility or impossibility of an *eventus* entitle us forthwith to reject the [mode of] knowledge without examining the credibility of the witness. If someone testifies to something that is very improbable and contradicts the ordinary course of events in nature, he cannot yet demand that we should believe him at once upon his statement; if he wishes this, he must furnish the proof. Thus, for instance, when Pontoppidan writes that in certain seas there is an exceedingly great fish which looks like a piece of land, and which he calls the Kraken, he must prove it, since such an animal has never yet been seen. If, on the other hand, someone testifies to something that evidently and fully agrees with experience and reason, we believe him without proof. But if the credibility of the witness cannot be shaken at all, while the narrative or testimony he gives contradicts reason and experience in the highest degree, the question arises: must we in this case give assent to his statement, and, as it were, deny our reason, or not?",[]],"blomberg-s08-681":["Answer: the best course is then neither to reject the [mode of] knowledge nor to accept it as true, but to suspend one's assent until one has more grounds for or against it. There are at times persons who state something, whose credibility we cannot in the least doubt, and of whom we cannot suppose that they had the *animus*, that is, the intention, of deceiving others, but whom we nevertheless may not trust. The fault in their statement then lies for the most part only in an experience not [properly] made, or in a *vitium* [defect] of the memory which does not retain the experience faithfully enough. Hence it also happens that people who have put forward something as very probable and asserted it as true, when asked whether they could swear to their statement, hesitate and must resort to all manner of evasions. They then commonly say that they may well have forgotten something since that time. Imagination may indeed also reproduce for them other images than the experiences they actually had.",[]],"blomberg-s08-682":["Belief [Glaube] can be taken in the moral sense, when one has a lack of all trust in the moral character of a witness. [The context indicates that moral unbelief is meant.] He who has such a moral unbelief holds all men to be liars; he trusts no man, believes no promises, but always doubts the sincerity of other people. Moral unbelief is opposed to the moral foundation of all human society: without faithfulness and belief, as has already been indicated above, no state can subsist. It is commonly found, however, in those who themselves possess a moral *pravitas* [depravity], so that one cannot accord them much belief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-683":["These people judge others too by themselves. Moral belief, on the other hand, trusts everyone, even without any promises. Of this kind is the belief in a future world. Incredulity [Ungläubigkeit], however, is distinct from unbelief [Unglauben]. It consists in refusing to give assent to the logical grounds of a testimony. This incredulity may also be entitled logical [un]belief [logische Glaube], and it is thus very different from moral unbelief.",[]],"blomberg-s08-684":["This logical unbelief lies not in a certain defect of the heart but of knowledge, in a certain inexperience, in which one does not know the grounds of others and does not see their importance. It is therefore found chiefly in those who have no proper acquaintance with literature [Litteratur]; to them everything seems paradoxical and ridiculous which is otherwise true and credible. We must therefore be very careful not to have dealings with such people, since we are quite unable to convince them.",[]],"blomberg-s08-685":["A seeing belief is one which is bound up with, and rests upon, the examination of the witness. A blind belief, on the other hand, is one which accepts a testimony without examination and inquiry into its credibility. The former leads to truth; the latter is the road to error. The latter is therefore harmful, the former useful.",[]],"blomberg-s08-686":["The union of all these three sources of knowledge rests on their having as ground not one proof but many. Belief always requires a *connubium* with the other sources of knowledge, namely reason and experience, in order that it may become evident and convincing. If one source of knowledge is not able to give a sufficient proof of the truth of a thing, we must call in the remaining *fontes cognitionis* [sources of knowledge] to our aid, and seek to produce from them a greater evidence. But if one source of knowledge suffices to give a complete proof of a thing, we have no need of the others. If something can be established completely and evidently through reason, we do not need experience to make the proof more complete. If experience is sufficient to give a complete proof of a thing, we need not consult reason.",[]],"blomberg-s09-001":["If a [mode of] knowledge contains in itself motives to actions, it is practical. All practical knowledge is knowledge either of ends or of means, and the latter must be distinguished from the former. Practical geometry teaches no ends, no motives to actions, but only means of attaining certain ends. It shows the conditions under which an end can be attained in respect of measurement.",[]],"blomberg-s09-002":["All doctrine of skill is a practical knowledge of means. All doctrine of morality, on the other hand, is a practical knowledge of ends, as is also the doctrine of prudence. Our author speaks throughout this whole section of knowledge as it is related to the free will. In logic, however, the relation of knowledge to the will is not considered at all; this belongs to morals. The relation of the free will is not an *objectum domesticum* [proper object] of logic. The practical science of skill gives us no motives whatsoever to certain actions. For any science which shows only means and no ends contains no motives; all motives, namely, must be derived from the end. A [mode of] knowledge is entitled practical in so far as it is *contradistinguished* from theoretical",[]],"blomberg-s09-003":["knowledge, that is, from mere contemplation and speculation. A [mode of] knowledge is practical either *explicite* or *implicite*: *explicite*, when it itself sets forth the rules for actions; *implicite*, when it offers the motives, or represents such truths as certain practical rules for actions can be derived from. For instance, the proposition, 'Thou shalt not lie; thou shalt be faithful and honest', is *explicite* practical. But the proposition, 'There is a God; consequently we are obliged to order our actions in such manner as accords with the holy will of this divine being', is *implicite* practical. In metaphysics there are for the most part pure speculations, not practical but theoretical, which therefore have no particular influence upon the free will. Such speculations are neither harmful nor useful, for they contain no grounds of actions whatsoever. Many subtleties and errors devised by men are nothing but pure speculations. We therefore pass over this section, because it is unsuitably placed here and belongs rather to morals than to logic, and because no advantage is to be hoped for in logic from treating it; for logic likewise occupies itself with speculative modes of knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s10-001":["Not every representation is a concept. A representation through the senses, for instance, is a sensation; a representation through the understanding is an appearance; a representation through reason is a concept. The senses feel [*empfinden*], the understanding co-ordinates, whereas reason subordinates. General representations are concepts, and concepts are general representations. Sensations, on the other hand, are only singular representations, and so likewise are appearances; concepts, however, are general, and arise through reason. So long as I am not yet able to subordinate a thing to any general representation, I have as yet no concept of it. Of many things we have learned knowledge, and yet no concept, properly so called.",[]],"blomberg-s10-002":["Between learned and unlearned concepts no specific difference is to be observed. The distinction between common and learned concepts is a better one. A concept is not, indeed, a simple representation. The concept, as the result of many representations, can be very rich, pregnant, beautiful, learned and vivid. A concept which possesses much vividness is an aesthetic concept. An aesthetic concept arises through a multitude and combination of many co-ordinated representations; a concept of reason, properly so called, arises through a multitude of subordinated representations. The matter of an aesthetic concept consists in its relation to our taste and feeling; its form consists in a multitude of representations co-ordinated with one another, whereby the requisite vividness is produced. Poets and orators in particular make use of such aesthetic concepts. When, for instance, they wish to describe summer or spring, they do not show the causes of these seasons; they merely depict the manifold changes which take place at these times in the fields, among the animals, in the woods, in the air, in the country and in the town. Of many things we have, indeed, sensations, but no concepts. We meet with concepts only in certain cases, namely, where reason subordinates the representations to one another.",[]],"blomberg-s10-003":["The division in this section [*§pho*] is in no way correct. Through abstraction we obtain no representations; representations must already be given prior to abstraction, and through it they merely become clear. All our modes of knowledge are either given or made, that is, invented. *Omnes conceptus sunt vel dati, vel facti, sive ficti* [All concepts are either given or made, that is, feigned].",[]],"blomberg-s10-004":["Many modes of knowledge are given to us through experience, and the matter of all knowledge arises through sensation, through the senses. Often, however, we invent modes of knowledge for ourselves through the imagination [*Phantasie*]; we form, as it were, a world of our own.",[]],"blomberg-s10-005":["*Conceptus dati* [given concepts] are, further, either *dati a priori*, through pure reason, or *dati a posteriori*, through experience. All *conceptus a posteriori dati* are abstracted *ab experientia* [from experience]. Others, however, do not arise through experience, but are abstracted from the laws of pure reason, for instance the concept of the possible and the impossible. A *conceptus abstractus*, however, signifies more especially a representation which has been abstracted from experience, and not one abstracted from reason.",[]],"blomberg-s10-006":["*Conceptus dati* are therefore either *abstracti*, [abstracted] from experience, or *rationales*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-007":["The concept of all species and genera of things which fall within our senses, of horses, sheep, etc., for instance, is a *conceptus abstractus*. When I say 'a horse', this is a concept given *per experientiam* [through experience]. The universality of the representations has, however, arisen through abstraction. In all concepts through experience, the universality arises through abstraction from singular concepts and images, while the representation itself arises through experience. The matter itself thus lies in experience, the form of universality in abstraction. Not the least knowledge arises through abstraction; universality alone arises through it. The first origin of our knowledge is therefore experience. Empirical concepts [*Erfahrungs-Begriffe*] are thus those which are given through experience and have become universal through abstraction. Hence all empirical concepts are abstracted concepts. Pure concepts of reason, on the other hand, are not given through experience by means of abstraction [*Absonderung*], but through pure reason; and it is precisely in this that they are distinguished from empirical concepts. The concept of right and wrong is in this way a pure concept of reason. These concepts would remain constant even supposing that no experience took place. However godless the world might be, everyone would still have the concept of right and wrong. Empirical concepts, on the other hand, cannot occur at all without experience. Were there no horse, no one would be in a position even to feign [*fingiren*] a horse.",[]],"blomberg-s10-008":["Invented [*gedichtete*] concepts, however, are those which have been made, in so far as they are not given. These invented concepts are entitled *ideae prototypa* [prototypes]; abstracted concepts, on the other hand, are *ectypa* [εκτυπα]. The former are the originals, the latter the copies. Whoever made up the delightful fairy-tales made nothing but prototypes, for nothing of the kind exists at all.",[]],"blomberg-s10-009":["The Stoic sage, on the other hand, is such a prototype, an archetype [Προτοτυπον ἀρχέτυπον]. All the definitions of the mathematicians contain nothing but *ideae prototypae*. The concept of the octagon, for instance, is not borrowed from experience; the mathematician thinks it through his pure reason. He represents to himself a figure which has eight lines and is enclosed by them.",[]],"blomberg-s10-010":["Every inventor must therefore invent [*dichten*] and feign; all discoveries [*Erfindungen*] are fictions, though not all fictions are discoveries. Fiction produces originals [*Urbilder*], abstraction copies [*Nachbilder*]; [fiction does so] through arbitrary combination or through arbitrary separation.",[]],"blomberg-s10-011":["*Fictiones* are made by omitting and adding. All fiction, further, is either voluntary or involuntary. Involuntary fiction is a chimera.",[]],"blomberg-s10-012":["Hypochondriac persons are very much subject to involuntary fictions. Here, however, the proper place is to speak of voluntary fictions, which are bound up with consciousness and with the will to have them. If man can subject everything to his choice [*Willkühr*], he is the most perfect of all beings; and voluntary fictions will therefore often be very useful to him.",[]],"blomberg-s10-013":["All concepts which do not arise through attention, through the senses, and which are also not given but arbitrarily invented, are pure concepts of reason. [*sic*: the transcript appears garbled here; the context requires that pure concepts of reason are neither derived from the senses nor invented.] Such are the concepts of the necessary and the contingent, of causes and effects. Concepts, however, which arise through the senses and are abstracted from experiences, are empirical concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-014":["We must take especial pains to distinguish empirical concepts from concepts of reason. What does not fall within the senses does not come before experience. From the confusion of empirical concepts with concepts of reason arises the *vitium subreptionis* [fallacy of subreption], which is dealt with in metaphysics. Right and wrong are not empirical concepts but pure concepts of reason. They would, however, be empirical concepts if the wrong were that which is not customary, that which men do not do.",[]],"blomberg-s10-015":["This, however, is never the case. Much is right which no one does, and wrong which all do. In the concept of cause and effect many commit the *vitium subreptionis*, in that they seek to derive it from experience, whereas it is a pure concept of reason. Empirical concepts all relate to space and time; concepts of reason, on the other hand, to the ground [*Fundament*] of cause and effect. Of possibility and impossibility we have no experience, but only of actuality. Also, we have experiences only of affirmative, not of negative representations: what cannot be cannot become sensation. When, for instance, we have read a book, we cannot learn immediately by experience that this or that passage is not contained in it; this we must make out by inference.",[]],"blomberg-s10-016":["Here we have nothing in particular to note, for metaphysics treats of it more fully. The only question that still remains for us is: how are concepts made out of many experiences? This is not so easy as it may perhaps appear at first sight. One may have made many experiences and yet have abstracted no empirical concepts from them.",[]],"blomberg-s10-017":["To obtain empirical concepts instruction is required. Anyone who has good *organa sensoria* [sense-organs] can experience; but to abstract from singular experiences universal empirical concepts, under which the *experientiae singulae* [singular experiences] can be subordinated, requires a skill in discerning what is common to many experiences.",[]],"blomberg-s10-018":["One cannot make money by robbing someone of it; and just so, one cannot make concepts through abstraction. Through abstraction our representations are only made universal, as has already been indicated above. If we have no representations of things at all, no abstraction will be able to make concepts for us. In logical abstraction we compare many concepts with one another, we see what they have in common, or wherein they agree; and thereby our representations become concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-019":["When many concepts have a mark [*Merckmahl*] in common, this mark is contained in them. The things are contained under the concept which has been abstracted from them. This concept is a common ground of knowledge [*Grund zu erkennen*]. It is contained in all the things, and all the things from which it is abstracted are under it. A concept can be abstracted from experience, and also from the laws of pure reason. The latter are not abstracted from things, but are entitled rational.",[]],"blomberg-s10-020":["By abstracted concepts, however, empirical concepts are always meant.",[]],"blomberg-s10-021":["We shall first say something of invented [*gedichteten*] concepts. Every concept which has been given neither through experience, that is, through the sensation of things by our senses, nor through reason, is a fictitious concept. Such a concept is made in one of two ways: either an experience still underlies it in respect of its matter, or not even the matter is taken from experience, and both matter and form are invented. The latter, however, is impossible. All fiction arises either through arbitrary combination or through separation: through combination, when I join together what is never combined and united in experience; through separation, when I abstract from a thing what in experience is never separated from it. In fictitious concepts arbitrary combination and arbitrary separation are for the most part combined with each other. The writer of romances ascribes to his hero all the praiseworthy qualities he still lacks, in order to present a truly perfect person. If he notices only a few faults in him, he at once abstracts from them and straightway removes them all from him. Or if this is often difficult or not at all [889/890] possible, he at least endeavours to make the hero praiseworthy even in his transgressions, and to be able to give all his vices such a colouring or semblance that they either lose the ugliness proper to them and striking to the eye, or even appear to be so many virtues.",[]],"blomberg-s10-022":["The Arcadian pastoral life, so celebrated among the ancients and so much embellished by the poets of that time, is of this kind. It is one of the most agreeable representations that one can ever have or imagine. Everything beautiful, graceful and charming is thought in it, and the greatest abundance of everything that can charm is combined with it; whereas each and every adversity and discontent, all the want which was in fact very often found in pastoral life, and usually is, is separated and removed from it, solely in order that a wholly perfect ideal of happiness might thereby be sketched.",[]],"blomberg-s10-023":["All *conceptus*, however, are either",[]],"blomberg-s10-024":["a) *conceptus singulares* or b) *conceptus communes*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-025":["In the former I think only one thing; through the latter, however, I go further, for I then think what is common to many things.",[]],"blomberg-s10-026":["The former concepts, then, consider something individual, for instance *Roma*, *Bucephalus*, etc. This is a *conceptus singularis*. The latter, on the other hand, relate to a *complexus* of many individual things, as, for instance, a city in general, a four-footed animal, etc. 'A man': that is a *conceptus communis*. The representations of immediate experience are all *conceptus singulares*, for they represent single things. Mediate empirical concepts, however, which are abstracted from many experiences, are *conceptus communes*; and so too are all our concepts of reason. The concept of God, indeed, applies only to one being, to whom alone it is to be ascribed; yet one can, without deciding whether more gods, or only one, are possible, frame several concepts of God. Afterwards, however, a proof must be added that the concept of God is a *conceptus singularis*. Every *conceptus communis* contains under itself all the concepts to which it is common. The concept of mortality, for instance, holds under itself all men and animals, because it is common to them. Yet such a *conceptus communis* is contained in all those things which it contains under itself. The *conceptus communis* is the ground of knowledge, or the mark, of all those that are under it [*unter ihnen*: read *ihm*]. It is, however, at the same time also a partial concept of the things it contains under itself, for it is abstracted from the things contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-027":["The marks are always co-ordinated in a thing, and together, as parts, make up the whole concept of the thing; the thing, on the other hand, is subordinated to the marks which have been given of it, and is contained in them [*sic*: presumably 'under them']. The *sphaera notionis* properly signifies the multitude of things which are comprehended under a concept as a *nota communis* [common mark]. Through a *nota communis* I can therefore think many things. The *sphaera* of a *conceptus communis* may be large or small. The very same concept under which many things are comprehended is contained in the things that are under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-028":["The larger the *sphaera* of a *conceptus communis*, and the more it holds under itself, the less is contained in it. A concept which contains much under itself arises from abstracting what is common to many things. It is therefore a mark of a mark; it has much under itself, but little in itself. For a mark of a mark contains still less than the first mark, of which it is a mark. When I think something of many things, what I think of these many things is very little. On the other hand, the less a concept contains under itself, the more it holds in itself; and a singular concept contains the most in itself, for here I know, indeed, only one object, but very much in it. The more I think in my concept, the less I think through the concept. A *conceptus communis* arises only through considering what is common to many objects; what is different in them is omitted and not considered. Such a *conceptus* contains little in itself, but is a concept of a large *sphaera*. The concept of body, for instance, has a large *sphaera*, but contains little in itself. A concept can become of greater extent, so as to contain more under itself, only by containing less in itself. This consideration teaches us, more especially, to estimate the value of our knowledge. Through reason we know nothing but *notae communes*, and so little of many things: much is contained under, but little in, the modes of knowledge of reason. Through experience we know few objects, but very much in them. Empirical concepts are *minores sphaerae*, but they contain much in themselves. A mark of a mark always has a larger *sphaera* than a nearer mark, that is, always contains more under itself than a *nota propior* [nearer mark]. The mark of a thing in general is the *nota remotissima* of a thing. After it comes the *nota remotior*, [then the] *remota*, and only then come the nearer marks. The *nota remotissima*, however, contains the most under itself. When several concepts stand in such a relation that one is contained under the other, the one which contains the other under itself is a *conceptus superior*, and the concept contained under the other is the *conceptus inferior*. One and the same concept, however, can, according to different relations, namely as it contains other concepts under itself and also stands under another, be now a *conceptus superior*, now a *conceptus inferior*. For instance, I say 'men'; then I compare man with the irrational animals and say 'animals'. The concept of man then contains a great deal under itself, for instance white men and Negroes and all sorts of nations, etc., and is in this respect a *conceptus superior*. But it is a *conceptus inferior* in so far as it is contained under the concept 'animals'. The *conceptus nullo inferior* [the concept inferior to no other], however, is the *conceptus summus*. To this belongs the concept of a thing, or of something possible. This *conceptus summus* is superior not merely *relative* but *absolute*. The *conceptus infimus*, on the other hand, is that which is inferior not only *relative* but *absolute*. *Conceptus singulares* are *infimi*; *conceptus universales*, however, are *summi*; and between these *conceptus infimi* and *summi* there is a *longa series* of *conceptus inferiores* and *superiores*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-029":["Every mark is a ground of knowledge of things; through reason we know all things only by means of marks. The further a concept is removed from the immediate mark of a thing, the higher it is, and the more it contains under itself. One and the same *conceptus communis* is, relatively, entitled a *genus* in respect of the *conceptus communes* which it contains under itself, and, relatively, a *species* in respect of the *conceptus communes* under which it is contained. For instance, the unlearned [*sic*: the sense requires 'the learned'] are a *genus* in respect of the particular kinds of learned men, philosophers, physicians, jurists, etc.; but they are a *species* in respect of the concept of a man in general. A *conceptus communis* is entitled a *species* because, *1mo*, it is contained under another *conceptus communis superior*, but also, 2, because it is itself a *conceptus communis*. For a *conceptus singularis*, even if it is subordinated to some *conceptus communis*, is not a *species*: if, for instance, among the learned I think of Herr von Wolff, this is no longer a *species* but an *individuum*. Every *species* is *infima* in so far as no *conceptus communes*, but merely *singulares*, are contained under it. *Species infima est, quae tantum individua sub se continet* [the lowest species is that which contains only individuals under it]. Otherwise a *species* can always again be thought as a *genus*, and other concepts subordinated to it. A philosopher, for instance, is a *species* of the learned; but this becomes a *genus* when I think under it more particularly the Wolffians, Crusians, etc. All *genera* contained under higher *genera* are to be regarded as *species* in respect of these [higher] *genera* [*generum, Specierum*: text appears corrupt]. The *genus summum*, however, is that which can no longer be a *species*; the *species infima* can never again be a *genus*. In the former case it is subordinated to no other [*sic*: text reads 'to another'], in the latter case others are subordinated to it [*sic*: the cases appear reversed]. Thus one and the same concept can be both *genus* and *species* in different respects. The subordination of concepts, however, can take place either *logice* or *realiter*. Logical subordination consists in my taking what is common to many concepts and thereby forming a universal concept, under which I can subordinate the singular representations. In this way I make for myself various *genera*, and subordinate to them the *species* and *individua*. Real subordination, on the other hand, consists in my actually connecting the concepts with one another, so that not only is one contained under the other, but they also cohere as causes and effects.",[]],"blomberg-s10-030":["We now come further to the *sphaera notionum*. The more abstracted a concept is, the larger its *sphaera*. The larger the *sphaera notionis*, the more things I can judge of, but the fewer things I can know [about]. The senses know much in few things, reason little in many things. Reason and experience must therefore be united as far as possible, so that what is lacking in the one ground of knowledge may be made good by the other.",[]],"blomberg-s10-031":["The concept which contains another under itself is a *conceptus latior* [wider concept]; the concept which is contained under another is a *conceptus angustior* [narrower concept]. Thus the concept of man is a *conceptus latior* in respect of the learned; the concept of the jurist, philosopher, etc., is a *conceptus angustior* in relation to the concept of the learned man. The first [*sic*: presumably 'narrowest'] concept is therefore that which contains only a single thing under itself, and through which I can know only a single [thing].",[]],"blomberg-s10-032":["The *conceptus latissimus*, however, is that through which I can know the most things, or to which the most things are subordinated and under which they are contained.",[]],"blomberg-s10-033":["A reciprocal concept [*Wechsel-Begriff*] is one which contains just as much under and in itself as another concept, and which can therefore fittingly be interchanged with it, so that the one can completely take the place of the other. A universal or common concept is that under which many are contained. Every *conceptus communis* is also at the same time a *conceptus universalis* in respect of the concepts contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-034":["A concept can be contained only in part under another concept. *Quare conceptus inferior semper est particularis, ratione conceptus superioris, quare conceptus superior vero semper est universalis, ratione conceptus inferioris* [Hence the lower concept is always particular in relation to the higher, while the higher concept is always universal in relation to the lower]. All abstract knowledge, in respect, namely, of the concepts contained under it [*ihnen*], is the most universal knowledge, that is, the knowledge which contains the *conceptus summi* under itself and descends from them to all the *conceptus inferiores* contained under them. Metaphysics is a science which contains such *conceptus summi* under itself; it therefore knows the most universal constitution of things.",[]],"blomberg-s10-035":["This section [*Paragraphum*] we may fittingly pass over, for here the subject is not inferences but only learned concepts. The place to speak of it will be in the doctrine of inferences, *quare hoc non tractare volumus anticipando* [hence we shall not treat of this by anticipation].",[]],"blomberg-s10-036":["Here the author speaks of the utility of abstract knowledge. It is very great; yet it is not very necessary to adduce and go through it in particular, since the greatest part of all our knowledge rests upon it.",[]],"blomberg-s10-037":["The author now speaks here of concepts [made] through arbitrary combination. All *conceptus ficti* are made either *per combinationem* or *per separationem arbitrariam*. It is, however, to be noted in general that in all our fictitious concepts only the form is, and comes to be, invented.",[]],"blomberg-s10-038":["The matter can never be invented. He who has no sight cannot invent a colour for himself; he who has no hearing cannot invent a tone. All form, however, is invented according to the relations of space and time, and according to the universal nature of our reason. All concepts are either empirical or transcendental concepts of reason. To the latter belong the representations of the possible and the impossible, of the necessary and the contingent. These by no means arise from experience, nor are they abstracted from it; they are produced by pure reason. Concepts are thus either given or made. They are given either through experience or through pure reason. They are made when they have been given neither through experience nor through pure reason. These made concepts, however, are always invented only in respect of their form. Through abstraction [*Absonderung*] I think of a part of the concept; through separation [*Trennung*], on the other hand, I deny something of my concept. Abstraction [*Absonderung, Abstraction*] and separation [*Trennung, Separation*] are thus distinct from each other. Fictitious concepts arise through separation alone, never through abstraction. A *fictio separando facta* [fiction made by separation] requires the removal of what is otherwise usually combined with a concept. Much is invented *separando*; all romances arise through this kind of fiction. In just this way we must also feign for ourselves the idea of God, by separating all imperfection from it and so arriving at the concept of the highest being. Through combination, on the other hand, we feign something when we put together much that is never connected in experience. In this way, as already indicated above, romances arise. The Stoic sage arises through this fiction: he is given more strength than is proper to man. Fictions are either *fictiones aestheticae* or *heuristicae*; the former serve for pleasure, the latter for discovery.",[]],"blomberg-s10-039":["Mathematics and physics have many *fictiones heuristicae*; such are hypotheses. *Fictiones*, further, are made either according to quality or according to quantity. *Fictiones aestheticae* according to quality are the best; those according to quantity are not exactly the best. For they require no great art, save merely in the description, in order thereby to obtain a vivid concept. Once I have an object, I can readily ascribe to it an enormous magnitude, which can indeed occasion admiration, but no emotion [*Rührung*]. There are, however, also involuntary fictions, or involuntary",[]],"blomberg-s10-040":["fictitious concepts of reason, for instance of spirits. These concepts have neither arisen nor been given through experience or through reason. We arrive at these involuntary concepts through an imperceptible fiction, and through secret inferences of which we are often not at all aware.",[]],"blomberg-s10-041":["The distinctness of a concept is a property of a logical perfection. Making distinct is done through analysis [*Zergliederung*]. When, however, I make distinct a concept which contains, for instance, many marks, I shall have to seek to avoid tautology among these marks; I shall therefore promote the precision of the marks, that is, I shall restrict the quantity of the marks without, however, detracting from their quality. The completeness [*Totalität*] of the distinct representation of a thing is also asked for, and this completeness I very often need. In any distinctness of concepts I can be content with partiality; but if my marks are sufficient for the whole concept of the thing, I have completeness. If precision is also asked for [*angefragt*], I have a complete, precise concept of a thing, and that is a definition. Definition, and the act of our mind [*Seele*] in it, may be perfectly compared with the reduction of fractions *ad minimos terminos* [to lowest terms] that occurs in arithmetic, e.g. 4/2 3/6 2/3 1/3 = 4/12 [the figures appear corrupt]. And this is also the aim of defining: we wish to have of a thing (1) a distinct, (2) a complete, and then (3) a precise concept. In order to examine whether a definition is correct, we therefore always ask, (1) whether this alleged definition is true as a proposition. In this way we can make use of many *definitiones* as true propositions, in which many otherwise imperceptible marks are presented to us. False *definitiones* can sometimes be of use to us even as true propositions; indeed, owing to the usefulness of such a true proposition, we can often quite well dispense with the whole definition. Suppose it does not fulfil what it promises; it can still be useful, it can always be of service, even though it does not attain its aim.",[]],"blomberg-s10-042":["(2) We ask whether the definition is distinct as a concept, whether I can know it clearly. For all distinctness it is required that a mark be a clear ground of knowledge of the thing. All tautology in definitions is therefore a lack of distinctness, and such a definition is not only no definition but also no distinct concept. All empty propositions are tautological. The soul, for instance, is a ruling monad in an animal. This proposition is not empty, but in it a *comparative obscurum per aeque obscurum* [something comparatively obscure through what is equally obscure] is explained. The uselessness of this proposition can, however, still be remedied here; with tautology this is not at all possible. (3) We ask whether the definition, as a distinct concept, is complete [*ausführlich*], that is, distinct [*sic*: presumably 'exhaustive']. *Nam definire est fines conceptus alicuius ponere* [for to define is to set the bounds of some concept]; that is, the definition is a determination of everything which belongs to the thing alone.",[]],"blomberg-s10-043":["*4to*, the question is whether this distinct and complete concept is also measured, that is, precise; namely, whether the marks of the definition do not follow from one another or are not subordinated to one another, but are co-ordinated with one another. The marks must stand together as *compartes* [co-parts] of a whole, but not alongside one another as members in a series. If everything were omitted without which a concept still remains distinct and complete, such a concept is entitled *conceptus rem adaequans* [a concept adequate to the thing]. Completeness [*completudo*] and precision of a concept, then, together make up its adequacy. An *adaequatum* thus admits of no comparative. Wolff says that a concept is adequate when the marks of a concept, and in turn the marks of these marks, are distinct. *Definitio autem adaequat conceptum* [a definition, however, makes the concept adequate].",[]],"blomberg-s10-044":["Lastly, precision belongs to it. This consists in no mark in the definition being already contained in another, or, what amounts to the same, in there being no repetition in it. If, for instance, I were to say, 'Body is an extended divisible matter', this would offend against precision. For the mark of divisibility already lies in the mark of matter, and consequently the mark of divisibility is merely repeated: it already lies in the one mark of body, namely in the concept of matter. In short, the most correct and best definition of precision is this:",[]],"blomberg-s10-045":["*Praecisio* is a *reductio* of a definition *ad minimos terminos*, just as in *arithmetica*, for instance, fractions are usually brought *ad minimos terminos*. Accordingly, a definition is nothing other than the relation of equality between two concepts, such that the one can always be substituted for the other.",[]],"blomberg-s10-046":["Now, however, we ask: is the concept in the definition completely equal to the concept of the *definitum*? And here we must note that *materialiter, id est quoad objectum* [materially, that is, as regards the object], these concepts are indeed always equal; but as regards the form they are not, and must not be, completely equal to each other. As regards the matter, I do indeed always think the same object, but not in the same way: what I previously represented confusedly in the *definitum*, I represent distinctly in the definition. Accordingly every definition must be distinct. For distinctness in general, and so also for the distinctness of a definition, marks are required. These marks must, first, be clear concepts, and indeed clearer than the concept of the *definitum*; and from this follows the canon: *ne definiatur obscurum per aeque obscurum* [let the obscure not be defined through what is equally obscure]. If, for instance, I were to say to someone who does not know what a *monas* is, 'The soul is a monad thinking in the body', this would offend against this rule. Secondly, these marks must also be clear grounds of knowledge. For, as a ground of knowledge, the mark must be distinguished from the consequence, and must therefore be thought otherwise, and far more clearly, than in the *definitum*; for he who merely says the same thing names no ground at all. From this follows the rule: *ne notae in definitione sint tautologicae* [let the marks in a definition not be tautological]. This is otherwise also called defining *per circulum*. The *circulus vitiosus in definiendo* is either *implicitus* (hidden) or *explicitus*, the latter when the marks are the same even in their expression. An example of the first: the definition, 'The ground is that on account of which [*warum*] something is known'. Here the 'on account of which' signifies the very same as *qua ratione*, and consequently an implicit tautology is concealed in it. Hence, too, the form in the definition must always be other than the form of the *definitum*. This, then, was the first requisite of a good definition, namely that it be distinct.",[]],"blomberg-s10-047":["Now the second requisite of a correct definition is that it be adequate, that is, that it contain neither more nor less than is contained in the *definitum*. This can also properly be entitled *completudo*, or exhaustiveness and sufficiency. To the *completudo* of a definition it belongs, first, that the marks be sufficient [to distinguish] the *definitum* from all others; and secondly, that they be sufficient also for knowledge of its identity with others. In short, the definition must be neither *latior* [wider] nor *angustior* [narrower]. But what does this mean? In comparing a thing with others, every ground of knowledge can be employed in two ways:",[]],"blomberg-s10-048":["first, in order to [know] from it the identity or",[]],"blomberg-s10-049":["secondly, the diversity of the thing from all others. A definition is therefore complete when the marks suffice",[]],"blomberg-s10-050":["first, [to know] the difference of the concept from all others which do not stand under its universal concept, and",[]],"blomberg-s10-051":["secondly, to know from them also its sameness with other concepts which stand together under one universal concept. The former is sufficiency for distinction; the latter, sufficiency for *agnitio* [recognition].",[]],"blomberg-s10-052":["We may now observe that the more marks a concept contains, the less is it the same as others. If, therefore, a definition contains too many marks, this may indeed contribute to the distinction of the concept, but not at all to its recognition. We can accordingly also say that a definition is *angustior* when it does not suffice for knowledge of identity, and *latior* when it does not suffice for knowledge of diversity. In other words, when there are too few marks the definition is *latior*; when there are too many, it is *angustior*. It is certainly curious that the means of knowing identity are exactly opposed to those which I employ in order to discern diversity from others. For the more I wish to know the difference, the more must I multiply the marks; and the more I wish to discern the identity, the more must I reduce their number. We may further note: *Quo major conceptus determinatus est, eo angustior est quoque definitio* [the more determinate the concept, the narrower is also the definition]. As regards persons, we may say that people of much wit [Witz] commonly make *definitiones latiores*. They will, for example, readily say that living beings are those which grow and generate. But one experience, for instance, also generates another, and yet it does not on that account generate a living being. This definition is therefore *latior*. It does not serve for the examination of all things and the distinguishing of the thing from others, but rather for knowledge of the sameness of the thing with others; and from this we see also that the *latitudo* of a definition pertains to knowledge of identity. The subtle reasoner [Grübler], on the other hand, often makes *definitiones angustiores*. He seeks to find differences where none are to be met with, and this is the so-called *argutatio inanis* [empty subtlety].",[]],"blomberg-s10-053":["From all that has been said there follows the logical rule, the third canon: *ne definitio nec latior nec angustior suo definito sit* [let the definition be neither wider nor narrower than its *definitum*]; or, in one expression: *definitio et definitum sint conceptus reciproci* [let definition and *definitum* be reciprocal concepts].",[]],"blomberg-s10-054":["This is the third requisite of a good definition, and it might seem that with this demand all further precautions in defining are at an end. But since one and the same mark, already once occurring in the definition, may under different expressions occur in it a second time, a certain *praecisio* is still required in the definition to prevent this. What it demands is that one concept or mark must not already be contained in another.",[]],"blomberg-s10-055":["As regards tautology, we have already condemned it above as a great fault, since in it the marks given are no grounds of knowledge at all, and the definition is confused with the *conceptus definiti*. But the case is otherwise when the *notae* in a definition are the same and differ from one another merely in expression. This is not so great a fault, and distinctness may still be present. The fault lies only in this, that one expresses one's thoughts too luxuriantly. To remove this from the definition a certain *praecisio* is very necessary. It serves, however, as has been said, rather for neatness than for the essence of the definition itself; yet it is always gladly desired in a definition. The case is the same as with a garment: it does me the same service whether it is made wide or fitted, but for the sake of neatness we reject the former and choose the latter.",[]],"blomberg-s10-056":["Offending against *praecisio* is, for example, the familiar and customary definition of the circular line. It is said that the circular line is a curved line whose several points are equidistant from the centre. But the words 'curved line' are here quite unnecessary, being already contained in the concept of what follows; for every line whose parts are equidistant from the centre must necessarily be curved. In a definition, therefore, we must not co-ordinate what is already contained under the same concepts.",[]],"blomberg-s10-057":["Description is distinguished from definition. A description is a distinct concept which is complete only *comparative*. Its exhaustiveness suffices merely for my purpose, though it may be wholly contrary to the purposes of all others. Description is thus not, like definition, absolutely complete; that is, it does not serve every aim, but only my private aim. It is not, however, on that account to be rejected.",[]],"blomberg-s10-058":["We may now observe: [934a] All definitions are either *definitiones nominales* or *definitiones reales*. Nominal definitions are those which contain all that is equal to the whole concept which we form of the thing; real definitions are those which contain all that belongs to the thing in and for itself.",[]],"blomberg-s10-059":["Or [otherwise expressed]:",[]],"blomberg-s10-060":["Those definitions are nominal whose marks, taken together, are adequate to the whole concept which we think by the expression of the *definitum*: for example, when I think and say that salt is what dissolves in water. This I do indeed think in the concept of salt, but these are not all the possible characteristics of salt itself. [934b] Real definitions, on the other hand, are those whose marks make up the whole [ähnlichen; reading doubtful] concept of the thing. Now",[]],"blomberg-s10-061":["first, all definitions of arbitrary concepts,",[]],"blomberg-s10-062":["secondly, all definitions of concepts of reason, and thirdly, all definitions of arbitrary inventions are real definitions. But why, we ask, are the definitions of arbitrary concepts always real definitions? Because it rests solely with me to devise the concept and to fix it as I please. The whole concept has thus no other reality than that which my devising wills. Consequently I can always posit in a thing all the elements which I name, and these must then also make up the whole [934c] possible concept of the thing; for the whole thing is actual only through my will.",[]],"blomberg-s10-063":["Secondly, the definitions of things which are arbitrary inventions, and consequently also the definitions of all works of art [Kunstwerke], are *definitiones reales*; for these are always first fixed by an arbitrary concept.",[]],"blomberg-s10-064":["We shall now note that all mathematical definitions are definitions of arbitrary concepts. The mathematicians have also some given concepts, but these they cannot define; nor ought the mathematician to do so, for otherwise he philosophises, and that is a gross fault in a mathematician, a fault into which Wolff himself fell. The mathematician does not concern himself in the least with what others think under the concept; he says: this I will so name, and so it shall be entitled. As soon, therefore, as the mathematician represents the concept in the definition as given, he philosophises; and only then can one dispute with him, not before. He then encroaches upon the philosopher, who can thereupon lay claim against him. For this reason a mathematician cannot define place, direction, straight line, and so forth, for these are all concepts already given.",[]],"blomberg-s10-065":["As regards this further point, [936-939] many arbitrary concepts, and also heuristic definitions, are indeed to be met with in philosophy; but they are in no way philosophical definitions.",[]],"blomberg-s10-066":["We can therefore now say: all definitions of arbitrary concepts are",[]],"blomberg-s10-067":["first, real; secondly, each of them is free from all error; thirdly, each of them is a complete definition. Arbitrary definitions cannot be false at all; for in saying 'I will that this shall be so entitled', [the definer] makes it so entitled, and he could commit no error even if he deliberately wished to. Only in respect of *praecisio* can he go wrong; but this is not an error, merely a fault. Here again we see what great advantage the mathematician has: all his concepts are true, and he can go wrong only in respect of *praecisio*. One may, however, say that he can offend against usage; for example, when he calls the circumference of a sphere or of a polygon the *peripheria*, whereas it ought to be called the *perimeter*. Further, the names of the things occurring in the arts are likewise always true; their inventor is always right, since they are arbitrary concepts. But when we wish to define the works of art of others, we must hear what those others mean by them. These definitions of arbitrary concepts are all synthetic and arise only *per synthesin*; for no concept can arise *per analysin*, but can only be given distinctness thereby. In arbitrary concepts, however, the concept is to arise, and hence all arbitrary definitions are made only *per synthesin*. A definition arises *per analysin* only in so far as the *definitum* is given to us confusedly,",[]],"blomberg-s10-068":["and I can then make it distinct *per analysin*. *Per synthesin*, on the other hand, I make the concept together with its distinctness. With regard to given concepts we may therefore note: all given concepts are given either through reason or through experience. Those given through reason are made [distinct] *per analysin*; those given through experience are made *realiter per synthesin*. Now all concepts of reason can be defined by *definitiones reales*. For since the concept is already given me through reason, I need only think in it what reason shows me in the thing, and in this way I then have a real definition. Hence we can also say that all metaphysical and moral definitions can be real, and are made only *per analysin*; for the concept is already given through reason, and I have merely to make it distinct, which is done only *per analysin*. Such, for example, is the concept of honesty, and so on. But how, secondly, do matters stand with empirical concepts in respect of their definition? Here we must note: the definitions of empirical concepts are never nominal otherwise than *per analysin*; and, secondly, empirical concepts are never real otherwise than *per synthesin*. With an empirical concept I always ask: what is given? I can then make a definition of it in two ways, *nempe vel definio conceptum datum, vel definiendum est definitum ipsum* [namely, either I define the given concept, or the *definitum* itself is to be defined]. The former is in all concepts a nominal definition, the latter a real definition. For my concept contains only what I think in the thing, not what belongs to it by its nature; if I wish to attain the latter, I must make a real definition. And in empirical concepts this is done only *per synthesin*, just as, on the other side, I obtain the nominal definition of them only *per analysin*.",[]],"blomberg-s10-069":["We now ask: is a definition possible at all in the case of empirical concepts? By no means; of all empirical concepts there can be only a description. At most it appears that nominal definitions might be possible. For to define *realiter* means to indicate all the marks which, taken together, make up the whole; a real definition is thus the *completudo* of the marks which belong to the thing itself alone. But in empirical concepts I define not the object but only the concept which one thinks in connection with the thing. The marks of experience are therefore changeable, and serve merely for nominal definition.",[]],"blomberg-s10-070":["Since, then, the concept is already given through experience, and I am to make it distinct, I need only enumerate all the marks which I think under the expression of the *definitum*; and so it does indeed appear that a nominal definition might still be had in its case. Yet not even this is possible. For in the very naming of empirical concepts we are not all of one opinion, since one person has quite a different experience of the thing from another. Hence of empirical concepts only a description can be conceived, and this I can regard only in relation to my own aim, not to every aim.",[]],"blomberg-s10-071":["But what, we ask, is our aim in this?",[]],"blomberg-s10-072":["The aim is not to obtain knowledge of the *definitum* through its analysis, but merely to give the *definitum* a meaning. The end in view is thus merely the [established] meaning [Vorbedeutung], in order that it be not wholly lost from the language. For in the Harz mountains, for example, they already call spruces firs and firs spruces. I define an empirical concept in order afterwards always to add something to it *per synthesin*; but I never define a concept of experience in order to come to know the object through analysis. I do not define a concept of experience, gold for example, in order to infer something from it and to draw consequences from the definition, but merely to fix thereby the verbal meaning of the *definitum*. Here we note that the definitions of morals are analytic, and serve to enable me to draw from the concept all possible consequences. The definitions of morals are all real, and I need therefore only analyse them. It is thus really curious that from the use of definitions we can infer their particular kind. Empirical definitions concern only the understanding, not reason (for it is merely through the understanding that I discern the meaning); they have, therefore, no philosophical use whatsoever. It follows that definitions of empirical concepts are quite unnecessary; for even if some could actually be given, they would still be merely nominal definitions. For in their case I do not say 'all this taken together makes up the whole concept of the thing', but only 'this is all that I think in the concept of the thing'. All empirical concepts are capable only of description; all arbitrary concepts are synthetic, and their definition real. Finally, all concepts of reason, being given, are analytic, and their definitions likewise real. We now make these observations. In all given concepts of reason the definition is not what comes first; what comes first is the confused concept of the *definitum*. For with given concepts of reason I am only to make the confused concept distinct through the definition, so that the confused concept of the *definitum* must precede all definition. Only in arbitrary concepts does the definition always come first, since it is the definition that then gives the concepts their *esse*. All distinctness of definitions can be attained only through reason *per analysin*, and the concept of reason becomes complete only successively, namely, as I consider one clear mark after another. And since every mode of knowledge which has clear marks is a judgment, all definitions are at the same time judgments. All analytic definitions are preceded by many *judicia elementaria*, which expound the marks contained in the concept of the *definitum*. It is sometimes convenient for my purpose to prove something where a single mark is necessary and the whole definition is often superfluous. For example, when I say that the soul of man is incorporeal, I do not need the whole definition; I take out only the one mark, namely divisibility, and say: every body is divisible, but the soul is simple; consequently it cannot be corporeal.",[]],"blomberg-s10-073":["In philosophy one must never close the definition too early, unless I can know the whole thing from a single mark; for this obstructs the way to knowledge of the thing. Before I close the definition I must long continue to analyse the concept and still suspend my judgment, so that in the meantime I may perhaps attain a more exhaustive knowledge of the object.",[]],"blomberg-s10-074":["As regards Wolff's fault, it consists in his having sought to introduce the mathematical procedure into philosophy. All concepts in *mathesis* are arbitrary concepts, and must not be given at all.",[]],"blomberg-s10-075":["The particular is known from the universal. In universal concepts I have",[]],"blomberg-s10-076":["first, to attend to the lower concepts which are contained in the universal,",[]],"blomberg-s10-077":["secondly, to the difference of these lower concepts also; and all this takes place in logical division. This synthetic method consists, namely, in my comparing the *sphaera* of the concept with all its lower concepts, yet in such a way that I attend also to their proper difference.",[]],"blomberg-s10-078":["To a correct *divisio* there belongs",[]],"blomberg-s10-079":["first, that it be complete; that is, that the *membra dividentia* (these are just the lower concepts), taken together, make up exactly the universal concept.",[]],"blomberg-s10-080":["Secondly, the *membra dividentia* must also be properly opposed to one another, so that I can truly say 'either-or'. There must thus always be an actual difference. For example, I shall not divide thus: all men are either virtuous, or vicious, or philosophers. This [last] concept does indeed lie in [the concept of] man, but it is not represented *in oppositione*. A division can be either a *codivisio* or a *subdivisio*. A subdivision is one in which the *membrum dividens* is in turn subordinated [i.e. itself divided].",[]],"blomberg-s10-081":["The rules which the author here adduces are of no use to us whatsoever. Through them I merely learn to express in formulae, and to represent *in abstracto*, what I already know, and what I actually practise *in concreto* if I simply follow my sound reason.",[]],"blomberg-s11-001":["Properly speaking, we are already acquainted with judgments from the preceding section, since we there treated of distinct concepts, and these can arise only through a judgment. For to know distinctly means to know everything through a clear mark; and to know something through a clear mark is just to judge. We can therefore also say that distinct concepts are those which are known through a judgment. Now the two concepts which are compared with one another, in such a way that the one contains the marks and the other the clarity [Klarheit; perhaps read: the one contains the mark, the other receives clarity], make up the matter of the judgment. The concept which is to be made distinct through comparison with its mark is entitled the subject; the concept which is added as mark, as ground of distinctness, is entitled the predicate.",[]],"blomberg-s11-002":["Comparison is twofold: I can compare two concepts either through the relation of connection or through the relation of conflict with one another. A judgment in which the predicate is thought in connection with the subject is entitled an affirmative judgment; a judgment in which the predicate is thought in conflict with the subject is entitled a negative judgment. The form of the judgment is the relation. The sign which indicates this relation, or the form, is the copula *est*. And since every relation is either affirmative or negative, the copula *est*, when simply affirmative, is accompanied by 'yes' (that is, simply by *est*), but when negative, by *id est non*. The negation 'no', or *non*, does not affect the matter at all, but only the form of the judgment. It must therefore be placed not with the subject or the predicate, but with the copula: for example, *anima non est mortalis*, not *anima est non mortalis*. For otherwise the negation of the judgment of reason affects not the form but the matter of the judgment, and consequently it is then not a negative judgment, but a so-called *judicium infinitum*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-003":["In every judgment there occurs",[]],"blomberg-s11-004":["first, the matter of the judgment, that is, subject and predicate. The matter of the judgment is entitled the *termini* of the judgment. For reasons which we shall hear later, we call the subject the *terminus minor*, while the predicate is with us entitled *terminus major*, and is always regarded as a universal concept under which the *conceptus subjecti* is contained. All judgments have distinctness as their end; and in a judgment the predicate is always represented as a ground of the distinctness of the subject. Since, now, the ground of distinctness always lies in a mark, we see that the subject must always be contained under the predicate as under a mark. The",[]],"blomberg-s11-005":["second element of every judgment is its form. This is always expressed by the copula *est*. The copula is posited *simpliciter* when it indicates the relation of two concepts in their connection; but when it expresses their relation in conflict, it is further accompanied by the word *non*. This *non* expresses the negation of the judgment, but, *NB*, only when it affects the form of the judgment, that is, the copula.",[]],"blomberg-s11-006":["Where the author says that all true judgments have a ground, [we observe:] the ground of truth is the condition of judgments. All indemonstrable judgments have no special ground of knowledge; they are themselves the grounds of knowledge, and consequently they cannot contain any grounds of truth. Of this kind, as the author here means it, is for example the judgment: every body is extended; for this judgment cannot be derived from anything further. When, now, the condition of truth is added to a judgment, the judgment is then determined. But every judgment is determined, and I cannot conceive how there should still be people who say that certain judgments are undetermined, among whom our author belongs. One ought properly to say 'unrestricted'; for whatever is not determined is never a judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-007":["All judgments are either universal or particular. A judgment is universal when the *nota* of the subject is either wholly contained in the *sphaera* of the predicate, or not contained in it at all. In the first case it is a universal affirmative judgment; in the other, namely when the *nota* of the subject lies wholly outside the *sphaera* of the predicate, a universal negative judgment. A particular judgment is one in which the *nota* of the subject is only in part contained, or not contained, under the *sphaera* of the predicate. In the former case it is a particular affirmative, in the latter a particular negative judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-008":["There are no further judgments *quoad quantitatem*. For as regards singular judgments (*judicia singularia*), those in which the subject is an individual, these already belong to universal judgments. We may, however, note this: a singular judgment must be either wholly affirmative or wholly negative. Nothing more is possible in its case, for it belongs to the universal",[]],"blomberg-s11-009":["judgment, and thus all singular judgments are universal. This, then, was the division of judgments *quoad quantitatem*, namely into universal and particular judgments. In respect of quality all judgments are either affirmative or negative, and indeed either universal affirmative, or universal negative, or particular affirmative, or particular negative. The logicians express the first by A, the second by E, the third by I and the fourth by O. This whole notation (the *Barbara Celarent*) is mere pedantry, as indeed the whole of syllogistic is mere pedantry; and I shall show later that every syllogism outside the first figure is not simple, as is falsely supposed, but is composed of a *consequentia immediata* and a *ratiocinium*. Although syllogistic is unnecessary, one must nevertheless be acquainted with it.",[]],"blomberg-s11-010":["The following observation is well worth noting: *Omnia judicia sunt vel problematica, vel assertoria* (decisive). A problematic judgment is one in which I merely consider the relation of two concepts indeterminately, but do not posit it. In it I weigh something as given, for investigation; that is, I fix no relation in it, but only see what would follow if it were actually so. Such judgments are nevertheless of great use. For through the relation of two such judgments we can learn to exercise a certain caution in judging, and to make our judgments advantageous.",[]],"blomberg-s11-011":["Every relation in judgments is either a relation of connection or a relation of conflict. The judgment by which the relation of connection of two judgments with one another is indicated is entitled a hypothetical judgment; that in which the relation of conflict of two judgments is indicated is entitled a disjunctive judgment. Judgments in which the relation of two judgments to one another is thought are called judgments of relation (*relativa*). These judgments consider the relation of one judgment to another either as regards connection, and they are then hypothetical judgments, or as regards conflict, and they are then disjunctive judgments. In the former we always find the relation of ground to consequences. In conditional judgments, that which contains the ground is entitled the *antecedens* or *prius*; that which contains the consequences is entitled the *consequens* or *posterius*. As regards disjunctive judgments, the relation of conflict in them is again of two kinds: it is a relation either of two or of more judgments which contradict one another. We shall show, however, that a true disjunctive judgment is possible only with two judgments and not with more; for no true and pure contradiction can occur otherwise than between two concepts which stand exactly opposed to one another. [Such a judgment] is then also entitled *propositio bimembris*. And since disjunctive judgments contain the relation of a true contradiction, they can comprise only two judgments. When we judge purely rationally, the *propositio disjunctiva* in the judgment must be *bimembris*. All disjunctive judgments whose *propositio disjunctiva* is not *bimembris* have arisen from induction; for how else do I know that I have correctly enumerated the cases than from experience, and so *per inductionem*? A disjunctive judgment, if it is to be certain without induction, must be *bimembris*. A disjunctive judgment is nothing more than a representation of a logical division, or *repraesentatio divisionis logicae*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-012":["An exponible judgment is one which in a concealed manner really contains two judgments. This is not to say that it has two subjects or predicates; rather, the judgments in it are twofold in quantity and also in quality, for they are both affirmative and negative. Logic has the duty of expressing [exprimiren; perhaps for: expounding] exponible judgments. What do the questions *Quae? Qualis? Quanta?* signify? First, the question *Qualis est propositio?* aims at the quality, namely whether the judgment was affirmative or negative. Secondly, *Quanta est propositio?* contained the question whether it was a universal or a particular judgment. Thirdly, *Quae est propositio?* is the question whether the judgment is *purum* or *modale*. A judgment is entitled modal which carries a condition of judging with the predicate. These questions must be answered according to the character of the judgment. Hence, when an empty judgment occurred, it was customary to say: *Quae? qualis? quanta?*, that is, there is not the least thing in the judgment.",[]],"blomberg-s11-013":["A judgment is practically expressed when it enunciates a possibly necessary action. To say that something is possibly necessary seems indeed contradictory; but here it is quite correct. For the action is always necessary, namely if I wish to bring the thing about; but the case is not necessary, only possible. It is very good that in judgments of this kind one does not employ the imperative, [965a] but only the infinitive, and expresses them by it, as in 'how, if', and so on.",[]],"blomberg-s11-014":["'Demonstrably certain' signifies nothing other than a mediately certain judgment. A judgment, on the other hand, is absolutely indemonstrable when it is immediately certain. And such judgments must be met with in the human understanding. For however many consequences we may have, and however we may know these from their grounds as from their marks, we must at last arrive at a ground which is not in turn the consequence of another ground, but is itself the very first mark of all consequences; and this is then immediately certain, an indemonstrably certain judgment. Whether there is only one such absolutely first ground or many is treated in metaphysics.",[]],"blomberg-s11-015":["All indemonstrable judgments, in so far as they are the ground of all judgments, are entitled principles [Grundsätze], and they are either theoretical or practical. A practically indemonstrable proposition is entitled a *postulatum*. All these *principia* are either material, in so far as they have a *medius terminus*, or formal, having no *medius terminus*. The latter have no determinate predicate, but concern merely the relation of predicates to subjects in general. To these belong the *principium contradictionis* and the *principium identitatis*. For them there is no determinate subject and predicate, no determinate case; they can be applied to any subject and predicate. Now in our judgments we have a twofold form, namely the synthetic and the analytic; and the *principium contradictionis* is analytic only.",[]],"blomberg-s11-016":["Here the author gives directions for demonstration. Just as there are tautological *definitiones*, so there are also tautological *resolutiones*, in which one answers with precisely what was asked; and this is what our author does here. When a *quaestio* is before us, we must attend very carefully whether the proffered answer and *resolutio* of the *quaestio* is not merely an analysis [Zergliederung] of the *quaestio* itself. For in that case I have not taught the other person how to resolve the *quaestio*; I have merely made known to him, by analysis, what was [already] contained in it. I have thus done no more than make him understand better what he asked me. The author often proceeds in this way. It is curious: he shows me only that I am to do what I am asking him about, and does not show me at all how I am to do it. In my last disputation, *De mundi sensibilis atque intelligibilis forma et principiis*, I too had various *quaestiones* to resolve. But I did not merely analyse the *quaestiones*; I in fact also indicated entirely correct and complete marks of the things in question. For instance, p. 30, § 25 (see the *Dict. Metaph.*, to which this disputation is bound in), where the following infallible mark of sensibility is adduced: *Si de conceptu quocunque intellectuali generaliter quicquam praedicatur, quod pertinet ad respectus Spatii atque Temporis: objective non est enuntiandum et non denotat nisi conditionem, sine qua conceptus datus sensitive cognoscibilis non est* [If anything which pertains to the relations of space and time is predicated universally of any intellectual concept whatsoever, it is not to be asserted objectively, and denotes only the condition without which the given concept is not sensitively knowable]. See ibid.",[]],"blomberg-s11-017":["Where the author speaks of intuitive judgments. An intuitive judgment [anschauendes Urtheil] is, properly, a singular judgment in so far as it is known immediately; a *judicium discursivum*, on the other hand, is an inferred judgment. All immediate judgments, however, are intuitive only because they are known immediately, in so far as their form is sensitive. For otherwise, when the form is rational, immediate judgments are none the less not intuitive judgments at all. Only discursive judgments could be *contradistinguished* from intuitive judgments; and the ancients entitled universal",[]],"blomberg-s11-018":["judgments *discursiva*, but singular judgments *intuitiva*; and so we shall take them here also. This, then, concerns intuitive judgments, or judgments of experience. Whether a judgment is a judgment of experience or not is a question for metaphysics to investigate. Logic does not consider judgments in so far as they are given, as metaphysics does, but in so far as it is to compare them. Logic shows only whether judgments are universal or particular, and is thus occupied only with *oppositio* and identity. All propositions are either theoretical or practical. The former are those which show only the relation of concepts to other concepts; the latter indicate the relation of an action to concepts. Both kinds of proposition are, however, either demonstrable or indemonstrable. Theoretically demonstrable propositions, when they arise through a *ratiocinium polysyllogisticum*, are entitled *theoremata*. Every demonstrable proposition which is merely monosyllogistic is entitled a *corollarium*. A practically demonstrable proposition is a *problema*; a practically indemonstrable proposition is a *postulatum*. In all theoretically demonstrable propositions the proposition itself is the *theorema*, the proof of the proposition is the *demonstratio*; [in practical propositions] the action which is to be performed is entitled the *quaestio*, and the propositions according to which it is known [how to perform it] are its *resolutio*. A *lemma* is a proposition which is indeed demonstrable, not however in this discipline but in another, from which it has been taken. Here we must be very much on our guard not to assume something and give it out as already proved in another science, when it has in fact not yet been proved there at all. Indeed, we often find in authors something which they assume at the beginning and promise to prove at the end; but the proof has been forgotten. *Scholia* are propositions which are added to a doctrinal system [Lehrgebäude] not as *essentialia* but only as *accidentalia*.",[]],"blomberg-s11-019":["Every inference [Schluß] is in general a judgment, with only this difference: if the judgment is mediate, the inference is a syllogism [Vernunftschluß], that is, a mediate inference; but if the judgment is immediate, the inference is only an inference of the understanding [Verstandesschluß]. That is to say, either one thing is judged from another *absque medio termino* [without a middle term], and the judgment, or *conclusio*, is then *immediata*; or one thing is inferred from another *per medium terminum*, and the inference is then *mediata*, a syllogism. [In what follows,] however, we shall consider only immediate inferences [mittelbahre Schlüße]. As regards their mode of origin, it is fivefold; for they can arise either",[]],"blomberg-s11-020":["1) *per judicia aequipollentia* [through equivalent judgments], or",[]],"blomberg-s11-021":["2) *per judicia subalternata* [through subalternate judgments], or",[]],"blomberg-s11-022":["3) *per judicia opposita* [through opposed judgments], or",[]],"blomberg-s11-023":["4) *per conversionem logicam* [through logical conversion], or",[]],"blomberg-s11-024":["5) *per contrapositionem* [through contraposition]. As regards the *consequentia per judicia aequipollentia*, since the two judgments in it are wholly identical with one another, it is properly no consequence at all; the so-called inference *per judicia aequipollentia* is thus no inference at all. The *consequentia per judicia subalternata* takes place without any change in the position of the *termini*; the quality also remains the same, and only the quantity is altered. In the *consequentia per conversionem logicam* likewise the quality must never be altered, but only the quantity. In the *conversio logica* [read: contraposition?], however, both the position of the *termini* and the quality must be altered. With regard to the *oppositio judiciorum* logicians have made the following division:",[]],"blomberg-s11-025":["1. Two judgments are opposed *contradictorie* when a particular negative judgment is opposed to an [universal] affirmative judgment (or conversely). For instance: All men are mortal; but some are not mortal.",[]],"blomberg-s11-026":["2. Judgments are *subcontraria* when, of two particular judgments having the same subject and predicate, the one is affirmative and the other negative.",[]],"blomberg-s11-027":["3. There is also a *contrarietas judicii*, which occurs when, of two judgments, the one is universal affirmative and the other universal negative. *Judicia subcontraria* contain less than, that is, not enough of, what is required for a proper opposition; *judicia contraria* contain more; and *judicia contradictoria* contain just as much as suffices for a proper opposition. We can therefore say: *judicia subcontraria*, because they do not contain enough, (1) can both be true, but (2) cannot both be false.",[]],"blomberg-s11-028":["Secondly, *judicia contraria* can both be false; for each contains too much, and may be false precisely in this excess. Of two pure oppositions it must always hold that *posito uno tollitur alterum, et vice versa* [if the one is posited, the other is cancelled, and conversely]. In every logical conversion of judgments (see above, § 346) the judgment which is to be converted is entitled the *conversum*, and the judgment which is to arise from this conversion the *convertens*. A *conversio* in which the quality [read: quantity?] of the judgment is not affected [gehindert] is entitled *simplex*; where it is affected, *conversio per accidens*. Universal affirmative judgments can be converted *per accidens*; all [universal] negative judgments can be converted *simpliciter*; and lastly, particular [affirmative] judgments can all be converted *simpliciter*. This is the sole rule of logical conversion. The rule of contraposition is this: only universal affirmative judgments can be contraposed.",[]],"blomberg-s12-001":["Syllogisms [Vernunft-Schlüße] are judgments which take place mediately, by means of a *medius terminus*. For, as was said above, there are also certain judgments and consequences which we have entitled *immediatae*; these were indemonstrable. These consequences of one judgment from other judgments, which are indemonstrable, we also entitled not syllogisms but inferences of the understanding. On the other hand, however, there are also certain consequences from other, mediate judgments, and these are always demonstrable. There are uncertain, merely apparent inferences, which are not syllogisms but only *themata* for them; of this kind are the so-called *ratiocinia disjunctiva* and *hypothetica*, or also *speciosa*. Of these inferences, which are only *themata* for syllogisms, we shall not treat here at all, but only of syllogisms themselves.",[]],"blomberg-s12-002":["When two concepts are combined, and the one is the mark of the other, a certain relation is posited in these concepts. This relation can be represented immediately, and it is then a *judicium*; but if it takes place *per notam intermediam*, it is a *ratiocinium*. In other words, a syllogism is nothing but the comparison of a *nota remota* with the thing itself by means of a *nota intermedia*. The mediate relation of a *nota remota* [to the given concept is this]: it is always *remota* in respect of the thing, but is also the *nota proxima* of the *nota intermedia*, and therefore a *nota rei ipsius*; [its relation] to the given concept is a *judicium medium*, that is, a *ratiocinium*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-003":["An adequate inference consists in a syllogism whose rule is this: *nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius* [a mark of a mark of a thing is a mark of the thing itself]. This is also the fundamental rule of all syllogistic, and at the same time constitutes the whole difference between a judgment and a syllogism. For to a *judicium* there belongs an immediate clear mark, whereas to a *ratiocinium* there belongs in addition a mediate clear mark. The *ratiocinium* is consequently the relation of the *nota remota*, through a *nota mediata vel intermedia*, to a certain given concept of a thing. In a *ratiocinium*, therefore, these three acts occur: *primo*, the *nota proxima* or *intermedia* is compared with the *nota remota*; secondly, the *nota proxima* is compared with the thing; and *tertio*, the *nota remota* is compared with the thing itself. The *nota intermedia* is also entitled the *medius terminus*. Since in every judgment the predicate *constitutes* the *major terminus*, we can say that in every syllogism there are three *termini*: (1) the proposition [read: the subject?] which I am to know by means of the *nota intermedia*; (2) the *nota remota*; and (3) the *terminus medius*, or *nota intermedia*, which contains the ground of the relation of the thing to the *nota remota*. And these make up the three *termini* [read: propositions?], namely the *major*, the *minor*, and the *conclusio*. In every syllogism (1) the *terminus major* is compared with the *terminus medius*, that is, the *nota remota* with the *nota proxima*; (2) the *terminus medius* with the *minor*, or subject; and finally (3) the *minor* with the *major*, or predicate.",[]],"blomberg-s12-004":["To infer in this way, then, requires simply the synthetic procedure of reason. This always requires that we descend from the universal to the particular; to reverse this is contrary to reason, and a leap is at once committed. Hence the *major propositio*, which contains the predicate of the conclusion, must always come first. That is, I must first go from the *nota remota* to the *nota proxima* [read: from the *nota proxima* to the *nota remota*?], then from the *nota proxima* to the thing, and finally from the thing to the *nota remota*. This is the synthesis of reason, and if we lose sight of it we fail also to observe due order. In every syllogism, therefore, there are certain *termini*, of which there must not be more than three; and in every proper syllogism there are likewise not more than three *judicia*. The first two are entitled the premisses, the third judgment the conclusion. The premisses constitute the matter, the conclusion the form, of syllogisms; the correct form of a syllogism will therefore rest solely upon the correctness of the consequence of the conclusion. In both [premisses] the *medius terminus*, or *nota intermedia*, always occurs (but not at all in the consequent itself). Every syllogism is nothing but a representation of a mark by means of a *terminus medius*. It belongs to a syllogism that one mark be subordinated to another mark of the thing, as a *nota notae*. In a syllogism, then, I compare the *nota remota* with the *nota proxima*, then the thing with the *nota proxima*, and finally the *nota remota* with the thing itself. All form of syllogisms is only twofold, and there can be no more; for the judgment is either immediate or mediate",[]],"blomberg-s12-005":["The fundamental rule[s] of all affirmative syllogisms are these:",[]],"blomberg-s12-006":["1. *Nota notae rei est nota rei ipsius* [a mark of a mark of a thing is a mark of the thing itself].",[]],"blomberg-s12-007":["The sole fundamental rule, however,",[]],"blomberg-s12-008":["2. of all negative syllogisms will accordingly be this:",[]],"blomberg-s12-009":["*Repugnans notae rei repugnat rei ipsi* [what contradicts a mark of a thing contradicts the thing itself]. This, then, is the fundamental rule of all syllogisms.",[]],"blomberg-s12-010":["Logicians have a certain *dictum de omni et nullo* which they regard as the very first ground of all *ratiocinia*. But the fundamental rules here adduced themselves precede it.",[]],"blomberg-s12-011":["The *dictum de omni et nullo* runs thus among logicians: What belongs to a universal concept belongs also to all concepts contained under it.",[]],"blomberg-s12-012":["When in a syllogism the relation of two judgments (not of two concepts) is considered, it is entitled a *ratiocinium extraordinarium*; or, when a syllogism is adopted in which the *major propositio* is not a *judicium ordinarium* but a *judicium extraordinarium*, it is then likewise entitled a *ratiocinium extraordinarium*. All *ratiocinia extraordinaria* are either *hypothetica* or *disjunctiva*. For in them either the relation of connection or the relation of conflict is indicated: in the former case the syllogism is entitled hypothetical, in the latter disjunctive. The *judicium hypotheticum* consists of only two propositions, and no *judicium hypotheticum* can therefore yield a *ratiocinium*. In a *judicium hypotheticum* only the consequence need be proved, since nothing is *enunciated* or asserted in it; whereas in *judicia categorica* not only the consequence but the assertion itself must be proved from sound grounds. The conditional inference is not a syllogism, but only the *substratum* for a syllogism, since it has still to be proved *per ratiocinium*. It is first of all the *quaestio* itself, the *datum* which is still to be proved. Properly, therefore, nothing is yet proved by it; a ground is merely stated, of which it is at the same time affirmed that its consequence is correct. How it is correct, I do not thereby in the least discern. It is merely, so to speak, the ground of proof [Beweißgrund], not the proof itself [Beweißthum]; that is, it gives only the matter, not the form, of the proof. This analysis is certainly not unnecessary, and is entirely correct. For in a *judicium conditionale* no subject is connected with a predicate through a mediate mark, and in such an inference there is no *major propositio* at all. They are therefore not regular syllogisms, but only *substrata* for them: in them we do not actually infer, but merely state what we intend to infer. All proof concerns either the truth of the *antecedens* or that of the *consequens*. Every *syllogismus hypotheticus* has two *modi*: a *modus ponens*, in which an affirmation is inferred in the conclusion, and a *modus tollens*, in which a negation is inferred. An affirmation is here inferred according to the rule: *atqui verum est prius, ergo quoque posterius* [but the prior is true, therefore the posterior also]. A negation, on the other hand, is inferred from the falsity of the *consequens* to the falsity of the *antecedens*, according to the rule: *atqui falsum est prius* [read: *posterius*], *ergo quoque posterius* [read: *prius*]; *remoto antecedente non tollitur consequens, sed sublata consequentia tollitur antecedens* [the removal of the antecedent does not cancel the consequent, but the cancelling of the consequent cancels the antecedent]. Here, therefore, I can never infer *a negatione antecedentis* to the *negatio consequentis*, though I can well infer conversely.",[]],"blomberg-s12-013":["As regards disjunctive syllogisms, they are those in which the *major propositio* is disjunctive. A disjunctive syllogism is a logical division: in it I represent to myself a divided concept and all the *membra* which together are equal to the *divisum*. The subject of the *major propositio* is the *divisum*, and the predicate is all the *membra dividentia*. Consequently the correctness of a disjunctive syllogism rests on the correctness of a logical division: all [the members] must stand to one another in true disjunction and opposition, and in addition the whole must be complete.",[]],"blomberg-s12-014":["A *dilemma* is properly a *ratiocinium hypotheticum* whose consequent is disjunctive. In it, therefore, we proceed according to the rules both of hypothetical and of disjunctive inference. *Atqui falsum est prius, ergo etiam posterius* [read, as above: *falsum est posterius, ergo etiam prius*]. A *dilemma* is never employed as a *modus ponendi*, but always as a *modus tollendi*. By a *dilemma* we seek not to prove a proposition in regular fashion, but only to convince someone of [his] error. The *dilemma* is also entitled *cornutum*, because by means of it we refute the other person and at the same time cut off every way of escape: whichever way he goes, he is caught. Hence the *dilemma* is much used in dialectic, and for this reason too the sceptics were very fond of it.",[]],"blomberg-s12-015":["Here the author speaks of immediate consequences. This is quite out of place, for they are not syllogisms at all, but only inferences of the understanding, since they have no *medius terminus*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-016":["[Syllogisms] are either formal syllogisms or *ratiocinia cryptica*. *Ratiocinia cryptica* are those in which I enunciate only two judgments but think the third along with them. In such a *syllogismus* we always say less than we deliberately think; and this is the so-called dissimulation, which is very often salutary and fair. Simulation, on the other hand, is when we speak otherwise than we think, and this is certainly to be wholly rejected as unfair.",[]],"blomberg-s12-017":["An *enthymema* is an inference in which only two judgments, namely one *praemissa* and the *conclusio*, are posited. To these belong also the *ratiocinia contracta*. These are *syllogismi* in which there are not two propositions, but only one proposition together with the *medius terminus*. Induction, which is especially noteworthy, also belongs here. The rule of reason for apodeictic certainty in inferences always proceeds from the universal to the particular. There are none the less inferences in which we infer from the particular to the universal. The former is *ratiocinatio rigida*, the latter *ratiocinatio laxa*. To the latter belong (1) *inductio* and (2) analogy. *Inductio* is the inference in which I hold what belongs to many things under a universal inference [read: concept] and concept to be true as though it belonged to all. I infer thus: what belongs to so many things, as many as I have ever known, must also belong to all that are of this kind and genus. This kind of inference is indeed wholly contrary to the logical rule; but we cannot dispense with it, and if it were abolished most of our modes of knowledge would have to be abolished with it.",[]],"blomberg-s12-018":["As regards, secondly, inference by analogy, it is nothing but an induction, though an induction only in respect of the predicate. That is, when two things have agreed in respect of all the properties which I have been able to know in them, they will also agree with one another in the remaining properties which I have not known in them; so runs the inference in respect of analogy. Analogy and induction are merely crutches for our understanding. For in universal propositions of truth we shall always be able to proceed with logical strictness; but in the employment of our knowledge (*a posteriori*) we must often make shift with the probable.",[]],"blomberg-s12-019":["There is a complete and also an incomplete induction. [It is] complete when its *membra* are equal to the given concept, or when everything can be derived from a particular concept.",[]],"blomberg-s12-020":["Every inference false in form is a *paralogismus*. Every false inference, however, which is meant to be a means of producing error in another person is entitled a *sophisma*. There are various *sophismata*, which might be given many names, but this is quite unnecessary. There are, however, some *sophismata* which deserve a place in logic, for some of them can be truer than others. The author also mentions here the *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*. For us only this need be noted: he who in a dispute forgets the question at issue commits a *sophisma ignorationis elenchi*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-021":["When syllogisms are *coordinated* with one another, there arises a *ratiocinium compositum*, or *polysyllogisticum*. A *ratiocinium* is entitled *polysyllogisticum* when the *conclusio* of one syllogism is the *praemissa* of another. The *syllogismus* whose *conclusio* is the premiss of another is entitled a *prosyllogismus*; the *syllogismus* whose *praemissa* is a conclusion of the preceding one is entitled an *episyllogismus*. Or, more briefly: the *prosyllogismus* contains the ground, the *episyllogismus* the consequence, of a syllogism. See the author, §§ 406, 408. There is a *sorites per syllogismos hypotheticos*, which is then also entitled *sorites hypotheticus*; or the *sorites* can arise *per syllogismos categoricos*, and it is then also entitled *categoricus*.",[]],"blomberg-s12-022":["Formal *ratiocinia* are used only in disputation, not in books and the like, for otherwise this would become too tedious. And as regards a multitude of syllogisms, they make things obscure rather than distinct. They are expressed only in order [to show] how reason actually thinks.",[]],"blomberg-s12-023":["Where the author speaks of the leap in proof, both legitimate and illegitimate, we need only note this: *nullus saltus in intellectu est possibilis, et non aliter saltus possibilis est, quam in enunciando* [no leap is possible in the understanding, and a leap is possible only in enunciation]. The immediate connection of a remote ground with its consequence is a leap. In thought such a leap is wholly impossible; but in enunciation it may well occur, and in that case it may sometimes be *legitimus*, namely when I believe that the other person will infallibly know what has been omitted, and so supply it in thought.",[]],"blomberg-s12-024":["The actual omission of a proposition which requires proof is entitled *petitio principii*. The term comes from *peto*, I beg. In this kind of persuasion I must, as it were, beg the other for his assent; and from this we see that it cannot be called a proof at all.",[]],"blomberg-s12-025":["The *circulus in demonstrando* is the fault of taking the conclusion of a proof as its own premiss. This occurs",[]],"blomberg-s12-026":["1) when we seek to prove what is indemonstrably true, or immediately certain;",[]],"blomberg-s12-027":["2) when we seek to prove indemonstrably false propositions. In both these cases the circle in proof is always unavoidable. But it chiefly arises",[]],"blomberg-s12-028":["3) when we are already certain, prior to all proof, of the truth of a proposition which is demonstrable. We then let the other person off the proof, and so a circle in proof can always creep in.",[]],"blomberg-s12-029":["A proof proves too little when it proves only part of the proposition. It proves too much when it proves more than can be proved. A proof which proves too little can be true; but a proof which proves too much is always false. For to say that the proof proves too much is to say that it proves more than is true, and this is nothing other than that it is false.",[]],"blomberg-s12-030":["Finally the author says something about the utility of syllogisms. This seems almost unnecessary; for who would not hold the employment of reason to be useful?",[]],"blomberg-s13-001":["Method [Lehrart] is nothing but the form of a whole of modes of knowledge, in so far as it is arranged according to the rules of logical perfection",[]],"blomberg-s13-002":["Since, however, logical perfection is twofold, namely logical perfection according to sound reason and logical perfection according to learning and science, method can be divided in like manner. For the rules of sound reason are distinct from the rules of science. In all sciences and in learning the method of sound reason must indeed prevail; but, conversely, not everything that is found in learning need also be found in sound reason. In all sciences I consider not how something appears in practice, but how it can be judged even prior to all practice; I consider how something can be thought also *in abstracto*. If, on the other hand, I proceed according to the rules of sound reason, I must always exhibit everything *in concreto*. The latter kind is a knowledge that carries a certain life with it; the former serves merely for speculation and the desire for knowledge. The methods of the learned differ, then:",[]],"blomberg-s13-003":["1) *quoad objectum*, that is, according to the matter which they comprise or to which they are directed. For the object can be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-004":["a) historical knowledge, or b) rational knowledge. To the latter belong, for instance, mathematics and philosophy; to the former geography and history proper [die eigentliche Geschichte] among the historical [disciplines].",[]],"blomberg-s13-005":["2) [They differ] also according to the subject. For there is",[]],"blomberg-s13-006":["a) a method of instruction for women, and a separate method for men;",[]],"blomberg-s13-007":["b) a method for the instruction of children, and another for adults;",[]],"blomberg-s13-008":["c) a quite separate method of instruction for persons destined for a lowly station, and again another for those destined for a higher station; and so forth. Our method must be arranged quite differently for a peasant than when it is applied even to a person of only middling rank.",[]],"blomberg-s13-009":["3) These differences may also concern the cognitive powers [Erkenntniß Kräfte]. For there may be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-010":["1. a method merely of sound reason, or",[]],"blomberg-s13-011":["2) the method may be scientific, or",[]],"blomberg-s13-012":["3) there may be a difference in the method itself, in its subject.",[]],"blomberg-s13-013":["4) The difference in respect of the second method, however, is this: the method can be either",[]],"blomberg-s13-014":["a) analytic",[]],"blomberg-s13-015":["b) synthetic.",[]],"blomberg-s13-016":["Analysis and synthesis are either of coordination or of subordination.",[]],"blomberg-s13-017":["α) The synthesis of coordination is the combination of the parts with the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s13-018":["β) The analysis of coordination is the resolution of the whole into its parts.",[]],"blomberg-s13-019":["γ) The synthesis of subordination is the combination of grounds with their consequences.",[]],"blomberg-s13-020":["δ) The analysis of subordination is the derivation of the consequence from its grounds.",[]],"blomberg-s13-021":["In all historical modes of knowledge the connection is that of coordination; in all rational modes of knowledge only the connection of subordination is to be found. In history I enumerate one thing after another, whether in space or in time; but in rational modes of knowledge and sciences we always derive one thing from another. In analytic coordination I proceed from the whole to its parts; in synthetic coordination from the parts to the whole.",[]],"blomberg-s13-022":["The analytic method is the most advisable in historical modes of knowledge. I must first form an idea of the whole, and then form an idea and concepts of the parts contained under it. In all rational modes of knowledge the form is that of subordination, and this is either synthetic or analytic: in the former I proceed from the ground to the consequence, in the latter from the consequence to the ground.",[]],"blomberg-s13-023":["The artificial method is *contradistinguished* from the natural. Just as we must distinguish between a rational [vernünftig] knowledge in the sense of one which does not contradict reason, and a rational knowledge in the sense of one which arises from reason, so too a mode of knowledge is called natural in a twofold sense: (1) when it agrees with the nature of the object and of the subject, and (2) when it springs from nature. Method must be natural not so much subjectively as objectively, that is, adequate to the object.",[]],"blomberg-s13-024":["Cf. Logik Philippi: xxviv:482.",[]],"blomberg-s13-025":["The *methodus tabellaris*, that is, understanding and outlining a whole science in tables, is very fine. But it requires insight not to make too many divisions, nor useless ones. Method by way of the dismemberment of a whole doctrinal system is no method; it is the mode of putting the parts together that constitutes the doctrinal system.",[]],"blomberg-s13-026":["*Affectatio* is the term for an unnatural art; everything is entitled affected by which one wishes to appear artful. Thus it is affected when one exhibits not one's art but traces of an artful inquiry. The affected is commonly bound up with pedantry, which shows itself especially when a single method is applied to everything, whether it suits or not.",[]],"blomberg-s13-027":["The Socratic method proceeds by dialogue, in which by well-arranged questions everything is drawn out of the pupil and he is led to the answers. It is of great use. It is a species of catechetical method, but it is very tiring.",[]],"blomberg-s13-028":["The Platonic method consisted in a free discourse, in which a system becomes apparent.",[]],"blomberg-s13-029":["In historical sciences there are two methods, the chronological and the geographical. Both are to be united; the latter is preferable to the former. In all connected modes of knowledge we must consider the whole before its parts, and among the parts the larger before the smaller, the higher divisions before the subdivisions.",[]],"blomberg-s13-030":["The author says of history that it does not belong to any doctrine; but it is just as much a doctrine as dogmatic truths are. We must distinguish:",[]],"blomberg-s13-031":["a) doctrine, that is, an interconnection of various modes of knowledge and teachings;",[]],"blomberg-s13-032":["b) discipline, when this interconnection is brought into a method;",[]],"blomberg-s13-033":["c) science, when the knowledge is completely worked out according to such a method.",[]],"blomberg-s13-034":["Science is a complete discipline. In doctrine I direct my attention only to what is taught; in discipline I look to the method. Doctrine and discipline are thus found in historical just as much as in dogmatic modes of knowledge. In sciences there must always be doctrine.",[]],"blomberg-s13-035":["We think tumultuarily when we think without method.",[]],"blomberg-s13-036":["To meditate is not to recall modes of knowledge which we have had, but to produce new ones which we have not yet had. Methodical meditation prevents us from procuring sufficient materials for meditation. At the beginning we meditate tumultuarily; we must write down what occurs to us, since thoughts sometimes come into our minds which we do not otherwise have. Let us first write down all thoughts as they come, without order; afterwards begin to coordinate them, and then to subordinate them. Every composition must be preceded by three tasks:",[]],"blomberg-s13-037":["1) We write down all thoughts without order.",[]],"blomberg-s13-038":["2) We make a general plan.",[]],"blomberg-s13-039":["3) We work out all the parts.",[]],"blomberg-s13-040":["Doubting exercises us in reflection, as does the endeavour to understand each person's opinion correctly and to be able to put oneself into it. It is good to alternate meditation with other, recreative sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s14-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi §§ 428–544, xxiv 484ff.",[]],"blomberg-s14-002":["The nature of words is a worthy object of the philosopher's consideration. Their right use is that they designate a thought exactly.",[]],"blomberg-s14-003":["A *terminus inanis* is one whose meaning we believe we understand, but in fact do not understand. An expression is deceptive (*deceptrix*) when we believe something to be possible, and it is nevertheless impossible.",[]],"blomberg-s14-004":["A *terminus familiaris* is an expression whose sense has never been examined, because it has never been attended to. Every language has such terms. I say, for instance, ‘süchtig’ [sickly]. Such *termini*, on closer examination, are often also deceptive.",[]],"blomberg-s14-005":["The word-mongering of the learned [*Krämerey der Gelahrten*] is a fraudulent traffic, in which words are passed off as things. What is more common than this expression, and [this] fraud! An expression is entitled intelligible when it can be understood. Something can be intelligible without being distinct. When two expressions have one meaning, or where at least their meanings are not very different, they are entitled synonymous expressions. If an expression has more than one meaning, it is entitled *terminus vagus, ambiguus, anceps* [a vague, ambiguous, doubtful term].",[]],"blomberg-s15-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 484ff.",[]],"blomberg-s15-002":["*Stylus* [*Styl*] properly signifies an implement of the sculptor. Here it signifies the character [of a person's manner] of expressing his thoughts. Everyone has his own particular style, just as everyone writes his own particular hand. Style is to be considered (1) in so far as it is appropriate to its object, (2) [in so far as it is appropriate] to the person (the subject) and his station, and (3) to those for whom one writes. A particular style is suited to each, and no one can put his thoughts into the form of another.",[]],"blomberg-s15-003":["The fitness [*Füglichkeit*] of the manner of writing consists in its being so constituted that it is not too lofty, but is suited to the matter. The euphony [*Wohllaut*] of the manner of writing belongs rather among chimeras than to what is genuine. The English say that Virgil's poems sound well; but if we were to hear an Englishman recite the verses, this would be intolerable to our ears. The *sonorum* [sonority] consists in the measure of the syllables. Congruence consists in the style's being in accordance with the matter. This can be learned only through experience.",[]],"blomberg-s16-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",[]],"blomberg-s16-002":["Of the *donum didacticum* [gift of teaching] and the requisites of a teacher. In general, two things must be found in a teacher. He must possess (1) a condescension to the weaker [capacities], and (2) a co-ascension [*Coadscendenz*] to the higher capacities",[]],"blomberg-s16-003":["1. Every teacher must know how to put himself in the place of his hearers. I can instruct another only by means of his own understanding; consequently I have to work upon a material which already lies in the other's head. This is entitled condescension. It presupposes that one knows how to put oneself in the place of those whom one instructs. Very few teachers of religion, and others, are able to do this.",[]],"blomberg-s16-004":["2. The second *requisitum* of a teacher is co-ascension [*Coadscendenz*], namely, that one rises up to those talents which are elevated above the middling. He who knows little certainly cannot do this. The best course is to choose for oneself a middle degree. But how must one conduct oneself in public lectures, where one does not know the capacity of the *auditores* [hearers]?",[]],"blomberg-s16-005":["Two things are to be distinguished from one another: to impart an opinion to someone, and to refute someone's opinion. In the first case the subject is empty; in the other I must first empty it. Before any instruction I must have the *data* [showing] wherein the other errs. Every error is to be regarded as a *phaenomenon* which deserves an explanation. I must first see how he could have arrived at the error. And I must also search out the part of truth in his error.",[]],"blomberg-s16-006":["A verbal dispute [*Wortstreit*] is when different persons believe that they are contending for different opinions, and yet disagree only in words, while in the matter itself they are agreed. Some have believed that [the dispute] concerning predestination is more a verbal dispute than a dispute about things. An *argumentum ad hominem* is an argument which, while not true for everyone, yet serves to reduce someone to silence, as when, for example, I draw something *ex concessis* [from what has been conceded], and from the particular propositions which another holds. These are good means of keeping someone off one's back and of ending the dispute, but not of finding the truth. *Retorsiones* and *reprehensiones* are personal disputes. Distinctions serve very well to extricate oneself from a tight corner. An *instantia* is when something is used universally, and I show that it is not universal. A defence which concerns the person is entitled an apology; if it concerns the judgments of others, it is entitled *refutatio*.",[]],"blomberg-s16-007":["A formal disputation is the proceeding in which, in the presence of learned men, one exposes certain propositions, in order to defend them against all objections. Disputing could be of great use, were it not a *certamen personarum* [contest of persons], and if one sought the truth not through contention but through agreement.",[]],"blomberg-s17-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 488ff.",[]],"blomberg-s17-002":["All learned writings are either historical or dogmatic. A writing can be historical as regards its matter, when it is something that has actually happened, or is knowledge of an *individuum*; and it can also be historical as regards its form. That form is entitled historical which is not rational, that is, in which I do not derive [anything] from grounds by means of universal knowledge.",[]],"blomberg-s17-003":["A mode of knowledge is entitled historico-dogmatic as regards its form when the *data* are *dogmata* but are expounded historically; as regards its matter, when some of it is historical and some dogmatic. Everything that happens is considered in connection with space and time. When we consider what happens in so far as it is at different times, this is entitled history; in so far as it is at the same time, it belongs to the field of geography. There are various kinds of geography and of history.",[]],"blomberg-s17-004":["The distinction which the author makes, in dividing history into political history, church history, learned history and private history, is determined by the different objects of historical modes of knowledge. There could also be a moral history, which would consider the morals of common life and also the moral *data*. Biography is the life of a single subject. All history, of whatever kind it may be, ought to have the end of extending reason; it ought to furnish the material for the employment of reason.",[]],"blomberg-s17-005":["That history is entitled pragmatic which becomes useful through universal rules. It can stand in relation either to speculation or to practical reason. If we merely learn the names of rulers and know [the facts of] their reigns, this furnishes little material for the employment of reason, that is, for speculation or for practical rules. At the outset, however, we must abstract from the employment of reason, and in good hope provide ourselves with a large store of historical information. Learned history becomes pragmatic when we consider learning solely in its relation to human reason, and attend to its growth or to the causes by which it has been held back. There are two methods of composing dogmatic writings: either tumultuarily or methodically. The latter method brings a system into being. A system is any whole whatsoever. Systematic writings are distinguished from tumultuary writings in that they constitute a connected whole. Extracts,",[]],"blomberg-s17-006":["*epitomae*, are actual systems. In dogmatic writings the *principia* of the dogmatists must be expounded, then drawn together *in concreto* and weighed; and thereupon something historical must be interspersed, in order to give evidence.",[]],"blomberg-s17-007":["The method in which one does not draw a mode of knowledge from its own sources, but takes it from those who have drawn from the source, is entitled compiling. A history is compiled when I have not written it from the first sources, but have put it together from later [writers]. One can also compile philosophical propositions, gathering together what others have thought by means of reason. One nevertheless shows skill when, with prudent choice, one assembles what is most important from authors of great acuteness and from the most thorough histories. The plagiarist is distinct from the compiler. The latter [*Der lezte*: i.e. the plagiarist] pretends that he has himself drawn from the first learned sources, although he presents only the product of another.",[]],"blomberg-s17-008":["The French are especially of such a *caractère* that with them the sciences grow more *extensive* than *intensive*. Much understanding is required for *epitomae*, in order to be able to choose what is most important and most comprehensible.",[]],"blomberg-s18-001":["Cf. Logik Philippi § 428-544, xxiv 493ff.",[]],"blomberg-s18-002":["The character of a learned man is twofold: the character of the head and that of the heart. The first concerns knowledge; the latter, inclination. The character of the heart has a close connection with the character of the head, if learning is to be advanced. The constitution of the heart has a great influence upon the direction of the understanding, and is therefore not a matter of indifference even in the merely speculative sciences.",[]],"blomberg-s18-003":["The character of the head concerns understanding, reason and taste. The capacity for imitation is directly opposed to the capacity of genius. Those who possess the capacity of genius sometimes lack the spirit of imitation, which, on the other hand, is in large degree characteristic of most of those who show no genius. The Russians are said to have the failing that they lack genius, and for that very reason cannot teach [the sciences]. Hence with them science always dies out. Genius is required for philosophy, and likewise for the fine sciences; but for the useful sciences the spirit of imitation [is required]. Mathematics needs only imitation. Learning and good taste are distinct from one another.",[]],"blomberg-s18-004":["Mother-wit [*Mutterwitz*] is the natural capacity of sound understanding and sound reason. It cannot be learned; what is afterwards learned is entitled school-wit [*Schulwitz*]. All instruction presupposes certain modes of knowledge, or at least the capacity for them. School-wit thus presupposes mother-wit. It is more tolerable to lack school-wit than to have it without mother-wit. [In such a case] what has been learned is fine in the man, but he himself is absurd in applying it. Hence pedantry arises, and one becomes through school-wit even more absurd than otherwise, since one then has more materials. School-wit is a completion of mother-wit.",[]],"blomberg-s18-005":["To the particular learned exercises belongs",[]],"blomberg-s18-006":["1. Learning. This takes place through oral exposition or through the reading of books. We learn either by producing a mode of knowledge or by reproducing modes of knowledge already possessed. To teach properly means to let modes of knowledge be produced through others. In this respect the learner is purely passive; but he must also be active. He must have receptivity, that is, a capacity of the understanding, and must also exercise it. Oral exposition has a great advantage over written, for the oral is not so artificial as the written. It is also more comprehensible, since it involves one act of the mind fewer than reading does: in reading, namely, I must also hear in thought.",[]],"blomberg-s18-007":["2. The reading of learned writings. A great deal depends, first, upon the order in which one reads, and upon the choice of the writing with which one begins. The question arises: should compendia or large works be read first? One should not begin with compendia which contain many thoughts in little space, but with those which contain few thoughts in a wide space. In mathematics and in history one must first make oneself acquainted with what is general. One must at first proceed by main heads. The historical books which one chooses to read must at first concern not so much the precise events as the general ones. One must follow a certain order, reading books of speculation in the morning and evening, and historical books at midday. One must read much and, so to speak, digest what has been read; and the field must not be changed too much. Every book must be read at least twice, once quickly, marking the passages which require explanation. Some books are of great importance and require much investigation; these must be read often: for example, Hume, Rousseau, Locke, who can be regarded as a grammar for the understanding, and Montesquieu on the Spirit of the Laws",[]],"blomberg-s18-008":["Too frequent repetition is just as harmful as too long an interruption. § 546: Imitation in matters of style is not advisable. § 548: No rule can be given for the discovery of new truths. § 549: An *autodidactus* is one who makes himself learned through meditation and reading. Such a one is always a great genius. Oral exposition is, however, always to be preferred; [through it] one can make more *reflexiones*.",[]],"blomberg-s18-009":["Learned diligence signifies the strength of the exertion which one applies in learning something. Man is by nature indolent, and idleness is itself the final end of his diligence. Future rest is the spur which sets men in activity. The greatness and worth of the objects can nevertheless greatly exercise diligence. Learned diligence, when it is continued, is entitled steady diligence. From this is distinguished vehement diligence, which consists in an excessively strong exertion.",[]],"blomberg-s18-010":["The mind suffers under vehement diligence; the representations are soon extinguished.",[]],"blomberg-s18-011":["One who studies must first carefully weigh the end and the extent of his powers. It is unfortunate that others prescribe the end for us, and that we do not yet know ourselves when we begin to study. One must never study with vehement diligence, but one must never cease to learn. Only when one is near the end does one know how to judge what one had to learn. It also belongs to the character of a learned man that he be a person without title, station or civil rank; above all, that he be a man of all stations, and that his knowledge be of general use, and not suited merely to the form of the school. The final end of learning ought to be to give the human race its true form, to free it from prejudices, to refine its manners and to elevate the powers of the soul. Then it is a benefaction to the human race; but if it is used merely for *operation* [i.e. for mere display or trade], it is to be regarded only as a kind of luxury",[]],"blomberg-s18-012":["End of the Logic",[]]};
